I content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped access control with user and tenant ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, webhooks, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | At GA                          |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                       |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                 |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, granting time-limited download access without requiring authentication; the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, allowing a client to upload or download without routing traffic through FileStorage                                                         |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Tenant Policy       | A set of rules defined by a tenant governing allowed file types, size limits, webhooks, and sharing models                                                                                                                                                        |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n+> **Note**: Stakeholder needs are managed at the project/task level by the steering committee and are not duplicated in\n+> module specs. Focus on **actors** (users, systems) that directly interact with this module.\n+\n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.\n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+#### Authorization Service\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service`\n+\n+**Role**: Evaluates access decisions for read, write, and delete operations on `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+> **Note**: Project-wide runtime, OS, architecture, lifecycle policy, and module integration patterns are defined in\n+> root PRD. Only document module-specific deviations or additional constraints here.\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints. FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Tenant-level policies (file types, size limits, webhooks, sharing restrictions)\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend transfer via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- Indefinite file retention (phase 1); tenant-level and per-file retention policies (phase 2)\n+- REST API access interface\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- S3-compatible API (phase 2)\n+- WebDAV API (phase 2)\n+- Streaming and range requests (phase 2)\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends (phase 2)\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service (phase 2)\n+- Ownership transfer (phase 2)\n+- Custom metadata limits (phase 2)\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Added as a backend capability","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:30:25Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:30:26Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840617669","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840617669"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840617669"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840617669/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":131,"original_line":130,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2830405291,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":152,"position":153,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840620936","pull_request_review_id":3840802576,"id":2840620936,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUG-I","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1102 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped access control with user and tenant ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, webhooks, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | At GA                          |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                       |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                 |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, granting time-limited download access without requiring authentication; the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, allowing a client to upload or download without routing traffic through FileStorage                                                         |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Tenant Policy       | A set of rules defined by a tenant governing allowed file types, size limits, webhooks, and sharing models                                                                                                                                                        |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n+> **Note**: Stakeholder needs are managed at the project/task level by the steering committee and are not duplicated in\n+> module specs. Focus on **actors** (users, systems) that directly interact with this module.\n+\n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.\n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+#### Authorization Service\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service`\n+\n+**Role**: Evaluates access decisions for read, write, and delete operations on `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+> **Note**: Project-wide runtime, OS, architecture, lifecycle policy, and module integration patterns are defined in\n+> root PRD. Only document module-specific deviations or additional constraints here.\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints. FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Tenant-level policies (file types, size limits, webhooks, sharing restrictions)\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend transfer via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- Indefinite file retention (phase 1); tenant-level and per-file retention policies (phase 2)\n+- REST API access interface\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- S3-compatible API (phase 2)\n+- WebDAV API (phase 2)\n+- Streaming and range requests (phase 2)\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends (phase 2)\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service (phase 2)\n+- Ownership transfer (phase 2)\n+- Custom metadata limits (phase 2)\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+- File versioning\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code\n+> coverage unless otherwise specified.\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, created date, modified date, owner, download\n+availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **SHOULD** support additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation — transfer of ownership is out of scope.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Immutable ownership simplifies the authorization model and prevents accidental privilege\n+escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based\n+and tenant-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules` (initiating actors);\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service` (decision service)\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules` (initiating actors);\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service` (decision service)\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and tenant policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. To limit exposure, signed URLs **MUST** use short expiration\n+times. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead (served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+For backends that do not support presigned URLs, FileStorage **MUST** fall back to serving the content through its own\n+endpoint with signature validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload or download file content directly to/from the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage.\n+FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A\n+direct transfer URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support both upload (PUT) and download (GET) operations\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file and a single operation type (upload or download)\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying all traffic through FileStorage creates a bottleneck\n+and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend transfer via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for compatible\n+backends, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the\n+service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** detect and clean up orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed (presigned URL\n+issued but upload not started, or upload partially completed). The system **MUST** reconcile file metadata records\n+against actual backend object existence and remove records with no corresponding backend object. Cleanup **MUST** be\n+automatic and based on a configurable TTL for unconfirmed uploads.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. Garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Tenant Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to define file size limits with two levels of granularity: a global maximum size for\n+any file, and per-mime-type size limits that override the global limit. Uploads exceeding limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to control storage consumption and prevent oversized uploads, with the flexibility to allow\n+larger files for specific types (e.g., video vs. text).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Webhook Triggers\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webhook-triggers`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to configure webhooks triggered on file write operations (upload, update, delete).\n+Tenant policy **MUST** define which events trigger which webhook URLs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with external systems for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content moderation,\n+or backup triggers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** integrate with the Quota Enforcement service to enforce per-tenant storage consumption limits.\n+Uploads that would exceed the tenant's storage quota **MUST** be rejected. The system **MUST** report current storage\n+consumption to the Quota Enforcement service.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, creation date, last modified date, owner) is\n+**NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download availability **MUST**\n+update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Added \"Owner Deletion Handling\" requirement. From my perspective it should be quite flexible and handled by the \"Serverless Runtime\" module","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:31:14Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:31:15Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840620936","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840620936"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840620936"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840620936/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":620,"original_line":530,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2816272426,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":552,"position":642,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840623320","pull_request_review_id":3840804921,"id":2840623320,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUHjY","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"151b1979fdfc0611b945b1c34160d3f46b88d7e6","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Read audit logging (p3) is absent from the In Scope list while file encryption (p3) is included.**\n\nSection 4.1 lists file encryption at line 126 as a p3 in-scope item, but read audit logging (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`, section 5.8, p3) — added in response to reviewer feedback — is missing from this list. The inconsistency gives a false impression that read audit is out of scope.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n - File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+- Optional read audit logging for download and metadata operations (per policy)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 96 - 126, Add the missing read\naudit logging item to the \"In Scope\" list in Section 4.1 to match the\nrequirement cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit (p3); specifically, insert a\nbullet like \"Read audit logging for file access\n(cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit)\" alongside the existing \"File encryption\"\nentry so the scope list is consistent with section 5.8 and other p3 items.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @nonameffh\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:31:48Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:48:40Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840623320","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840623320"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840623320"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840623320/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":96,"original_start_line":96,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":126,"original_line":126,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":148,"position":148,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840623380","pull_request_review_id":3840804980,"id":2840623380,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUHkU","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1102 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped access control with user and tenant ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, webhooks, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | At GA                          |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                       |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                 |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, granting time-limited download access without requiring authentication; the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, allowing a client to upload or download without routing traffic through FileStorage                                                         |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Tenant Policy       | A set of rules defined by a tenant governing allowed file types, size limits, webhooks, and sharing models                                                                                                                                                        |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n+> **Note**: Stakeholder needs are managed at the project/task level by the steering committee and are not duplicated in\n+> module specs. Focus on **actors** (users, systems) that directly interact with this module.\n+\n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.\n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+#### Authorization Service\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service`\n+\n+**Role**: Evaluates access decisions for read, write, and delete operations on `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+> **Note**: Project-wide runtime, OS, architecture, lifecycle policy, and module integration patterns are defined in\n+> root PRD. Only document module-specific deviations or additional constraints here.\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints. FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Tenant-level policies (file types, size limits, webhooks, sharing restrictions)\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend transfer via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- Indefinite file retention (phase 1); tenant-level and per-file retention policies (phase 2)\n+- REST API access interface\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- S3-compatible API (phase 2)\n+- WebDAV API (phase 2)\n+- Streaming and range requests (phase 2)\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends (phase 2)\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service (phase 2)\n+- Ownership transfer (phase 2)\n+- Custom metadata limits (phase 2)\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+- File versioning\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code\n+> coverage unless otherwise specified.\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, created date, modified date, owner, download\n+availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **SHOULD** support additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation — transfer of ownership is out of scope.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Immutable ownership simplifies the authorization model and prevents accidental privilege\n+escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based\n+and tenant-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules` (initiating actors);\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service` (decision service)\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules` (initiating actors);\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service` (decision service)\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and tenant policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. To limit exposure, signed URLs **MUST** use short expiration\n+times. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead (served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+For backends that do not support presigned URLs, FileStorage **MUST** fall back to serving the content through its own\n+endpoint with signature validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload or download file content directly to/from the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage.\n+FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A\n+direct transfer URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support both upload (PUT) and download (GET) operations\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file and a single operation type (upload or download)\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying all traffic through FileStorage creates a bottleneck\n+and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend transfer via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for compatible\n+backends, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the\n+service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** detect and clean up orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed (presigned URL\n+issued but upload not started, or upload partially completed). The system **MUST** reconcile file metadata records\n+against actual backend object existence and remove records with no corresponding backend object. Cleanup **MUST** be\n+automatic and based on a configurable TTL for unconfirmed uploads.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. Garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Tenant Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to define file size limits with two levels of granularity: a global maximum size for\n+any file, and per-mime-type size limits that override the global limit. Uploads exceeding limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to control storage consumption and prevent oversized uploads, with the flexibility to allow\n+larger files for specific types (e.g., video vs. text).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Webhook Triggers\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webhook-triggers`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to configure webhooks triggered on file write operations (upload, update, delete).\n+Tenant policy **MUST** define which events trigger which webhook URLs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with external systems for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content moderation,\n+or backup triggers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** integrate with the Quota Enforcement service to enforce per-tenant storage consumption limits.\n+Uploads that would exceed the tenant's storage quota **MUST** be rejected. The system **MUST** report current storage\n+consumption to the Quota Enforcement service.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, creation date, last modified date, owner) is\n+**NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download availability **MUST**\n+update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Good point, added as p3","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:31:49Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:31:49Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840623380","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840623380"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840623380"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840623380/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":721,"original_line":557,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2816277304,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":579,"position":743,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840630069","pull_request_review_id":3840811792,"id":2840630069,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUJM1","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1102 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped access control with user and tenant ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, webhooks, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | At GA                          |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                       |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                 |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, granting time-limited download access without requiring authentication; the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, allowing a client to upload or download without routing traffic through FileStorage                                                         |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Tenant Policy       | A set of rules defined by a tenant governing allowed file types, size limits, webhooks, and sharing models                                                                                                                                                        |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n+> **Note**: Stakeholder needs are managed at the project/task level by the steering committee and are not duplicated in\n+> module specs. Focus on **actors** (users, systems) that directly interact with this module.\n+\n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.\n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+#### Authorization Service\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service`\n+\n+**Role**: Evaluates access decisions for read, write, and delete operations on `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+> **Note**: Project-wide runtime, OS, architecture, lifecycle policy, and module integration patterns are defined in\n+> root PRD. Only document module-specific deviations or additional constraints here.\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints. FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Tenant-level policies (file types, size limits, webhooks, sharing restrictions)\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend transfer via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- Indefinite file retention (phase 1); tenant-level and per-file retention policies (phase 2)\n+- REST API access interface\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- S3-compatible API (phase 2)\n+- WebDAV API (phase 2)\n+- Streaming and range requests (phase 2)\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends (phase 2)\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service (phase 2)\n+- Ownership transfer (phase 2)\n+- Custom metadata limits (phase 2)\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+- File versioning\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code\n+> coverage unless otherwise specified.\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, created date, modified date, owner, download\n+availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **SHOULD** support additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation — transfer of ownership is out of scope.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Immutable ownership simplifies the authorization model and prevents accidental privilege\n+escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user.","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","original_commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"> I would suggest to enforce every file to have a GTS type associated, so that we could easily implement access check for various actors. For example all the autogenerated files coming from LLMs would have a `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~` and user attached files would have `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.user.uploaded.v1~`.\r\n> \r\n> There could be more cases like this, for example `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.feedback.user.sysinfo.v1~` that would represent sysinfo reports from a use machine, and so on\r\n> \r\n> This approach would help us to isolate access to these group of files for different actors (modules), so that user feedback uploader would be able to see only own files\r\n\r\nReally good idea, added to the requirements","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:33:27Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:33:28Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840630069","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840630069"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840630069"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840630069/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":251,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2828192396,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":273,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840631703","pull_request_review_id":3840813417,"id":2840631703,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUJmX","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1102 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped access control with user and tenant ownership model","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","original_commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Updated","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:33:51Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:33:51Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840631703","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840631703"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840631703"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840631703/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":30,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2828224518,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":50,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840634294","pull_request_review_id":3840816032,"id":2840634294,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUKO2","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1102 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped access control with user and tenant ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, webhooks, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | At GA                          |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                       |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                 |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, granting time-limited download access without requiring authentication; the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, allowing a client to upload or download without routing traffic through FileStorage                                                         |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Tenant Policy       | A set of rules defined by a tenant governing allowed file types, size limits, webhooks, and sharing models                                                                                                                                                        |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n+> **Note**: Stakeholder needs are managed at the project/task level by the steering committee and are not duplicated in\n+> module specs. Focus on **actors** (users, systems) that directly interact with this module.\n+\n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.\n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+#### Authorization Service","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","original_commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:34:29Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:34:32Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840634294","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840634294"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840634294"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840634294/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":84,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2828235701,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":106,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840634760","pull_request_review_id":3840816462,"id":2840634760,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUKWI","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1102 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped access control with user and tenant ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, webhooks, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | At GA                          |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                       |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                 |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, granting time-limited download access without requiring authentication; the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, allowing a client to upload or download without routing traffic through FileStorage                                                         |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Tenant Policy       | A set of rules defined by a tenant governing allowed file types, size limits, webhooks, and sharing models                                                                                                                                                        |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n+> **Note**: Stakeholder needs are managed at the project/task level by the steering committee and are not duplicated in","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","original_commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Yes, missed some templating artifacts","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:34:35Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:34:36Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840634760","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840634760"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840634760"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840634760/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":63,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2828232538,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":83,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840636830","pull_request_review_id":3840818458,"id":2840636830,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUK2e","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1102 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped access control with user and tenant ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, webhooks, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | At GA                          |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                       |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                 |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, granting time-limited download access without requiring authentication; the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, allowing a client to upload or download without routing traffic through FileStorage                                                         |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Tenant Policy       | A set of rules defined by a tenant governing allowed file types, size limits, webhooks, and sharing models                                                                                                                                                        |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n+> **Note**: Stakeholder needs are managed at the project/task level by the steering committee and are not duplicated in\n+> module specs. Focus on **actors** (users, systems) that directly interact with this module.\n+\n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.\n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+#### Authorization Service\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service`\n+\n+**Role**: Evaluates access decisions for read, write, and delete operations on `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+> **Note**: Project-wide runtime, OS, architecture, lifecycle policy, and module integration patterns are defined in\n+> root PRD. Only document module-specific deviations or additional constraints here.\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints. FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Tenant-level policies (file types, size limits, webhooks, sharing restrictions)\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend transfer via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- Indefinite file retention (phase 1); tenant-level and per-file retention policies (phase 2)\n+- REST API access interface\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- S3-compatible API (phase 2)\n+- WebDAV API (phase 2)\n+- Streaming and range requests (phase 2)\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends (phase 2)\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service (phase 2)\n+- Ownership transfer (phase 2)\n+- Custom metadata limits (phase 2)\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+- File versioning\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code\n+> coverage unless otherwise specified.\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, created date, modified date, owner, download\n+availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **SHOULD** support additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation — transfer of ownership is out of scope.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Immutable ownership simplifies the authorization model and prevents accidental privilege\n+escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based\n+and tenant-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules` (initiating actors);\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service` (decision service)\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules` (initiating actors);\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service` (decision service)\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and tenant policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","original_commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Agree, updated","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:35:04Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:35:04Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840636830","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840636830"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840636830"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840636830/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":303,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2830345855,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":325,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840638019","pull_request_review_id":3840819749,"id":2840638019,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pULJD","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1102 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped access control with user and tenant ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, webhooks, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | At GA                          |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+|---------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                       |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                 |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, granting time-limited download access without requiring authentication; the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned URL pointing directly to the storage backend, generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, allowing a client to upload or download without routing traffic through FileStorage                                                         |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Tenant Policy       | A set of rules defined by a tenant governing allowed file types, size limits, webhooks, and sharing models                                                                                                                                                        |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n+> **Note**: Stakeholder needs are managed at the project/task level by the steering committee and are not duplicated in\n+> module specs. Focus on **actors** (users, systems) that directly interact with this module.\n+\n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.\n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+#### Authorization Service\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service`\n+\n+**Role**: Evaluates access decisions for read, write, and delete operations on `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+> **Note**: Project-wide runtime, OS, architecture, lifecycle policy, and module integration patterns are defined in\n+> root PRD. Only document module-specific deviations or additional constraints here.\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints. FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Tenant-level policies (file types, size limits, webhooks, sharing restrictions)\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend transfer via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- Indefinite file retention (phase 1); tenant-level and per-file retention policies (phase 2)\n+- REST API access interface\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- S3-compatible API (phase 2)\n+- WebDAV API (phase 2)\n+- Streaming and range requests (phase 2)\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends (phase 2)\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service (phase 2)\n+- Ownership transfer (phase 2)\n+- Custom metadata limits (phase 2)\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+- File versioning\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+> **Testing strategy**: All requirements verified via automated tests (unit, integration, e2e) targeting 90%+ code\n+> coverage unless otherwise specified.\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, created date, modified date, owner, download\n+availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **SHOULD** support additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation — transfer of ownership is out of scope.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Immutable ownership simplifies the authorization model and prevents accidental privilege\n+escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based\n+and tenant-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules` (initiating actors);\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service` (decision service)\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules` (initiating actors);\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-authz-service` (decision service)\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and tenant policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. To limit exposure, signed URLs **MUST** use short expiration\n+times. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead (served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+For backends that do not support presigned URLs, FileStorage **MUST** fall back to serving the content through its own\n+endpoint with signature validation.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload or download file content directly to/from the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage.\n+FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A\n+direct transfer URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support both upload (PUT) and download (GET) operations\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file and a single operation type (upload or download)\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying all traffic through FileStorage creates a bottleneck\n+and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend transfer via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for compatible\n+backends, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the\n+service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** detect and clean up orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed (presigned URL\n+issued but upload not started, or upload partially completed). The system **MUST** reconcile file metadata records\n+against actual backend object existence and remove records with no corresponding backend object. Cleanup **MUST** be\n+automatic and based on a configurable TTL for unconfirmed uploads.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. Garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Tenant Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to define file size limits with two levels of granularity: a global maximum size for\n+any file, and per-mime-type size limits that override the global limit. Uploads exceeding limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to control storage consumption and prevent oversized uploads, with the flexibility to allow\n+larger files for specific types (e.g., video vs. text).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Webhook Triggers\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webhook-triggers`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to configure webhooks triggered on file write operations (upload, update, delete).\n+Tenant policy **MUST** define which events trigger which webhook URLs.","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","original_commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Good catch, replaced by Event Broker integration","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:35:22Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:35:23Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840638019","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840638019"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840638019"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840638019/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":448,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2830380894,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":470,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840692002","pull_request_review_id":3840878381,"id":2840692002,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUYUi","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"151b1979fdfc0611b945b1c34160d3f46b88d7e6","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:48:03Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:48:03Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840692002","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840692002"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840692002"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840692002/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":96,"original_start_line":96,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":126,"original_line":126,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2840623320,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":148,"position":148,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840694693","pull_request_review_id":3840881599,"id":2840694693,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUY-l","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"151b1979fdfc0611b945b1c34160d3f46b88d7e6","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@nonameffh`, thank you for adding read audit logging to the In Scope list! The p3 items are now consistent across sections 4.1 and 5.8.\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : For MODIFIED requirements, paste the complete updated requirement block (header + all scenarios) with behavior changes; the archiver replaces the entire requirement\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/specs/**/spec.md : Use `## ADDED` for new capabilities that stand alone; use `## MODIFIED` for behavior changes to existing requirements (include full requirement content); use `## REMOVED` for deprecated features; use `## RENAMED` for name-only changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/proposal.md : In `proposal.md`, include sections: Why (1-2 sentences on problem/opportunity), What Changes (bullet list with breaking changes marked **BREAKING**), and Impact (affected specs and code)\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:1380-1392\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T12:24:51.660Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, specify that key rotation for signed/pre-signed URLs is a backend configuration responsibility, not a FileStorage module responsibility. Clarify that FileStorage uses backend credentials (e.g., AWS keys, GCS service accounts, Azure SAS tokens) supplied via configuration, and that key rotation and credential management are handled by the backend infrastructure or provider per their security policies. This should be documented explicitly to prevent future assumption that FileStorage handles rotation; no code changes are required in the FileStorage module for rotation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nIf you found this review helpful, would you consider giving us a shout-out on [X](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?related=CodeRabbitAI&text=Found%20a%20scope%20mismatch%3A%20read-audit%20logging%20(cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit)%20was%20missing%20from%20Section%204.1%20alongside%20file%20encryption%20%E2%80%94%20fixed%20to%20avoid%20false%20out-of-scope%20claims.%20Try%20%40coderabbitai%20%F0%9F%94%8D%F0%9F%9A%80&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcyberfabric%2Fcyberfabric-core%2Fpull%2F635%23discussion_r2840623320)?\n\nThank you for using CodeRabbit!\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:48:41Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T12:48:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840694693","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840694693"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840694693"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840694693/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":96,"original_start_line":96,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":126,"original_line":126,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2840623320,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":148,"position":148,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725562","pull_request_review_id":3840917307,"id":2840725562,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUgg6","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"75f6fd009fea8f95fc17d533e9f2170a4edd68f7","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**\"Phase 2\" is not a timeframe — it creates a circular reference.**\n\nAll other rows in the Success Metrics table use calendar-relative timeframes (\"6 months after GA\", \"3 months after module adoption\", \"Ongoing after GA\", \"At GA\"). Using \"Phase 2\" as a timeframe value makes the metric's delivery window unresolvable until Phases are formally defined (which is also currently missing — see the scope section). Change this to a calendar-relative timeframe once the Phase definitions are established.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | [calendar timeframe TBD after Phase definition]     |\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 38 - 44, Update the Success\nMetrics table row for \"Audit coverage for file write operations\" in PRD.md:\nreplace the non-calendar timeframe \"Phase 2\" with a concrete calendar-relative\ntimeframe (e.g., \"9 months after GA\" or another agreed-upon date relative to\nGA/module adoption) so the delivery window is resolvable; if the exact duration\nis undecided, insert a temporary calendar-relative placeholder and add an action\nitem for owners to finalize it when phase definitions are available.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:56:00Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:00:12Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725562","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725562"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725562"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725562/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":38,"original_start_line":38,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":44,"original_line":44,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":64,"position":64,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725572","pull_request_review_id":3840917307,"id":2840725572,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUghE","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes inaccessible while all\n+  non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### EventBroker Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-eventbroker`\n+\n+**Direction**: bidirectional (publishes file events; consumes platform events such as owner deletion)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous event publishing and consumption via EventBroker module\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform event protocol; event schema changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Serverless Runtime Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-serverless-runtime`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Serverless Runtime)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Workflow invocation for configurable lifecycle operations (e.g., owner deletion disposition)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform Serverless Runtime invocation protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### Upload and Share a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-upload-share`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- Authorization Service grants write access\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. User uploads file content with metadata (name, mime_type, GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage persists content, assigns ownership to the user, and stores metadata (including GTS file type)\n+6. FileStorage emits audit record for the upload","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"75f6fd009fea8f95fc17d533e9f2170a4edd68f7","original_commit_id":"75f6fd009fea8f95fc17d533e9f2170a4edd68f7","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Audit emission steps missing `*(Phase 2)*` annotation — inconsistent with other use cases.**\n\n`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail` is `p2`, so audit emission is a Phase 2 behavior. Three audit steps are missing the annotation present in all other use cases:\n\n| Use case | Location | Current text |\n|---|---|---|\n| Upload and Share a File | Step 6, line 1036 | `FileStorage emits audit record for the upload` |\n| Manage Shareable Links | Step 6, line 1237 | `FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation` |\n| Manage Shareable Links | Alt flow, line 1250 | `audit record emitted for link creation` |\n\nCompare to the correctly annotated \"Direct Upload from External Client\" step 7 (`*(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload`) and \"Delete a File\" step 6 (`*(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the deletion`).\n\nAlso fix the \"Manage Shareable Links\" postcondition at line 1243 (`- Audit record emitted`) to read `- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted`.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fixes</summary>\n\n```diff\n-6. FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n```\n\n```diff\n-6. FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation\n```\n\n```diff\n-- Audit record emitted\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n```\n\n```diff\n-creates and returns the link URL; audit record emitted for link creation\n+creates and returns the link URL; *(Phase 2)* audit record emitted for link creation\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 1237-1237, 1250-1250\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` at line 1036, In the PRD use cases, the\naudit-emission steps for Phase 2 are missing the annotation: update the three\noccurrences by prepending \"*(Phase 2)* \" to the step texts \"FileStorage emits\naudit record for the upload\" (Upload and Share a File), \"FileStorage emits audit\nrecord for the link revocation\" (Manage Shareable Links), and \"audit record\nemitted for link creation\" (Manage Shareable Links alt flow); also change the\nManage Shareable Links postcondition \"- Audit record emitted\" to \"- *(Phase 2)*\nAudit record emitted\" so all audit steps consistently indicate Phase 2.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit a019023","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:56:00Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:11:17Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725572","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725572"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725572"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725572/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":1036,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1058,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725578","pull_request_review_id":3840917307,"id":2840725578,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUghK","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes inaccessible while all\n+  non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### EventBroker Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-eventbroker`\n+\n+**Direction**: bidirectional (publishes file events; consumes platform events such as owner deletion)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous event publishing and consumption via EventBroker module\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform event protocol; event schema changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Serverless Runtime Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-serverless-runtime`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Serverless Runtime)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Workflow invocation for configurable lifecycle operations (e.g., owner deletion disposition)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform Serverless Runtime invocation protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### Upload and Share a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-upload-share`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- Authorization Service grants write access\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. User uploads file content with metadata (name, mime_type, GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage persists content, assigns ownership to the user, and stores metadata (including GTS file type)\n+6. FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+7. FileStorage returns persistent URL and file identifier\n+8. User creates a shareable link with desired scope and expiration\n+9. FileStorage returns the shareable link URL\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File stored with metadata and ownership\n+- Shareable link active with configured scope and expiration\n+- Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type**: FileStorage rejects the upload with a validation error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated (type or size)\n+\n+### Fetch File for Module Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls download with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage retrieves file content from the storage backend\n+4. FileStorage returns content with metadata (mime_type, size, GTS file type)\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Content and metadata returned to the requesting module\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Generate and Access Signed URL\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-signed-url`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User owns the file or has write access\n+- Backend supports presigned URLs capability\n+- Policy permits signed URL sharing\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests a signed URL for a file with a specified expiration\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file`\n+3. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates sharing policy allows signed URLs; in phase 1 all sharing models are\n+   permitted\n+4. FileStorage generates a presigned download URL using its own backend credentials, scoped to the file and time-limited\n+5. FileStorage records the signed URL metadata (file, expiration, owner) for visibility and audit\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for signed URL creation\n+7. Owner shares the signed URL with an external consumer\n+8. External consumer downloads the file directly from the storage backend using the signed URL — no authentication\n+   required, backend validates signature\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content delivered to external consumer directly from storage backend without authentication\n+- Signed URL metadata recorded for visibility and audit\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted for signed URL creation\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Expired URL**: Storage backend rejects the request (signature expiration enforced by backend)\n+- **Invalid signature**: Storage backend rejects the request\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Sharing model restricted by policy**: FileStorage returns policy-violation error\n+\n+### Validate File Metadata Before Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-get-metadata`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls get_metadata with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage returns metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, owner, availability) without transferring content\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Metadata returned; no content transferred\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Direct Upload from External Client\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-direct-upload`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- Client is authenticated with a valid API token\n+- Storage backend supports presigned URLs (e.g., S3, GCS, Azure Blob)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Client requests a direct transfer URL for upload from FileStorage, providing file metadata (name, mime_type, size,\n+   GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage registers the file metadata (including GTS file type) and ownership, assigns the target backend path\n+6. FileStorage generates a presigned upload URL using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access key), scoped to the\n+   assigned path and time-limited\n+7. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+8. FileStorage returns the presigned URL and file identifier to the client\n+9. Client uploads file content directly to the storage backend using the presigned URL\n+10. Storage backend validates the signature against its own key material and accepts the upload\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File metadata and ownership registered in FileStorage before upload\n+- File content stored on backend via presigned URL — never transited through FileStorage\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type at step 2**: FileStorage rejects with a validation error; no presigned URL issued\n+- **Authorization denied at step 3**: FileStorage returns access-denied error; no presigned URL issued\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation at step 4**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated\n+- **Presigned URL expired**: Backend rejects the upload; client must request a new presigned URL from FileStorage\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable; client must use standard (proxied) upload instead\n+- **Upload never completed (abandoned or failed)**: Metadata registered at step 5 remains unconfirmed; garbage\n+  collection handles cleanup per `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+### Delete a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-delete-file`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests deletion of a file by its identifier\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for delete on `gts.x.fstorage.file`\n+3. FileStorage revokes all active shareable links associated with the file\n+4. FileStorage deletes the file content from the storage backend\n+5. FileStorage removes file metadata and ownership records\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the deletion\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content removed from storage backend\n+- All associated links invalidated\n+- Metadata and ownership records removed\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"75f6fd009fea8f95fc17d533e9f2170a4edd68f7","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Postcondition \"All associated links invalidated\" contradicts the signed URL non-revocability design.**\n\nThe requirement `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file` (line 164) explicitly scopes deletion cascade to shareable links only. The signed URL non-revocability is stated in `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls` (lines 363–365) and `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`. The postcondition at line 1209 says \"All associated links invalidated,\" which implies signed URLs are also invalidated — contradicting the design.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Postconditions**:\n-\n-- File content removed from storage backend\n-- All associated links invalidated\n-- Metadata and ownership records removed\n-- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content removed from storage backend\n+- All associated shareable links invalidated; issued signed URLs remain valid until their expiration (non-revocable by design)\n+- Metadata and ownership records removed\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 1206 - 1211, The postcondition\n\"All associated links invalidated\" conflicts with the signed URL\nnon-revocability requirements; update the postcondition text to explicitly limit\ninvalidation to shareable/manageable links only (e.g., \"All associated\nshareable/manageable links invalidated; signed URLs remain valid\") and reference\nthe related requirements cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file,\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls, and cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links to\nensure the wording aligns with the non-revocability design.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit a019023","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:56:00Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:11:23Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725578","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725578"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725578"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725578/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":1251,"original_start_line":1206,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":1257,"original_line":1211,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1233,"position":1279,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725588","pull_request_review_id":3840917307,"id":2840725588,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUghU","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes inaccessible while all\n+  non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### EventBroker Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-eventbroker`\n+\n+**Direction**: bidirectional (publishes file events; consumes platform events such as owner deletion)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous event publishing and consumption via EventBroker module\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform event protocol; event schema changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Serverless Runtime Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-serverless-runtime`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Serverless Runtime)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Workflow invocation for configurable lifecycle operations (e.g., owner deletion disposition)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform Serverless Runtime invocation protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### Upload and Share a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-upload-share`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- Authorization Service grants write access\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. User uploads file content with metadata (name, mime_type, GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage persists content, assigns ownership to the user, and stores metadata (including GTS file type)\n+6. FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+7. FileStorage returns persistent URL and file identifier\n+8. User creates a shareable link with desired scope and expiration\n+9. FileStorage returns the shareable link URL\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File stored with metadata and ownership\n+- Shareable link active with configured scope and expiration\n+- Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type**: FileStorage rejects the upload with a validation error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated (type or size)\n+\n+### Fetch File for Module Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls download with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage retrieves file content from the storage backend\n+4. FileStorage returns content with metadata (mime_type, size, GTS file type)\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Content and metadata returned to the requesting module\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Generate and Access Signed URL\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-signed-url`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User owns the file or has write access\n+- Backend supports presigned URLs capability\n+- Policy permits signed URL sharing\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests a signed URL for a file with a specified expiration\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file`\n+3. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates sharing policy allows signed URLs; in phase 1 all sharing models are\n+   permitted\n+4. FileStorage generates a presigned download URL using its own backend credentials, scoped to the file and time-limited\n+5. FileStorage records the signed URL metadata (file, expiration, owner) for visibility and audit\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for signed URL creation\n+7. Owner shares the signed URL with an external consumer\n+8. External consumer downloads the file directly from the storage backend using the signed URL — no authentication\n+   required, backend validates signature\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content delivered to external consumer directly from storage backend without authentication\n+- Signed URL metadata recorded for visibility and audit\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted for signed URL creation\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Expired URL**: Storage backend rejects the request (signature expiration enforced by backend)\n+- **Invalid signature**: Storage backend rejects the request\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Sharing model restricted by policy**: FileStorage returns policy-violation error\n+\n+### Validate File Metadata Before Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-get-metadata`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls get_metadata with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage returns metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, owner, availability) without transferring content\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Metadata returned; no content transferred\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Direct Upload from External Client\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-direct-upload`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- Client is authenticated with a valid API token\n+- Storage backend supports presigned URLs (e.g., S3, GCS, Azure Blob)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Client requests a direct transfer URL for upload from FileStorage, providing file metadata (name, mime_type, size,\n+   GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage registers the file metadata (including GTS file type) and ownership, assigns the target backend path\n+6. FileStorage generates a presigned upload URL using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access key), scoped to the\n+   assigned path and time-limited\n+7. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+8. FileStorage returns the presigned URL and file identifier to the client\n+9. Client uploads file content directly to the storage backend using the presigned URL\n+10. Storage backend validates the signature against its own key material and accepts the upload\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File metadata and ownership registered in FileStorage before upload\n+- File content stored on backend via presigned URL — never transited through FileStorage\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type at step 2**: FileStorage rejects with a validation error; no presigned URL issued\n+- **Authorization denied at step 3**: FileStorage returns access-denied error; no presigned URL issued\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation at step 4**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated\n+- **Presigned URL expired**: Backend rejects the upload; client must request a new presigned URL from FileStorage\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable; client must use standard (proxied) upload instead\n+- **Upload never completed (abandoned or failed)**: Metadata registered at step 5 remains unconfirmed; garbage\n+  collection handles cleanup per `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+### Delete a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-delete-file`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests deletion of a file by its identifier\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for delete on `gts.x.fstorage.file`\n+3. FileStorage revokes all active shareable links associated with the file\n+4. FileStorage deletes the file content from the storage backend\n+5. FileStorage removes file metadata and ownership records\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the deletion\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content removed from storage backend\n+- All associated links invalidated\n+- Metadata and ownership records removed\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Cross-tenant attempt**: FileStorage returns access-denied error (tenant boundary enforcement)\n+\n+### Manage Shareable Links\n \n-**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-manage-links`\n \n-**Role**: Fetches input media (images, audio, video, documents) before provider calls and stores generated output media.\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n \n-## 3. Functional Requirements\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-#### Fetch Media by URL\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-fetch-media-v1`\n+**Main Flow**:\n \n+1. Owner requests the list of all active shareable links and signed URLs for a file\n+2. FileStorage returns the list with each link's scope, expiration, and creation date\n+3. Owner identifies a link to revoke\n+4. Owner requests revocation of the link by its identifier\n+5. FileStorage invalidates the link immediately\n+6. FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"75f6fd009fea8f95fc17d533e9f2170a4edd68f7","original_commit_id":"75f6fd009fea8f95fc17d533e9f2170a4edd68f7","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**\"Manage Shareable Links\" is the only use case without an explicit authorization check step.**\n\nEvery other use case with security implications includes a dedicated FileStorage-calls-Authorization-Service step (e.g., step 2 in \"Fetch File,\" \"Validate Metadata,\" \"Delete a File,\" etc.). \"Manage Shareable Links\" moves directly from \"Owner requests the list\" to \"FileStorage returns the list\" with no authorization check in between. This creates an inconsistency that could lead implementors to skip the authorization check for link listing and revocation — both of which expose sensitive information about how a file is shared.\n\nAdd an explicit authorization check as step 2, matching the pattern used across all other use cases.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n **Main Flow**:\n \n 1. Owner requests the list of all active shareable links and signed URLs for a file\n-2. FileStorage returns the list with each link's scope, expiration, and creation date\n-3. Owner identifies a link to revoke\n-4. Owner requests revocation of the link by its identifier\n-5. FileStorage invalidates the link immediately\n-6. FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file`\n+3. FileStorage returns the list with each link's scope, expiration, and creation date\n+4. Owner identifies a link to revoke\n+5. Owner requests revocation of the link by its identifier\n+6. FileStorage invalidates the link immediately\n+7. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- suggestion_start -->\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Committable suggestion</summary>\n\n> ‼️ **IMPORTANT**\n> Carefully review the code before committing. Ensure that it accurately replaces the highlighted code, contains no missing lines, and has no issues with indentation. Thoroughly test & benchmark the code to ensure it meets the requirements.\n\n```suggestion\n**Main Flow**:\n\n1. Owner requests the list of all active shareable links and signed URLs for a file\n2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file`\n3. FileStorage returns the list with each link's scope, expiration, and creation date\n4. Owner identifies a link to revoke\n5. Owner requests revocation of the link by its identifier\n6. FileStorage invalidates the link immediately\n7. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- suggestion_end -->\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 1230 - 1237, Update the\n\"Manage Shareable Links\" main flow to include an explicit authorization check as\nstep 2 (mirroring other use cases): after \"Owner requests the list of all active\nshareable links...\" insert \"FileStorage calls Authorization Service to verify\nthe owner's permission to list/manage shareable links\" before returning the\nlist, and ensure the revocation path similarly documents an Authorization\nService call prior to invalidation; reference the \"Manage Shareable Links\" flow,\nFileStorage, and Authorization Service when making the change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit a019023","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:56:00Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:11:22Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725588","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725588"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725588"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725588/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1230,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1237,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1264,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725602","pull_request_review_id":3840917307,"id":2840725602,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUghi","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes inaccessible while all\n+  non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### EventBroker Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-eventbroker`\n+\n+**Direction**: bidirectional (publishes file events; consumes platform events such as owner deletion)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous event publishing and consumption via EventBroker module\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform event protocol; event schema changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Serverless Runtime Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-serverless-runtime`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Serverless Runtime)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Workflow invocation for configurable lifecycle operations (e.g., owner deletion disposition)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform Serverless Runtime invocation protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### Upload and Share a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-upload-share`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- Authorization Service grants write access\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. User uploads file content with metadata (name, mime_type, GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage persists content, assigns ownership to the user, and stores metadata (including GTS file type)\n+6. FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+7. FileStorage returns persistent URL and file identifier\n+8. User creates a shareable link with desired scope and expiration\n+9. FileStorage returns the shareable link URL\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File stored with metadata and ownership\n+- Shareable link active with configured scope and expiration\n+- Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type**: FileStorage rejects the upload with a validation error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated (type or size)\n+\n+### Fetch File for Module Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls download with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage retrieves file content from the storage backend\n+4. FileStorage returns content with metadata (mime_type, size, GTS file type)\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Content and metadata returned to the requesting module\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Generate and Access Signed URL\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-signed-url`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User owns the file or has write access\n+- Backend supports presigned URLs capability\n+- Policy permits signed URL sharing\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests a signed URL for a file with a specified expiration\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file`\n+3. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates sharing policy allows signed URLs; in phase 1 all sharing models are\n+   permitted\n+4. FileStorage generates a presigned download URL using its own backend credentials, scoped to the file and time-limited\n+5. FileStorage records the signed URL metadata (file, expiration, owner) for visibility and audit\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for signed URL creation\n+7. Owner shares the signed URL with an external consumer\n+8. External consumer downloads the file directly from the storage backend using the signed URL — no authentication\n+   required, backend validates signature\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content delivered to external consumer directly from storage backend without authentication\n+- Signed URL metadata recorded for visibility and audit\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted for signed URL creation\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Expired URL**: Storage backend rejects the request (signature expiration enforced by backend)\n+- **Invalid signature**: Storage backend rejects the request\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Sharing model restricted by policy**: FileStorage returns policy-violation error\n+\n+### Validate File Metadata Before Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-get-metadata`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls get_metadata with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage returns metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, owner, availability) without transferring content\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Metadata returned; no content transferred\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Direct Upload from External Client\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-direct-upload`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- Client is authenticated with a valid API token\n+- Storage backend supports presigned URLs (e.g., S3, GCS, Azure Blob)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Client requests a direct transfer URL for upload from FileStorage, providing file metadata (name, mime_type, size,\n+   GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage registers the file metadata (including GTS file type) and ownership, assigns the target backend path\n+6. FileStorage generates a presigned upload URL using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access key), scoped to the\n+   assigned path and time-limited\n+7. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+8. FileStorage returns the presigned URL and file identifier to the client\n+9. Client uploads file content directly to the storage backend using the presigned URL\n+10. Storage backend validates the signature against its own key material and accepts the upload\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File metadata and ownership registered in FileStorage before upload\n+- File content stored on backend via presigned URL — never transited through FileStorage\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type at step 2**: FileStorage rejects with a validation error; no presigned URL issued\n+- **Authorization denied at step 3**: FileStorage returns access-denied error; no presigned URL issued\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation at step 4**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated\n+- **Presigned URL expired**: Backend rejects the upload; client must request a new presigned URL from FileStorage\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable; client must use standard (proxied) upload instead\n+- **Upload never completed (abandoned or failed)**: Metadata registered at step 5 remains unconfirmed; garbage\n+  collection handles cleanup per `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+### Delete a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-delete-file`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests deletion of a file by its identifier\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for delete on `gts.x.fstorage.file`\n+3. FileStorage revokes all active shareable links associated with the file\n+4. FileStorage deletes the file content from the storage backend\n+5. FileStorage removes file metadata and ownership records\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the deletion\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content removed from storage backend\n+- All associated links invalidated\n+- Metadata and ownership records removed\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Cross-tenant attempt**: FileStorage returns access-denied error (tenant boundary enforcement)\n+\n+### Manage Shareable Links\n \n-**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-manage-links`\n \n-**Role**: Fetches input media (images, audio, video, documents) before provider calls and stores generated output media.\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n \n-## 3. Functional Requirements\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-#### Fetch Media by URL\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-fetch-media-v1`\n+**Main Flow**:\n \n+1. Owner requests the list of all active shareable links and signed URLs for a file\n+2. FileStorage returns the list with each link's scope, expiration, and creation date\n+3. Owner identifies a link to revoke\n+4. Owner requests revocation of the link by its identifier\n+5. FileStorage invalidates the link immediately\n+6. FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation\n \n-The system must retrieve file content and metadata by URL for LLM Gateway consumption.\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+- Revoked link returns access-denied on subsequent access\n+- Remaining links unaffected\n+- Audit record emitted\n \n-#### Store Generated Content\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-store-content-v1`\n+- **No active links**: FileStorage returns an empty list\n+- **Link not found**: FileStorage returns link_not_found error\n+- **Owner creates a new link**: Owner requests a shareable link with desired scope and expiration; FileStorage creates\n+  and returns the link URL; audit record emitted for link creation\n \n+### Multi-Backend Deployment\n \n-The system must store generated media (images, audio, video) and return accessible URL.\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-backend-config`\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n \n-#### Get Metadata\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata-v1`\n+- FileStorage is deployed with a configured storage backend\n \n+**Main Flow**:\n \n-The system must return file metadata (size, mime_type) without fetching full content for validation purposes.\n+1. Deployment A configures FileStorage with an S3-compatible backend (e.g., AWS S3)\n+2. Deployment B configures FileStorage with a different backend (e.g., Azure Blob Storage)\n+3. Both deployments expose identical FileStorage SDK and REST APIs\n+4. CyberFabric modules interact with FileStorage through the SDK trait without awareness of the underlying backend\n+5. Upload, download, delete, metadata, and link operations behave identically regardless of backend\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-## 4. Use Cases\n+- All functional requirements are met identically across different backend configurations\n+- Consuming modules require zero code changes when the backend changes\n \n-#### UC-001: Fetch Media by URL\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media-v1`\n+- **Backend-specific feature unavailable**: FileStorage returns a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable\n+  (e.g., signed URL or direct transfer request rejected when backend does not support presigned URLs)\n \n-**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+### Configure Policy\n \n-**Preconditions**: File exists at URL.\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-configure-policy`\n \n-**Flow**:\n-1. LLM Gateway sends fetch(url)\n-2. FileStorage retrieves file content\n-3. FileStorage returns content + metadata\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n \n-**Postconditions**: Content returned to Gateway.\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-**Acceptance criteria**:\n-- Returns file_not_found if file does not exist\n-- Returns content with mime_type and size\n-- Supports streaming for large files\n+- User has tenant administration privileges (for tenant-level policy) or is an authenticated user (for user-level\n+  policy)\n \n-#### UC-002: Store Generated Content\n+**Main Flow**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-store-content-v1`\n+1. Tenant admin or user defines policies: allowed file types, size limits (global and per-type), enabled event types,\n+   and permitted sharing models\n+2. FileStorage validates and stores the policy configuration\n+3. Subsequent file operations are enforced against the effective policy (most restrictive per aspect across tenant and\n+   user levels)\n \n-**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-**Preconditions**: Content available for storage.\n+- Policy active and enforced on all file operations\n \n-**Flow**:\n-1. LLM Gateway sends store(content, metadata)\n-2. FileStorage persists content\n-3. FileStorage returns accessible URL\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-**Postconditions**: File stored, URL returned.\n+- **Invalid policy**: FileStorage returns validation error with details\n \n-**Acceptance criteria**:\n-- URL immediately accessible after store returns\n-- Metadata stored with file (mime_type, source, timestamps)\n-- Storage_unavailable error if service down\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n \n-#### UC-003: Get Metadata\n+- [ ] File upload returns persistent URL and stores metadata (name, size, type, dates, owner)\n+- [ ] File download returns content with correct metadata\n+- [ ] File deletion cascades to all associated shareable links\n+- [ ] Authorization checked for every file operation via Authorization Service\n+- [ ] Tenant boundary enforced — cross-tenant modification/deletion rejected\n+- [ ] Shareable links work with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- [ ] Signed URLs point directly to storage backend, grant download access without authentication, and storage backend\n+  rejects after expiration\n+- [ ] File owner can list active shareable links and signed URLs; can revoke shareable links (signed URLs are\n+  non-revocable)\n+- [ ] Audit record emitted for every write operation\n+- [ ] Policies enforce file type and size restrictions on upload (most restrictive wins across tenant and user levels)\n+- [ ] Direct transfer presigned URLs allow upload directly to storage backend without proxying through FileStorage\n+- [ ] Presigned URLs are generated using FileStorage's own backend credentials and validated by the backend via\n+  signature verification\n+- [ ] Signed URL and direct transfer requests rejected with clear error when backend does not support presigned URLs\n+- [ ] file_not_found error returned for non-existent files\n+- [ ] access_denied error returned for unauthorized operations\n+- [ ] Metadata-only queries complete without transferring file content\n+- [ ] File content is immutable — no in-place content update; changes require a new upload\n+- [ ] Custom metadata is updatable by the file owner; system-managed metadata is not user-updatable\n+- [ ] Custom metadata update changes the file's last modified date\n+- [ ] File ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer or owner deletion workflows\n+- [ ] Every file has a mandatory GTS file type assigned at upload time; uploads without a file type are rejected\n+- [ ] GTS file type is immutable after creation\n+- [ ] Authorization requests include the file's GTS type, enabling per-type access decisions\n+- [ ] A module authorized only for type A cannot access files of type B\n+- [ ] FileStorage SDK and REST API behave identically regardless of configured storage backend\n+- [ ] File listing returns metadata only, is paginated, and requires owner type filter\n+- [ ] Multipart upload assembles parts into a complete file with correct metadata\n+- [ ] Upload rejected when declared mime_type does not match actual file content\n+- [ ] Orphaned metadata records from unconfirmed direct uploads are detected, reconciled against backend state, and\n+  cleaned up automatically\n+- [ ] File owner can toggle download availability via metadata update\n+- [ ] Each backend declares its supported capabilities (presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload)\n+- [ ] Consumers can discover backend capabilities at runtime\n+- [ ] Operations requiring an unsupported capability return a clear error\n+- [ ] File versioning creates a new version on each upload; previous versions remain accessible by opaque version ID\n+- [ ] All versions of a file are listable with version ID, size, timestamp, and current-version flag\n+- [ ] Soft-delete hides the current version while non-current versions remain retrievable\n+- [ ] Permanent delete of a specific version removes only that version\n+- [ ] Declared capabilities are independently configurable (enable/disable) per backend\n+- [ ] A capability disabled by configuration behaves identically to an unsupported capability\n+- [ ] Download and metadata responses include ETag header\n+- [ ] Conditional download with If-None-Match returns 304 Not Modified when file is unchanged\n+- [ ] Metadata update with If-Match returns 412 Precondition Failed when file state has changed\n+- [ ] Retried upload with the same idempotency key returns the original result without creating a duplicate file\n+- [ ] Owner deletion event from EventBroker triggers a configurable Serverless Runtime workflow for file disposition\n+- [ ] Files of a deleted owner are retained as orphaned when no workflow is configured\n+- [ ] Server-side encryption is applied when the encryption capability is available and enabled for the backend","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"75f6fd009fea8f95fc17d533e9f2170a4edd68f7","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Multiple p2/p3 requirements have no acceptance criterion in §9.**\n\nThe following requirements are fully specified in §5 but have no testable acceptance criterion in §9. Notably, `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption` (p3) does have a criterion (line 1358) while `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit` (also p3) does not, showing the gap is not a deliberate p3 exclusion.\n\n| Requirement ID | Priority | Missing criterion |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota` | p2 | Upload rejected when storage quota would be exceeded |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting` | p2 | Usage report emitted asynchronously on storage-consuming write operations |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events` | p2 | File write events emitted to EventBroker per policy |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests` | p2 | HTTP Range requests return partial content per RFC 7233 |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api` | p2 | S3-compatible API accepts upload and download operations |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api` | p2 | WebDAV API exposes file operations for OS-level mounting |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies` | p2 | Files with expired retention are automatically deleted with audit record |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions` | p2 | Links with a restricted sharing model are rejected |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends` | p2 | Storage backend connectable at runtime without service rebuild |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer` | p2 | File ownership transferable to another user or tenant via explicit operation |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits` | p2 | Metadata operations exceeding configured limits are rejected |\n| `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit` | p3 | Read audit records emitted for proxied downloads when enabled by policy; not emitted for presigned URL downloads |\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 1307 - 1358, The Acceptance\nCriteria section is missing testable items for several p2/p3 requirements; add\nexplicit criteria for each listed requirement ID (e.g.,\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota, cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting,\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events, cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests,\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api, cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api,\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies,\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions,\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends,\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer,\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits, cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit) by\ninserting short, testable acceptance criteria mirroring the behavior described\nin §5 (e.g., \"Upload rejected when storage quota would be exceeded\" for\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota, \"Usage report emitted asynchronously on\nstorage-consuming write operations\" for cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting,\n\"Read audit records emitted for proxied downloads when enabled; not emitted for\npresigned URL downloads\" for cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit, and equivalent\nsingle-line criteria for each remaining ID) ensuring wording matches other\nchecklist items and uses consistent phrasing and priority labels.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit a019023","created_at":"2026-02-23T12:56:00Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:11:27Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725602","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725602"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840725602"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840725602/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":1386,"original_start_line":1307,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":1444,"original_line":1358,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1423,"position":1509,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840744527","pull_request_review_id":3840936921,"id":2840744527,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUlJP","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"75f6fd009fea8f95fc17d533e9f2170a4edd68f7","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Not applicable for now, other items are related to phase 1. Phase 2 doesn't have a clear time frame","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:00:02Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:00:02Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840744527","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840744527"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840744527"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840744527/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":38,"original_start_line":38,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":44,"original_line":44,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2840725562,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":64,"position":64,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840747057","pull_request_review_id":3840939669,"id":2840747057,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pUlwx","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1397 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"75f6fd009fea8f95fc17d533e9f2170a4edd68f7","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@nonameffh`, understood — keeping \"Phase 2\" as the timeframe for audit coverage is fine while phase timing is still being defined. When Phase 2 gets a concrete schedule, this row can be updated to match the calendar-relative format of the other metrics.\n\n🐰\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:1380-1392\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T12:24:51.660Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, specify that key rotation for signed/pre-signed URLs is a backend configuration responsibility, not a FileStorage module responsibility. Clarify that FileStorage uses backend credentials (e.g., AWS keys, GCS service accounts, Azure SAS tokens) supplied via configuration, and that key rotation and credential management are handled by the backend infrastructure or provider per their security policies. This should be documented explicitly to prevent future assumption that FileStorage handles rotation; no code changes are required in the FileStorage module for rotation.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:00:35Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:00:35Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840747057","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840747057"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840747057"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840747057/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":38,"original_start_line":38,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":44,"original_line":44,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2840725562,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":64,"position":64,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827010","pull_request_review_id":3841022586,"id":2840827010,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pU5SC","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Glossary: \"non-revocable\" for Signed URLs should note file-deletion behavior.**\n\nThe glossary describes signed URLs as \"non-revocable\" without noting the edge case that file deletion removes the underlying content — causing the storage backend to return a 404 (or equivalent) even for a valid, non-expired signed URL. The use case postconditions at lines 1209–1210 document this correctly, but a reader relying only on the glossary definition will be surprised.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested addition</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| Signed URL | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated\n-  consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable —\n-  the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration |\n+| Signed URL | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated\n+  consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable —\n+  the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration. Note: the URL signature cannot\n+  be invalidated by FileStorage, but if the underlying file is deleted, the storage backend will return\n+  a content-not-found response even for a valid, non-expired signed URL. |\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` at line 56, Update the \"Signed URL\"\nglossary entry to note that while the URL signature itself is non-revocable,\ndeletion of the underlying file/content will cause the storage backend to return\na 404 (or equivalent) for that signed URL; explicitly mention this edge case and\nadd a cross-reference to the existing use-case postconditions that document the\nsame behavior so readers are not surprised.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:18:51Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:18:53Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827010","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827010"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827010"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827010/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":56,"original_line":56,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":76,"position":76,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827014","pull_request_review_id":3841022586,"id":2840827014,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pU5SG","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Usage reporting doesn't cover ownership transfer — potential metering inaccuracy.**\n\n`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting` reports on operations that change storage consumption (upload, delete, version creation/deletion). However, ownership transfer (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) shifts a file's quota from one owner to another without changing total platform storage — it does change per-owner consumption.\n\nIf the Usage Collector tracks per-owner storage for billing or quota enforcement, ownership transfers must also trigger a usage update (debit old owner, credit new owner). The current requirement would produce stale per-owner usage figures after any transfer.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested addition</summary>\n\n```diff\n ...MUST be emitted on every write operation that changes storage consumption (upload,\n-delete, version creation, version deletion).\n+delete, version creation, version deletion). Ownership transfer operations **MUST** also\n+emit a usage report update to reflect the storage reallocation between the previous and\n+new owner.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 517 - 528, Update the usage\nreporting requirement (cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting) to also mandate\nemitting asynchronous, non-blocking usage updates on ownership transfer events\n(cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer), explicitly stating that transfers\nmust produce a debit for the previous owner and a credit for the new owner so\nper-owner consumption remains accurate for billing/quota; ensure the wording\npreserves the existing async/non-blocking constraint and that reports include\nper-tenant/per-owner total bytes and file counts.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commits fc6fbbe to 8752e46","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:18:52Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T17:47:45Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827014","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827014"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827014"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827014/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":536,"original_start_line":517,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":551,"original_line":528,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":550,"position":573,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827017","pull_request_review_id":3841022586,"id":2840827017,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pU5SJ","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota` omits version creation from quota check scope.**\n\n`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting` (line 523) explicitly lists *version creation* as a storage-consuming write operation alongside uploads. However, the quota enforcement requirement (line 534) only mentions \"uploads.\" When versioning is enabled, each upload to an existing path creates a new version and consumes additional quota — these writes currently bypass the quota pre-check.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads\n-that increase storage consumption. Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota\n-**MUST** be rejected.\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any write\n+operation that increases storage consumption (uploads and, when versioning is enabled,\n+version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 530 - 539, Update the quota\nrequirement `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota` to include version creation\nand any other storage-consuming write operations (not just \"uploads\") in its\npre-check scope: explicitly state that before accepting uploads or actions that\ncreate new file versions (or otherwise increase stored bytes) the system MUST\nconsult the Quota Enforcement service and reject operations that would exceed\nthe owner's quota; mirror the terminology used in\n`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting` so version creation is handled\nconsistently.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commits fc6fbbe to 8752e46\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:18:52Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T17:47:43Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827017","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827017"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827017"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827017/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":553,"original_start_line":530,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":564,"original_line":539,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":561,"position":586,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827023","pull_request_review_id":3841022586,"id":2840827023,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pU5SP","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes inaccessible while all\n+  non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Clarify physical vs. logical semantics of \"soft-delete\" in versioning.**\n\nLine 656 says the current version \"becomes inaccessible\" after a soft-delete, but doesn't specify whether the backend object is physically retained or deleted. The distinction has concrete downstream effects:\n\n- **Storage quota**: If the content is retained on the backend, it still consumes the owner's storage quota.\n- **GC**: The GC worker (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`) may or may not need to handle soft-deleted version content.\n- **Recovery**: Undelete is only possible if the backend object is retained.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes\n-inaccessible while all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version): FileStorage **MUST** mark the current\n+version as logically deleted (inaccessible via normal download/metadata APIs) while\n+retaining the underlying backend object. All non-current versions remain retrievable by\n+their version identifiers. The soft-deleted current version continues to consume storage\n+quota until it is permanently deleted.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 644 - 668, Clarify the\n\"soft-delete\" semantics in the File Versioning section\n(cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning) by explicitly stating whether a\nsoft-delete is logical-only (marks the current version inaccessible while the\nbackend object is physically retained) or physical (backend object removed), and\nlist the implications: whether retained data counts against storage quota,\nwhether the GC worker (cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads) must consider\nsoft-deleted content for cleanup, and whether undelete/recovery of the current\nversion is supported; update the wording around \"becomes inaccessible\" to\ninclude these outcomes and the required tenant, audit, and authorization\nbehavior for retained vs. removed objects.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commits fc6fbbe to 8752e46\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:18:52Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T17:47:46Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827023","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827023"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827023"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827023/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":669,"original_start_line":644,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":704,"original_line":668,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":690,"position":726,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827028","pull_request_review_id":3841022586,"id":2840827028,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pU5SU","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes inaccessible while all\n+  non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Define the scope of idempotency keys to prevent cross-caller collisions.**\n\nThe requirement doesn't specify whether idempotency keys are scoped per-user, per-tenant, or globally. Without explicit scoping, an implementation that uses a shared global key namespace would allow:\n\n- User B to supply a key previously used by User A and receive A's file URL/identifier — a confidentiality leak.\n- Tenant A's keys to collide with Tenant B's keys in a multi-tenant deployment.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested addition</summary>\n\n```diff\n The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a\n unique idempotency key with an upload request.\n+Idempotency keys **MUST** be scoped to the requesting caller (user or service identity) —\n+the same key submitted by two different callers **MUST** be treated as two independent\n+requests.\n If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST**\n return the result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 836 - 846, The PRD's\nidempotency requirement (cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency) lacks key\nscoping and risks cross-caller collisions; update the requirement text to\nspecify the idempotency key scope (e.g., scoped per-tenant and optionally\nper-user/session) and state that the server MUST namespace keys by tenant_id\n(and user_id when applicable), enforce isolation in the idempotency lookup, and\nexpire keys after the configurable window; reference the requirement id\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency and amend associated acceptance\ncriteria and actor descriptions to reflect per-tenant/per-user scoping and\nconfidentiality guarantees.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:churro -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commits fc6fbbe to 8752e46","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:18:52Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T17:47:47Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827028","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827028"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840827028"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840827028/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":872,"original_start_line":836,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":889,"original_line":846,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":868,"position":911,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840880844","pull_request_review_id":3841080475,"id":2840880844,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pVGbM","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes inaccessible while all\n+  non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"It's planned to cover in the design","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:30:42Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:30:42Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840880844","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840880844"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840880844"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840880844/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":872,"original_start_line":836,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":889,"original_line":846,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2840827028,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":868,"position":911,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840889412","pull_request_review_id":3841089501,"id":2840889412,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pVIhE","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes inaccessible while all\n+  non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Not applicable for requirements, should be in design","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:32:41Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:32:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840889412","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840889412"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840889412"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840889412/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":669,"original_start_line":644,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":704,"original_line":668,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2840827023,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":690,"position":726,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840891251","pull_request_review_id":3841091475,"id":2840891251,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pVI9z","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) such that the current version becomes inaccessible while all\n+  non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@nonameffh`, understood — you're right that those implementation details (physical vs. logical storage, GC worker behavior, quota accounting) belong in DESIGN.md rather than the PRD. The PRD correctly states the requirement at the appropriate level: what soft-delete must accomplish from the user's perspective.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/specs/**/spec.md : Use `## ADDED` for new capabilities that stand alone; use `## MODIFIED` for behavior changes to existing requirements (include full requirement content); use `## REMOVED` for deprecated features; use `## RENAMED` for name-only changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : For MODIFIED requirements, paste the complete updated requirement block (header + all scenarios) with behavior changes; the archiver replaces the entire requirement\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : In spec delta files, use ADDED for new orthogonal capabilities, MODIFIED for behavior changes of existing requirements, and RENAMED for name-only changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/proposal.md : In `proposal.md`, include sections: Why (1-2 sentences on problem/opportunity), What Changes (bullet list with breaking changes marked **BREAKING**), and Impact (affected specs and code)\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:1380-1392\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T12:24:51.660Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, specify that key rotation for signed/pre-signed URLs is a backend configuration responsibility, not a FileStorage module responsibility. Clarify that FileStorage uses backend credentials (e.g., AWS keys, GCS service accounts, Azure SAS tokens) supplied via configuration, and that key rotation and credential management are handled by the backend infrastructure or provider per their security policies. This should be documented explicitly to prevent future assumption that FileStorage handles rotation; no code changes are required in the FileStorage module for rotation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:33:07Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:33:07Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840891251","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840891251"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840891251"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840891251/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":669,"original_start_line":644,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":704,"original_line":668,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2840827023,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":690,"position":726,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840893586","pull_request_review_id":3841093878,"id":2840893586,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pVJiS","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Version creation is upload too","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:33:38Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:33:39Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840893586","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840893586"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840893586"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840893586/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":553,"original_start_line":530,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":564,"original_line":539,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2840827017,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":561,"position":586,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840896223","pull_request_review_id":3841096563,"id":2840896223,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6pVKLf","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1419 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch. Content-type validation does not apply to\n+direct uploads via presigned URLs because FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying\n+on strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-tenant\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be asynchronous and\n+**MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Asynchronous\n+reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting uploads that increase storage consumption.\n+Uploads that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"a019023ceaa441c3c4aed7ba421b66787016584b","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@nonameffh`, thank you for the clarification! I understand — when versioning is enabled, uploading to an existing file path is still an upload operation; it simply results in creating a new version. The quota enforcement requirement as written already covers version creation since it's scoped to \"uploads that increase storage consumption.\"\n\n\n\n🐰✨\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Learnings added</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:530-539\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T13:34:08.693Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, the term \"upload\" in the context of quota enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`) is inclusive of version creation — when versioning is enabled, uploading to an existing path is still an upload operation that creates a new version. No separate mention of \"version creation\" is needed in the quota requirement.\n```\n\n> Note: Learnings are effective only in the context of similar code segments. To apply general coding guidelines, please configure [review instructions](https://docs.coderabbit.ai/guides/review-instructions). You can manage existing learnings in the [app](https://app.coderabbit.ai/learnings).\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/specs/**/spec.md : Use `## ADDED` for new capabilities that stand alone; use `## MODIFIED` for behavior changes to existing requirements (include full requirement content); use `## REMOVED` for deprecated features; use `## RENAMED` for name-only changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : For MODIFIED requirements, paste the complete updated requirement block (header + all scenarios) with behavior changes; the archiver replaces the entire requirement\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : In spec delta files, use ADDED for new orthogonal capabilities, MODIFIED for behavior changes of existing requirements, and RENAMED for name-only changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/proposal.md : In `proposal.md`, include sections: Why (1-2 sentences on problem/opportunity), What Changes (bullet list with breaking changes marked **BREAKING**), and Impact (affected specs and code)\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:1380-1392\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T12:24:51.660Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, specify that key rotation for signed/pre-signed URLs is a backend configuration responsibility, not a FileStorage module responsibility. Clarify that FileStorage uses backend credentials (e.g., AWS keys, GCS service accounts, Azure SAS tokens) supplied via configuration, and that key rotation and credential management are handled by the backend infrastructure or provider per their security policies. This should be documented explicitly to prevent future assumption that FileStorage handles rotation; no code changes are required in the FileStorage module for rotation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-23T13:34:15Z","updated_at":"2026-02-23T13:34:15Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840896223","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840896223"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2840896223"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2840896223/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":553,"original_start_line":530,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":564,"original_line":539,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2840827017,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":561,"position":586,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144303","pull_request_review_id":3909221215,"id":2900144303,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3LCv","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Require enough bytes for multipart type validation.**\n\nThis assumes the first part always contains enough signature bytes to classify the file. With client-controlled part sizes, a tiny first chunk can make validation impossible or inconsistent. Please require either a minimum size for part 1 or buffering until FileStorage has enough leading bytes to validate before accepting later parts.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 229 - 233, The\nmultipart-upload flow (cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload) currently\nassumes the first part contains enough signature bytes to validate mime_type;\nmodify the upload handler that processes the first part (e.g., the FileStorage\nmethod that accepts multipart parts / acceptMultipartPart or\nhandleMultipartPart) to either enforce a minimum first-part size or buffer\nincoming data until enough leading bytes are accumulated to classify the file’s\nmagic bytes, perform the mime_type validation as soon as enough bytes are\navailable, and if validation fails call the multipart abort routine\n(abortMultipartUpload) and reject/ignore all subsequent parts; ensure the logic\ndistinguishes “first part not yet sufficient” (buffer and delay accepting later\nparts) versus “validated and accept” so downstream parts are not processed\nprematurely.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:grasshopper -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @nonameffh\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-03-07T17:51:27Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:51:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900144303","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144303"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900144303"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144303/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":235,"original_start_line":229,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":240,"original_line":233,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":255,"position":262,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144305","pull_request_review_id":3909221215,"id":2900144305,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3LCx","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+\n+Content-type validation does not apply to direct uploads (single-part or multipart) via presigned URLs because\n+FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. First-part validation for multipart uploads provides the same level of guarantee as single-part\n+validation — magic bytes reside at the start of the file and are contained in the first part. Post-assembly\n+re-validation would require downloading the assembled file from the backend, negating the efficiency benefits of\n+multipart upload. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying on\n+strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-owner\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion) and on ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`). For ownership transfers, the system **MUST** emit a usage report for both\n+the previous owner (storage decrease) and the new owner (storage increase). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be\n+asynchronous and **MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Ownership\n+transfers shift per-owner storage consumption without changing total platform storage — without debit/credit reporting,\n+billing and quota data become stale after transfers. Asynchronous reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by\n+usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any operation that increases storage\n+consumption (including uploads and version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform. Quota checks must cover all storage-consuming operations, not only initial uploads, to\n+prevent quota bypass through versioned overwrites.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) by placing a logical delete marker on the current version. The\n+  delete marker makes the current version inaccessible through normal file access (download, metadata queries) while\n+  all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers. Soft-deleted content is **not** physically\n+  removed from the storage backend — it continues to exist and **MUST** count against the owner's storage quota\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`)\n+- Restore a soft-deleted file by removing the delete marker, making the most recent non-current version the current\n+  version again. Restore **MUST** require the same authorization as upload\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Reconcile delete semantics with the base `Delete File` flow.**\n\nThis section makes plain file deletion on a versioned object a soft-delete of the current version, but `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file` and the \"Delete a File\" use case still describe delete as permanent removal of content and metadata. Please make the API contract explicit for versioned files: either plain delete becomes soft-delete, permanent delete requires a different operation, or the caller must choose a delete mode. Right now the PRD defines both behaviors.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 672 - 679, The PRD currently\ndefines conflicting semantics for deleting versioned files vs the base \"Delete\nFile\" flow (see references to Delete File, the \"Delete a File\" use case, and\nrequirement cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file); pick and document one explicit\ncontract: either (A) make the base Delete File operation soft-delete for\nversioned objects and state that permanent deletion requires a separate\noperation, or (B) add a deleteMode parameter (e.g., \"soft\" | \"permanent\") to the\nDelete File API and describe authorization, quota impact, and behavior for\ncurrent vs non-current versions; update all relevant sections to consistently\nstate which API/parameter to call for permanent removal, the required auth (same\nas upload for restore), and that soft-deleted data still counts against\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:grasshopper -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit 1afc2c9","created_at":"2026-03-07T17:51:27Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:47Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900144305","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144305"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900144305"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144305/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":681,"original_start_line":672,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":688,"original_line":679,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":701,"position":710,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144307","pull_request_review_id":3909221215,"id":2900144307,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3LCz","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+\n+Content-type validation does not apply to direct uploads (single-part or multipart) via presigned URLs because\n+FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. First-part validation for multipart uploads provides the same level of guarantee as single-part\n+validation — magic bytes reside at the start of the file and are contained in the first part. Post-assembly\n+re-validation would require downloading the assembled file from the backend, negating the efficiency benefits of\n+multipart upload. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying on\n+strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-owner\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion) and on ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`). For ownership transfers, the system **MUST** emit a usage report for both\n+the previous owner (storage decrease) and the new owner (storage increase). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be\n+asynchronous and **MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Ownership\n+transfers shift per-owner storage consumption without changing total platform storage — without debit/credit reporting,\n+billing and quota data become stale after transfers. Asynchronous reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by\n+usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any operation that increases storage\n+consumption (including uploads and version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform. Quota checks must cover all storage-consuming operations, not only initial uploads, to\n+prevent quota bypass through versioned overwrites.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) by placing a logical delete marker on the current version. The\n+  delete marker makes the current version inaccessible through normal file access (download, metadata queries) while\n+  all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers. Soft-deleted content is **not** physically\n+  removed from the storage backend — it continues to exist and **MUST** count against the owner's storage quota\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`)\n+- Restore a soft-deleted file by removing the delete marker, making the most recent non-current version the current\n+  version again. Restore **MUST** require the same authorization as upload\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+Garbage collection (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`) does **NOT** apply to soft-deleted versions —\n+soft-delete is an intentional owner action, not an orphaned state. Cleanup of soft-deleted versions is governed by\n+retention policies (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`).\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2). Logical delete markers (rather than physical\n+removal) enable restoration and follow the established pattern of S3 versioned deletes, GCS soft-delete, and Azure Blob\n+soft-delete. Counting soft-deleted content against quota prevents quota bypass through repeated soft-delete cycles.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+Idempotency keys **MUST** be scoped to the file owner specified in the upload request — the same entity that will own\n+the resulting file (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`). When the owner is a tenant, the key is unique within that\n+tenant's namespace. When the owner is a user, the key is unique within that user's namespace. The same key value used by\n+different owners **MUST** be treated as distinct keys. The system **MUST NOT** allow idempotency key lookups to cross\n+owner boundaries — a request **MUST NOT** be able to detect whether a different owner has used a given key.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks. Owner-scoped key namespacing prevents cross-tenant information leaks and aligns with\n+the platform's tenant boundary enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Update the external integrations to the new per-owner model.**\n\nThe requirements now define usage reporting and quota checks in terms of the file **owner** (user or tenant), but these sections still describe Usage Collector and Quota Enforcement as per-tenant only. That mismatch will leak into the downstream contract design for user-owned files and ownership transfer accounting. Please align both descriptions with the owner-based semantics used in §5.5. \n\n\nAlso applies to: 1427-1428\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 1018 - 1032, The Usage\nCollector (ID: cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector) and Quota\nEnforcement (ID: cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement) contract\ndescriptions must be updated from “per-tenant” to per-owner semantics: change\nthe Protocol/Format for Usage Collector to “Asynchronous per-owner usage\nreports” including explicit owner identifiers (owner_id and owner_type:\nuser|tenant) and ownership metadata, and change Quota Enforcement to\n“Synchronous per-owner quota check requests” that accept owner_id/owner_type and\nconsent to ownership-transfer accounting; also update the Compatibility notes to\nreference owner-based semantics in §5.5. Apply the same owner-based wording to\nthe other occurrence of these contracts in the document.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:grasshopper -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit 1afc2c9","created_at":"2026-03-07T17:51:27Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900144307","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144307"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900144307"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144307/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":1027,"original_start_line":1018,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":1043,"original_line":1032,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1054,"position":1065,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144310","pull_request_review_id":3909221215,"id":2900144310,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3LC2","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+\n+Content-type validation does not apply to direct uploads (single-part or multipart) via presigned URLs because\n+FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. First-part validation for multipart uploads provides the same level of guarantee as single-part\n+validation — magic bytes reside at the start of the file and are contained in the first part. Post-assembly\n+re-validation would require downloading the assembled file from the backend, negating the efficiency benefits of\n+multipart upload. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying on\n+strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-owner\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion) and on ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`). For ownership transfers, the system **MUST** emit a usage report for both\n+the previous owner (storage decrease) and the new owner (storage increase). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be\n+asynchronous and **MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Ownership\n+transfers shift per-owner storage consumption without changing total platform storage — without debit/credit reporting,\n+billing and quota data become stale after transfers. Asynchronous reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by\n+usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any operation that increases storage\n+consumption (including uploads and version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform. Quota checks must cover all storage-consuming operations, not only initial uploads, to\n+prevent quota bypass through versioned overwrites.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) by placing a logical delete marker on the current version. The\n+  delete marker makes the current version inaccessible through normal file access (download, metadata queries) while\n+  all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers. Soft-deleted content is **not** physically\n+  removed from the storage backend — it continues to exist and **MUST** count against the owner's storage quota\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`)\n+- Restore a soft-deleted file by removing the delete marker, making the most recent non-current version the current\n+  version again. Restore **MUST** require the same authorization as upload\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+Garbage collection (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`) does **NOT** apply to soft-deleted versions —\n+soft-delete is an intentional owner action, not an orphaned state. Cleanup of soft-deleted versions is governed by\n+retention policies (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`).\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2). Logical delete markers (rather than physical\n+removal) enable restoration and follow the established pattern of S3 versioned deletes, GCS soft-delete, and Azure Blob\n+soft-delete. Counting soft-deleted content against quota prevents quota bypass through repeated soft-delete cycles.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+Idempotency keys **MUST** be scoped to the file owner specified in the upload request — the same entity that will own\n+the resulting file (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`). When the owner is a tenant, the key is unique within that\n+tenant's namespace. When the owner is a user, the key is unique within that user's namespace. The same key value used by\n+different owners **MUST** be treated as distinct keys. The system **MUST NOT** allow idempotency key lookups to cross\n+owner boundaries — a request **MUST NOT** be able to detect whether a different owner has used a given key.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks. Owner-scoped key namespacing prevents cross-tenant information leaks and aligns with\n+the platform's tenant boundary enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### EventBroker Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-eventbroker`\n+\n+**Direction**: bidirectional (publishes file events; consumes platform events such as owner deletion)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous event publishing and consumption via EventBroker module\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform event protocol; event schema changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Serverless Runtime Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-serverless-runtime`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Serverless Runtime)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Workflow invocation for configurable lifecycle operations (e.g., owner deletion disposition)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform Serverless Runtime invocation protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### Upload and Share a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-upload-share`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- Authorization Service grants write access\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. User uploads file content with metadata (name, mime_type, GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage persists content, assigns ownership to the user, and stores metadata (including GTS file type)\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+7. FileStorage returns persistent URL and file identifier\n+8. User creates a shareable link with desired scope and expiration\n+9. FileStorage returns the shareable link URL\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File stored with metadata and ownership\n+- Shareable link active with configured scope and expiration\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type**: FileStorage rejects the upload with a validation error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated (type or size)\n+\n+### Fetch File for Module Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls download with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage retrieves file content from the storage backend\n+4. FileStorage returns content with metadata (mime_type, size, GTS file type)\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Content and metadata returned to the requesting module\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Generate and Access Signed URL\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-signed-url`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User owns the file or has write access\n+- Backend supports presigned URLs capability\n+- Policy permits signed URL sharing\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests a signed URL for a file with a specified expiration\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+3. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates sharing policy allows signed URLs; in phase 1 all sharing models are\n+   permitted\n+4. FileStorage generates a presigned download URL using its own backend credentials, scoped to the file and time-limited\n+5. FileStorage records the signed URL metadata (file, expiration, owner) for visibility and audit\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for signed URL creation\n+7. Owner shares the signed URL with an external consumer\n+8. External consumer downloads the file directly from the storage backend using the signed URL — no authentication\n+   required, backend validates signature\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content delivered to external consumer directly from storage backend without authentication\n+- Signed URL metadata recorded for visibility and audit\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted for signed URL creation\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Expired URL**: Storage backend rejects the request (signature expiration enforced by backend)\n+- **Invalid signature**: Storage backend rejects the request\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Sharing model restricted by policy**: FileStorage returns policy-violation error\n+\n+### Validate File Metadata Before Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-get-metadata`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls get_metadata with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage returns metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, owner, availability) without transferring content\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Metadata returned; no content transferred\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Direct Upload from External Client\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-direct-upload`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- Client is authenticated with a valid API token\n+- Storage backend supports presigned URLs (e.g., S3, GCS, Azure Blob)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Client requests a direct transfer URL for upload from FileStorage, providing file metadata (name, mime_type, size,\n+   GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage registers the file metadata (including GTS file type) and ownership, assigns the target backend path\n+6. FileStorage generates a presigned upload URL using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access key), scoped to the\n+   assigned path and time-limited\n+7. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+8. FileStorage returns the presigned URL and file identifier to the client\n+9. Client uploads file content directly to the storage backend using the presigned URL\n+10. Storage backend validates the signature against its own key material and accepts the upload\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File metadata and ownership registered in FileStorage before upload\n+- File content stored on backend via presigned URL — never transited through FileStorage\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type at step 2**: FileStorage rejects with a validation error; no presigned URL issued\n+- **Authorization denied at step 3**: FileStorage returns access-denied error; no presigned URL issued\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation at step 4**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated\n+- **Presigned URL expired**: Backend rejects the upload; client must request a new presigned URL from FileStorage\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable; client must use standard (proxied) upload instead\n+- **Upload never completed (abandoned or failed)**: Metadata registered at step 5 remains unconfirmed; garbage\n+  collection handles cleanup per `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+### Delete a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-delete-file`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests deletion of a file by its identifier\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for delete on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+3. FileStorage revokes all active shareable links associated with the file\n+4. FileStorage deletes the file content from the storage backend\n+5. FileStorage removes file metadata and ownership records\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the deletion\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content removed from storage backend\n+- All associated shareable links invalidated; issued signed URLs remain valid until their expiration\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`) — the underlying content is no longer available on the backend\n+- Metadata and ownership records removed\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Cross-tenant attempt**: FileStorage returns access-denied error (tenant boundary enforcement)\n+\n+### Manage Shareable Links\n \n-**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-manage-links`\n \n-**Role**: Fetches input media (images, audio, video, documents) before provider calls and stores generated output media.\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n \n-## 3. Functional Requirements\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-#### Fetch Media by URL\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-fetch-media-v1`\n+**Main Flow**:\n \n+1. Owner requests the list of all active shareable links and signed URLs for a file\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+3. FileStorage returns the list with each link's scope, expiration, and creation date\n+4. Owner identifies a link to revoke\n+5. Owner requests revocation of the link by its identifier\n+6. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+7. FileStorage invalidates the link immediately\n+8. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation\n \n-The system must retrieve file content and metadata by URL for LLM Gateway consumption.\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+- Revoked link returns access-denied on subsequent access\n+- Remaining links unaffected\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n \n-#### Store Generated Content\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-store-content-v1`\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- **No active links**: FileStorage returns an empty list\n+- **Link not found**: FileStorage returns link_not_found error\n+- **Owner creates a new link**: Owner requests a shareable link with desired scope and expiration; FileStorage creates\n+  and returns the link URL; *(Phase 2)* audit record emitted for link creation\n \n+### Multi-Backend Deployment\n \n-The system must store generated media (images, audio, video) and return accessible URL.\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-backend-config`\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n \n-#### Get Metadata\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata-v1`\n+- FileStorage is deployed with a configured storage backend\n \n+**Main Flow**:\n \n-The system must return file metadata (size, mime_type) without fetching full content for validation purposes.\n+1. Deployment A configures FileStorage with an S3-compatible backend (e.g., AWS S3)\n+2. Deployment B configures FileStorage with a different backend (e.g., Azure Blob Storage)\n+3. Both deployments expose identical FileStorage SDK and REST APIs\n+4. CyberFabric modules interact with FileStorage through the SDK trait without awareness of the underlying backend\n+5. Upload, download, delete, metadata, and link operations behave identically regardless of backend\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-## 4. Use Cases\n+- All functional requirements are met identically across different backend configurations\n+- Consuming modules require zero code changes when the backend changes\n \n-#### UC-001: Fetch Media by URL\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media-v1`\n+- **Backend-specific feature unavailable**: FileStorage returns a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable\n+  (e.g., signed URL or direct transfer request rejected when backend does not support presigned URLs)\n \n-**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+### Configure Policy\n \n-**Preconditions**: File exists at URL.\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-configure-policy`\n \n-**Flow**:\n-1. LLM Gateway sends fetch(url)\n-2. FileStorage retrieves file content\n-3. FileStorage returns content + metadata\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n \n-**Postconditions**: Content returned to Gateway.\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-**Acceptance criteria**:\n-- Returns file_not_found if file does not exist\n-- Returns content with mime_type and size\n-- Supports streaming for large files\n+- User has tenant administration privileges (for tenant-level policy) or is an authenticated user (for user-level\n+  policy)\n \n-#### UC-002: Store Generated Content\n+**Main Flow**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-store-content-v1`\n+1. Tenant admin or user defines policies: allowed file types, size limits (global and per-type), enabled event types,\n+   and permitted sharing models\n+2. FileStorage validates and stores the policy configuration\n+3. Subsequent file operations are enforced against the effective policy (most restrictive per aspect across tenant and\n+   user levels)\n \n-**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-**Preconditions**: Content available for storage.\n+- Policy active and enforced on all file operations\n \n-**Flow**:\n-1. LLM Gateway sends store(content, metadata)\n-2. FileStorage persists content\n-3. FileStorage returns accessible URL\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-**Postconditions**: File stored, URL returned.\n+- **Invalid policy**: FileStorage returns validation error with details\n \n-**Acceptance criteria**:\n-- URL immediately accessible after store returns\n-- Metadata stored with file (mime_type, source, timestamps)\n-- Storage_unavailable error if service down\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n \n-#### UC-003: Get Metadata\n+- [ ] File upload returns persistent URL and stores metadata (name, size, type, dates, owner)\n+- [ ] File download returns content with correct metadata\n+- [ ] File deletion cascades to all associated shareable links\n+- [ ] Authorization checked for every file operation via Authorization Service\n+- [ ] Tenant boundary enforced — cross-tenant modification/deletion rejected\n+- [ ] Shareable links work with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- [ ] Signed URLs point directly to storage backend, grant download access without authentication, and storage backend\n+  rejects after expiration\n+- [ ] File owner can list active shareable links and signed URLs; can revoke shareable links (signed URLs are\n+  non-revocable)\n+- [ ] Audit record emitted for every write operation\n+- [ ] Policies enforce file type and size restrictions on upload (most restrictive wins across tenant and user levels)\n+- [ ] Direct transfer presigned URLs allow upload directly to storage backend without proxying through FileStorage\n+- [ ] Presigned URLs are generated using FileStorage's own backend credentials and validated by the backend via\n+  signature verification\n+- [ ] Signed URL and direct transfer requests rejected with clear error when backend does not support presigned URLs\n+- [ ] file_not_found error returned for non-existent files\n+- [ ] access_denied error returned for unauthorized operations\n+- [ ] Metadata-only queries complete without transferring file content\n+- [ ] File content is immutable — no in-place content update; changes require a new upload\n+- [ ] Custom metadata is updatable by the file owner; system-managed metadata is not user-updatable\n+- [ ] Custom metadata update changes the file's last modified date\n+- [ ] File ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer or owner deletion workflows\n+- [ ] Every file has a mandatory GTS file type assigned at upload time; uploads without a file type are rejected\n+- [ ] GTS file type is immutable after creation\n+- [ ] Authorization requests include the file's GTS type, enabling per-type access decisions\n+- [ ] A module authorized only for type A cannot access files of type B\n+- [ ] FileStorage SDK and REST API behave identically regardless of configured storage backend\n+- [ ] File listing returns metadata only, is paginated, and requires owner type filter\n+- [ ] Multipart upload assembles parts into a complete file with correct metadata\n+- [ ] Upload rejected when declared mime_type does not match actual file content\n+- [ ] Orphaned metadata records from unconfirmed direct uploads are detected, reconciled against backend state, and\n+  cleaned up automatically\n+- [ ] File owner can toggle download availability via metadata update\n+- [ ] Each backend declares its supported capabilities (presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload)\n+- [ ] Consumers can discover backend capabilities at runtime\n+- [ ] Operations requiring an unsupported capability return a clear error\n+- [ ] File versioning creates a new version on each upload; previous versions remain accessible by opaque version ID\n+- [ ] All versions of a file are listable with version ID, size, timestamp, and current-version flag\n+- [ ] Soft-delete places a logical delete marker; current version becomes inaccessible but content is not physically\n+  removed\n+- [ ] Soft-deleted content counts against storage quota\n+- [ ] Restore of a soft-deleted file removes the delete marker and reinstates the previous current version\n+- [ ] Garbage collection does not clean up soft-deleted versions\n+- [ ] Permanent delete of a specific version removes only that version\n+- [ ] Declared capabilities are independently configurable (enable/disable) per backend\n+- [ ] A capability disabled by configuration behaves identically to an unsupported capability\n+- [ ] Download and metadata responses include ETag header\n+- [ ] Conditional download with If-None-Match returns 304 Not Modified when file is unchanged\n+- [ ] Metadata update with If-Match returns 412 Precondition Failed when file state has changed\n+- [ ] Retried upload with the same idempotency key returns the original result without creating a duplicate file\n+- [ ] Owner deletion event from EventBroker triggers a configurable Serverless Runtime workflow for file disposition\n+- [ ] Files of a deleted owner are retained as orphaned when no workflow is configured\n+- [ ] Server-side encryption is applied when the encryption capability is available and enabled for the backend\n+- [ ] Upload rejected when storage quota would be exceeded (Quota Enforcement service check)\n+- [ ] Usage report emitted asynchronously on every storage-consuming write operation; file operations not blocked if\n+  Usage Collector is unavailable","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add acceptance checks for the new owner-scoping rules.**\n\nThe checklist verifies retry behavior and generic usage emission, but it does not cover the two security-sensitive clarifications added above: the same idempotency key reused by different owners must stay isolated, and ownership transfer must emit usage updates for both the previous and new owner. Those should be explicit acceptance criteria as well.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 1397 - 1403, Update the\nacceptance checklist to include two owner-scoping checks: (1) add an item that\n\"Retried upload with the same idempotency key by a different owner does not\nreturn or create the original owner's file\" to assert idempotency keys are\nisolated per owner; and (2) add an item that \"Ownership transfer emits usage\nreports for both previous and new owner\" to ensure the transfer path triggers\nusage emission for both parties; place these items alongside the existing retry\nand usage criteria in the same checklist block so testers can verify these\nsecurity-sensitive behaviors.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:grasshopper -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Addressed in commit 1afc2c9\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-03-07T17:51:27Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:31Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900144310","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144310"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900144310"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900144310/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":1439,"original_start_line":1397,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":1447,"original_line":1403,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1468,"position":1512,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900277534","pull_request_review_id":3909353613,"id":2900277534,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3rke","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+\n+Content-type validation does not apply to direct uploads (single-part or multipart) via presigned URLs because\n+FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. First-part validation for multipart uploads provides the same level of guarantee as single-part\n+validation — magic bytes reside at the start of the file and are contained in the first part. Post-assembly\n+re-validation would require downloading the assembled file from the backend, negating the efficiency benefits of\n+multipart upload. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying on\n+strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-owner\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion) and on ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`). For ownership transfers, the system **MUST** emit a usage report for both\n+the previous owner (storage decrease) and the new owner (storage increase). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be\n+asynchronous and **MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Ownership\n+transfers shift per-owner storage consumption without changing total platform storage — without debit/credit reporting,\n+billing and quota data become stale after transfers. Asynchronous reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by\n+usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any operation that increases storage\n+consumption (including uploads and version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform. Quota checks must cover all storage-consuming operations, not only initial uploads, to\n+prevent quota bypass through versioned overwrites.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) by placing a logical delete marker on the current version. The\n+  delete marker makes the current version inaccessible through normal file access (download, metadata queries) while\n+  all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers. Soft-deleted content is **not** physically\n+  removed from the storage backend — it continues to exist and **MUST** count against the owner's storage quota\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`)\n+- Restore a soft-deleted file by removing the delete marker, making the most recent non-current version the current\n+  version again. Restore **MUST** require the same authorization as upload\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+Garbage collection (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`) does **NOT** apply to soft-deleted versions —\n+soft-delete is an intentional owner action, not an orphaned state. Cleanup of soft-deleted versions is governed by\n+retention policies (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`).\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2). Logical delete markers (rather than physical\n+removal) enable restoration and follow the established pattern of S3 versioned deletes, GCS soft-delete, and Azure Blob\n+soft-delete. Counting soft-deleted content against quota prevents quota bypass through repeated soft-delete cycles.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+Idempotency keys **MUST** be scoped to the file owner specified in the upload request — the same entity that will own\n+the resulting file (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`). When the owner is a tenant, the key is unique within that\n+tenant's namespace. When the owner is a user, the key is unique within that user's namespace. The same key value used by\n+different owners **MUST** be treated as distinct keys. The system **MUST NOT** allow idempotency key lookups to cross\n+owner boundaries — a request **MUST NOT** be able to detect whether a different owner has used a given key.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks. Owner-scoped key namespacing prevents cross-tenant information leaks and aligns with\n+the platform's tenant boundary enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### EventBroker Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-eventbroker`\n+\n+**Direction**: bidirectional (publishes file events; consumes platform events such as owner deletion)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous event publishing and consumption via EventBroker module\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform event protocol; event schema changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Serverless Runtime Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-serverless-runtime`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Serverless Runtime)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Workflow invocation for configurable lifecycle operations (e.g., owner deletion disposition)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform Serverless Runtime invocation protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### Upload and Share a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-upload-share`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- Authorization Service grants write access\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. User uploads file content with metadata (name, mime_type, GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage persists content, assigns ownership to the user, and stores metadata (including GTS file type)\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+7. FileStorage returns persistent URL and file identifier\n+8. User creates a shareable link with desired scope and expiration\n+9. FileStorage returns the shareable link URL\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File stored with metadata and ownership\n+- Shareable link active with configured scope and expiration\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type**: FileStorage rejects the upload with a validation error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated (type or size)\n+\n+### Fetch File for Module Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls download with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage retrieves file content from the storage backend\n+4. FileStorage returns content with metadata (mime_type, size, GTS file type)\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Content and metadata returned to the requesting module\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Generate and Access Signed URL\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-signed-url`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User owns the file or has write access\n+- Backend supports presigned URLs capability\n+- Policy permits signed URL sharing\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests a signed URL for a file with a specified expiration\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+3. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates sharing policy allows signed URLs; in phase 1 all sharing models are\n+   permitted\n+4. FileStorage generates a presigned download URL using its own backend credentials, scoped to the file and time-limited\n+5. FileStorage records the signed URL metadata (file, expiration, owner) for visibility and audit\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for signed URL creation\n+7. Owner shares the signed URL with an external consumer\n+8. External consumer downloads the file directly from the storage backend using the signed URL — no authentication\n+   required, backend validates signature\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content delivered to external consumer directly from storage backend without authentication\n+- Signed URL metadata recorded for visibility and audit\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted for signed URL creation\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Expired URL**: Storage backend rejects the request (signature expiration enforced by backend)\n+- **Invalid signature**: Storage backend rejects the request\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Sharing model restricted by policy**: FileStorage returns policy-violation error\n+\n+### Validate File Metadata Before Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-get-metadata`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls get_metadata with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage returns metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, owner, availability) without transferring content\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Metadata returned; no content transferred\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Direct Upload from External Client\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-direct-upload`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- Client is authenticated with a valid API token\n+- Storage backend supports presigned URLs (e.g., S3, GCS, Azure Blob)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Client requests a direct transfer URL for upload from FileStorage, providing file metadata (name, mime_type, size,\n+   GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage registers the file metadata (including GTS file type) and ownership, assigns the target backend path\n+6. FileStorage generates a presigned upload URL using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access key), scoped to the\n+   assigned path and time-limited\n+7. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+8. FileStorage returns the presigned URL and file identifier to the client\n+9. Client uploads file content directly to the storage backend using the presigned URL\n+10. Storage backend validates the signature against its own key material and accepts the upload\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File metadata and ownership registered in FileStorage before upload\n+- File content stored on backend via presigned URL — never transited through FileStorage\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type at step 2**: FileStorage rejects with a validation error; no presigned URL issued\n+- **Authorization denied at step 3**: FileStorage returns access-denied error; no presigned URL issued\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation at step 4**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated\n+- **Presigned URL expired**: Backend rejects the upload; client must request a new presigned URL from FileStorage\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable; client must use standard (proxied) upload instead\n+- **Upload never completed (abandoned or failed)**: Metadata registered at step 5 remains unconfirmed; garbage\n+  collection handles cleanup per `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+### Delete a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-delete-file`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests deletion of a file by its identifier\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for delete on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+3. FileStorage revokes all active shareable links associated with the file\n+4. FileStorage deletes the file content from the storage backend\n+5. FileStorage removes file metadata and ownership records\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the deletion\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content removed from storage backend\n+- All associated shareable links invalidated; issued signed URLs remain valid until their expiration\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`) — the underlying content is no longer available on the backend\n+- Metadata and ownership records removed\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Cross-tenant attempt**: FileStorage returns access-denied error (tenant boundary enforcement)\n+\n+### Manage Shareable Links\n \n-**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-manage-links`\n \n-**Role**: Fetches input media (images, audio, video, documents) before provider calls and stores generated output media.\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n \n-## 3. Functional Requirements\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-#### Fetch Media by URL\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-fetch-media-v1`\n+**Main Flow**:\n \n+1. Owner requests the list of all active shareable links and signed URLs for a file\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+3. FileStorage returns the list with each link's scope, expiration, and creation date\n+4. Owner identifies a link to revoke\n+5. Owner requests revocation of the link by its identifier\n+6. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+7. FileStorage invalidates the link immediately\n+8. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation\n \n-The system must retrieve file content and metadata by URL for LLM Gateway consumption.\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+- Revoked link returns access-denied on subsequent access\n+- Remaining links unaffected\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n \n-#### Store Generated Content\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-store-content-v1`\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- **No active links**: FileStorage returns an empty list\n+- **Link not found**: FileStorage returns link_not_found error\n+- **Owner creates a new link**: Owner requests a shareable link with desired scope and expiration; FileStorage creates\n+  and returns the link URL; *(Phase 2)* audit record emitted for link creation\n \n+### Multi-Backend Deployment\n \n-The system must store generated media (images, audio, video) and return accessible URL.\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-backend-config`\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n \n-#### Get Metadata\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata-v1`\n+- FileStorage is deployed with a configured storage backend\n \n+**Main Flow**:\n \n-The system must return file metadata (size, mime_type) without fetching full content for validation purposes.\n+1. Deployment A configures FileStorage with an S3-compatible backend (e.g., AWS S3)\n+2. Deployment B configures FileStorage with a different backend (e.g., Azure Blob Storage)\n+3. Both deployments expose identical FileStorage SDK and REST APIs\n+4. CyberFabric modules interact with FileStorage through the SDK trait without awareness of the underlying backend\n+5. Upload, download, delete, metadata, and link operations behave identically regardless of backend\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-## 4. Use Cases\n+- All functional requirements are met identically across different backend configurations\n+- Consuming modules require zero code changes when the backend changes\n \n-#### UC-001: Fetch Media by URL\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media-v1`\n+- **Backend-specific feature unavailable**: FileStorage returns a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable\n+  (e.g., signed URL or direct transfer request rejected when backend does not support presigned URLs)\n \n-**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+### Configure Policy\n \n-**Preconditions**: File exists at URL.\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-configure-policy`\n \n-**Flow**:\n-1. LLM Gateway sends fetch(url)\n-2. FileStorage retrieves file content\n-3. FileStorage returns content + metadata\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n \n-**Postconditions**: Content returned to Gateway.\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-**Acceptance criteria**:\n-- Returns file_not_found if file does not exist\n-- Returns content with mime_type and size\n-- Supports streaming for large files\n+- User has tenant administration privileges (for tenant-level policy) or is an authenticated user (for user-level\n+  policy)\n \n-#### UC-002: Store Generated Content\n+**Main Flow**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-store-content-v1`\n+1. Tenant admin or user defines policies: allowed file types, size limits (global and per-type), enabled event types,\n+   and permitted sharing models\n+2. FileStorage validates and stores the policy configuration\n+3. Subsequent file operations are enforced against the effective policy (most restrictive per aspect across tenant and\n+   user levels)\n \n-**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-**Preconditions**: Content available for storage.\n+- Policy active and enforced on all file operations\n \n-**Flow**:\n-1. LLM Gateway sends store(content, metadata)\n-2. FileStorage persists content\n-3. FileStorage returns accessible URL\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-**Postconditions**: File stored, URL returned.\n+- **Invalid policy**: FileStorage returns validation error with details\n \n-**Acceptance criteria**:\n-- URL immediately accessible after store returns\n-- Metadata stored with file (mime_type, source, timestamps)\n-- Storage_unavailable error if service down\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n \n-#### UC-003: Get Metadata\n+- [ ] File upload returns persistent URL and stores metadata (name, size, type, dates, owner)\n+- [ ] File download returns content with correct metadata\n+- [ ] File deletion cascades to all associated shareable links\n+- [ ] Authorization checked for every file operation via Authorization Service\n+- [ ] Tenant boundary enforced — cross-tenant modification/deletion rejected\n+- [ ] Shareable links work with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- [ ] Signed URLs point directly to storage backend, grant download access without authentication, and storage backend\n+  rejects after expiration\n+- [ ] File owner can list active shareable links and signed URLs; can revoke shareable links (signed URLs are\n+  non-revocable)\n+- [ ] Audit record emitted for every write operation\n+- [ ] Policies enforce file type and size restrictions on upload (most restrictive wins across tenant and user levels)\n+- [ ] Direct transfer presigned URLs allow upload directly to storage backend without proxying through FileStorage\n+- [ ] Presigned URLs are generated using FileStorage's own backend credentials and validated by the backend via\n+  signature verification\n+- [ ] Signed URL and direct transfer requests rejected with clear error when backend does not support presigned URLs\n+- [ ] file_not_found error returned for non-existent files\n+- [ ] access_denied error returned for unauthorized operations\n+- [ ] Metadata-only queries complete without transferring file content\n+- [ ] File content is immutable — no in-place content update; changes require a new upload\n+- [ ] Custom metadata is updatable by the file owner; system-managed metadata is not user-updatable\n+- [ ] Custom metadata update changes the file's last modified date\n+- [ ] File ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer or owner deletion workflows\n+- [ ] Every file has a mandatory GTS file type assigned at upload time; uploads without a file type are rejected\n+- [ ] GTS file type is immutable after creation\n+- [ ] Authorization requests include the file's GTS type, enabling per-type access decisions\n+- [ ] A module authorized only for type A cannot access files of type B\n+- [ ] FileStorage SDK and REST API behave identically regardless of configured storage backend\n+- [ ] File listing returns metadata only, is paginated, and requires owner type filter\n+- [ ] Multipart upload assembles parts into a complete file with correct metadata\n+- [ ] Upload rejected when declared mime_type does not match actual file content\n+- [ ] Orphaned metadata records from unconfirmed direct uploads are detected, reconciled against backend state, and\n+  cleaned up automatically\n+- [ ] File owner can toggle download availability via metadata update\n+- [ ] Each backend declares its supported capabilities (presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload)\n+- [ ] Consumers can discover backend capabilities at runtime\n+- [ ] Operations requiring an unsupported capability return a clear error\n+- [ ] File versioning creates a new version on each upload; previous versions remain accessible by opaque version ID\n+- [ ] All versions of a file are listable with version ID, size, timestamp, and current-version flag\n+- [ ] Soft-delete places a logical delete marker; current version becomes inaccessible but content is not physically\n+  removed\n+- [ ] Soft-deleted content counts against storage quota\n+- [ ] Restore of a soft-deleted file removes the delete marker and reinstates the previous current version\n+- [ ] Garbage collection does not clean up soft-deleted versions\n+- [ ] Permanent delete of a specific version removes only that version\n+- [ ] Declared capabilities are independently configurable (enable/disable) per backend\n+- [ ] A capability disabled by configuration behaves identically to an unsupported capability\n+- [ ] Download and metadata responses include ETag header\n+- [ ] Conditional download with If-None-Match returns 304 Not Modified when file is unchanged\n+- [ ] Metadata update with If-Match returns 412 Precondition Failed when file state has changed\n+- [ ] Retried upload with the same idempotency key returns the original result without creating a duplicate file\n+- [ ] Owner deletion event from EventBroker triggers a configurable Serverless Runtime workflow for file disposition\n+- [ ] Files of a deleted owner are retained as orphaned when no workflow is configured\n+- [ ] Server-side encryption is applied when the encryption capability is available and enabled for the backend\n+- [ ] Upload rejected when storage quota would be exceeded (Quota Enforcement service check)\n+- [ ] Usage report emitted asynchronously on every storage-consuming write operation; file operations not blocked if\n+  Usage Collector is unavailable","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Resolved","created_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:06Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:06Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900277534","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900277534"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900277534"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900277534/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":1439,"original_start_line":1397,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":1447,"original_line":1403,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2900144310,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":1468,"position":1512,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278150","pull_request_review_id":3909354285,"id":2900278150,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3ruG","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+\n+Content-type validation does not apply to direct uploads (single-part or multipart) via presigned URLs because\n+FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. First-part validation for multipart uploads provides the same level of guarantee as single-part\n+validation — magic bytes reside at the start of the file and are contained in the first part. Post-assembly\n+re-validation would require downloading the assembled file from the backend, negating the efficiency benefits of\n+multipart upload. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying on\n+strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-owner\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion) and on ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`). For ownership transfers, the system **MUST** emit a usage report for both\n+the previous owner (storage decrease) and the new owner (storage increase). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be\n+asynchronous and **MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Ownership\n+transfers shift per-owner storage consumption without changing total platform storage — without debit/credit reporting,\n+billing and quota data become stale after transfers. Asynchronous reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by\n+usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any operation that increases storage\n+consumption (including uploads and version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform. Quota checks must cover all storage-consuming operations, not only initial uploads, to\n+prevent quota bypass through versioned overwrites.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) by placing a logical delete marker on the current version. The\n+  delete marker makes the current version inaccessible through normal file access (download, metadata queries) while\n+  all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers. Soft-deleted content is **not** physically\n+  removed from the storage backend — it continues to exist and **MUST** count against the owner's storage quota\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`)\n+- Restore a soft-deleted file by removing the delete marker, making the most recent non-current version the current\n+  version again. Restore **MUST** require the same authorization as upload\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+Garbage collection (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`) does **NOT** apply to soft-deleted versions —\n+soft-delete is an intentional owner action, not an orphaned state. Cleanup of soft-deleted versions is governed by\n+retention policies (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`).\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2). Logical delete markers (rather than physical\n+removal) enable restoration and follow the established pattern of S3 versioned deletes, GCS soft-delete, and Azure Blob\n+soft-delete. Counting soft-deleted content against quota prevents quota bypass through repeated soft-delete cycles.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+Idempotency keys **MUST** be scoped to the file owner specified in the upload request — the same entity that will own\n+the resulting file (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`). When the owner is a tenant, the key is unique within that\n+tenant's namespace. When the owner is a user, the key is unique within that user's namespace. The same key value used by\n+different owners **MUST** be treated as distinct keys. The system **MUST NOT** allow idempotency key lookups to cross\n+owner boundaries — a request **MUST NOT** be able to detect whether a different owner has used a given key.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks. Owner-scoped key namespacing prevents cross-tenant information leaks and aligns with\n+the platform's tenant boundary enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed","created_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:28Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:28Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278150","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278150"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278150"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278150/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":1027,"original_start_line":1018,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":1043,"original_line":1032,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2900144307,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":1054,"position":1065,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278277","pull_request_review_id":3909354404,"id":2900278277,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3rwF","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+\n+Content-type validation does not apply to direct uploads (single-part or multipart) via presigned URLs because\n+FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. First-part validation for multipart uploads provides the same level of guarantee as single-part\n+validation — magic bytes reside at the start of the file and are contained in the first part. Post-assembly\n+re-validation would require downloading the assembled file from the backend, negating the efficiency benefits of\n+multipart upload. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying on\n+strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-owner\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion) and on ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`). For ownership transfers, the system **MUST** emit a usage report for both\n+the previous owner (storage decrease) and the new owner (storage increase). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be\n+asynchronous and **MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Ownership\n+transfers shift per-owner storage consumption without changing total platform storage — without debit/credit reporting,\n+billing and quota data become stale after transfers. Asynchronous reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by\n+usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any operation that increases storage\n+consumption (including uploads and version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform. Quota checks must cover all storage-consuming operations, not only initial uploads, to\n+prevent quota bypass through versioned overwrites.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) by placing a logical delete marker on the current version. The\n+  delete marker makes the current version inaccessible through normal file access (download, metadata queries) while\n+  all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers. Soft-deleted content is **not** physically\n+  removed from the storage backend — it continues to exist and **MUST** count against the owner's storage quota\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`)\n+- Restore a soft-deleted file by removing the delete marker, making the most recent non-current version the current\n+  version again. Restore **MUST** require the same authorization as upload\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+Garbage collection (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`) does **NOT** apply to soft-deleted versions —\n+soft-delete is an intentional owner action, not an orphaned state. Cleanup of soft-deleted versions is governed by\n+retention policies (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`).\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2). Logical delete markers (rather than physical\n+removal) enable restoration and follow the established pattern of S3 versioned deletes, GCS soft-delete, and Azure Blob\n+soft-delete. Counting soft-deleted content against quota prevents quota bypass through repeated soft-delete cycles.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+Idempotency keys **MUST** be scoped to the file owner specified in the upload request — the same entity that will own\n+the resulting file (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`). When the owner is a tenant, the key is unique within that\n+tenant's namespace. When the owner is a user, the key is unique within that user's namespace. The same key value used by\n+different owners **MUST** be treated as distinct keys. The system **MUST NOT** allow idempotency key lookups to cross\n+owner boundaries — a request **MUST NOT** be able to detect whether a different owner has used a given key.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks. Owner-scoped key namespacing prevents cross-tenant information leaks and aligns with\n+the platform's tenant boundary enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### EventBroker Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-eventbroker`\n+\n+**Direction**: bidirectional (publishes file events; consumes platform events such as owner deletion)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous event publishing and consumption via EventBroker module\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform event protocol; event schema changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Serverless Runtime Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-serverless-runtime`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Serverless Runtime)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Workflow invocation for configurable lifecycle operations (e.g., owner deletion disposition)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform Serverless Runtime invocation protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+## 8. Use Cases\n+\n+### Upload and Share a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-upload-share`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- Authorization Service grants write access\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. User uploads file content with metadata (name, mime_type, GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage persists content, assigns ownership to the user, and stores metadata (including GTS file type)\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+7. FileStorage returns persistent URL and file identifier\n+8. User creates a shareable link with desired scope and expiration\n+9. FileStorage returns the shareable link URL\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File stored with metadata and ownership\n+- Shareable link active with configured scope and expiration\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type**: FileStorage rejects the upload with a validation error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated (type or size)\n+\n+### Fetch File for Module Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls download with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage retrieves file content from the storage backend\n+4. FileStorage returns content with metadata (mime_type, size, GTS file type)\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Content and metadata returned to the requesting module\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Generate and Access Signed URL\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-signed-url`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User owns the file or has write access\n+- Backend supports presigned URLs capability\n+- Policy permits signed URL sharing\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests a signed URL for a file with a specified expiration\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+3. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates sharing policy allows signed URLs; in phase 1 all sharing models are\n+   permitted\n+4. FileStorage generates a presigned download URL using its own backend credentials, scoped to the file and time-limited\n+5. FileStorage records the signed URL metadata (file, expiration, owner) for visibility and audit\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for signed URL creation\n+7. Owner shares the signed URL with an external consumer\n+8. External consumer downloads the file directly from the storage backend using the signed URL — no authentication\n+   required, backend validates signature\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content delivered to external consumer directly from storage backend without authentication\n+- Signed URL metadata recorded for visibility and audit\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted for signed URL creation\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Expired URL**: Storage backend rejects the request (signature expiration enforced by backend)\n+- **Invalid signature**: Storage backend rejects the request\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Sharing model restricted by policy**: FileStorage returns policy-violation error\n+\n+### Validate File Metadata Before Processing\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-get-metadata`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- File exists at the specified URL\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Module calls get_metadata with a file URL\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for read on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file's GTS type in resource context\n+3. FileStorage returns metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, owner, availability) without transferring content\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- Metadata returned; no content transferred\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+\n+### Direct Upload from External Client\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-direct-upload`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- Client is authenticated with a valid API token\n+- Storage backend supports presigned URLs (e.g., S3, GCS, Azure Blob)\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Client requests a direct transfer URL for upload from FileStorage, providing file metadata (name, mime_type, size,\n+   GTS file type)\n+2. FileStorage validates the GTS file type format\n+3. FileStorage checks authorization for write on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` with the file type in resource context\n+4. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage validates file against policies (type, size); in phase 1 all uploads are accepted\n+5. FileStorage registers the file metadata (including GTS file type) and ownership, assigns the target backend path\n+6. FileStorage generates a presigned upload URL using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access key), scoped to the\n+   assigned path and time-limited\n+7. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the upload\n+8. FileStorage returns the presigned URL and file identifier to the client\n+9. Client uploads file content directly to the storage backend using the presigned URL\n+10. Storage backend validates the signature against its own key material and accepts the upload\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File metadata and ownership registered in FileStorage before upload\n+- File content stored on backend via presigned URL — never transited through FileStorage\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Missing or invalid GTS file type at step 2**: FileStorage rejects with a validation error; no presigned URL issued\n+- **Authorization denied at step 3**: FileStorage returns access-denied error; no presigned URL issued\n+- *(Phase 2)* **Policy violation at step 4**: FileStorage returns error indicating which policy was violated\n+- **Presigned URL expired**: Backend rejects the upload; client must request a new presigned URL from FileStorage\n+- **Backend does not support presigned URLs or capability is disabled**: FileStorage returns an error indicating the\n+  capability is unavailable; client must use standard (proxied) upload instead\n+- **Upload never completed (abandoned or failed)**: Metadata registered at step 5 remains unconfirmed; garbage\n+  collection handles cleanup per `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+### Delete a File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-delete-file`\n+\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Preconditions**:\n+\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n+\n+**Main Flow**:\n+\n+1. Owner requests deletion of a file by its identifier\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for delete on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+3. FileStorage revokes all active shareable links associated with the file\n+4. FileStorage deletes the file content from the storage backend\n+5. FileStorage removes file metadata and ownership records\n+6. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the deletion\n+\n+**Postconditions**:\n+\n+- File content removed from storage backend\n+- All associated shareable links invalidated; issued signed URLs remain valid until their expiration\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`) — the underlying content is no longer available on the backend\n+- Metadata and ownership records removed\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n+\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n+\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- **File not found**: FileStorage returns file_not_found error\n+- **Cross-tenant attempt**: FileStorage returns access-denied error (tenant boundary enforcement)\n+\n+### Manage Shareable Links\n \n-**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-manage-links`\n \n-**Role**: Fetches input media (images, audio, video, documents) before provider calls and stores generated output media.\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n \n-## 3. Functional Requirements\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-#### Fetch Media by URL\n+- User is authenticated\n+- User owns the file\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-fetch-media-v1`\n+**Main Flow**:\n \n+1. Owner requests the list of all active shareable links and signed URLs for a file\n+2. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+3. FileStorage returns the list with each link's scope, expiration, and creation date\n+4. Owner identifies a link to revoke\n+5. Owner requests revocation of the link by its identifier\n+6. FileStorage checks authorization for the owner on `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~`\n+7. FileStorage invalidates the link immediately\n+8. *(Phase 2)* FileStorage emits audit record for the link revocation\n \n-The system must retrieve file content and metadata by URL for LLM Gateway consumption.\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+- Revoked link returns access-denied on subsequent access\n+- Remaining links unaffected\n+- *(Phase 2)* Audit record emitted\n \n-#### Store Generated Content\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-store-content-v1`\n+- **Authorization denied**: FileStorage returns access-denied error\n+- **No active links**: FileStorage returns an empty list\n+- **Link not found**: FileStorage returns link_not_found error\n+- **Owner creates a new link**: Owner requests a shareable link with desired scope and expiration; FileStorage creates\n+  and returns the link URL; *(Phase 2)* audit record emitted for link creation\n \n+### Multi-Backend Deployment\n \n-The system must store generated media (images, audio, video) and return accessible URL.\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-backend-config`\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n \n-#### Get Metadata\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata-v1`\n+- FileStorage is deployed with a configured storage backend\n \n+**Main Flow**:\n \n-The system must return file metadata (size, mime_type) without fetching full content for validation purposes.\n+1. Deployment A configures FileStorage with an S3-compatible backend (e.g., AWS S3)\n+2. Deployment B configures FileStorage with a different backend (e.g., Azure Blob Storage)\n+3. Both deployments expose identical FileStorage SDK and REST APIs\n+4. CyberFabric modules interact with FileStorage through the SDK trait without awareness of the underlying backend\n+5. Upload, download, delete, metadata, and link operations behave identically regardless of backend\n \n-**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-## 4. Use Cases\n+- All functional requirements are met identically across different backend configurations\n+- Consuming modules require zero code changes when the backend changes\n \n-#### UC-001: Fetch Media by URL\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-fetch-media-v1`\n+- **Backend-specific feature unavailable**: FileStorage returns a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable\n+  (e.g., signed URL or direct transfer request rejected when backend does not support presigned URLs)\n \n-**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+### Configure Policy\n \n-**Preconditions**: File exists at URL.\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-configure-policy`\n \n-**Flow**:\n-1. LLM Gateway sends fetch(url)\n-2. FileStorage retrieves file content\n-3. FileStorage returns content + metadata\n+**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n \n-**Postconditions**: Content returned to Gateway.\n+**Preconditions**:\n \n-**Acceptance criteria**:\n-- Returns file_not_found if file does not exist\n-- Returns content with mime_type and size\n-- Supports streaming for large files\n+- User has tenant administration privileges (for tenant-level policy) or is an authenticated user (for user-level\n+  policy)\n \n-#### UC-002: Store Generated Content\n+**Main Flow**:\n \n-- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-usecase-store-content-v1`\n+1. Tenant admin or user defines policies: allowed file types, size limits (global and per-type), enabled event types,\n+   and permitted sharing models\n+2. FileStorage validates and stores the policy configuration\n+3. Subsequent file operations are enforced against the effective policy (most restrictive per aspect across tenant and\n+   user levels)\n \n-**Actor**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-llm-gateway`\n+**Postconditions**:\n \n-**Preconditions**: Content available for storage.\n+- Policy active and enforced on all file operations\n \n-**Flow**:\n-1. LLM Gateway sends store(content, metadata)\n-2. FileStorage persists content\n-3. FileStorage returns accessible URL\n+**Alternative Flows**:\n \n-**Postconditions**: File stored, URL returned.\n+- **Invalid policy**: FileStorage returns validation error with details\n \n-**Acceptance criteria**:\n-- URL immediately accessible after store returns\n-- Metadata stored with file (mime_type, source, timestamps)\n-- Storage_unavailable error if service down\n+## 9. Acceptance Criteria\n \n-#### UC-003: Get Metadata\n+- [ ] File upload returns persistent URL and stores metadata (name, size, type, dates, owner)\n+- [ ] File download returns content with correct metadata\n+- [ ] File deletion cascades to all associated shareable links\n+- [ ] Authorization checked for every file operation via Authorization Service\n+- [ ] Tenant boundary enforced — cross-tenant modification/deletion rejected\n+- [ ] Shareable links work with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- [ ] Signed URLs point directly to storage backend, grant download access without authentication, and storage backend\n+  rejects after expiration\n+- [ ] File owner can list active shareable links and signed URLs; can revoke shareable links (signed URLs are\n+  non-revocable)\n+- [ ] Audit record emitted for every write operation\n+- [ ] Policies enforce file type and size restrictions on upload (most restrictive wins across tenant and user levels)\n+- [ ] Direct transfer presigned URLs allow upload directly to storage backend without proxying through FileStorage\n+- [ ] Presigned URLs are generated using FileStorage's own backend credentials and validated by the backend via\n+  signature verification\n+- [ ] Signed URL and direct transfer requests rejected with clear error when backend does not support presigned URLs\n+- [ ] file_not_found error returned for non-existent files\n+- [ ] access_denied error returned for unauthorized operations\n+- [ ] Metadata-only queries complete without transferring file content\n+- [ ] File content is immutable — no in-place content update; changes require a new upload\n+- [ ] Custom metadata is updatable by the file owner; system-managed metadata is not user-updatable\n+- [ ] Custom metadata update changes the file's last modified date\n+- [ ] File ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer or owner deletion workflows\n+- [ ] Every file has a mandatory GTS file type assigned at upload time; uploads without a file type are rejected\n+- [ ] GTS file type is immutable after creation\n+- [ ] Authorization requests include the file's GTS type, enabling per-type access decisions\n+- [ ] A module authorized only for type A cannot access files of type B\n+- [ ] FileStorage SDK and REST API behave identically regardless of configured storage backend\n+- [ ] File listing returns metadata only, is paginated, and requires owner type filter\n+- [ ] Multipart upload assembles parts into a complete file with correct metadata\n+- [ ] Upload rejected when declared mime_type does not match actual file content\n+- [ ] Orphaned metadata records from unconfirmed direct uploads are detected, reconciled against backend state, and\n+  cleaned up automatically\n+- [ ] File owner can toggle download availability via metadata update\n+- [ ] Each backend declares its supported capabilities (presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload)\n+- [ ] Consumers can discover backend capabilities at runtime\n+- [ ] Operations requiring an unsupported capability return a clear error\n+- [ ] File versioning creates a new version on each upload; previous versions remain accessible by opaque version ID\n+- [ ] All versions of a file are listable with version ID, size, timestamp, and current-version flag\n+- [ ] Soft-delete places a logical delete marker; current version becomes inaccessible but content is not physically\n+  removed\n+- [ ] Soft-deleted content counts against storage quota\n+- [ ] Restore of a soft-deleted file removes the delete marker and reinstates the previous current version\n+- [ ] Garbage collection does not clean up soft-deleted versions\n+- [ ] Permanent delete of a specific version removes only that version\n+- [ ] Declared capabilities are independently configurable (enable/disable) per backend\n+- [ ] A capability disabled by configuration behaves identically to an unsupported capability\n+- [ ] Download and metadata responses include ETag header\n+- [ ] Conditional download with If-None-Match returns 304 Not Modified when file is unchanged\n+- [ ] Metadata update with If-Match returns 412 Precondition Failed when file state has changed\n+- [ ] Retried upload with the same idempotency key returns the original result without creating a duplicate file\n+- [ ] Owner deletion event from EventBroker triggers a configurable Serverless Runtime workflow for file disposition\n+- [ ] Files of a deleted owner are retained as orphaned when no workflow is configured\n+- [ ] Server-side encryption is applied when the encryption capability is available and enabled for the backend\n+- [ ] Upload rejected when storage quota would be exceeded (Quota Enforcement service check)\n+- [ ] Usage report emitted asynchronously on every storage-consuming write operation; file operations not blocked if\n+  Usage Collector is unavailable","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@nonameffh`, confirmed! Both acceptance criteria items are now present in Section 9:\n- \"Retried upload with the same idempotency key by a different owner does not return or create the original owner's file\"\n- \"Ownership transfer emits usage reports for both previous and new owner\"\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n\n\n(\\_(\\\n(=' :')\n(,(')(')\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:1380-1392\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T12:24:51.660Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, specify that key rotation for signed/pre-signed URLs is a backend configuration responsibility, not a FileStorage module responsibility. Clarify that FileStorage uses backend credentials (e.g., AWS keys, GCS service accounts, Azure SAS tokens) supplied via configuration, and that key rotation and credential management are handled by the backend infrastructure or provider per their security policies. This should be documented explicitly to prevent future assumption that FileStorage handles rotation; no code changes are required in the FileStorage module for rotation.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:530-539\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T13:34:14.555Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, the term \"upload\" in the context of quota enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`) is inclusive of version creation — when versioning is enabled, uploading to an existing path is still an upload operation that creates a new version. No separate mention of \"version creation\" is needed in the quota requirement.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : For MODIFIED requirements, paste the complete updated requirement block (header + all scenarios) with behavior changes; the archiver replaces the entire requirement\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nIf you found this review helpful, would you consider giving us a shout-out on [X](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?related=CodeRabbitAI&text=Added%20owner-scoping%20acceptance%20checks%3A%20idempotency%20keys%20must%20be%20isolated%20per%20owner%2C%20and%20ownership%20transfer%20must%20emit%20usage%20for%20both%20old%20%26%20new%20owners.%20Solid%20security%20win%20%E2%80%94%20try%20%40coderabbitai%20%F0%9F%94%92%E2%9C%85&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcyberfabric%2Fcyberfabric-core%2Fpull%2F635%23discussion_r2900144310)?\n\nThank you for using CodeRabbit!\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:32Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:32Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278277","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278277"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278277"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278277/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":1439,"original_start_line":1397,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":1447,"original_line":1403,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2900144310,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1468,"position":1512,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278531","pull_request_review_id":3909354579,"id":2900278531,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3r0D","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+\n+Content-type validation does not apply to direct uploads (single-part or multipart) via presigned URLs because\n+FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. First-part validation for multipart uploads provides the same level of guarantee as single-part\n+validation — magic bytes reside at the start of the file and are contained in the first part. Post-assembly\n+re-validation would require downloading the assembled file from the backend, negating the efficiency benefits of\n+multipart upload. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying on\n+strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-owner\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion) and on ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`). For ownership transfers, the system **MUST** emit a usage report for both\n+the previous owner (storage decrease) and the new owner (storage increase). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be\n+asynchronous and **MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Ownership\n+transfers shift per-owner storage consumption without changing total platform storage — without debit/credit reporting,\n+billing and quota data become stale after transfers. Asynchronous reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by\n+usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any operation that increases storage\n+consumption (including uploads and version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform. Quota checks must cover all storage-consuming operations, not only initial uploads, to\n+prevent quota bypass through versioned overwrites.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) by placing a logical delete marker on the current version. The\n+  delete marker makes the current version inaccessible through normal file access (download, metadata queries) while\n+  all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers. Soft-deleted content is **not** physically\n+  removed from the storage backend — it continues to exist and **MUST** count against the owner's storage quota\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`)\n+- Restore a soft-deleted file by removing the delete marker, making the most recent non-current version the current\n+  version again. Restore **MUST** require the same authorization as upload\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Updated to S3-like behavior","created_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:41Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278531","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278531"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278531"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278531/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":681,"original_start_line":672,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":688,"original_line":679,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2900144305,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":701,"position":710,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278842","pull_request_review_id":3909355073,"id":2900278842,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3r46","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"nonameffh","id":5434885,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjU0MzQ4ODU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5434885?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh","html_url":"https://github.com/nonameffh","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nonameffh/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Clarifications added","created_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:55Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:55Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278842","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278842"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278842"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278842/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":235,"original_start_line":229,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":240,"original_line":233,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2900144303,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":255,"position":262,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278888","pull_request_review_id":3909355110,"id":2900278888,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3r5o","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+\n+Content-type validation does not apply to direct uploads (single-part or multipart) via presigned URLs because\n+FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. First-part validation for multipart uploads provides the same level of guarantee as single-part\n+validation — magic bytes reside at the start of the file and are contained in the first part. Post-assembly\n+re-validation would require downloading the assembled file from the backend, negating the efficiency benefits of\n+multipart upload. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying on\n+strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-owner\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion) and on ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`). For ownership transfers, the system **MUST** emit a usage report for both\n+the previous owner (storage decrease) and the new owner (storage increase). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be\n+asynchronous and **MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Ownership\n+transfers shift per-owner storage consumption without changing total platform storage — without debit/credit reporting,\n+billing and quota data become stale after transfers. Asynchronous reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by\n+usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any operation that increases storage\n+consumption (including uploads and version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform. Quota checks must cover all storage-consuming operations, not only initial uploads, to\n+prevent quota bypass through versioned overwrites.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) by placing a logical delete marker on the current version. The\n+  delete marker makes the current version inaccessible through normal file access (download, metadata queries) while\n+  all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers. Soft-deleted content is **not** physically\n+  removed from the storage backend — it continues to exist and **MUST** count against the owner's storage quota\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`)\n+- Restore a soft-deleted file by removing the delete marker, making the most recent non-current version the current\n+  version again. Restore **MUST** require the same authorization as upload\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Treat version identifiers as opaque strings — the system **MUST NOT** assume any specific format, ordering, or\n+  parseable structure of version identifiers across storage backends\n+\n+Garbage collection (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`) does **NOT** apply to soft-deleted versions —\n+soft-delete is an intentional owner action, not an orphaned state. Cleanup of soft-deleted versions is governed by\n+retention policies (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`).\n+\n+The system **MUST** apply the same authorization, tenant boundary enforcement, and audit requirements to all versioned\n+operations as to non-versioned file operations.\n+\n+**Rationale**: File versioning enables recovery from accidental overwrites and deletions, supports audit and compliance\n+workflows that require historical access to file content, and aligns with capabilities universally available across\n+major storage backends (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, MinIO, Ceph, Backblaze B2). Logical delete markers (rather than physical\n+removal) enable restoration and follow the established pattern of S3 versioned deletes, GCS soft-delete, and Azure Blob\n+soft-delete. Counting soft-deleted content against quota prevents quota bypass through repeated soft-delete cycles.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Encryption\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-encryption`\n+\n+File encryption requires the server-side encryption backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+When the encryption capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST** support server-side encryption of file\n+content at rest, configurable per backend and per policy.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive deployments require encryption at rest to meet compliance\n+requirements (GDPR, HIPAA, SOC 2) and protect stored data against unauthorized physical or logical access to the\n+storage backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.8 Audit\n+\n+#### Audit Trail\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-audit-trail`\n+\n+The system **MUST** produce an audit record for every write operation (upload, delete, metadata update, link creation,\n+link revocation). Audit records **MUST** include the operation type, actor identity, file identifier, timestamp, and\n+outcome (success or failure).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Audit trails are required for security forensics, compliance reporting, and operational troubleshooting.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Read Audit Logging\n+\n+- [ ] `p3` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support optional audit logging for read operations (downloads and metadata queries), configurable\n+per policy. When enabled by policy, the system **MUST** produce an audit record for every read operation\n+that passes through FileStorage — proxied downloads and shareable link access. Read audit logging does not apply to\n+presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and security-sensitive owners require visibility into who accessed their files and\n+when. Making read audit optional per policy avoids the performance and storage overhead of logging every read\n+across the platform, while enabling it where compliance demands it.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.9 Pluggable Storage Backends\n+\n+#### Backend Abstraction\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-abstraction`\n+\n+The system **MUST** abstract the storage layer behind a common interface, enabling support for multiple backend types (\n+S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV, local filesystem).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different deployments and tenants have different storage infrastructure; a common interface allows\n+backend selection without changing the module's core logic.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Backend Capabilities\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`\n+\n+The system **MUST** define a capability model for storage backends. Each backend **MUST** declare which optional\n+capabilities it supports. The system **MUST** support at least the following capabilities:\n+\n+- **Presigned URLs** — the backend can generate cryptographically signed, time-limited URLs for direct client-to-backend\n+  upload and download without proxying through FileStorage\n+- **Versioning** — the backend can maintain multiple versions of a file, identified by opaque version identifiers\n+- **Multipart Upload** — the backend natively supports chunked upload with independent part transfers and server-side\n+  assembly\n+- **Server-Side Encryption** — the backend can encrypt file content at rest using backend-managed or customer-provided\n+  keys\n+\n+Each declared capability **MUST** be independently configurable as enabled or disabled per backend. A capability that is\n+supported by the backend but disabled by configuration **MUST** behave identically to an unsupported capability — the\n+system **MUST NOT** expose or use it. Only capabilities that are both declared by the backend and enabled in\n+configuration are considered available.\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose the set of available (declared and enabled) capabilities per backend so that consumers can\n+discover them at runtime. When a consumer requests an operation that depends on an unavailable capability, the system\n+**MUST** return a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. Capability declarations **MUST** be part of the\n+backend configuration — not inferred at runtime from probing.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Storage backends vary widely in feature support. A formal capability model enables FileStorage to adapt\n+behavior per backend, allows consumers to discover and handle feature availability, and replaces ad-hoc fallback logic\n+with a consistent, extensible pattern. Per-backend capability toggling allows administrators to disable features for\n+security or operational reasons — e.g., disabling presigned URLs to force all traffic through FileStorage for audit\n+visibility, even when the backend supports them.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Runtime Backend Configuration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-runtime-backends`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow tenants to connect and configure storage backends at runtime without requiring service rebuild\n+or redeployment.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enterprise tenants need to bring their own storage (BYOS) and switch backends based on cost, compliance,\n+or geographic requirements.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.10 Access Interfaces\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-rest-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a REST API for all file operations (upload, download, delete, metadata, link management).\n+\n+**Rationale**: REST is the standard access interface for CyberFabric modules and platform UI.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### S3-Compatible API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-s3-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose an S3-compatible API for file upload and download operations, enabling integration with\n+existing S3 tooling and SDKs.\n+\n+**Rationale**: S3 is the de facto standard for object storage APIs; compatibility enables direct integration with tools,\n+libraries, and workflows that already support S3.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### WebDAV API\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-webdav-api`\n+\n+The system **MUST** expose a WebDAV API for file access, enabling native filesystem-like mounting on client operating\n+systems.\n+\n+**Rationale**: WebDAV enables direct OS-level access to stored files without custom client software, supporting use\n+cases like document editing and file management through native file explorers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming and Range Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-range-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support HTTP Range requests (RFC 7233) for partial content download, enabling seeking within large\n+files, resumable downloads, and parallel download of file segments.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets), clients need partial access for seeking, preview generation, and\n+resuming interrupted downloads without re-transferring the entire file.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.11 Cache & Idempotency\n+\n+#### Conditional Requests\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-conditional-requests`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support conditional HTTP requests (RFC 7232) for all operations served by FileStorage (proxied\n+downloads, metadata requests, metadata updates). The system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Return an `ETag` header with every download and metadata response\n+- Support `If-None-Match` on download and metadata requests — return `304 Not Modified` when the file has not changed,\n+  avoiding content transfer\n+- Support `If-Match` on metadata update requests — reject the update with `412 Precondition Failed` when the file state\n+  has changed since the client last read it, preventing lost updates from concurrent modifications\n+\n+Conditional requests do not apply to presigned URL downloads, which bypass FileStorage and are served directly by the\n+storage backend.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Conditional downloads eliminate redundant bandwidth for unchanged files and enable downstream caching by\n+browsers and reverse proxies. Conditional updates prevent silent data loss when multiple clients modify file metadata\n+concurrently. Both follow standard HTTP semantics (RFC 7232) understood by all HTTP clients. Since FileStorage manages\n+file metadata for all backends, ETags are a FileStorage-level feature independent of backend capabilities.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Upload Idempotency\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-idempotency`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support idempotent uploads. A client **MUST** be able to provide a unique idempotency key with an\n+upload request. If a subsequent upload request arrives with the same idempotency key, the system **MUST** return the\n+result of the original upload instead of creating a duplicate file. Idempotency keys **MUST** expire after a\n+configurable window.\n+\n+Idempotency keys **MUST** be scoped to the file owner specified in the upload request — the same entity that will own\n+the resulting file (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`). When the owner is a tenant, the key is unique within that\n+tenant's namespace. When the owner is a user, the key is unique within that user's namespace. The same key value used by\n+different owners **MUST** be treated as distinct keys. The system **MUST NOT** allow idempotency key lookups to cross\n+owner boundaries — a request **MUST NOT** be able to detect whether a different owner has used a given key.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Upload requests can fail ambiguously — the connection drops but the upload succeeds server-side. Without\n+idempotency, client retries create duplicate files. Idempotency keys enable safe retries for single-part and multipart\n+uploads across unreliable networks. Owner-scoped key namespacing prevents cross-tenant information leaks and aligns with\n+the platform's tenant boundary enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Metadata Query Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-metadata-latency`\n+\n+File metadata queries **MUST** complete within 25ms at p95.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <25ms p95  \n+**Rationale**: Metadata queries are used for pre-fetch validation in latency-sensitive paths (e.g., a module checks file\n+size before processing).  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Content Transfer Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-transfer-latency`\n+\n+Content download latency **MUST** have no fixed overhead exceeding 50ms at p95; total transfer time is proportional to\n+file size.\n+\n+**Threshold**: <50ms + transfer time p95  \n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is called synchronously in request paths of consuming modules; excessive overhead compounds\n+across requests with multiple files.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### URL Availability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-url-availability`\n+\n+Stored file URLs and shareable links **MUST** remain accessible for the duration of their configured lifetime with\n+availability matching the platform SLA.\n+\n+**Threshold**: URL availability matches platform SLA for the duration of the retention/expiration period  \n+**Rationale**: Consumers depend on URL stability — broken links disrupt downstream workflows and user experience.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Audit Completeness\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-audit-completeness`\n+\n+Audit records **MUST** be emitted for 100% of write operations with no silent drops under normal operating conditions.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% audit coverage for write operations  \n+**Rationale**: Incomplete audit trails undermine compliance and forensic investigations.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Data Durability and Recovery\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-durability`\n+\n+File content and metadata **MUST** achieve a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of zero for committed writes — no\n+acknowledged upload may be silently lost. The Recovery Time Objective (RTO) for service restoration after an outage\n+**MUST NOT** exceed 15 minutes. These targets apply to the FileStorage service layer; underlying storage backend\n+durability (e.g., S3 99.999999999% durability) is inherited from the backend and not controlled by FileStorage.\n+\n+**Threshold**: RPO = 0 (no data loss for committed writes); RTO ≤ 15 minutes  \n+**Rationale**: File loss after a successful upload acknowledgment breaks consumer trust and disrupts downstream\n+workflows. The RPO=0 target ensures write-ahead semantics where acknowledgment implies durability. The 15-minute RTO\n+balances recovery speed with operational complexity for a non-user-facing backend service.  \n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+#### Scalability & Capacity\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-nfr-scalability`\n+\n+FileStorage **MUST** support horizontal scaling to handle concurrent file operations without degradation. The system\n+**MUST** support at least 1,000 concurrent file operations (uploads + downloads + metadata queries combined) per\n+deployment instance. The system **MUST** scale linearly — adding instances **MUST** proportionally increase throughput\n+without introducing coordination bottlenecks between instances.\n+\n+**Threshold**: ≥1,000 concurrent operations per instance; linear horizontal scaling\n+**Rationale**: As platform adoption grows, file operation volume grows proportionally. Without explicit scalability\n+requirements, the architecture may adopt patterns (global locks, shared mutable state) that prevent horizontal scaling.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md § NFR Allocation for how this is realized\n+\n+### 6.2 NFR Exclusions\n+\n+None — all project-default NFRs apply to this module.\n+\n+### 6.3 Applicability Notes\n+\n+The following NFR categories from the platform checklist are **not applicable** to this module:\n+\n+| Category                 | Rationale                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n+|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Safety**               | FileStorage is a data storage service with no physical actuators, safety-critical control loops, or human safety implications.                                                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **UX**                   | FileStorage is a backend service consumed via SDK and APIs. It has no user-facing UI; UX concerns are the responsibility of consuming modules and platform UI.                                                                                                                                          |\n+| **Internationalization** | FileStorage stores and returns opaque binary content and metadata strings. It does not render, translate, or localize content. File names and metadata values are preserved as-is.                                                                                                                      |\n+| **Privacy by Design**    | FileStorage treats all files as opaque blobs and does not inspect, index, or process file content. Privacy controls (data minimization, consent, right to erasure) are enforced at the platform and consuming-module level. Tenant isolation and access control are covered by functional requirements. |\n+| **Compliance**           | FileStorage does not implement domain-specific compliance logic (GDPR, HIPAA, SOX). It provides the building blocks (audit trail, tenant isolation, retention policies, encryption) that enable consuming modules and platform operators to achieve compliance.                                         |\n+| **Operations**           | Operational concerns (deployment, monitoring, alerting, runbooks) follow platform-wide standards and are not module-specific.                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| **Maintainability**      | Maintainability follows platform-wide coding standards, testing requirements, and CI/CD practices. No module-specific maintainability NFRs beyond the platform baseline.                                                                                                                                |\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### FileStorage SDK Trait\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-sdk-trait`\n+\n+**Type**: Rust trait (SDK crate)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: Async trait providing upload, download, delete, metadata, and link management operations.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for trait signature changes.\n+\n+#### REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API (OpenAPI 3.0)  \n+**Stability**: unstable  \n+**Description**: HTTP REST API for all file operations, metadata management, and link management.  \n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Major version bump required for endpoint removal or request/response schema incompatible\n+changes.\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### CyberFabric Module Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library (consumed by CyberFabric modules)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: In-process Rust SDK trait via ClientHub  \n+**Compatibility**: Trait versioned with SDK crate; breaking changes require coordinated release with consuming modules.\n+\n+#### Authorization Service Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-authz`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Authorization Service)  \n+**Protocol/Format**: Access decision requests for `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources  \n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform authorization protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Usage Collector Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-usage-collector`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Usage Collector)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Asynchronous usage reports (per-tenant storage consumption)\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform usage reporting protocol; changes require coordinated release.\n+\n+#### Quota Enforcement Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-contract-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+**Direction**: required from external service (Quota Enforcement)\n+**Protocol/Format**: Synchronous quota check requests before storage-consuming operations\n+**Compatibility**: Contract follows platform quota enforcement protocol; changes require coordinated release.","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@nonameffh`, confirmed — both the **Usage Collector Contract** and **Quota Enforcement Contract** descriptions now use per-owner semantics (`Asynchronous per-owner usage reports` and `Synchronous per-owner quota check requests`), with explicit cross-references to `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting` and `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota` respectively. The fix aligns correctly with the owner-based model in §5.5. ✅\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:1380-1392\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T12:24:51.660Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, specify that key rotation for signed/pre-signed URLs is a backend configuration responsibility, not a FileStorage module responsibility. Clarify that FileStorage uses backend credentials (e.g., AWS keys, GCS service accounts, Azure SAS tokens) supplied via configuration, and that key rotation and credential management are handled by the backend infrastructure or provider per their security policies. This should be documented explicitly to prevent future assumption that FileStorage handles rotation; no code changes are required in the FileStorage module for rotation.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: MikeFalcon77\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 900\nFile: modules/mini-chat/mini-chat-sdk/src/plugin_api.rs:14-32\nTimestamp: 2026-03-06T02:17:20.889Z\nLearning: In `cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core` mini-chat module, quota enforcement is per-user, not per-tenant. The policy trait methods (`get_current_policy_version`, `get_policy_snapshot`, `get_user_limits`) in `modules/mini-chat/mini-chat-sdk/src/plugin_api.rs` and all related domain repos/infra adapters intentionally accept only `user_id: Uuid` (not `tenant_id`). Do not flag the absence of `tenant_id` in these signatures as a bug.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:530-539\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T13:34:14.555Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, the term \"upload\" in the context of quota enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`) is inclusive of version creation — when versioning is enabled, uploading to an existing path is still an upload operation that creates a new version. No separate mention of \"version creation\" is needed in the quota requirement.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: MikeFalcon77\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 900\nFile: modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md:365-367\nTimestamp: 2026-03-06T01:56:58.923Z\nLearning: Mini‑Chat DESIGN.md — quota_usage addressing: All references to per-bucket reserves/commits must include tenant_id in the row key, i.e., (tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start, bucket). Bullets in §3.2/quota_service must not drop tenant_id to stay consistent with the quota_usage UNIQUE constraint and lookup semantics.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: aviator5\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 912\nFile: modules/credstore/plugins/static-credstore-plugin/src/domain/client.rs:20-31\nTimestamp: 2026-03-06T16:02:38.384Z\nLearning: In `cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core` static-credstore-plugin (`modules/credstore/plugins/static-credstore-plugin/src/domain/client.rs`), returning nil `owner_id`/`owner_tenant_id` in `SecretMetadata` for anonymous callers accessing global/shared secrets is intentional and correct. Global secrets have no inherent owner; the nil sentinel in storage is resolved from `SecurityContext` at lookup time, and an anonymous context (nil subject) correctly produces nil owner metadata. Do not flag this as an unsafe nil materialization or require an auth guard solely based on nil owner fields in the response.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : For MODIFIED requirements, paste the complete updated requirement block (header + all scenarios) with behavior changes; the archiver replaces the entire requirement\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nIf you found this review helpful, would you consider giving us a shout-out on [X](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?related=CodeRabbitAI&text=Updated%20external%20integrations%20to%20per-owner%20model%3A%20Usage%20Collector%20%E2%86%92%20async%20per-owner%20reports%20(owner_id%2Fowner_type%20%2B%20ownership%20metadata)%3B%20Quota%20Enforcement%20%E2%86%92%20sync%20per-owner%20checks%20with%20transfer%20accounting.%20Try%20%40coderabbitai%20%F0%9F%9A%80&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcyberfabric%2Fcyberfabric-core%2Fpull%2F635%23discussion_r2900144307)?\n\nThank you for using CodeRabbit!\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:57Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:50:57Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278888","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278888"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900278888"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900278888/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":1027,"original_start_line":1018,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":1043,"original_line":1032,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2900144307,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1054,"position":1065,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900279459","pull_request_review_id":3909355700,"id":2900279459,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3sCj","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+\n+Content-type validation does not apply to direct uploads (single-part or multipart) via presigned URLs because\n+FileStorage does not receive the file content in that flow.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without content inspection, a client can declare `image/png` but upload an executable, trivially\n+bypassing file type policies. Content-type validation ensures declared types are trustworthy for downstream consumers\n+and policy enforcement. First-part validation for multipart uploads provides the same level of guarantee as single-part\n+validation — magic bytes reside at the start of the file and are contained in the first part. Post-assembly\n+re-validation would require downloading the assembled file from the backend, negating the efficiency benefits of\n+multipart upload. Direct uploads trade server-side content validation for transfer efficiency — consumers relying on\n+strict type guarantees should use proxied uploads.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.2 Ownership & Access Control\n+\n+#### File Ownership\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-ownership`\n+\n+The system **MUST** associate every file with an owner. Ownership **MUST** be assignable to either a specific user or a\n+tenant at upload time. Ownership is immutable after creation except through explicit ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`) or owner deletion workflows (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Ownership determines who can manage (delete, share, update metadata) a file and establishes the basis for\n+access control decisions. Restricting ownership changes to explicit transfer operations simplifies the authorization\n+model and prevents accidental privilege escalation.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Authorization Checks\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`\n+\n+The system **MUST** verify authorization for every file operation by requesting an access decision from the\n+Authorization Service. Read, write, and delete operations **MUST** be checked against `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~` resources in\n+the context of the requesting user. Authorization requests **MUST** include the file's GTS type\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`) in the resource context to enable per-type access decisions.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All file access must be governed by the platform's centralized authorization model to enforce role-based,\n+tenant-scoped, and type-scoped permissions.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Tenant Boundary Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-tenant-boundary`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce tenant isolation: file deletion and metadata update operations **MUST NOT** cross tenant\n+boundaries. A user in one tenant **MUST NOT** delete or update metadata of files owned by another tenant. Cross-tenant\n+read access is intentionally permitted via shareable links with tenant-hierarchy scope (see\n+`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant platforms require strict data isolation for write operations to prevent unauthorized\n+cross-tenant modification, while supporting controlled read sharing across tenant hierarchies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Data Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-data-classification`\n+\n+FileStorage treats all stored files as opaque binary blobs and does **NOT** inspect, classify, or label file content by\n+sensitivity level. Data classification (public, internal, confidential, restricted) is the responsibility of consuming\n+modules and policies. FileStorage enforces access control through its authorization model and tenant boundaries\n+regardless of data sensitivity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: FileStorage is a general-purpose storage service that serves modules with diverse data sensitivity\n+requirements. Embedding classification logic in the storage layer would couple it to domain-specific semantics. Instead,\n+consuming modules classify their own data and rely on FileStorage's authorization and tenant isolation to enforce access\n+boundaries appropriate to the sensitivity level.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Type Classification\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`\n+\n+The system **MUST** require a GTS file type identifier on every file at upload time. The file type classifies the file\n+by domain and purpose following the GTS type format (e.g. `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`\n+for LLM-generated files). The file type **MUST** be:\n+\n+- Mandatory — uploads without a file type **MUST** be rejected\n+- Immutable — the file type **MUST NOT** be changeable after creation\n+- Stored as system-managed metadata — returned in all metadata queries alongside other system fields\n+- Validated — the system **MUST** verify that the provided type follows the GTS type format\n+\n+The system **MUST** be able to use the file type to make per-type access decisions, enabling isolation\n+between actors and modules — a module **MUST** only be able to access files of types it is authorized for. File type\n+authorization is enforced through the existing authorization model (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-authorization`).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without file type classification, any module with general file access can read files created by any other\n+module, breaking isolation between platform components. GTS types enable fine-grained, per-actor access control — e.g.,\n+the LLM Gateway can only access LLM-generated files, the Feedback module can only access feedback-related files —\n+without requiring separate storage namespaces or custom authorization logic per module.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Ownership Transfer\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the current file owner to transfer ownership of a file to another user or to the tenant.\n+Ownership transfer **MUST** be an audited operation and **MUST** require authorization of both the current owner and the\n+receiving entity.\n+\n+**Rationale**: As teams evolve, files may need to change hands — e.g., when a user leaves the organization or when\n+personal files should become shared tenant resources.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Link Management & Sharing\n+\n+#### Create Shareable Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-shareable-links`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support creating unique shareable links for files with the following access scopes:\n+\n+- **Public** — accessible to anyone, including unauthenticated users\n+- **Tenant** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant\n+- **Tenant hierarchy** — accessible to any authenticated user within the file's tenant and its child tenants\n+\n+Shareable links are served by FileStorage — all requests pass through FileStorage, which validates the link, enforces\n+scope-based access control, and serves the file content from the storage backend. The desired sharing scope(s) **MUST**\n+be specifiable at file creation time and when creating additional links for existing files.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Different use cases require different visibility: public links for external sharing, tenant links for\n+internal collaboration, hierarchy links for parent-child tenant structures. Routing through FileStorage enables\n+scope-based access control, revocation, and audit logging on every access.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Signed Download URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned download URLs that point directly to the storage backend, granting\n+time-limited download access without requiring authentication. FileStorage generates these URLs using its own backend\n+credentials. A signed URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (bypassing FileStorage for content delivery)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the storage backend validates against its own key material\n+- Include an expiration timestamp after which the URL becomes invalid\n+- Be scoped to a single file (download only)\n+- Not require the consumer to present authentication credentials\n+\n+Signed URLs are **not revocable** — once issued, they remain valid until expiration because the storage backend\n+validates the signature independently of FileStorage. If revocable access is needed, use shareable links instead\n+(served through FileStorage with revocation support).\n+\n+Signed URL expiration **MUST** be constrained from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported signed URL expiration in configuration. This\n+  is a hard ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define maximum and default signed URL expiration. When both tenant and user\n+  policies apply, the most restrictive value wins. Requested expiration exceeding the effective limit **MUST** be\n+  rejected. When no expiration is specified, the policy default applies.\n+\n+Signed URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`).\n+For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled, FileStorage **MUST** reject the signed URL request\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Signed URLs enable secure file sharing with external systems and unauthenticated consumers (e.g.,\n+embedding in emails, third-party integrations) while maintaining time-bounded access control. Routing downloads through\n+the storage backend directly eliminates FileStorage as a bottleneck for shared content — following the pattern\n+established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens. The non-revocable nature follows the same\n+constraint inherent in S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed URLs, and Azure SAS tokens.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Link Expiration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-link-expiration`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support configurable expiration for any shareable link or signed URL. Expiration **MUST** be\n+specifiable at link creation time. For shareable links, FileStorage enforces expiration and **MUST** return an\n+access-denied response after the link expires. For signed URLs, the storage backend enforces expiration — the\n+backend validates the signature and rejects expired URLs independently of FileStorage.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Time-limited access prevents stale links from remaining accessible indefinitely, reducing the attack\n+surface for shared files. Expiration enforcement follows the traffic path: FileStorage enforces for shareable links\n+(which it serves), and the storage backend enforces for signed URLs (which bypass FileStorage).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Manage Links\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-manage-links`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to list all active shareable links and issued signed URLs for a file. The owner **MUST**\n+be able to revoke (delete) individual shareable links. Signed URLs cannot be revoked (they remain valid until\n+expiration) but the owner **MUST** be able to view their expiration status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need visibility into how their files are shared and the ability to revoke shareable link access\n+when no longer needed. Signed URLs are non-revocable by design (backend validates independently), so short expiration\n+is the primary access control mechanism.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+### 5.4 Direct-to-Backend Transfer\n+\n+#### Direct Transfer URLs\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-direct-transfer`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support generating presigned direct transfer URLs that point to the storage backend, allowing\n+clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage. FileStorage\n+generates these URLs using its own backend credentials (e.g., AWS access keys, GCS service account). A direct transfer\n+URL **MUST**:\n+\n+- Be generated by FileStorage using its own credentials with the storage backend\n+- Point directly to the storage backend (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint)\n+- Contain a cryptographic signature that the backend validates against its own key material\n+- Support upload (PUT) operations only — direct downloads are covered by signed URLs (\n+  `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-signed-urls`)\n+- Be time-limited with a configurable expiration\n+- Be scoped to a single file\n+\n+The client authenticates with FileStorage (using its API token), FileStorage verifies authorization, registers the file\n+metadata (including the target backend path), and then issues the presigned URL. The client uses the presigned URL\n+directly with the backend — no further authentication or callback is required because the backend trusts the signature\n+generated with FileStorage's credentials.\n+\n+Direct transfer URL generation requires the presigned URLs backend capability\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). For backends that do not declare this capability or have it disabled,\n+FileStorage **MUST** reject the direct transfer request with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable.\n+Clients must use standard (proxied) upload for backends without presigned URL support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: For large files (video, datasets, backups), proxying upload traffic through FileStorage creates a\n+bottleneck and doubles bandwidth consumption. Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs eliminates this overhead for\n+backends that declare the presigned URLs capability, following the pattern established by S3 presigned URLs, GCS signed\n+URLs, and Azure SAS tokens — where the service with backend credentials signs on behalf of the client.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Garbage Collection for Unconfirmed Uploads\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads`\n+\n+The system **MUST** automatically detect and remove orphaned records from direct uploads that were never completed.\n+An unconfirmed or incomplete upload **MUST** become eligible for garbage collection after the expiration of the\n+pre-signed URL plus a configurable grace period. After the eligibility window has passed, the system **MUST** reconcile\n+file metadata records against actual backend object existence — remove records with no corresponding backend object,\n+and confirm records whose corresponding backend object exists but was never acknowledged.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Since metadata is registered before the presigned URL is issued, failed or abandoned uploads leave\n+metadata records pointing to non-existent backend objects. The presigned URL expiration bounds the upload window but\n+does not guarantee upload outcome, so garbage collection prevents stale metadata accumulation and\n+ensures consistency between FileStorage records and backend state.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.5 Policies (Phase 2)\n+\n+#### Allowed File Types Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-allowed-types-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define policies specifying which file types (by mime_type) are permitted for\n+upload. Uploads of disallowed types **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants need to restrict uploads to approved file types for security and compliance (e.g., blocking\n+executable files).  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Size Limits Policy\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-size-limits-policy`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce file size limits from two sources:\n+\n+- **Backend limit** — each storage backend declares its maximum supported file size in configuration. This is a hard\n+  ceiling that no policy can override.\n+- **Policy limits** — tenants and users define a global maximum size and optional per-mime-type overrides (e.g., 100 MB\n+  general, 1 GB for `video/*`). When both tenant and user policies apply, the most restrictive value wins.\n+\n+Uploads exceeding any applicable limit **MUST** be rejected with an error identifying which limit was violated.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Backend limits reflect physical constraints of the storage system. Policy limits give tenants and users\n+granular control over storage consumption. The most-restrictive-wins model ensures no level can override another's\n+constraints.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### File Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-events`\n+\n+The system **MUST** emit events to the EventBroker module on file write operations (upload, update, delete). Owner\n+policy **MUST** define which event types are enabled.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables integration with downstream consumers for workflows such as antivirus scanning, content\n+moderation, indexing, or backup triggers — without coupling FileStorage to specific consumers.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Sharing Model Restrictions\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-sharing-restrictions`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to restrict which sharing models (public, tenant, tenant hierarchy, signed URLs) are\n+available within their tenant. Attempts to create links with restricted sharing models **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Tenants in regulated environments may need to prohibit public sharing or signed URLs to enforce data\n+governance policies.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Storage Usage Reporting\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-usage-reporting`\n+\n+The system **MUST** report storage usage data to the Usage Collector service. Usage reports **MUST** include per-owner\n+storage consumption (total bytes, file count) and **MUST** be emitted on every write operation that changes storage\n+consumption (upload, delete, version creation, version deletion) and on ownership transfer\n+(`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-ownership-transfer`). For ownership transfers, the system **MUST** emit a usage report for both\n+the previous owner (storage decrease) and the new owner (storage increase). The reporting mechanism **MUST** be\n+asynchronous and **MUST NOT** block file operations if the Usage Collector is temporarily unavailable.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Centralized usage data is required for metering, billing, capacity planning, and analytics. Ownership\n+transfers shift per-owner storage consumption without changing total platform storage — without debit/credit reporting,\n+billing and quota data become stale after transfers. Asynchronous reporting ensures file operations are not degraded by\n+usage collection availability.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Storage Quota Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`\n+\n+The system **MUST** check with the Quota Enforcement service before accepting any operation that increases storage\n+consumption (including uploads and version creation). Operations that would exceed the owner's storage quota **MUST** be\n+rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without storage quotas, tenants can consume unbounded storage, increasing costs and risking resource\n+exhaustion for the platform. Quota checks must cover all storage-consuming operations, not only initial uploads, to\n+prevent quota bypass through versioned overwrites.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.6 Metadata\n+\n+#### Rich Metadata Storage\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage`\n+\n+The system **MUST** store and return the following system-managed metadata for every file:\n+\n+- File name (original upload name)\n+- File size (bytes)\n+- File type (mime_type)\n+- GTS file type (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-type-classification`)\n+- Creation date\n+- Last modified date\n+- Owner (user or tenant reference)\n+- Download availability (whether the file is currently accessible for download; controlled by the file owner)\n+\n+In addition, the system **MUST** support user-defined custom metadata as arbitrary key-value string pairs. Custom\n+metadata **MUST** be specifiable at upload time and updatable after upload. The system **MUST** return custom metadata\n+alongside system-managed metadata in metadata queries.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Rich metadata enables file browsing, search, validation, and governance across the platform. Custom\n+metadata enables consumers to attach domain-specific context (tags, categories, processing status, source identifiers)\n+without schema changes — following the established pattern used by S3 object metadata, GCS custom metadata, and Azure\n+Blob metadata.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Update Custom Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata`\n+\n+The file owner **MUST** be able to update custom metadata (user-defined key-value pairs) and download availability on\n+an existing file. All other system-managed metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, creation date, last modified\n+date, owner) is **NOT** updatable by users — it is maintained by the system. Updating custom metadata or download\n+availability **MUST** update the file's last modified date.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Custom metadata evolves as files are processed, categorized, or annotated by consuming modules. System\n+metadata reflects the immutable physical properties of the file and must remain authoritative.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Custom Metadata Limits\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-limits`\n+\n+The system **MUST** enforce configurable limits on custom metadata: maximum number of key-value pairs per file, maximum\n+key name length, maximum value length, and maximum total custom metadata size per file. Metadata operations exceeding\n+limits **MUST** be rejected.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Without limits, custom metadata can be abused for general-purpose data storage, inflating metadata\n+storage costs and degrading query performance.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+### 5.7 File Retention & Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Indefinite Retention\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-indefinite`\n+\n+In phase 1, files **MUST** be retained indefinitely until explicitly deleted by the file owner. The system **MUST NOT**\n+automatically delete or expire file content based on age or inactivity. Shareable links and signed URLs expire per their\n+configured expiration, but the underlying file content remains available.\n+\n+**Rationale**: In the absence of tenant-level retention policies (phase 2), indefinite retention is the safest default —\n+it prevents accidental data loss and gives consuming modules predictable storage semantics.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Retention Policies\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-retention-policies`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow owners to define retention policies specifying automatic file expiration based on age,\n+inactivity, or custom metadata criteria. The system **MUST** also support per-file retention overrides set by the file\n+owner. When a file's retention period expires, the system **MUST** delete the file content, metadata, and all associated\n+links, and emit an audit record.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Regulated environments and cost-conscious tenants need automated lifecycle management to enforce data\n+retention compliance and control storage growth.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+#### Owner Deletion Handling\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-owner-deletion`\n+\n+The system **MUST** handle file owner removal (user or tenant deletion) by consuming owner deletion events from the\n+EventBroker. Upon receiving an owner deletion event, the system **MUST** execute a configurable workflow via the\n+Serverless Runtime to determine the disposition of all files owned by the deleted entity. The workflow **MUST** be able\n+to:\n+\n+- Delete all files owned by the removed owner\n+- Archive files (mark as archived and disable further modifications while preserving content)\n+- Transfer ownership to another user or tenant\n+- Apply any combination of the above based on file metadata or custom criteria\n+\n+The specific disposition logic **MUST** be defined as a Serverless Runtime workflow or function, configurable per\n+deployment. If no workflow is configured, the system **MUST** retain files indefinitely (no automatic deletion) and\n+mark them as orphaned for manual resolution.\n+\n+**Rationale**: When users leave an organization or tenants are decommissioned, their files require deliberate handling —\n+blind deletion risks data loss, while indefinite retention risks compliance violations. Delegating disposition to\n+Serverless Runtime workflows enables deployment-specific logic (legal holds, data migration, cascading cleanup) without\n+embedding policy decisions in FileStorage.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### File Versioning\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning`\n+\n+File versioning requires the versioning backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). When the\n+versioning capability is available for a backend, the system **MUST**:\n+\n+- Create a new version with an opaque version identifier on every file upload to the same logical path\n+- Retrieve a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- Retrieve metadata of a specific file version by its version identifier\n+- List all versions (current and non-current) of a file, including each version's identifier, size, last modified\n+  timestamp, and whether it is the current version\n+- Soft-delete a file (without specifying a version) by placing a logical delete marker on the current version. The\n+  delete marker makes the current version inaccessible through normal file access (download, metadata queries) while\n+  all non-current versions remain retrievable by their version identifiers. Soft-deleted content is **not** physically\n+  removed from the storage backend — it continues to exist and **MUST** count against the owner's storage quota\n+  (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`)\n+- Restore a soft-deleted file by removing the delete marker, making the most recent non-current version the current\n+  version again. Restore **MUST** require the same authorization as upload\n+- Permanently delete a specific file version by its version identifier","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@nonameffh`, confirmed — the updated `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file` requirement now clearly follows S3 versioned-bucket semantics:\n\n- **Non-versioned file**: permanent deletion (content, metadata, ownership, shareable links all removed).\n- **Versioned file, no version identifier**: soft-delete marker placed on the current version; shareable links preserved but resolve to soft-deleted state; content still counts against quota.\n- **Versioned file, with version identifier**: permanent removal of that specific version.\n\nThe \"Delete a File\" use case and the `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-file-versioning` requirement are consistent with this. The conflict is fully resolved.\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:530-539\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T13:34:14.555Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, the term \"upload\" in the context of quota enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`) is inclusive of version creation — when versioning is enabled, uploading to an existing path is still an upload operation that creates a new version. No separate mention of \"version creation\" is needed in the quota requirement.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : For MODIFIED requirements, paste the complete updated requirement block (header + all scenarios) with behavior changes; the archiver replaces the entire requirement\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:1380-1392\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T12:24:51.660Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, specify that key rotation for signed/pre-signed URLs is a backend configuration responsibility, not a FileStorage module responsibility. Clarify that FileStorage uses backend credentials (e.g., AWS keys, GCS service accounts, Azure SAS tokens) supplied via configuration, and that key rotation and credential management are handled by the backend infrastructure or provider per their security policies. This should be documented explicitly to prevent future assumption that FileStorage handles rotation; no code changes are required in the FileStorage module for rotation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nIf you found this review helpful, would you consider giving us a shout-out on [X](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?related=CodeRabbitAI&text=%40coderabbitai%20flagged%20conflicting%20delete%20semantics%20for%20versioned%20files%3B%20PRD%20reconciled%20to%20S3-like%20soft-delete%20(permanent%20requires%20separate%20op).%20Brilliant%20catch%20%E2%80%94%20try%20%40coderabbitai%20%F0%9F%9A%80&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcyberfabric%2Fcyberfabric-core%2Fpull%2F635%23discussion_r2900144305)?\n\nThank you for using CodeRabbit!\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-03-07T18:51:24Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:51:24Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900279459","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900279459"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900279459"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900279459/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":681,"original_start_line":672,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":688,"original_line":679,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2900144305,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":701,"position":710,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900279876","pull_request_review_id":3909356201,"id":2900279876,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6s3sJE","diff_hunk":"@@ -1,141 +1,1457 @@\n-# PRD\n+# PRD — File Storage\n \n ## 1. Overview\n \n-**Purpose**: FileStorage provides media storage and retrieval for LLM Gateway inputs and outputs.\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n \n-FileStorage is a dependency service that handles binary content (images, audio, video, documents) for AI workloads. LLM Gateway fetches user-uploaded media before sending to providers and stores provider-generated content (images, audio, video) for delivery to consumers.\n+FileStorage is a universal file storage and management service for the CyberFabric platform. It provides upload,\n+download, metadata management, access control, and sharing capabilities for any module or user within the platform.\n \n-The service provides URL-based access to files with metadata queries for validation (size limits, mime types) before processing.\n+The service supports pluggable storage backends, multiple access protocols (REST, S3-compatible, WebDAV), tenant-scoped\n+access control with an ownership model, and policy-driven governance for file types, sizes, and sharing.\n \n-**Target Users**:\n-- **LLM Gateway** - Primary consumer for media fetch and store operations\n-- **Platform Services** - Other services requiring file storage\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n \n-**Key Problems Solved**:\n-- **Media handling**: Centralized storage for AI input/output media\n-- **URL-based access**: Consistent URL scheme for file references\n-- **Metadata queries**: Pre-fetch validation of file size and type\n+CyberFabric modules and platform users require file storage for various purposes: modules handle multimodal AI content\n+(images, audio, video, documents), documents and artifacts, reporting outputs, and platform users need direct file\n+access through standard protocols.\n \n-**Success Criteria**:\n-- All scenarios (S1-S3) implemented and operational\n-- File fetch latency appropriate for media size\n-- Storage availability matches platform SLA\n+Without a dedicated storage service, each module implements ad-hoc file handling, media gets inlined as base64 in API\n+payloads (bloating requests and hitting size limits), provider-generated URLs expire leaving consumers with broken\n+links, and there is no unified access control or policy enforcement across the platform.\n \n-**Capabilities**:\n-- Fetch media by URL\n-- Store generated content\n-- Get file metadata\n-- Support for images, audio, video, documents\n+FileStorage solves this by providing a centralized, tenant-aware storage service with persistent URLs, pluggable\n+backends, and standardized access interfaces — functioning as a superset of S3 and WebDAV capabilities within the\n+CyberFabric security and governance model.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Unified file storage accessible by all CyberFabric modules and platform users\n+- Tenant-scoped and origin-module-scoped access control with tenant, user and module ownership model\n+- Flexible sharing via public, tenant-scoped, and signed URLs\n+- Policy-driven governance over file types, sizes, events, and sharing models\n+- Audit trail for all write operations\n+- Pluggable storage backends without service rebuild\n+\n+### 1.4 Success Metrics\n+\n+| Metric                                   | Baseline                                 | Target                                                           | Timeframe                      |\n+|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n+| Module adoption rate                     | 0% (ad-hoc file handling)                | 90%+ of file-dependent modules use FileStorage SDK               | 6 months after GA              |\n+| Base64-inlined media payloads            | Present in LLM Gateway and other modules | 0 base64 file payloads in modules that adopted FileStorage       | 3 months after module adoption |\n+| Broken/expired provider URLs             | Recurring in downstream workflows        | 0 broken URLs for files within retention period                  | Ongoing after GA               |\n+| Audit coverage for file write operations | No centralized audit                     | 100% of write operations audited                                 | Phase 2                        |\n+| Multi-backend deployment                 | Single ad-hoc storage per module         | At least 2 backend types validated (e.g., S3 + local filesystem) | At GA                          |\n+\n+### 1.5 Glossary\n+\n+| Term                | Definition                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+|---------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| File                | Binary content stored in FileStorage with associated metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n+| File URL            | Persistent URL pointing to content stored in FileStorage                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n+| Metadata            | File properties: system-managed (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, dates, owner, availability) and user-defined custom key-value pairs                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Custom Metadata     | User-defined key-value pairs attached to a file, analogous to S3 object metadata                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n+| Owner               | Entity (user or tenant) that owns a file and controls its sharing and lifecycle                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n+| Shareable Link      | A unique URL served by FileStorage that grants access to a file with a specific scope and expiration; FileStorage validates the link and enforces access control on every request                                                                                                       |\n+| Signed URL          | A presigned backend URL (download-only) for sharing with external or unauthenticated consumers; generated by FileStorage using its own backend credentials, time-limited, non-revocable — the storage backend validates the signature and enforces expiration                           |\n+| Direct Transfer URL | A presigned backend URL (upload-only) for authenticated clients to upload file content directly to the backend without routing traffic through FileStorage; generated by FileStorage after authorization, time-limited                                                                  |\n+| Storage Backend     | An underlying storage system (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, NFS, FTP, SMB, WebDAV) used for persisting file content                                                                                                                                                                              |\n+| Policy              | A set of rules (allowed file types, size limits, events, sharing models) that constrain file operations; applicable at the tenant level and the user level independently — when both apply, the most restrictive value per aspect wins                                                  |\n+| File Version        | An immutable snapshot of file content created on each upload to the same logical path when versioning is enabled; identified by an opaque version identifier assigned by the storage backend                                                                                            |\n+| Version Identifier  | An opaque string assigned by the storage backend that uniquely identifies a specific version of a file; format varies by backend and must not be parsed or assumed                                                                                                                      |\n+| File Type (GTS)     | A GTS type identifier assigned to every file at upload time that classifies the file by domain, actor, and purpose (e.g., `gts.x.fstorage.file.type.v1~x.genai.llm.autogenerated.v1~`); used by the Authorization Service to enforce per-type access control between actors and modules |\n+| Backend Capability  | An optional feature that a storage backend may or may not support (e.g., presigned URLs, versioning, multipart upload); FileStorage discovers available capabilities per backend and adapts its behavior accordingly                                                                    |\n \n ## 2. Actors\n \n ### 2.1 Human Actors\n \n-<!-- No direct human actors for LLM Gateway scope -->\n+#### Platform User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`\n+\n+**Role**: Authenticated user who uploads, downloads, and manages files through the platform UI or API.  \n+**Needs**: Direct file access, sharing capabilities, metadata management, and self-service link management.\n \n ### 2.2 System Actors\n \n-#### LLM Gateway\n+#### CyberFabric Modules\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+**Role**: Any CyberFabric module requiring file upload, download, metadata retrieval, or link management (e.g., LLM\n+Gateway for multimodal media, document management modules, reporting modules).\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+### 3.1 Module-Specific Environment Constraints\n+\n+FileStorage operates within the standard CyberFabric runtime environment. Authentication and identity management are\n+fully delegated to the platform — FileStorage does not implement its own authentication layer. All incoming requests are\n+pre-authenticated by the platform infrastructure, and FileStorage receives the caller's identity context (user, tenant,\n+roles) from the platform authentication middleware.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Upload, download, delete, and list files\n+- Rich file metadata storage, retrieval, and update\n+- File ownership by user or tenant\n+- GTS file type classification for per-actor access control\n+- Authorization checks via Authorization Service\n+- Shareable links with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\n+- Signed URLs for unauthenticated, time-limited downloads\n+- Link expiration and lifecycle management\n+- Audit trail for all write operations and optional read audit logging\n+- Policies (file types, size limits, events, sharing restrictions) at tenant and user levels\n+- Pluggable storage backend abstraction\n+- Multipart (chunked) upload for large files\n+- Content-type validation against actual file content\n+- Direct-to-backend upload via presigned URLs for compatible backends\n+- Garbage collection for unconfirmed direct uploads\n+- File retention and lifecycle management\n+- REST API access interface\n+- S3-compatible API\n+- WebDAV API\n+- Streaming and range requests\n+- Runtime tenant-configurable storage backends\n+- Storage quota enforcement via Quota Enforcement service\n+- Ownership transfer\n+- Custom metadata limits\n+- File versioning\n+- Conditional requests (ETags) for cache validation and concurrent update protection\n+- Upload idempotency\n+- Owner deletion handling via EventBroker and Serverless Runtime workflows\n+- File encryption (server-side, per backend capability and configuration)\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Content transformation or transcoding\n+- CDN distribution\n+- Full-text search within file content\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core File Operations\n+\n+#### Upload File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-upload-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** accept file content with metadata and persist it, returning a persistent, accessible URL. File\n+content is immutable after upload — to change content, a new file **MUST** be uploaded.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to store files — modules store generated content, documents, and\n+artifacts, users upload files directly. Immutable content simplifies caching, integrity verification, and backend\n+replication.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Download File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-download-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** retrieve file content and metadata by URL for consumption by requesting actors.\n+\n+**Rationale**: All platform modules and users need to retrieve stored files — modules fetch media and documents, users\n+download files directly.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Delete File\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-delete-file`\n+\n+The system **MUST** allow the file owner to permanently delete a file and all its associated shareable links.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Owners need to remove files that are no longer needed; deletion must cascade to all links to prevent\n+dangling references.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Get File Metadata\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-get-metadata`\n+\n+The system **MUST** return file metadata (name, size, mime_type, GTS file type, created date, modified date, owner,\n+download availability, and custom metadata) without transferring file content.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Consumers validate file properties (size limits, type compatibility) and read custom metadata before\n+initiating downloads, avoiding wasted bandwidth on incompatible files.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### List Files\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-list-files`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support listing files with their metadata (no content transfer). The caller **MUST** specify the\n+owner type as a mandatory filter:\n+\n+- **User-owned** — files owned by a specific user\n+- **Tenant-owned** — files owned by the tenant\n+\n+The response **MUST** be paginated following the platform API guidelines (cursor-based or offset-based pagination with\n+configurable page size). The system **MUST** support optional additional filters (mime_type, date range, custom metadata\n+keys).\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users and modules need to discover and browse files they own or have access to. Mandatory owner type\n+filtering prevents unbounded queries across all files and aligns with the ownership model.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Multipart Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`\n+\n+The system **MUST** support multipart (chunked) upload for large files. Multipart upload requires the multipart\n+upload backend capability (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-backend-capabilities`). A multipart upload **MUST**:\n+\n+- Allow the client to split a file into multiple parts and upload them independently\n+- Support resumable uploads — if a part fails, only that part needs re-uploading\n+- Assemble parts into a complete file upon finalization\n+- Apply the same authorization, metadata, and audit requirements as single-part uploads\n+\n+For backends that do not declare the multipart upload capability, the system **MUST** reject multipart upload requests\n+with a clear error indicating the capability is unavailable. There is no FileStorage-level fallback for multipart —\n+clients must use single-part upload for backends without native multipart support.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Single-request uploads are impractical for large files (video, datasets, backups) due to timeouts,\n+memory constraints, and network reliability. Multipart upload enables reliable transfer of arbitrarily large files.\n+Implementing multipart at the FileStorage layer without backend support would require full content buffering, negating\n+the scalability benefits. Rejecting with a clear error lets clients adapt their upload strategy per backend.  \n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-platform-user`, `cpt-cf-file-storage-actor-cf-modules`\n+\n+#### Content-Type Validation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-content-type-validation`\n+\n+The system **MUST** validate the declared mime_type against the actual file content (magic bytes / file signature) on\n+proxied uploads (where file content passes through FileStorage). If the declared type does not match the detected type,\n+the system **MUST** reject the upload with an error indicating the mismatch.\n+\n+For proxied multipart uploads (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-multipart-upload`), the system **MUST** validate the declared\n+mime_type against the content of the **first uploaded part**, which contains the file's magic bytes / file signature.\n+Validation **MUST** occur when the first part is received — before subsequent parts are accepted. If the first part does\n+not match the declared type, the system **MUST** abort the multipart upload and reject all subsequent parts.\n+","path":"modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"1afc2c96fffb9b095d1b436484cbfdb9d2506efa","original_commit_id":"8752e4620d5f1970a7abb0c3e4ec056083478d68","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@nonameffh`, the clarification in the rationale addresses the concern well. The added explanation — that backends declaring the multipart upload capability enforce a minimum part size (e.g., 5 MB for S3) that far exceeds the longest magic-byte sequence (~12 bytes) — directly closes the gap: since only backends with native multipart support are used for multipart uploads, the minimum part size constraint is always in effect, guaranteeing the first part contains sufficient leading bytes for content-type validation. No additional buffering logic is needed.\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:530-539\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T13:34:14.555Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, the term \"upload\" in the context of quota enforcement (`cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-storage-quota`) is inclusive of version creation — when versioning is enabled, uploading to an existing path is still an upload operation that creates a new version. No separate mention of \"version creation\" is needed in the quota requirement.\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : For MODIFIED requirements, paste the complete updated requirement block (header + all scenarios) with behavior changes; the archiver replaces the entire requirement\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: nonameffh\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 635\nFile: modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md:1380-1392\nTimestamp: 2026-02-23T12:24:51.660Z\nLearning: In modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md, specify that key rotation for signed/pre-signed URLs is a backend configuration responsibility, not a FileStorage module responsibility. Clarify that FileStorage uses backend credentials (e.g., AWS keys, GCS service accounts, Azure SAS tokens) supplied via configuration, and that key rotation and credential management are handled by the backend infrastructure or provider per their security policies. This should be documented explicitly to prevent future assumption that FileStorage handles rotation; no code changes are required in the FileStorage module for rotation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\nIf you found this review helpful, would you consider giving us a shout-out on [X](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?related=CodeRabbitAI&text=Found%20multipart%20upload%20bug%3A%20first%20part%20may%20be%20too%20small%20to%20detect%20file%20magic%20%E2%80%94%20enforce%20min%20first-part%20size%20or%20buffer%20until%20enough%20leading%20bytes%2C%20abort%20on%20invalid%20parts.%20Try%20%40coderabbitai%20%F0%9F%9A%80%F0%9F%94%92&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fcyberfabric%2Fcyberfabric-core%2Fpull%2F635%23discussion_r2900144303)?\n\nThank you for using CodeRabbit!\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: tweet message by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-03-07T18:51:43Z","updated_at":"2026-03-07T18:51:43Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900279876","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900279876"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#discussion_r2900279876"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2900279876/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":235,"original_start_line":229,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":240,"original_line":233,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2900144303,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":255,"position":262,"subject_type":"line"}],"source_reviews":[{"id":3810468210,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7jHyFy","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 4**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md`:\n- Around line 99-133: Add a new subsection titled \"Phases\" under the existing\n\"Scope\" (or immediately after \"Overview\") that explicitly defines \"Phase 1\" and\n\"Phase 2\" (referencing the p1/p2 annotations used throughout), list expected\ndelivery milestones/timelines for each phase, specify clear acceptance criteria\nfor moving from Phase 1 to Phase 2 (e.g., feature-complete checklist, QA/pass\ncriteria, performance/security gates), and explicitly map each phase to the\nSuccess Metrics referenced in section \"1.4 Success Metrics\" so stakeholders can\nsee which metrics are targeted in each phase.\n- Around line 1081-1097: The Risks table entry for \"Signed URL key compromise\"\nlists \"Key rotation support\" as a mitigation but the Open Questions still asks\n\"What is the key rotation strategy for signed URL generation?\"; update the PRD\nto be consistent by either (A) making key rotation a confirmed requirement—move\na concise requirement/spec into section 5.3 (Link Management & Sharing)\ndescribing rotation cadence, key storage/rollover and revocation behavior and\nthen remove the open question about strategy, or (B) if the strategy is\nundecided, change the Risks mitigation text to a provisional wording like \"Key\nrotation considered; strategy TBD\" and retain the Open Question; reference the\n\"Signed URL key compromise\" risk row in section 12 and the \"What is the key\nrotation strategy for signed URL generation?\" bullet in section 13 when making\nthe edit.\n- Line 753: The \"Upload and Share a File\" heading under the \"8. Use Cases\"\nsection is using level-4 (####) and jumps the hierarchy; update that heading and\nall other use-case headings in the same section from level-4 to level-3 (change\n'####' to '###') so they are proper subheadings of \"## 8. Use Cases\" (start by\nediting the \"Upload and Share a File\" heading and then apply the same\n'####'→'###' change to every subsequent use case heading in that section).\n- Around line 405-417: The PRD's garbage-collection requirement for unconfirmed\ndirect uploads (ID: cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-gc-direct-uploads) lacks key\nimplementation details; update the document (or add a DESIGN appendix) to\nspecify when TTL starts (e.g., at presigned URL issuance timestamp stored in\nFileStorage metadata), define detection logic for \"never started\" vs \"partial\"\nuploads (e.g., check backend object existence + object size/ETag vs metadata\nexpected size, and/or a completed flag/event), and state whether GC relies\nsolely on backend existence checks or also on metadata timestamps/event\ncallbacks; reference the FileStorage metadata schema and the GC worker/service\nname (e.g., FileStorage GC worker) and include configuration parameters for TTL\nand reconciliation frequency.\n- Line 992: Replace the redundant phrase \"REST API interfaces\" with a simplified\nform such as \"REST APIs\" (or \"REST API endpoints\" if you want to emphasize\nendpoints) in the sentence \"Both deployments expose identical FileStorage SDK\nand REST API interfaces\"; update that exact phrase to read \"REST APIs\" so the\nline becomes \"Both deployments expose identical FileStorage SDK and REST APIs.\"\n- Around line 480-515: The metadata list under requirement\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage currently lists \"Download availability\"\nbut the update behavior is defined in cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata,\nwhich is confusing; update the PRD by either moving \"Download availability\" into\na new \"Owner-controlled system metadata\" category or (preferably) append a\nparenthetical to the existing line so it reads \"Download availability\n(owner-updatable)\" to clearly indicate it is system-managed but\nuser-controllable and avoid readers having to cross-reference\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata.\n- Around line 56-58: Update the glossary entries for \"Signed URL\" and \"Direct\nTransfer URL\" to clearly distinguish their functionality: change the Signed URL\ndefinition (the \"Signed URL\" entry) to include \"(download-only, time-limited\naccess for unauthenticated users)\" and change the Direct Transfer URL definition\n(the \"Direct Transfer URL\" entry) to include \"(supports both upload and download\noperations, time-limited and generated with backend credentials)\"; keep the rest\nof each definition intact so they still describe presigned URL behavior and\nstorage backend validation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all nitpicks with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md`:\n- Line 992: Replace the redundant phrase \"REST API interfaces\" with a simplified\nform such as \"REST APIs\" (or \"REST API endpoints\" if you want to emphasize\nendpoints) in the sentence \"Both deployments expose identical FileStorage SDK\nand REST API interfaces\"; update that exact phrase to read \"REST APIs\" so the\nline becomes \"Both deployments expose identical FileStorage SDK and REST APIs.\"\n- Around line 480-515: The metadata list under requirement\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage currently lists \"Download availability\"\nbut the update behavior is defined in cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata,\nwhich is confusing; update the PRD by either moving \"Download availability\" into\na new \"Owner-controlled system metadata\" category or (preferably) append a\nparenthetical to the existing line so it reads \"Download availability\n(owner-updatable)\" to clearly indicate it is system-managed but\nuser-controllable and avoid readers having to cross-reference\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata.\n- Around line 56-58: Update the glossary entries for \"Signed URL\" and \"Direct\nTransfer URL\" to clearly distinguish their functionality: change the Signed URL\ndefinition (the \"Signed URL\" entry) to include \"(download-only, time-limited\naccess for unauthenticated users)\" and change the Direct Transfer URL definition\n(the \"Direct Transfer URL\" entry) to include \"(supports both upload and download\noperations, time-limited and generated with backend credentials)\"; keep the rest\nof each definition intact so they still describe presigned URL behavior and\nstorage backend validation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`992-992`: **Minor: Simplify redundant phrasing.**\n\nStatic analysis correctly flags \"REST API interfaces\" as redundant since \"API\" already stands for \"Application Programming Interface.\" Consider simplifying to \"REST APIs\" or \"REST API endpoints.\"\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` at line 992, Replace the redundant phrase\n\"REST API interfaces\" with a simplified form such as \"REST APIs\" (or \"REST API\nendpoints\" if you want to emphasize endpoints) in the sentence \"Both deployments\nexpose identical FileStorage SDK and REST API interfaces\"; update that exact\nphrase to read \"REST APIs\" so the line becomes \"Both deployments expose\nidentical FileStorage SDK and REST APIs.\"\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`480-515`: **Consider clarifying the categorization of \"download availability\" in metadata.**\n\nLine 491 lists \"Download availability\" as part of system-managed metadata, but line 507 states the owner can update \"download availability\" along with custom metadata. While this is intentionally designed (download availability is system-managed but user-controllable), the categorization might confuse readers.\n\nConsider either:\n1. Moving \"download availability\" to a separate category (e.g., \"Owner-controlled system metadata\") in the metadata list (line 484-491), or\n2. Adding a parenthetical note in line 491: \"Download availability (owner-updatable)\"\n\nThis would make the updateability clear at first read without requiring cross-referencing to line 507.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 480 - 515, The metadata list\nunder requirement cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-metadata-storage currently lists\n\"Download availability\" but the update behavior is defined in\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata, which is confusing; update the PRD by\neither moving \"Download availability\" into a new \"Owner-controlled system\nmetadata\" category or (preferably) append a parenthetical to the existing line\nso it reads \"Download availability (owner-updatable)\" to clearly indicate it is\nsystem-managed but user-controllable and avoid readers having to cross-reference\ncpt-cf-file-storage-fr-update-metadata.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`56-58`: **Clarify the distinction between Signed URLs and Direct Transfer URLs.**\n\nThe glossary defines both \"Signed URL\" (line 56) and \"Direct Transfer URL\" (line 57) as presigned URLs pointing to the storage backend, but their functional differences could be clearer. From the definitions:\n- **Signed URL**: Download-only, time-limited access for unauthenticated users\n- **Direct Transfer URL**: Both upload and download operations\n\nConsider adding a brief clarifying statement in each definition to highlight this key difference. For example, in the Signed URL definition, add \"(download only)\" and in Direct Transfer URL, emphasize \"(supports both upload and download operations)\".\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 56 - 58, Update the glossary\nentries for \"Signed URL\" and \"Direct Transfer URL\" to clearly distinguish their\nfunctionality: change the Signed URL definition (the \"Signed URL\" entry) to\ninclude \"(download-only, time-limited access for unauthenticated users)\" and\nchange the Direct Transfer URL definition (the \"Direct Transfer URL\" entry) to\ninclude \"(supports both upload and download operations, time-limited and\ngenerated with backend credentials)\"; keep the rest of each definition intact so\nthey still describe presigned URL behavior and storage backend validation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#pullrequestreview-3810468210","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/635#pullrequestreview-3810468210"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/635"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-16T20:44:24Z","commit_id":"1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed"},{"id":3840804921,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7k7gg5","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 1**\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`94-133`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Phases are still not formally defined anywhere in the document.**\n\nThe document uses `p1`/`p2`/`p3` priority annotations on every requirement and inline `*(Phase 2)*` markers throughout use cases — but no section defines what Phase 1 and Phase 2 actually mean: scope, delivery milestones, acceptance criteria for phase transition, or mapping to the Success Metrics timeframes in section 1.4 (which itself references \"Phase 2\" in the timeframe column).\n\nThis was raised in a previous review pass; the bot's verification script at that time confirmed the definition section was not added despite the issue being marked \"Fixed.\"\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 94 - 133, The document uses\np1/p2/p3 priority tags and inline \"*(Phase 2)*\" markers but never defines what\nPhase 1/Phase 2 mean; add a new \"Phases and Release Criteria\" section\n(referenced from 4. Scope and 1.4) that explicitly defines Phase 1/Phase 2/Phase\n3 scope boundaries, delivery milestones, acceptance criteria for moving between\nphases, and how each phase maps to the Success Metrics timeframes in section\n1.4; update any inline markers (e.g., p1/p2/p3 and \"*(Phase 2)*\") to reference\nthis new section and ensure timelines/milestones and acceptance tests are listed\nfor each phase.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1307-1359`: **Acceptance criteria mix all phases without annotations — Phase 1 MVP scope is unreadable from this section.**\n\nThe ~50 criteria run from p1 basics (line 1309: \"File upload returns persistent URL\") through p2 features (line 1314: \"Shareable links work with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\") to p3 capabilities (line 1358: \"Server-side encryption is applied...\") with no distinction. With phases undefined (see the comment on section 4), this makes it impossible to determine from section 9 alone what must pass for a Phase 1 release.\n\nConsider prefixing each criterion with its phase tag (e.g., `[p1]`, `[p2]`, `[p3]`) or splitting into phase-keyed subsections, once the Phases definition is added.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md` around lines 1307 - 1359, The Acceptance\nCriteria list in \"Section 9. Acceptance Criteria\" currently mixes Phase 1/2/3\nitems (e.g., \"File upload returns persistent URL\", \"Shareable links work with\npublic, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy scopes\", \"Server-side encryption is\napplied...\"), making the Phase 1 MVP unclear; update the criteria by tagging or\ngrouping each item with its phase so reviewers can tell MVP scope at a\nglance—either prefix every bullet with a phase tag like [p1]/[p2]/[p3] or split\nthe section into subsections (Acceptance Criteria — Phase 1, Phase 2, Phase 3)\nand ensure this aligns with the Phases definition referenced in Section 4.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md`:\n- Around line 96-126: Add the missing read audit logging item to the \"In Scope\"\nlist in Section 4.1 to match the requirement cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit\n(p3); specifically, insert a bullet like \"Read audit logging for file access\n(cpt-cf-file-storage-fr-read-audit)\" alongside the existing \"File encryption\"\nentry so the scope list is consistent with section 5.8 and other p3 items.\n\n---\n\nDuplicate comments:\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md`:\n- Around line 94-133: The document uses p1/p2/p3 priority tags and inline\n\"*(Phase 2)*\" markers but never defines what Phase 1/Phase 2 mean; add a new\n\"Phases and Release Criteria\" section (referenced from 4. Scope and 1.4) that\nexplicitly defines Phase 1/Phase 2/Phase 3 scope boundaries, delivery\nmilestones, acceptance criteria for moving between phases, and how each phase\nmaps to the Success Metrics timeframes in section 1.4; update any inline markers\n(e.g., p1/p2/p3 and \"*(Phase 2)*\") to reference this new section and ensure\ntimelines/milestones and acceptance tests are listed for each phase.\n\n---\n\nNitpick comments:\nIn `@modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md`:\n- Around line 1307-1359: The Acceptance Criteria list in \"Section 9. Acceptance\nCriteria\" currently mixes Phase 1/2/3 items (e.g., \"File upload returns\npersistent URL\", \"Shareable links work with public, tenant, and tenant-hierarchy\nscopes\", \"Server-side encryption is applied...\"), making the Phase 1 MVP\nunclear; update the criteria by tagging or grouping each item with its phase so\nreviewers can tell MVP scope at a glance—either prefix every bullet with a phase\ntag like [p1]/[p2]/[p3] or split the section into subsections (Acceptance\nCriteria — Phase 1, Phase 2, Phase 3) and ensure this aligns with the Phases\ndefinition referenced in Section 4.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>ℹ️ Review info</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commits</summary>\n\nReviewing files that changed from the base of the PR and between 1bd89f5c05130dba9fe8dccca97060ee9ce576ed and 151b1979fdfc0611b945b1c34160d3f46b88d7e6.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📒 Files selected for processing (2)</summary>\n\n* `modules/file-storage/README.md`\n* `modules/file-storage/docs/PRD.md`\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This 