abbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T11:35:29Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T11:35:30Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2811979579","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811979579"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2811979579"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811979579/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":138,"original_line":104,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":104,"position":138,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811979727","pull_request_review_id":3807935135,"id":2811979727,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nm2fP","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"hitengajjar","id":17683811,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NjgzODEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17683811?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar","html_url":"https://github.com/hitengajjar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"1. Explicitly notify client of downgrade done and quality of answers may not be same as previously chosen model\n2. disable selection of exhausted models with time when they would be available.","created_at":"2026-02-16T11:35:31Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T11:39:52Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2811979727","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811979727"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2811979727"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811979727/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":201,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":201,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811988662","pull_request_review_id":3807935135,"id":2811988662,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nm4q2","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"hitengajjar","id":17683811,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NjgzODEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17683811?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar","html_url":"https://github.com/hitengajjar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"PII information?","created_at":"2026-02-16T11:37:49Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T11:39:52Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2811988662","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811988662"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2811988662"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811988662/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":417,"original_line":241,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":241,"position":417,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811992037","pull_request_review_id":3807935135,"id":2811992037,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nm5fl","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a maximum of 8 KiB per field (append `…[TRUNCATED]`).","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"hitengajjar","id":17683811,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NjgzODEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17683811?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar","html_url":"https://github.com/hitengajjar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"> 8 KiB\n\nshould be configurable and subject the system limits (like Audit event payload not bigger than 16KiB)","created_at":"2026-02-16T11:38:43Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T11:39:52Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2811992037","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811992037"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2811992037"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2811992037/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":243,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":243,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812021502","pull_request_review_id":3808161373,"id":2812021502,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nnAr-","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@\n+---\n+\n+status: accepted\n+date: 2026-02-14\n+decision-makers: Mike Yastrebtsov, Alex Henry\n+---------------------------------------------","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/0003-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution.md","commit_id":"9c82796210566777a498f8bafc5ef1b518fd1333","original_commit_id":"9c82796210566777a498f8bafc5ef1b518fd1333","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"fixed","created_at":"2026-02-16T11:45:48Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T11:45:48Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812021502","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812021502"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812021502"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812021502/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":6,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2810123673,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":6,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812142492","pull_request_review_id":3808308737,"id":2812142492,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nneOc","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"394e53b565fed671657dc0ecb9f81d440ffba717","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Doc summary is not a UX feature, it's a context and cost control mechanism.\r\n\r\nWithout it, we repeatedly pay for re-retrieval and re-injection of document fragments across turns, which increases token usage over time.\r\nThe intent is not unconditional summarization, but controlled / trigger-based generation to stabilize long conversations and bounded context.","created_at":"2026-02-16T12:15:33Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T12:15:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812142492","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812142492"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812142492"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812142492/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":168,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811966308,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":168,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538236","pull_request_review_id":3808748377,"id":2812538236,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no-18","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed. Narrowed goal wording to \"completed chat turns and policy decisions\" — matches DESIGN.md:1207 scoping (one structured event per turn, minimal content).","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:37Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:37Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538236","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538236"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538236"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538236/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":23,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811812551,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":23,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538347","pull_request_review_id":3808748481,"id":2812538347,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no-3r","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:38Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:39Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538347","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538347"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538347"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538347/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":74,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811840723,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":74,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538454","pull_request_review_id":3808748560,"id":2812538454,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no-5W","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed. Expanded to: \"P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\"","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:40Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:40Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538454","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538454"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538454"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538454/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":95,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811850534,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":95,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538582","pull_request_review_id":3808748677,"id":2812538582,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no-7W","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Added explicit out-of-scope entries for: web search (P1+), URL extraction, admin config UI, multiple quota periods, module-level i18n, per-feature flags. Multi-modal is already covered by line 96 (\"Image or non-document file support\"). Session timeout is covered by streaming cancellation on disconnect + temporary chat TTL; chats do not expire by default.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:41Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538582","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538582"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538582"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538582/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":96,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811858412,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":96,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538704","pull_request_review_id":3808748770,"id":2812538704,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no-9Q","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed. Changed to: \"If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`.\"","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:43Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:43Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538704","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538704"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538704"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538704/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":119,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811889904,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":119,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538799","pull_request_review_id":3808748913,"id":2812538799,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no--v","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a maximum of 8 KiB per field (append `…[TRUNCATED]`).","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed. Changed to configurable default (8 KiB) and added constraint: total event payload must not exceed `audit_service` event size limit.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:44Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:44Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538799","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538799"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538799"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538799/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":243,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811992037,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":243,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538908","pull_request_review_id":3808749017,"id":2812538908,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no_Ac","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a maximum of 8 KiB per field (append `…[TRUNCATED]`).\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and cost analysis require a complete record of all AI usage. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Agreed. Fixed rationale — audit is for compliance and incident response. Cost analytics are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (`cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not audit events.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:45Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:45Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538908","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538908"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812538908"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812538908/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":251,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811954061,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":251,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812539005","pull_request_review_id":3808749112,"id":2812539005,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no_B9","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"The design already implements two-phase deletion: soft-delete (`deleted_at` set, disappears from APIs) followed by hard-purge via periodic cleanup job with configurable grace period — see DESIGN.md lines 1204, 1545-1556. Added the 24h retention window to Section 13.1 as an explicit configurable default (`temporary_chat_retention_hours: 24`).","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:47Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:51:47Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812539005","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812539005"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812539005"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812539005/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":308,"original_line":190,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811972744,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":190,"position":308,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542396","pull_request_review_id":3808752794,"id":2812542396,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no_28","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Not P0. The interaction model is request-response: user sends message, assistant responds. Elicitation requires a new turn type, UI contract changes, and state machine extensions to `chat_turns`. Can be tracked as a P2+ item if needed.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:37Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:37Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542396","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542396"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542396"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542396/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":44,"original_line":30,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811817394,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":30,"position":44,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542486","pull_request_review_id":3808752957,"id":2812542486,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no_4W","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"3ccec3c26580af6997d471473868786d6cd02c80","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"P0 uses token-based budgeting with daily/monthly periods. The `quota_service` (DESIGN.md Section 3.2) enforces this via two-phase reserve/commit. Credit-based budgeting or multi-system translation is an architectural change beyond P0 scope. Token-based aligns with how providers report usage.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:38Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:38Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542486","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542486"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542486"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542486/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":35,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811823793,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":35,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542608","pull_request_review_id":3808753132,"id":2812542608,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no_6Q","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"The OAGW definition is intentionally generic — \"handles external API calls and credential injection\" covers any outbound service. Credentials come from CredStore via OAGW; Mini Chat never touches them (constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`). P0 outbound targets: LLM provider only. WebSearch is not a P0 dependency.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:39Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:39Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542608","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542608"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542608"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542608/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":60,"original_line":38,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811826536,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":38,"position":60,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542764","pull_request_review_id":3808753296,"id":2812542764,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no_8s","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"P0 quota is binary: preflight allows / downgrades / rejects with `quota_exceeded`. The `done` SSE event returns actual token usage and model per turn, so the client has post-turn visibility. Proactive threshold warnings (\"80% of daily limit\") need a new field in the stable SSE contract and threshold tracking in `quota_service`. Not a P0 cost-control requirement.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:41Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542764","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542764"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542764"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542764/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":70,"original_line":48,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811833606,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":48,"position":70,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542921","pull_request_review_id":3808753411,"id":2812542921,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6no__J","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"- **Multiple LLM providers**: out of scope (Section 4.2 line 93).\n- **User model selection**: deployment-configured at P0, not user-facing (DESIGN.md `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`, lines 159-162).\n- **Edit/retry/delete/copy messages**: not P0. Retry partially covered by `request_id` replay.\n- **Markdown**: already covered — LLM output is natively markdown. SSE `delta` type field reserves future structured types (DESIGN.md line 350).\n- **Feedback, prompt templates**: not P0.\n\nCan be tracked as separate P2+ items.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:42Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:52:42Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542921","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542921"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812542921"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812542921/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":74,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811906070,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":74,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546212","pull_request_review_id":3808757357,"id":2812546212,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npAyk","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Same as the threshold comment on line 48 — P0 quota is preflight-only with allow/downgrade/reject. Threshold warnings are a P2+ UX enhancement.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:23Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:23Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546212","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546212"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546212"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546212/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":101,"original_line":83,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811843262,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":83,"position":101,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546306","pull_request_review_id":3808757495,"id":2812546306,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npA0C","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Already covered:\n- Per-message token consumption: `messages.input_tokens`/`output_tokens` (DESIGN.md lines 754-755), aggregated in `quota_usage`. The `done` SSE event returns actual usage per turn.\n- Live sessions: `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}` gauge.\n- Consumed tokens: `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens` histogram + `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}` counter.\n- Per-tenant/user attribution: audit events + internal `quota_usage` records. Prometheus labels intentionally exclude high-cardinality tenant/user IDs (DESIGN.md line 1334).\n\nA reporting/billing UI is out of P0 scope. Raw data is available for downstream consumers.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:24Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:24Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546306","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546306"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546306"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546306/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":103,"original_line":85,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811856250,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":85,"position":103,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546403","pull_request_review_id":3808757590,"id":2812546403,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npA1j","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Title auto-generation is already in the design — `chats.title` is \"user-set or auto-generated\" (DESIGN.md line 726). Title is returned in list and get endpoints.\n\nSearch by title is not P0 (Section 4.2: \"Full-text search across chat history\" is out of scope).\n\nTitle editing needs an update endpoint. PRD Chat CRUD currently defines create, list, get, delete — no update. If title editing is needed for P0, we add `PATCH /v1/chats/{id}`. Separate scope discussion.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:25Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:25Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546403","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546403"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546403"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546403/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":109,"original_line":88,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811848155,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":88,"position":109,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546560","pull_request_review_id":3808757710,"id":2812546560,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npA4A","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Files are never injected wholesale into the context window. Upload flow (DESIGN.md Section 3.6) indexes files in the tenant vector store via Files API + Vector Stores API. At query time, `file_search` retrieves relevant excerpts only. A 10 MiB PDF is searchable without consuming 10 MiB of context.\n\nUpload size limit: PRD Section 13.1, 16 MiB, configurable per deployment. Per-type limits add config surface without P0 value — the limit is about network/storage cost, not context consumption.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:27Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:27Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546560","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546560"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546560"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546560/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":150,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811963258,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":150,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546664","pull_request_review_id":3808757812,"id":2812546664,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npA5o","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"394e53b565fed671657dc0ecb9f81d440ffba717","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"The summary solves a specific problem: without it, the LLM has zero awareness of attached documents unless `file_search` triggers. A general question like \"what documents do I have?\" has no search query to execute — the LLM needs a brief summary in context.\n\nToken cost is bounded (one short summary per doc, generated once) and charged to the system operational bucket (`requester_type=system`), not the user quota (PRD line 178, DESIGN.md line 256). It is the lowest-priority item in context assembly and gets truncated first if budget is tight (DESIGN.md line 1236).","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:28Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:53:28Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546664","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546664"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812546664"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812546664/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":168,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811966308,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":168,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812549107","pull_request_review_id":3808760944,"id":2812549107,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npBfz","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"(1) The `done` SSE event includes `usage.model` — the actual model used (DESIGN.md line 409). If downgraded from `gpt-5.2` to `gpt-5-mini`, the field reflects it. Client can compare against the expected default.\n\n(2) Model selection is not user-facing at P0 — deployment-configured (PRD Section 13.1 lines 712-713). The system picks the effective model based on quota state (DESIGN.md line 162). A model availability API is P2+ if user-facing model choice is introduced.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:54:02Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:54:02Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812549107","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812549107"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812549107"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812549107/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":201,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811979727,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":201,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812549205","pull_request_review_id":3808761081,"id":2812549205,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npBhV","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"The audit event is one structured record per completed chat turn — not per token or SSE event. Payload is metadata: tenant, user, chat ref, event type, model, token counts, policy decisions. Does not include full prompt/response by default. Content is minimal and governed by redaction rules (PRD lines 240-251, DESIGN.md line 1207). This granularity is standard for compliance in multi-tenant SaaS — \"who used what model, how many tokens, what policy decisions\" per interaction.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:54:03Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:54:04Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812549205","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812549205"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812549205"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812549205/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":404,"original_line":228,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811952356,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":228,"position":404,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812549310","pull_request_review_id":3808761253,"id":2812549310,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npBi-","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"PII is acknowledged in DESIGN.md line 1194: \"Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data.\" P0 redaction targets deterministic patterns (API keys, tokens, credentials) because they have reliable regex signatures. PII detection (names, addresses, phone numbers) needs heuristic/ML-based classification — explicitly deferred: DESIGN.md line 1224, \"Full DLP-based content redaction is deferred to P2.\" Audit content is already minimal (DESIGN.md line 1207) and access to payloads is governed by `audit_service` platform-level controls.","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:54:05Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T13:54:05Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812549310","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812549310"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812549310"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812549310/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":417,"original_line":241,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811988662,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":241,"position":417,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572909","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572909,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHTt","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,129 @@\n+---\n+status: accepted\n+date: 2026-02-14\n+decision-makers: Mike Yastrebtsov, Alex Henry\n+---\n+\n+<!--\n+=============================================================================\n+ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD (ADR) - based on MADR format\n+=============================================================================\n+PURPOSE: Capture WHY a significant technical decision was made - context,\n+options considered, trade-offs, and consequences.\n+\n+SCOPE:\n+✓ Context and problem statement\n+✓ Decision drivers (constraints, quality attributes)\n+✓ Options considered with pros/cons\n+✓ Chosen option with justification\n+✓ Consequences (good and bad)\n+\n+NOT IN THIS DOCUMENT (see other templates):\n+✗ Requirements -> PRD.md\n+✗ Full architecture description -> DESIGN.md\n+✗ Implementation details -> features/\n+\n+RULES:\n+- ADRs represent actual decision dilemma and decision state\n+- DESIGN is the primary artifact (\"what\"); ADRs annotate DESIGN with rationale (\"why\")\n+- Avoid \"everything is a decision\"; write ADRs only when the rationale needs to be explained and recorded for traceability\n+- Decision history is in git, not in documents\n+- Use single ADR per decision\n+\n+STANDARDS ALIGNMENT:\n+- MADR (Markdown Any Decision Records)\n+- IEEE 42010 (architecture decisions as first-class elements)\n+- ISO/IEC 15288 / 12207 (decision analysis process)\n+  ==============================================================================\n+  -->\n+\n+# Group chat ownership and usage attribution model (tenant-owned, sender-attributed)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution`","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/0003-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n\n```shell\n#!/bin/bash\n# First, let's verify the ADR file exists and check its content\necho \"=== Checking ADR-0003 file ===\"\nfind modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR -name \"*0003*\" -type f\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Content of ADR-0003 file ===\"\ncat modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/0003-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution.md | head -50\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Current ADR references in DESIGN.md ===\"\nrg -n 'ADR.*000[1-3]|cpt-cf' modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md || echo \"No ADR references found\"\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Traceability section in DESIGN.md ===\"\nrg -n -A 15 '^## 5\\. Traceability' modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md || echo \"Traceability section not found\"\n```\n\nRepository: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core\n\nLength of output: 9812\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n**Add ADR-0003 reference to DESIGN.md Traceability section.**\n\nADR-0003 (`cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution`) defines the P2+ group chat ownership and usage attribution model but is not referenced in DESIGN.md's Traceability section. Add it alongside ADR-0001 and ADR-0002 in section 5.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 GitHub Actions: Cypilot</summary>\n\n[error] 42-42: Constraints: ID not referenced from required artifact kind (cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn\n`@modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/0003-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution.md`\nat line 42, Add a reference to ADR-0003\n(`cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution`) in the DESIGN.md\nTraceability section (section 5) alongside ADR-0001 and ADR-0002; edit the\nTraceability list in DESIGN.md to include the ADR ID and a short title or link\nto ADR-0003 so the ownership and usage attribution model is discoverable from\nthe design docs.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @MikeFalcon77\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:23Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:24Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572909","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572909"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572909"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572909/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":42,"original_line":42,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":42,"position":42,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572916","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572916,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHT0","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🔴 Critical_\n\n**Fix list structure for llm_provider component ID.**\n\nLine 238 is missing a leading bullet (`-`) before the `**ID**:` declaration, which breaks the list structure and causes the pipeline validation failure. This inconsistency also triggers the MD005 markdown linting error at line 241.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n \n-**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n   - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- suggestion_start -->\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Committable suggestion</summary>\n\n> ‼️ **IMPORTANT**\n> Carefully review the code before committing. Ensure that it accurately replaces the highlighted code, contains no missing lines, and has no issues with indentation. Thoroughly test & benchmark the code to ensure it meets the requirements.\n\n```suggestion\n- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- suggestion_end -->\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md` around lines 238 - 240, The list item for\nthe component ID is missing its leading dash which breaks the markdown list;\nedit the DESIGN.md entry for the component with ID\n`cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider` (the `llm_provider` component block)\nand add a leading \"-\" before the \"**ID**:\" line so the `**ID**:\ncpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider` and its subsequent bullet lines become\na single list item, resolving the MD005 lint error and pipeline validation\nfailure.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @MikeFalcon77\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:23Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:10Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572916","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572916"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572916"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572916/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":238,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":240,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":240,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572922","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572922,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHT6","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add language tags to SSE event example fenced code blocks.**\n\nMultiple fenced code blocks in the SSE Event Definitions section (lines 340-440) are missing language identifiers, violating MD040. These are JSON event payloads and should be tagged with `json`.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed fix (example pattern)</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```json\n event: delta\n data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n```\n\nApply the same change to all SSE event example blocks at lines 340, 357, 375, 399, 417, and 431.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 markdownlint-cli2 (0.20.0)</summary>\n\n[warning] 340-340: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n---\n\n[warning] 357-357: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n---\n\n[warning] 375-375: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n---\n\n[warning] 399-399: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n---\n\n[warning] 417-417: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n---\n\n[warning] 431-431: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n---\n\n[warning] 440-440: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md` around lines 340 - 440, The fenced code\nblocks in the \"SSE Event Definitions\" section are missing language identifiers\n(they contain JSON payloads like the examples starting with \"event: delta\",\n\"event: tool\", \"event: citations\", \"event: done\", \"event: error\", and \"event:\nping\"); update each of those examples by adding the json language tag to the\nopening triple-backticks (i.e., change ``` to ```json for each SSE example\nblock) so they pass MD040 linting and correctly highlight the JSON payloads.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @MikeFalcon77\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:23Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:17Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572922","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572922"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572922"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572922/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":340,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":440,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":440,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572927","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572927,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHT_","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```\n+ping*  (delta | tool | citations)*  (done | error)\n+```\n+\n+- Zero or more `ping` events may appear at any point.\n+- `delta`, `tool`, and `citations` events may interleave in any order.\n+- Exactly one terminal event (`done` or `error`) ends the stream.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-event-translation\"></a>\n+#### Provider Event Translation\n+\n+Provider-specific streaming events are internal to `llm_provider` and `chat_service`. They are never forwarded to clients. The translation layer maps provider events to the stable SSE protocol defined above.\n+\n+| Provider Event | Stable SSE Event | Notes |\n+|----------------|-----------------|-------|\n+| `response.output_text.delta` | `event: delta` (`type: \"text\"`) | Text content mapped 1:1. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.searching` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"start\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | Emitted when file_search tool is invoked. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.completed` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"done\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | `details` populated from search results metadata. |\n+| File search annotations in response | `event: citations` | Extracted from provider annotations, mapped to `items[]` schema. When provider annotations include ranges, `items[].span` SHOULD be populated as character offsets into the final assistant text. |\n+| `response.completed` | `event: done` | `usage` from `response.usage`; `provider.response_id` from `response.id`. |\n+| Provider HTTP error / disconnect | `event: error` (`code: \"provider_error\"` or `\"provider_timeout\"`) | Error details sanitized; provider internals not exposed. |\n+| Provider 429 | `event: error` (`code: \"rate_limited\"`) | After OAGW retry exhaustion. |\n+\n+This mapping is intentionally provider-agnostic in the stable contract. If the provider changes its event format or a new provider is added, only the translation layer in `llm_provider` is updated. The client contract remains unchanged.\n+\n+**Error Codes**:\n+\n+| Code | HTTP Status | Description |\n+|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `feature_not_licensed` | 403 | Tenant lacks `ai_chat` feature |\n+| `insufficient_permissions` | 403 | Subject lacks permission for the requested action (AuthZ Resolver denied) |\n+| `chat_not_found` | 404 | Chat does not exist or not accessible under current authorization constraints |\n+| `quota_exceeded` | 429 | User exceeded daily/monthly usage limit |\n+| `rate_limited` | 429 | Too many requests in time window |\n+| `file_too_large` | 413 | Uploaded file exceeds size limit |\n+| `unsupported_file_type` | 415 | File type not supported for upload |\n+| `provider_error` | 502 | LLM provider returned an error |\n+| `provider_timeout` | 504 | LLM provider request timed out |\n+\n+### 3.4 Internal Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency Module | Interface Used | Purpose |\n+|-------------------|----------------|---------|\n+| api_gateway (platform) | Axum router / middleware | HTTP request handling, SSE transport |\n+| authn (platform) | Middleware (JWT/opaque token) | Extract `user_id` + `tenant_id` from request |\n+| license_manager (platform) | Middleware | Check tenant has `ai_chat` feature; reject with 403 if not |\n+| authz_resolver (platform) | Access evaluation API (`/access/v1/evaluation`) | Obtain authorization decisions + SQL-compilable constraints for chat operations |\n+| audit_service (platform) | Event emitter | Receive structured audit events (prompts, responses, usage, policy decisions) |\n+| outbound_gateway (platform) | Internal HTTP | Egress to LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) with credential injection |\n+\n+**Dependency Rules**:\n+- `chat_service` never calls the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) directly; all external calls go through OAGW\n+- `SecurityContext` (user_id, tenant_id) propagated through all in-process calls\n+- `license_manager` runs as middleware before `chat_service` is invoked\n+- `chat_service` calls `authz_resolver` before every database query; on PDP denial or PDP unreachable, fail-closed (deny access)\n+- `chat_service` emits audit events to `audit_service` after each turn; mini-chat does not store audit data locally\n+\n+### 3.5 External Dependencies\n+\n+#### LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\n+\n+Both providers expose a compatible API surface. OAGW routes requests to the configured provider and injects credentials accordingly.\n+\n+| API | Purpose | OAGW Route | OpenAI Endpoint | Azure OpenAI Endpoint |\n+|-----|---------|------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| Responses API (streaming) | Chat completion with tool support | `POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Responses API (non-streaming) | Thread summary generation, doc summary | `POST /outbound/llm/responses` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Files API | Upload user documents | `POST /outbound/llm/files` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/files` |\n+| Vector Stores API | Manage per-tenant vector stores, add/remove files | `POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/vector_stores/*` |\n+| File Search (tool) | Retrieve document excerpts during chat | - (invoked as tool within Responses API call) | `file_search` tool | `file_search` tool (identical contract) |\n+\n+Note: Azure OpenAI path variants differ by rollout; OAGW owns the exact path mapping for each API.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-api-mapping\"></a>\n+**Provider API Mapping** - authentication and endpoint differences:\n+\n+| Aspect | OpenAI | Azure OpenAI |\n+|--------|--------|--------------|\n+| **Base URL** | `https://api.openai.com/v1` | `https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1` |\n+| **Authentication** | `Authorization: Bearer {api_key}` | `api-key: {key}` header or Entra ID bearer token |\n+| **API version** | Not required | Azure may require an `api-version` query parameter depending on feature/rollout; OAGW owns this provider-specific detail |\n+| **File upload `purpose`** | `assistants` (P0) | `assistants` only (`user_data` not supported) |\n+| **Vector stores per `file_search`** | Multiple | **One** (sufficient for P0: one store per tenant) |\n+| **SSE format** | `event:` + `data:` lines, structured events | Identical format |\n+| **`user` field** | Supported | Supported (feeds into Azure abuse monitoring) |\n+| **`metadata` object** | Supported | Supported |\n+\n+OAGW MUST inject the required `api-version` query parameter when calling Azure OpenAI endpoints.\n+\n+**OAGW throttling scope**: OAGW handles provider-side rate limiting only - retry on provider 429 (with `Retry-After` respect, max 1 retry), circuit breaker when provider error rate exceeds threshold, and global concurrency cap as an SRE safety valve. Product-level quota enforcement (per-user, per-tenant, model downgrade) is NOT an OAGW concern - it is handled entirely by `chat_service` / `quota_service` before any outbound call (see constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+#### PostgreSQL\n+\n+| Usage | Purpose |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| Primary datastore | Chats, messages, attachments, summaries, quota counters, tenant vector store mappings |\n+\n+### 3.6 Interactions & Sequences\n+\n+#### Send Message with Streaming Response\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-send-message`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant AuthZ as authz_resolver (PDP)\n+    participant CS as chat_service (PEP)\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream\n+    AG->>AG: AuthN + license_manager\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(chat_id, user_msg, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>AuthZ: Evaluate(subject, action: \"send_message\", resource: {type: chat, id: chat_id})\n+    AuthZ-->>CS: decision + constraints\n+\n+    alt PDP unreachable / timeout\n+        CS-->>AG: 403 Forbidden\n+        AG-->>UI: 403\n+    else decision = false\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Load chat, recent messages, thread_summary, attachments, tenant vector_store_id (with constraints in WHERE)\n+\n+    alt 0 rows returned\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Build ContextPlan (system prompt + summary + doc summaries + recent msgs + user msg)\n+    CS->>CS: Preflight (reserve) quota check (estimate tokens from ContextPlan + max_output -> allow / downgrade / reject)\n+\n+    alt quota exhausted\n+        CS-->>AG: 429 quota_exceeded\n+        AG-->>UI: SSE error event\n+    end\n+\n+    Note over CS, OAI: Single provider call per user turn. file_search is enabled as a tool within the same streaming Responses API request.\n+\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream (tools include file_search, store=tenant_store)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API (streaming, tool calling enabled)\n+    OAI-->>OG: SSE tokens\n+    OG-->>CS: Token stream\n+    CS-->>AG: Token stream\n+    AG-->>UI: SSE tokens\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Persist user msg + assistant msg + usage\n+    CS->>CS: Commit actual usage to quota_service (debit input_tokens + output_tokens)\n+\n+    participant AS as audit_service\n+    CS->>AS: Emit audit event (usage, policy decisions)\n+\n+    opt Thread summary update triggered (background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Full lifecycle of a user message - from authorization through streaming LLM response to persistence and optional thread compression. Authorization is evaluated before any database access. The PEP sends an evaluation request to the AuthZ Resolver with the chat's resource type and ID; the returned constraints are applied to the DB query's WHERE clause. If the PDP is unreachable, the request is rejected immediately (fail-closed, 403). If the PDP denies access for an operation with a concrete `chat_id`, the PEP returns 404 to avoid disclosing resource existence. If the constrained query returns 0 rows, the PEP returns 404.\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-file-upload`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments (multipart)\n+    AG->>CS: UploadAttachment(chat_id, file, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>DB: Insert attachment metadata (status: pending)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/files (upload)\n+    OG->>OAI: Files API upload\n+    OAI-->>OG: file_id\n+    OG-->>CS: file_id\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/{tenant_store}/files (add file)\n+    OG->>OAI: Add file to vector store\n+    OAI-->>OG: OK\n+    OG-->>CS: OK\n+    CS->>DB: Update attachment (status: ready, provider_file_id)\n+\n+    opt Generate doc summary (Variant 1, background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue doc summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+\n+    CS-->>AG: attachment_id, status\n+    AG-->>UI: 201 Created\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: File upload flow - the file is uploaded to the LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) via OAGW, added to the tenant's vector store, optionally summarized, and metadata is persisted locally.\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-cancellation`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant LP as llm_provider\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: SSE connection established\n+    AG->>AG: Create CancellationToken\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(..., cancel_token)\n+    CS->>LP: stream_response(..., cancel_token)\n+    LP->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream\n+    OG->>OAI: Streaming in progress\n+\n+    alt UI disconnect\n+        UI--xAG: Client disconnects / stop button\n+        AG->>AG: Detect broken SSE write -> cancel token\n+    else Server timeout\n+        CS->>CS: Timeout elapsed -> cancel token\n+    end\n+\n+    LP->>LP: Token cancelled -> abort HTTP request\n+    LP->>OG: Close connection (hard cancel)\n+    OG->>OAI: TCP RST / connection closed\n+    CS->>CS: Persist partial response (usage may be unknown)\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Cancellation propagates end-to-end via a shared `CancellationToken`. When triggered, `llm_provider` performs a hard cancel - aborting the outbound HTTP connection so the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) stops generating immediately. The partial response is persisted. Because provider usage is typically only delivered on a completed response, `input_tokens`/`output_tokens` may be NULL or approximate for cancelled turns; quota enforcement uses the bounded best-effort debit described in `quota_service`.\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Update\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-thread-summary`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Check summary trigger (msg count > 20 OR tokens > budget OR every 15 user turns)\n+    CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    Note over CS: Background worker executes summary task asynchronously\n+    CS->>DB: Load current thread_summary + old messages batch (10-20 msgs)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses (update summary prompt)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API\n+    OAI-->>OG: Updated summary\n+    OG-->>CS: Updated summary\n+    CS->>DB: Save new thread_summary\n+    CS->>DB: Mark summarized messages as compressed\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Thread summary is updated asynchronously after a chat turn when trigger conditions are met. Summary generation is a background task and MUST be attributed as `requester_type=system` so that its usage is not charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+Summary quality gate (P0): the system MUST detect and mitigate obviously-bad summaries without ML-based evaluation.\n+\n+- After generating a summary, `chat_service` MUST validate the candidate summary text.\n+- If summary length < `X` OR entropy < `Y`, `chat_service` MUST attempt regeneration.\n+- If regeneration fails quality checks or the provider call fails, `chat_service` MUST fall back by keeping the previous summary unchanged and MUST NOT mark the message batch as compressed.\n+\n+`X` and `Y` are configurable thresholds. Entropy is a deterministic proxy computed as normalized token entropy over whitespace-delimited tokens:\n+\n+`H_norm = (-sum(p_i * log2(p_i))) / log2(N)` where `p_i` is the empirical frequency of token `i` and `N` is the number of distinct tokens.\n+\n+Observability:\n+\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}` for each regeneration attempt (`reason` from a bounded allowlist such as `too_short|low_entropy|provider_error|invalid_format`).\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total` when the fallback behavior above is used.\n+\n+### 3.7 Database Schemas & Tables\n+\n+#### Table: chats\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chats`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Chat identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | Owning user |\n+| title | VARCHAR(255) | Chat title (user-set or auto-generated) |\n+| is_temporary | BOOLEAN | If true, auto-deleted after 24h |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last activity time |\n+| deleted_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Soft delete timestamp (nullable) |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `created_at`\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, updated_at DESC)` for listing chats\n+\n+#### Table: messages\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-messages`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Message identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key (nullable). Used for completed replay and traceability. Running state is tracked in `chat_turns`. |\n+| role | VARCHAR(16) | `user`, `assistant`, or `system` |\n+| content | TEXT | Message content |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| request_kind | VARCHAR(16) | `chat`, `summary`, or `doc_summary` (nullable) |\n+| features_used | JSONB | Feature flags and counters (nullable) |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Actual input tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Actual output tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| model | VARCHAR(64) | Actual model used for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| is_compressed | BOOLEAN | True if included in a thread summary |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `chat_id`, `role`, `content`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE. UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)` WHERE `request_id IS NOT NULL`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(chat_id, created_at)` for loading recent messages\n+\n+#### Table: chat_turns\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chat-turns`\n+\n+Tracks idempotency and in-progress generation state for `request_id`. This avoids ambiguous interpretation of `messages.request_id` when a generation is still running.\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Turn identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key |\n+| requester_type | VARCHAR(16) | `user` or `system` |\n+| requester_user_id | UUID | User ID when requester_type=`user` (nullable for system) |\n+| state | VARCHAR(16) | `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier (nullable until request starts) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID (nullable) |\n+| assistant_message_id | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID (nullable until completed) |\n+| error_code | VARCHAR(64) | Terminal error code (nullable) |\n+| started_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Start time |\n+| completed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Completion time (nullable) |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+A `chat_turns` row MUST be created before starting the outbound provider request; initial state is `running`.\n+\n+State machine:\n+- Allowed transitions: `running` -> `completed` \\| `failed` \\| `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states MUST be immutable\n+- At most one `running` turn per `(chat_id, request_id)`\n+\n+#### Table: attachments\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-attachments`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Attachment identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| filename | VARCHAR(255) | Original filename |\n+| content_type | VARCHAR(128) | MIME type |\n+| size_bytes | BIGINT | File size |\n+| provider_file_id | VARCHAR(128) | LLM provider file ID - OpenAI `file-*` or Azure OpenAI `assistant-*` (nullable until upload completes) |\n+| status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `ready`, `failed` |\n+| doc_summary | TEXT | LLM-generated document summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_model | VARCHAR(64) | Model used to generate the summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When the summary was last generated (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `in_progress`, `done`, `failed` (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_attempts | INTEGER | Cleanup retry attempts (default 0) |\n+| last_cleanup_error | TEXT | Last cleanup error (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When cleanup state was last updated (nullable) |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Upload time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `chat_id`, `filename`, `status`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: thread_summaries\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-thread-summaries`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Summary identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id, UNIQUE) |\n+| summary_text | TEXT | Compressed conversation summary |\n+| summarized_up_to | UUID | Last message ID included in this summary |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count of summary |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `chat_id`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: tenant_vector_stores\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-vector-stores`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant identifier |\n+| vector_store_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider vector store ID (OpenAI `vs_*` or Azure OpenAI equivalent) |\n+| provider | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier: `openai` or `azure_openai` |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `tenant_id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `vector_store_id`, `provider`, `created_at`. One vector store per tenant.\n+\n+Creation protocol (P0): `chat_service` uses a get-or-create flow with database uniqueness as the race arbiter.\n+\n+1. Attempt to read the row by `tenant_id`.\n+2. If not present, create the vector store via OAGW and attempt INSERT.\n+3. If INSERT fails due to unique violation (concurrent creator), re-read the row and use the existing `vector_store_id`. Best-effort delete the newly created provider vector store to avoid orphans.\n+\n+ **P0**: run a periodic reconcile/orphan reaper job (for example nightly) to reconcile provider state with `tenant_vector_stores`:\n+ - If a provider vector store exists but is not referenced in DB -> delete it.\n+ - If a DB row exists but the provider vector store is missing -> recreate the vector store and update the DB row.\n+\n+#### Table: quota_usage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-quota-usage`\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-quota-usage-accounting`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Record identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | User |\n+| period_type | VARCHAR(16) | `daily` or `monthly` |\n+| period_start | DATE | Start of the period |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Total input tokens consumed |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Total output tokens consumed |\n+| file_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of file search tool calls |\n+| web_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of web search calls (P1+) |\n+| rag_retrieval_calls | INTEGER | Number of internal RAG retrieval calls (P1+) |\n+| premium_model_calls | INTEGER | Calls to premium models |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+Commit semantics: quota updates MUST be atomic per period record. Implementations SHOULD use a transaction with row locking or a single UPDATE statement to avoid race conditions under parallel streams.\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` for quota lookups\n+\n+#### Projection Table: tenant_closure\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-closure-ref`\n+\n+Mini Chat does NOT require the `tenant_closure` local projection table for chat content access in P0. Chat content is owner-only and requires exact `owner_tenant_id` + `user_id` predicates.\n+\n+The `tenant_closure` projection table exists in the platform authorization model for modules that use hierarchical tenant scoping, but it is unused for Mini Chat content operations.\n+\n+Schema is defined in the [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#table-schemas-local-projections).\n+\n+**P2+ note**: When chat sharing (projects) is introduced, `resource_group_membership` and optionally `resource_group_closure` tables will also be required.\n+\n+### 3.8 Authorization (PEP)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-authz-pep`\n+\n+Mini Chat acts as a Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) per the platform's PDP/PEP authorization model defined in [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). The `chat_service` builds evaluation requests, sends them to the AuthZ Resolver (PDP), and compiles returned constraints into SQL WHERE clauses.\n+\n+Policy (P0): chat content is owner-only. For all content operations, authorization MUST enforce:\n+\n+- `owner_tenant_id == subject.tenant_id`\n+- `user_id == subject.id`\n+\n+Mini Chat does not use tenant hierarchy or subtree-based authorization for content operations.\n+\n+#### Resource Type\n+\n+The authorized resource is **Chat**. Sub-resources (Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary) do not have independent authorization - they are accessed through their parent chat, and the chat's authorization decision covers all child operations.\n+\n+| Attribute | Value |\n+|-----------|-------|\n+| GTS Type ID | `gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~` |\n+| Primary table | `chats` |\n+| Authorization granularity | Chat-level (sub-resources inherit) |\n+\n+#### PEP Configuration\n+\n+**Capabilities** (declared in `context.capabilities`):\n+\n+| Capability | P0 | P2+ | Rationale |\n+|------------|-----|------|-----------|\n+| `tenant_hierarchy` | No | No | Chat content is owner-only; `tenant_closure` is unused for Mini Chat content operations |\n+| `group_membership` | No | Yes | Needed when chat sharing via projects is introduced |\n+| `group_hierarchy` | No | Maybe | Needed if projects have nested hierarchy |\n+\n+**Supported properties** (declared in `context.supported_properties`):\n+\n+| Resource Property | SQL Column | Description |\n+|-------------------|------------|-------------|\n+| `owner_tenant_id` | `chats.tenant_id` | Owning tenant |\n+| `user_id` | `chats.user_id` | Owning user |\n+| `id` | `chats.id` | Chat identifier |\n+\n+#### Per-Operation Authorization Matrix\n+\n+| Endpoint | Action | `resource.id` | `require_constraints` | Expected P0 Predicates |\n+|----------|--------|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n+| `POST /v1/chats` | `create` | absent | `false` | decision only (no constraints) |\n+| `GET /v1/chats` | `list` | absent | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}` | `read` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `DELETE /v1/chats/{id}` | `delete` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | `update` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | `send_message` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | `upload` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | `read_turn` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+\n+**Notes**:\n+- `send_message` and `upload` are actions on the Chat resource, not on Message or Attachment resources. The `resource.id` is the chat's ID.\n+- For streaming (`send_message`), authorization is evaluated once at SSE connection establishment. The entire streaming session operates under the initial authorization decision. No per-message re-authorization.\n+- For `create`, the PEP passes `resource.properties.owner_tenant_id` and `resource.properties.user_id` from the SecurityContext. The PDP validates permission without returning constraints.\n+\n+#### Evaluation Request/Response Examples\n+\n+**Example 1: List Chats** (`GET /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"list\" },\n+  \"resource\": { \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\" },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response (P0 - user-owned chats only):\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+ORDER BY updated_at DESC\n+```\n+\n+**Example 2: Get Chat** (`GET /v1/chats/{id}`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"read\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"chat-456\"\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE id = 'chat-456'\n+  AND tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+```\n+\n+Result: 1 row -> return chat; 0 rows -> 404 Not Found (hides existence from unauthorized users).\n+\n+**Example 3: Create Chat** (`POST /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"create\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"properties\": {\n+      \"owner_tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\",\n+      \"user_id\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+    }\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": false,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{ \"decision\": true }\n+```\n+\n+PEP proceeds with INSERT. No constraints needed for create.\n+\n+**Example 4: Send Message** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`)\n+\n+Same authorization flow as Example 2 (Get Chat), but with `\"action\": { \"name\": \"send_message\" }` and `\"resource.id\"` set to the chat ID. Authorization is evaluated once before the SSE stream is established. The constraints are applied to the query that loads the chat and its messages from `chat_store`.\n+\n+#### Fail-Closed Behavior\n+\n+Mini Chat follows the platform's fail-closed rules (see [Authorization Design - Fail-Closed Rules](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#fail-closed-rules)):\n+\n+| Condition | PEP Action |\n+|-----------|------------|\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` present | 404 Not Found (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` absent | 403 Forbidden (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| PDP unreachable / timeout | 403 Forbidden (fail-closed) |\n+| `decision: true` + no constraints + `require_constraints: true` | 403 Forbidden |\n+| Unknown predicate type in constraints | Treat constraint as false; if all constraints false -> 403 |\n+| Unknown `resource_property` in predicate | Treat constraint as false; log error (PDP contract violation) |\n+| Empty `constraints: []` | 403 Forbidden |\n+\n+#### Token Scopes\n+\n+Mini Chat recognizes the following token scopes for third-party application narrowing:\n+\n+| Scope | Permits |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| `ai:chat` | All chat operations (umbrella scope) |\n+| `ai:chat:read` | `list`, `read` actions only |\n+| `ai:chat:write` | `create`, `update`, `delete`, `send_message`, `upload` actions |\n+\n+First-party applications (UI) use `token_scopes: [\"*\"]`. Third-party integrations receive narrowed scopes. Scope enforcement is handled by the PDP - the PEP includes `token_scopes` in the evaluation request context.\n+\n+#### P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\n+\n+When Projects / chat sharing is introduced (P2+), the authorization model extends naturally:\n+\n+1. Add `group_membership` capability (and optionally `group_hierarchy`).\n+2. Maintain `resource_group_membership` projection table mapping chat IDs to project group IDs.\n+3. The PDP returns additional access paths via OR'd constraints - e.g., one constraint for owned chats (`user_id` predicate), another for shared-via-project chats (`in_group` predicate).\n+4. `supported_properties` remains unchanged (the `id` property is used for group membership joins).\n+\n+No changes to the PEP flow or constraint compilation logic are needed. The PDP's response structure naturally handles multiple access paths through OR'd constraints.\n+\n+## 4. Additional Context\n+\n+### P0 Scope Boundaries\n+\n+**Included in P0**:\n+- Single-tenant vector store per tenant (not per workspace)\n+- Thread summary as only compression mechanism\n+- On-upload document summary via File Search (Variant 1 from draft)\n+- Quota enforcement: daily + monthly per user\n+- Temporary chats with 24h scheduled cleanup\n+- File Search per-message call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2 tool calls per message)\n+\n+**Deferred to P2+**:\n+- Projects / chat sharing\n+- Full-text search across chats\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are both supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies (beyond simple limits)\n+- Per-workspace vector stores\n+\n+### Data Classification and Retention (P0)\n+\n+Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data. Mini Chat treats messages and summaries as customer content and applies data minimization and retention controls.\n+\n+**Data classes**:\n+- Chat content: `messages.content`, `thread_summaries.summary_text`, `attachments.doc_summary`\n+- Operational metadata: IDs, timestamps, provider correlation IDs, quotas\n+- Audit events: structured records emitted to platform `audit_service`\n+\n+**Retention**:\n+- Temporary chats are scheduled for cleanup after 24h and MUST be deleted within 25h (PRD SLA).\n+- Non-temporary chats are retained until explicit deletion by default, subject to operator-configured retention policies.\n+- Soft-deleted chats (`deleted_at` set) are hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+**Audit content handling (P0)**:\n+- Audit events include the minimal content required for security and incident response.\n+- `chat_service` MUST redact secret patterns (tokens, keys, credentials) before sending content to `audit_service`.\n+- Redaction MUST be testable and based on a bounded allowlist of rule classes.\n+\n+| Rule class | Example (non-exhaustive) | Action |\n+|-----------|---------------------------|--------|\n+| `bearer_token` | `Authorization: Bearer eyJ...` | Replace token with `Bearer [REDACTED]` |\n+| `jwt` | `eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJS...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_JWT]` |\n+| `openai_api_key` | `sk-...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]` |\n+| `aws_access_key_id` | `AKIA...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]` |\n+| `azure_api_key` | `api-key: ...`, `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: ...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]` |\n+| `api_key_fields` | `api_key=...`, `x-api-key=...`, `client_secret=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `password_fields` | `password=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `pem_private_key` | `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` | Replace block with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]` |\n+| `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+- Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add blank line after redaction rules table.**\n\nThe table ending at line 1221 is missing a blank line before the following paragraph, violating MD058 (tables should be surrounded by blank lines).\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n | `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+\n - Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 markdownlint-cli2 (0.20.0)</summary>\n\n[warning] 1221-1221: Tables should be surrounded by blank lines\n\n(MD058, blanks-around-tables)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md` around lines 1211 - 1222, The Markdown\ntable listing redaction Rule classes (the table starting with `| Rule class |\nExample (non-exhaustive) | Action |` and rows like `bearer_token`, `jwt`, etc.)\nis not followed by a blank line before the paragraph beginning \"Audit events\nMUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes...\"; add a single blank line after\nthe table to satisfy MD058 (tables must be surrounded by blank lines) so the\nparagraph is separated from the table.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @MikeFalcon77\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:23Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:17Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572927","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572927"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572927"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572927/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1211,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1222,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1222,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572932","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572932,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHUE","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```\n+ping*  (delta | tool | citations)*  (done | error)\n+```\n+\n+- Zero or more `ping` events may appear at any point.\n+- `delta`, `tool`, and `citations` events may interleave in any order.\n+- Exactly one terminal event (`done` or `error`) ends the stream.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-event-translation\"></a>\n+#### Provider Event Translation\n+\n+Provider-specific streaming events are internal to `llm_provider` and `chat_service`. They are never forwarded to clients. The translation layer maps provider events to the stable SSE protocol defined above.\n+\n+| Provider Event | Stable SSE Event | Notes |\n+|----------------|-----------------|-------|\n+| `response.output_text.delta` | `event: delta` (`type: \"text\"`) | Text content mapped 1:1. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.searching` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"start\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | Emitted when file_search tool is invoked. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.completed` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"done\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | `details` populated from search results metadata. |\n+| File search annotations in response | `event: citations` | Extracted from provider annotations, mapped to `items[]` schema. When provider annotations include ranges, `items[].span` SHOULD be populated as character offsets into the final assistant text. |\n+| `response.completed` | `event: done` | `usage` from `response.usage`; `provider.response_id` from `response.id`. |\n+| Provider HTTP error / disconnect | `event: error` (`code: \"provider_error\"` or `\"provider_timeout\"`) | Error details sanitized; provider internals not exposed. |\n+| Provider 429 | `event: error` (`code: \"rate_limited\"`) | After OAGW retry exhaustion. |\n+\n+This mapping is intentionally provider-agnostic in the stable contract. If the provider changes its event format or a new provider is added, only the translation layer in `llm_provider` is updated. The client contract remains unchanged.\n+\n+**Error Codes**:\n+\n+| Code | HTTP Status | Description |\n+|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `feature_not_licensed` | 403 | Tenant lacks `ai_chat` feature |\n+| `insufficient_permissions` | 403 | Subject lacks permission for the requested action (AuthZ Resolver denied) |\n+| `chat_not_found` | 404 | Chat does not exist or not accessible under current authorization constraints |\n+| `quota_exceeded` | 429 | User exceeded daily/monthly usage limit |\n+| `rate_limited` | 429 | Too many requests in time window |\n+| `file_too_large` | 413 | Uploaded file exceeds size limit |\n+| `unsupported_file_type` | 415 | File type not supported for upload |\n+| `provider_error` | 502 | LLM provider returned an error |\n+| `provider_timeout` | 504 | LLM provider request timed out |\n+\n+### 3.4 Internal Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency Module | Interface Used | Purpose |\n+|-------------------|----------------|---------|\n+| api_gateway (platform) | Axum router / middleware | HTTP request handling, SSE transport |\n+| authn (platform) | Middleware (JWT/opaque token) | Extract `user_id` + `tenant_id` from request |\n+| license_manager (platform) | Middleware | Check tenant has `ai_chat` feature; reject with 403 if not |\n+| authz_resolver (platform) | Access evaluation API (`/access/v1/evaluation`) | Obtain authorization decisions + SQL-compilable constraints for chat operations |\n+| audit_service (platform) | Event emitter | Receive structured audit events (prompts, responses, usage, policy decisions) |\n+| outbound_gateway (platform) | Internal HTTP | Egress to LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) with credential injection |\n+\n+**Dependency Rules**:\n+- `chat_service` never calls the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) directly; all external calls go through OAGW\n+- `SecurityContext` (user_id, tenant_id) propagated through all in-process calls\n+- `license_manager` runs as middleware before `chat_service` is invoked\n+- `chat_service` calls `authz_resolver` before every database query; on PDP denial or PDP unreachable, fail-closed (deny access)\n+- `chat_service` emits audit events to `audit_service` after each turn; mini-chat does not store audit data locally\n+\n+### 3.5 External Dependencies\n+\n+#### LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\n+\n+Both providers expose a compatible API surface. OAGW routes requests to the configured provider and injects credentials accordingly.\n+\n+| API | Purpose | OAGW Route | OpenAI Endpoint | Azure OpenAI Endpoint |\n+|-----|---------|------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| Responses API (streaming) | Chat completion with tool support | `POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Responses API (non-streaming) | Thread summary generation, doc summary | `POST /outbound/llm/responses` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Files API | Upload user documents | `POST /outbound/llm/files` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/files` |\n+| Vector Stores API | Manage per-tenant vector stores, add/remove files | `POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/vector_stores/*` |\n+| File Search (tool) | Retrieve document excerpts during chat | - (invoked as tool within Responses API call) | `file_search` tool | `file_search` tool (identical contract) |\n+\n+Note: Azure OpenAI path variants differ by rollout; OAGW owns the exact path mapping for each API.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-api-mapping\"></a>\n+**Provider API Mapping** - authentication and endpoint differences:\n+\n+| Aspect | OpenAI | Azure OpenAI |\n+|--------|--------|--------------|\n+| **Base URL** | `https://api.openai.com/v1` | `https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1` |\n+| **Authentication** | `Authorization: Bearer {api_key}` | `api-key: {key}` header or Entra ID bearer token |\n+| **API version** | Not required | Azure may require an `api-version` query parameter depending on feature/rollout; OAGW owns this provider-specific detail |\n+| **File upload `purpose`** | `assistants` (P0) | `assistants` only (`user_data` not supported) |\n+| **Vector stores per `file_search`** | Multiple | **One** (sufficient for P0: one store per tenant) |\n+| **SSE format** | `event:` + `data:` lines, structured events | Identical format |\n+| **`user` field** | Supported | Supported (feeds into Azure abuse monitoring) |\n+| **`metadata` object** | Supported | Supported |\n+\n+OAGW MUST inject the required `api-version` query parameter when calling Azure OpenAI endpoints.\n+\n+**OAGW throttling scope**: OAGW handles provider-side rate limiting only - retry on provider 429 (with `Retry-After` respect, max 1 retry), circuit breaker when provider error rate exceeds threshold, and global concurrency cap as an SRE safety valve. Product-level quota enforcement (per-user, per-tenant, model downgrade) is NOT an OAGW concern - it is handled entirely by `chat_service` / `quota_service` before any outbound call (see constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+#### PostgreSQL\n+\n+| Usage | Purpose |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| Primary datastore | Chats, messages, attachments, summaries, quota counters, tenant vector store mappings |\n+\n+### 3.6 Interactions & Sequences\n+\n+#### Send Message with Streaming Response\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-send-message`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant AuthZ as authz_resolver (PDP)\n+    participant CS as chat_service (PEP)\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream\n+    AG->>AG: AuthN + license_manager\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(chat_id, user_msg, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>AuthZ: Evaluate(subject, action: \"send_message\", resource: {type: chat, id: chat_id})\n+    AuthZ-->>CS: decision + constraints\n+\n+    alt PDP unreachable / timeout\n+        CS-->>AG: 403 Forbidden\n+        AG-->>UI: 403\n+    else decision = false\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Load chat, recent messages, thread_summary, attachments, tenant vector_store_id (with constraints in WHERE)\n+\n+    alt 0 rows returned\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Build ContextPlan (system prompt + summary + doc summaries + recent msgs + user msg)\n+    CS->>CS: Preflight (reserve) quota check (estimate tokens from ContextPlan + max_output -> allow / downgrade / reject)\n+\n+    alt quota exhausted\n+        CS-->>AG: 429 quota_exceeded\n+        AG-->>UI: SSE error event\n+    end\n+\n+    Note over CS, OAI: Single provider call per user turn. file_search is enabled as a tool within the same streaming Responses API request.\n+\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream (tools include file_search, store=tenant_store)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API (streaming, tool calling enabled)\n+    OAI-->>OG: SSE tokens\n+    OG-->>CS: Token stream\n+    CS-->>AG: Token stream\n+    AG-->>UI: SSE tokens\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Persist user msg + assistant msg + usage\n+    CS->>CS: Commit actual usage to quota_service (debit input_tokens + output_tokens)\n+\n+    participant AS as audit_service\n+    CS->>AS: Emit audit event (usage, policy decisions)\n+\n+    opt Thread summary update triggered (background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Full lifecycle of a user message - from authorization through streaming LLM response to persistence and optional thread compression. Authorization is evaluated before any database access. The PEP sends an evaluation request to the AuthZ Resolver with the chat's resource type and ID; the returned constraints are applied to the DB query's WHERE clause. If the PDP is unreachable, the request is rejected immediately (fail-closed, 403). If the PDP denies access for an operation with a concrete `chat_id`, the PEP returns 404 to avoid disclosing resource existence. If the constrained query returns 0 rows, the PEP returns 404.\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-file-upload`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments (multipart)\n+    AG->>CS: UploadAttachment(chat_id, file, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>DB: Insert attachment metadata (status: pending)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/files (upload)\n+    OG->>OAI: Files API upload\n+    OAI-->>OG: file_id\n+    OG-->>CS: file_id\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/{tenant_store}/files (add file)\n+    OG->>OAI: Add file to vector store\n+    OAI-->>OG: OK\n+    OG-->>CS: OK\n+    CS->>DB: Update attachment (status: ready, provider_file_id)\n+\n+    opt Generate doc summary (Variant 1, background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue doc summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+\n+    CS-->>AG: attachment_id, status\n+    AG-->>UI: 201 Created\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: File upload flow - the file is uploaded to the LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) via OAGW, added to the tenant's vector store, optionally summarized, and metadata is persisted locally.\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-cancellation`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant LP as llm_provider\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: SSE connection established\n+    AG->>AG: Create CancellationToken\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(..., cancel_token)\n+    CS->>LP: stream_response(..., cancel_token)\n+    LP->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream\n+    OG->>OAI: Streaming in progress\n+\n+    alt UI disconnect\n+        UI--xAG: Client disconnects / stop button\n+        AG->>AG: Detect broken SSE write -> cancel token\n+    else Server timeout\n+        CS->>CS: Timeout elapsed -> cancel token\n+    end\n+\n+    LP->>LP: Token cancelled -> abort HTTP request\n+    LP->>OG: Close connection (hard cancel)\n+    OG->>OAI: TCP RST / connection closed\n+    CS->>CS: Persist partial response (usage may be unknown)\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Cancellation propagates end-to-end via a shared `CancellationToken`. When triggered, `llm_provider` performs a hard cancel - aborting the outbound HTTP connection so the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) stops generating immediately. The partial response is persisted. Because provider usage is typically only delivered on a completed response, `input_tokens`/`output_tokens` may be NULL or approximate for cancelled turns; quota enforcement uses the bounded best-effort debit described in `quota_service`.\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Update\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-thread-summary`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Check summary trigger (msg count > 20 OR tokens > budget OR every 15 user turns)\n+    CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    Note over CS: Background worker executes summary task asynchronously\n+    CS->>DB: Load current thread_summary + old messages batch (10-20 msgs)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses (update summary prompt)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API\n+    OAI-->>OG: Updated summary\n+    OG-->>CS: Updated summary\n+    CS->>DB: Save new thread_summary\n+    CS->>DB: Mark summarized messages as compressed\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Thread summary is updated asynchronously after a chat turn when trigger conditions are met. Summary generation is a background task and MUST be attributed as `requester_type=system` so that its usage is not charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+Summary quality gate (P0): the system MUST detect and mitigate obviously-bad summaries without ML-based evaluation.\n+\n+- After generating a summary, `chat_service` MUST validate the candidate summary text.\n+- If summary length < `X` OR entropy < `Y`, `chat_service` MUST attempt regeneration.\n+- If regeneration fails quality checks or the provider call fails, `chat_service` MUST fall back by keeping the previous summary unchanged and MUST NOT mark the message batch as compressed.\n+\n+`X` and `Y` are configurable thresholds. Entropy is a deterministic proxy computed as normalized token entropy over whitespace-delimited tokens:\n+\n+`H_norm = (-sum(p_i * log2(p_i))) / log2(N)` where `p_i` is the empirical frequency of token `i` and `N` is the number of distinct tokens.\n+\n+Observability:\n+\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}` for each regeneration attempt (`reason` from a bounded allowlist such as `too_short|low_entropy|provider_error|invalid_format`).\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total` when the fallback behavior above is used.\n+\n+### 3.7 Database Schemas & Tables\n+\n+#### Table: chats\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chats`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Chat identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | Owning user |\n+| title | VARCHAR(255) | Chat title (user-set or auto-generated) |\n+| is_temporary | BOOLEAN | If true, auto-deleted after 24h |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last activity time |\n+| deleted_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Soft delete timestamp (nullable) |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `created_at`\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, updated_at DESC)` for listing chats\n+\n+#### Table: messages\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-messages`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Message identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key (nullable). Used for completed replay and traceability. Running state is tracked in `chat_turns`. |\n+| role | VARCHAR(16) | `user`, `assistant`, or `system` |\n+| content | TEXT | Message content |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| request_kind | VARCHAR(16) | `chat`, `summary`, or `doc_summary` (nullable) |\n+| features_used | JSONB | Feature flags and counters (nullable) |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Actual input tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Actual output tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| model | VARCHAR(64) | Actual model used for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| is_compressed | BOOLEAN | True if included in a thread summary |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `chat_id`, `role`, `content`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE. UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)` WHERE `request_id IS NOT NULL`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(chat_id, created_at)` for loading recent messages\n+\n+#### Table: chat_turns\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chat-turns`\n+\n+Tracks idempotency and in-progress generation state for `request_id`. This avoids ambiguous interpretation of `messages.request_id` when a generation is still running.\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Turn identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key |\n+| requester_type | VARCHAR(16) | `user` or `system` |\n+| requester_user_id | UUID | User ID when requester_type=`user` (nullable for system) |\n+| state | VARCHAR(16) | `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier (nullable until request starts) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID (nullable) |\n+| assistant_message_id | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID (nullable until completed) |\n+| error_code | VARCHAR(64) | Terminal error code (nullable) |\n+| started_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Start time |\n+| completed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Completion time (nullable) |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+A `chat_turns` row MUST be created before starting the outbound provider request; initial state is `running`.\n+\n+State machine:\n+- Allowed transitions: `running` -> `completed` \\| `failed` \\| `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states MUST be immutable\n+- At most one `running` turn per `(chat_id, request_id)`\n+\n+#### Table: attachments\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-attachments`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Attachment identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| filename | VARCHAR(255) | Original filename |\n+| content_type | VARCHAR(128) | MIME type |\n+| size_bytes | BIGINT | File size |\n+| provider_file_id | VARCHAR(128) | LLM provider file ID - OpenAI `file-*` or Azure OpenAI `assistant-*` (nullable until upload completes) |\n+| status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `ready`, `failed` |\n+| doc_summary | TEXT | LLM-generated document summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_model | VARCHAR(64) | Model used to generate the summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When the summary was last generated (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `in_progress`, `done`, `failed` (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_attempts | INTEGER | Cleanup retry attempts (default 0) |\n+| last_cleanup_error | TEXT | Last cleanup error (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When cleanup state was last updated (nullable) |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Upload time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `chat_id`, `filename`, `status`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: thread_summaries\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-thread-summaries`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Summary identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id, UNIQUE) |\n+| summary_text | TEXT | Compressed conversation summary |\n+| summarized_up_to | UUID | Last message ID included in this summary |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count of summary |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `chat_id`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: tenant_vector_stores\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-vector-stores`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant identifier |\n+| vector_store_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider vector store ID (OpenAI `vs_*` or Azure OpenAI equivalent) |\n+| provider | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier: `openai` or `azure_openai` |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `tenant_id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `vector_store_id`, `provider`, `created_at`. One vector store per tenant.\n+\n+Creation protocol (P0): `chat_service` uses a get-or-create flow with database uniqueness as the race arbiter.\n+\n+1. Attempt to read the row by `tenant_id`.\n+2. If not present, create the vector store via OAGW and attempt INSERT.\n+3. If INSERT fails due to unique violation (concurrent creator), re-read the row and use the existing `vector_store_id`. Best-effort delete the newly created provider vector store to avoid orphans.\n+\n+ **P0**: run a periodic reconcile/orphan reaper job (for example nightly) to reconcile provider state with `tenant_vector_stores`:\n+ - If a provider vector store exists but is not referenced in DB -> delete it.\n+ - If a DB row exists but the provider vector store is missing -> recreate the vector store and update the DB row.\n+\n+#### Table: quota_usage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-quota-usage`\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-quota-usage-accounting`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Record identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | User |\n+| period_type | VARCHAR(16) | `daily` or `monthly` |\n+| period_start | DATE | Start of the period |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Total input tokens consumed |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Total output tokens consumed |\n+| file_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of file search tool calls |\n+| web_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of web search calls (P1+) |\n+| rag_retrieval_calls | INTEGER | Number of internal RAG retrieval calls (P1+) |\n+| premium_model_calls | INTEGER | Calls to premium models |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+Commit semantics: quota updates MUST be atomic per period record. Implementations SHOULD use a transaction with row locking or a single UPDATE statement to avoid race conditions under parallel streams.\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` for quota lookups\n+\n+#### Projection Table: tenant_closure\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-closure-ref`\n+\n+Mini Chat does NOT require the `tenant_closure` local projection table for chat content access in P0. Chat content is owner-only and requires exact `owner_tenant_id` + `user_id` predicates.\n+\n+The `tenant_closure` projection table exists in the platform authorization model for modules that use hierarchical tenant scoping, but it is unused for Mini Chat content operations.\n+\n+Schema is defined in the [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#table-schemas-local-projections).\n+\n+**P2+ note**: When chat sharing (projects) is introduced, `resource_group_membership` and optionally `resource_group_closure` tables will also be required.\n+\n+### 3.8 Authorization (PEP)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-authz-pep`\n+\n+Mini Chat acts as a Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) per the platform's PDP/PEP authorization model defined in [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). The `chat_service` builds evaluation requests, sends them to the AuthZ Resolver (PDP), and compiles returned constraints into SQL WHERE clauses.\n+\n+Policy (P0): chat content is owner-only. For all content operations, authorization MUST enforce:\n+\n+- `owner_tenant_id == subject.tenant_id`\n+- `user_id == subject.id`\n+\n+Mini Chat does not use tenant hierarchy or subtree-based authorization for content operations.\n+\n+#### Resource Type\n+\n+The authorized resource is **Chat**. Sub-resources (Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary) do not have independent authorization - they are accessed through their parent chat, and the chat's authorization decision covers all child operations.\n+\n+| Attribute | Value |\n+|-----------|-------|\n+| GTS Type ID | `gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~` |\n+| Primary table | `chats` |\n+| Authorization granularity | Chat-level (sub-resources inherit) |\n+\n+#### PEP Configuration\n+\n+**Capabilities** (declared in `context.capabilities`):\n+\n+| Capability | P0 | P2+ | Rationale |\n+|------------|-----|------|-----------|\n+| `tenant_hierarchy` | No | No | Chat content is owner-only; `tenant_closure` is unused for Mini Chat content operations |\n+| `group_membership` | No | Yes | Needed when chat sharing via projects is introduced |\n+| `group_hierarchy` | No | Maybe | Needed if projects have nested hierarchy |\n+\n+**Supported properties** (declared in `context.supported_properties`):\n+\n+| Resource Property | SQL Column | Description |\n+|-------------------|------------|-------------|\n+| `owner_tenant_id` | `chats.tenant_id` | Owning tenant |\n+| `user_id` | `chats.user_id` | Owning user |\n+| `id` | `chats.id` | Chat identifier |\n+\n+#### Per-Operation Authorization Matrix\n+\n+| Endpoint | Action | `resource.id` | `require_constraints` | Expected P0 Predicates |\n+|----------|--------|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n+| `POST /v1/chats` | `create` | absent | `false` | decision only (no constraints) |\n+| `GET /v1/chats` | `list` | absent | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}` | `read` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `DELETE /v1/chats/{id}` | `delete` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | `update` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | `send_message` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | `upload` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | `read_turn` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+\n+**Notes**:\n+- `send_message` and `upload` are actions on the Chat resource, not on Message or Attachment resources. The `resource.id` is the chat's ID.\n+- For streaming (`send_message`), authorization is evaluated once at SSE connection establishment. The entire streaming session operates under the initial authorization decision. No per-message re-authorization.\n+- For `create`, the PEP passes `resource.properties.owner_tenant_id` and `resource.properties.user_id` from the SecurityContext. The PDP validates permission without returning constraints.\n+\n+#### Evaluation Request/Response Examples\n+\n+**Example 1: List Chats** (`GET /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"list\" },\n+  \"resource\": { \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\" },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response (P0 - user-owned chats only):\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+ORDER BY updated_at DESC\n+```\n+\n+**Example 2: Get Chat** (`GET /v1/chats/{id}`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"read\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"chat-456\"\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE id = 'chat-456'\n+  AND tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+```\n+\n+Result: 1 row -> return chat; 0 rows -> 404 Not Found (hides existence from unauthorized users).\n+\n+**Example 3: Create Chat** (`POST /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"create\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"properties\": {\n+      \"owner_tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\",\n+      \"user_id\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+    }\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": false,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{ \"decision\": true }\n+```\n+\n+PEP proceeds with INSERT. No constraints needed for create.\n+\n+**Example 4: Send Message** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`)\n+\n+Same authorization flow as Example 2 (Get Chat), but with `\"action\": { \"name\": \"send_message\" }` and `\"resource.id\"` set to the chat ID. Authorization is evaluated once before the SSE stream is established. The constraints are applied to the query that loads the chat and its messages from `chat_store`.\n+\n+#### Fail-Closed Behavior\n+\n+Mini Chat follows the platform's fail-closed rules (see [Authorization Design - Fail-Closed Rules](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#fail-closed-rules)):\n+\n+| Condition | PEP Action |\n+|-----------|------------|\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` present | 404 Not Found (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` absent | 403 Forbidden (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| PDP unreachable / timeout | 403 Forbidden (fail-closed) |\n+| `decision: true` + no constraints + `require_constraints: true` | 403 Forbidden |\n+| Unknown predicate type in constraints | Treat constraint as false; if all constraints false -> 403 |\n+| Unknown `resource_property` in predicate | Treat constraint as false; log error (PDP contract violation) |\n+| Empty `constraints: []` | 403 Forbidden |\n+\n+#### Token Scopes\n+\n+Mini Chat recognizes the following token scopes for third-party application narrowing:\n+\n+| Scope | Permits |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| `ai:chat` | All chat operations (umbrella scope) |\n+| `ai:chat:read` | `list`, `read` actions only |\n+| `ai:chat:write` | `create`, `update`, `delete`, `send_message`, `upload` actions |\n+\n+First-party applications (UI) use `token_scopes: [\"*\"]`. Third-party integrations receive narrowed scopes. Scope enforcement is handled by the PDP - the PEP includes `token_scopes` in the evaluation request context.\n+\n+#### P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\n+\n+When Projects / chat sharing is introduced (P2+), the authorization model extends naturally:\n+\n+1. Add `group_membership` capability (and optionally `group_hierarchy`).\n+2. Maintain `resource_group_membership` projection table mapping chat IDs to project group IDs.\n+3. The PDP returns additional access paths via OR'd constraints - e.g., one constraint for owned chats (`user_id` predicate), another for shared-via-project chats (`in_group` predicate).\n+4. `supported_properties` remains unchanged (the `id` property is used for group membership joins).\n+\n+No changes to the PEP flow or constraint compilation logic are needed. The PDP's response structure naturally handles multiple access paths through OR'd constraints.\n+\n+## 4. Additional Context\n+\n+### P0 Scope Boundaries\n+\n+**Included in P0**:\n+- Single-tenant vector store per tenant (not per workspace)\n+- Thread summary as only compression mechanism\n+- On-upload document summary via File Search (Variant 1 from draft)\n+- Quota enforcement: daily + monthly per user\n+- Temporary chats with 24h scheduled cleanup\n+- File Search per-message call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2 tool calls per message)\n+\n+**Deferred to P2+**:\n+- Projects / chat sharing\n+- Full-text search across chats\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are both supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies (beyond simple limits)\n+- Per-workspace vector stores\n+\n+### Data Classification and Retention (P0)\n+\n+Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data. Mini Chat treats messages and summaries as customer content and applies data minimization and retention controls.\n+\n+**Data classes**:\n+- Chat content: `messages.content`, `thread_summaries.summary_text`, `attachments.doc_summary`\n+- Operational metadata: IDs, timestamps, provider correlation IDs, quotas\n+- Audit events: structured records emitted to platform `audit_service`\n+\n+**Retention**:\n+- Temporary chats are scheduled for cleanup after 24h and MUST be deleted within 25h (PRD SLA).\n+- Non-temporary chats are retained until explicit deletion by default, subject to operator-configured retention policies.\n+- Soft-deleted chats (`deleted_at` set) are hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+**Audit content handling (P0)**:\n+- Audit events include the minimal content required for security and incident response.\n+- `chat_service` MUST redact secret patterns (tokens, keys, credentials) before sending content to `audit_service`.\n+- Redaction MUST be testable and based on a bounded allowlist of rule classes.\n+\n+| Rule class | Example (non-exhaustive) | Action |\n+|-----------|---------------------------|--------|\n+| `bearer_token` | `Authorization: Bearer eyJ...` | Replace token with `Bearer [REDACTED]` |\n+| `jwt` | `eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJS...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_JWT]` |\n+| `openai_api_key` | `sk-...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]` |\n+| `aws_access_key_id` | `AKIA...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]` |\n+| `azure_api_key` | `api-key: ...`, `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: ...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]` |\n+| `api_key_fields` | `api_key=...`, `x-api-key=...`, `client_secret=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `password_fields` | `password=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `pem_private_key` | `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` | Replace block with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]` |\n+| `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+- Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.\n+- Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a maximum of 8 KiB per field (append `…[TRUNCATED]`).\n+- Full DLP-based content redaction is deferred to P2.\n+\n+### Context Plan Assembly Rules\n+\n+On each user message, `chat_service` assembles a `ContextPlan` in this order:\n+\n+1. **System prompt** - fixed instructions for the assistant. The system prompt is configuration and is not persisted as a `messages` row. For debugging, the prompt version SHOULD be recorded on the assistant message (for example in `features_used` or provider metadata).\n+2. **Thread summary** - if exists, replaces older history\n+3. **Document summaries** - short descriptions of attached documents\n+4. **Recent messages** - last 6-10 messages not covered by summary\n+5. **User message** - current turn\n+\n+If the total exceeds the input token budget, truncation happens in reverse priority: old messages first, then doc summaries, then retrieval excerpts. Summary and system prompt are never truncated.\n+\n+### File Search Trigger Heuristics\n+\n+File Search is invoked when:\n+- User explicitly references documents (\"in the file\", \"in section\", \"according to the document\")\n+- Documents are attached and the query likely relates to them\n+- User requests citations or sources\n+\n+Limits: per-message file_search tool call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2); max calls per day/user tracked in `quota_usage`.\n+\n+### Three-Tier Rate Limiting & Throttling\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-throttling-tiers`\n+\n+Rate limiting and quota enforcement are split into three tiers with strict ownership boundaries. These tiers MUST NOT be mixed - each has a single owner and distinct responsibility.\n+\n+| Tier | Owner | What It Controls | Examples                                                                                              |\n+|------|-------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Product quota** | `quota_service` (in `chat_service`) | Per-user daily/monthly token budgets, premium/standard model allocation, file_search call limits, auto-downgrade | \"Premium calls exhausted -> downgrade to gpt-5-mini\"; \"Daily limit reached -> 429 `quota_exceeded`\" |\n+| **Platform rate limit** | `api_gateway` middleware | Per-user/per-IP request rate, concurrent stream caps, abuse protection | \"20 rps per user\"; \"Max 5 concurrent SSE streams\"                                                     |\n+| **Provider rate limit** | OAGW | Provider 429 handling, `Retry-After` respect, circuit breaker, global concurrency cap | \"OpenAI 429 -> wait `Retry-After` -> retry once -> propagate 429 upstream\"                            |\n+\n+**Key rules**:\n+- Product quota decisions happen BEFORE the request reaches OAGW. If quota is exhausted, the request never leaves `chat_service`.\n+- OAGW does NOT know about tenants, users, licenses, or premium status. It handles only provider-level concerns (retry, circuit breaker, concurrency cap).\n+- Provider 429 from OAGW is propagated to `chat_service`, which maps it to `rate_limited` (429) for the client with a meaningful error message.\n+- Mid-stream quota abort is NOT supported - quota is checked at preflight only. Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+**Quota counting flow**:\n+\n+```\n+Preflight (reserve) (before LLM call):\n+  estimate = tokens(ContextPlan) + max_output_tokens\n+  if estimate > remaining_quota -> downgrade model or reject\n+\n+Commit (after done event):\n+  actual = response.usage.input_tokens + response.usage.output_tokens\n+  quota_usage += actual\n+  (if actual > estimate -> debit overshoot, never cancel completed response)\n+```\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add language tag to quota counting flow pseudocode.**\n\nThe fenced code block at line 1267 showing the quota counting flow should use a language tag (`text`) to satisfy MD040.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n Preflight (reserve) (before LLM call):\n   estimate = tokens(ContextPlan) + max_output_tokens\n   if estimate > remaining_quota -> downgrade model or reject\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 markdownlint-cli2 (0.20.0)</summary>\n\n[warning] 1267-1267: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md` around lines 1267 - 1277, Add a language\ntag \"text\" to the fenced code block that shows the quota counting flow\npseudocode (the block starting with \"Preflight (reserve) (before LLM call):\") so\nthe fence becomes ```text and satisfies MD040; keep the pseudocode content\nunchanged and only add the language identifier to the opening fence.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @MikeFalcon77\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:23Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:20Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572932","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572932"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572932"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572932/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1267,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1277,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1277,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572940","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572940,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHUM","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```\n+ping*  (delta | tool | citations)*  (done | error)\n+```\n+\n+- Zero or more `ping` events may appear at any point.\n+- `delta`, `tool`, and `citations` events may interleave in any order.\n+- Exactly one terminal event (`done` or `error`) ends the stream.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-event-translation\"></a>\n+#### Provider Event Translation\n+\n+Provider-specific streaming events are internal to `llm_provider` and `chat_service`. They are never forwarded to clients. The translation layer maps provider events to the stable SSE protocol defined above.\n+\n+| Provider Event | Stable SSE Event | Notes |\n+|----------------|-----------------|-------|\n+| `response.output_text.delta` | `event: delta` (`type: \"text\"`) | Text content mapped 1:1. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.searching` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"start\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | Emitted when file_search tool is invoked. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.completed` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"done\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | `details` populated from search results metadata. |\n+| File search annotations in response | `event: citations` | Extracted from provider annotations, mapped to `items[]` schema. When provider annotations include ranges, `items[].span` SHOULD be populated as character offsets into the final assistant text. |\n+| `response.completed` | `event: done` | `usage` from `response.usage`; `provider.response_id` from `response.id`. |\n+| Provider HTTP error / disconnect | `event: error` (`code: \"provider_error\"` or `\"provider_timeout\"`) | Error details sanitized; provider internals not exposed. |\n+| Provider 429 | `event: error` (`code: \"rate_limited\"`) | After OAGW retry exhaustion. |\n+\n+This mapping is intentionally provider-agnostic in the stable contract. If the provider changes its event format or a new provider is added, only the translation layer in `llm_provider` is updated. The client contract remains unchanged.\n+\n+**Error Codes**:\n+\n+| Code | HTTP Status | Description |\n+|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `feature_not_licensed` | 403 | Tenant lacks `ai_chat` feature |\n+| `insufficient_permissions` | 403 | Subject lacks permission for the requested action (AuthZ Resolver denied) |\n+| `chat_not_found` | 404 | Chat does not exist or not accessible under current authorization constraints |\n+| `quota_exceeded` | 429 | User exceeded daily/monthly usage limit |\n+| `rate_limited` | 429 | Too many requests in time window |\n+| `file_too_large` | 413 | Uploaded file exceeds size limit |\n+| `unsupported_file_type` | 415 | File type not supported for upload |\n+| `provider_error` | 502 | LLM provider returned an error |\n+| `provider_timeout` | 504 | LLM provider request timed out |\n+\n+### 3.4 Internal Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency Module | Interface Used | Purpose |\n+|-------------------|----------------|---------|\n+| api_gateway (platform) | Axum router / middleware | HTTP request handling, SSE transport |\n+| authn (platform) | Middleware (JWT/opaque token) | Extract `user_id` + `tenant_id` from request |\n+| license_manager (platform) | Middleware | Check tenant has `ai_chat` feature; reject with 403 if not |\n+| authz_resolver (platform) | Access evaluation API (`/access/v1/evaluation`) | Obtain authorization decisions + SQL-compilable constraints for chat operations |\n+| audit_service (platform) | Event emitter | Receive structured audit events (prompts, responses, usage, policy decisions) |\n+| outbound_gateway (platform) | Internal HTTP | Egress to LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) with credential injection |\n+\n+**Dependency Rules**:\n+- `chat_service` never calls the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) directly; all external calls go through OAGW\n+- `SecurityContext` (user_id, tenant_id) propagated through all in-process calls\n+- `license_manager` runs as middleware before `chat_service` is invoked\n+- `chat_service` calls `authz_resolver` before every database query; on PDP denial or PDP unreachable, fail-closed (deny access)\n+- `chat_service` emits audit events to `audit_service` after each turn; mini-chat does not store audit data locally\n+\n+### 3.5 External Dependencies\n+\n+#### LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\n+\n+Both providers expose a compatible API surface. OAGW routes requests to the configured provider and injects credentials accordingly.\n+\n+| API | Purpose | OAGW Route | OpenAI Endpoint | Azure OpenAI Endpoint |\n+|-----|---------|------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| Responses API (streaming) | Chat completion with tool support | `POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Responses API (non-streaming) | Thread summary generation, doc summary | `POST /outbound/llm/responses` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Files API | Upload user documents | `POST /outbound/llm/files` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/files` |\n+| Vector Stores API | Manage per-tenant vector stores, add/remove files | `POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/vector_stores/*` |\n+| File Search (tool) | Retrieve document excerpts during chat | - (invoked as tool within Responses API call) | `file_search` tool | `file_search` tool (identical contract) |\n+\n+Note: Azure OpenAI path variants differ by rollout; OAGW owns the exact path mapping for each API.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-api-mapping\"></a>\n+**Provider API Mapping** - authentication and endpoint differences:\n+\n+| Aspect | OpenAI | Azure OpenAI |\n+|--------|--------|--------------|\n+| **Base URL** | `https://api.openai.com/v1` | `https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1` |\n+| **Authentication** | `Authorization: Bearer {api_key}` | `api-key: {key}` header or Entra ID bearer token |\n+| **API version** | Not required | Azure may require an `api-version` query parameter depending on feature/rollout; OAGW owns this provider-specific detail |\n+| **File upload `purpose`** | `assistants` (P0) | `assistants` only (`user_data` not supported) |\n+| **Vector stores per `file_search`** | Multiple | **One** (sufficient for P0: one store per tenant) |\n+| **SSE format** | `event:` + `data:` lines, structured events | Identical format |\n+| **`user` field** | Supported | Supported (feeds into Azure abuse monitoring) |\n+| **`metadata` object** | Supported | Supported |\n+\n+OAGW MUST inject the required `api-version` query parameter when calling Azure OpenAI endpoints.\n+\n+**OAGW throttling scope**: OAGW handles provider-side rate limiting only - retry on provider 429 (with `Retry-After` respect, max 1 retry), circuit breaker when provider error rate exceeds threshold, and global concurrency cap as an SRE safety valve. Product-level quota enforcement (per-user, per-tenant, model downgrade) is NOT an OAGW concern - it is handled entirely by `chat_service` / `quota_service` before any outbound call (see constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+#### PostgreSQL\n+\n+| Usage | Purpose |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| Primary datastore | Chats, messages, attachments, summaries, quota counters, tenant vector store mappings |\n+\n+### 3.6 Interactions & Sequences\n+\n+#### Send Message with Streaming Response\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-send-message`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant AuthZ as authz_resolver (PDP)\n+    participant CS as chat_service (PEP)\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream\n+    AG->>AG: AuthN + license_manager\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(chat_id, user_msg, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>AuthZ: Evaluate(subject, action: \"send_message\", resource: {type: chat, id: chat_id})\n+    AuthZ-->>CS: decision + constraints\n+\n+    alt PDP unreachable / timeout\n+        CS-->>AG: 403 Forbidden\n+        AG-->>UI: 403\n+    else decision = false\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Load chat, recent messages, thread_summary, attachments, tenant vector_store_id (with constraints in WHERE)\n+\n+    alt 0 rows returned\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Build ContextPlan (system prompt + summary + doc summaries + recent msgs + user msg)\n+    CS->>CS: Preflight (reserve) quota check (estimate tokens from ContextPlan + max_output -> allow / downgrade / reject)\n+\n+    alt quota exhausted\n+        CS-->>AG: 429 quota_exceeded\n+        AG-->>UI: SSE error event\n+    end\n+\n+    Note over CS, OAI: Single provider call per user turn. file_search is enabled as a tool within the same streaming Responses API request.\n+\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream (tools include file_search, store=tenant_store)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API (streaming, tool calling enabled)\n+    OAI-->>OG: SSE tokens\n+    OG-->>CS: Token stream\n+    CS-->>AG: Token stream\n+    AG-->>UI: SSE tokens\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Persist user msg + assistant msg + usage\n+    CS->>CS: Commit actual usage to quota_service (debit input_tokens + output_tokens)\n+\n+    participant AS as audit_service\n+    CS->>AS: Emit audit event (usage, policy decisions)\n+\n+    opt Thread summary update triggered (background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Full lifecycle of a user message - from authorization through streaming LLM response to persistence and optional thread compression. Authorization is evaluated before any database access. The PEP sends an evaluation request to the AuthZ Resolver with the chat's resource type and ID; the returned constraints are applied to the DB query's WHERE clause. If the PDP is unreachable, the request is rejected immediately (fail-closed, 403). If the PDP denies access for an operation with a concrete `chat_id`, the PEP returns 404 to avoid disclosing resource existence. If the constrained query returns 0 rows, the PEP returns 404.\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-file-upload`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments (multipart)\n+    AG->>CS: UploadAttachment(chat_id, file, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>DB: Insert attachment metadata (status: pending)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/files (upload)\n+    OG->>OAI: Files API upload\n+    OAI-->>OG: file_id\n+    OG-->>CS: file_id\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/{tenant_store}/files (add file)\n+    OG->>OAI: Add file to vector store\n+    OAI-->>OG: OK\n+    OG-->>CS: OK\n+    CS->>DB: Update attachment (status: ready, provider_file_id)\n+\n+    opt Generate doc summary (Variant 1, background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue doc summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+\n+    CS-->>AG: attachment_id, status\n+    AG-->>UI: 201 Created\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: File upload flow - the file is uploaded to the LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) via OAGW, added to the tenant's vector store, optionally summarized, and metadata is persisted locally.\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-cancellation`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant LP as llm_provider\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: SSE connection established\n+    AG->>AG: Create CancellationToken\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(..., cancel_token)\n+    CS->>LP: stream_response(..., cancel_token)\n+    LP->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream\n+    OG->>OAI: Streaming in progress\n+\n+    alt UI disconnect\n+        UI--xAG: Client disconnects / stop button\n+        AG->>AG: Detect broken SSE write -> cancel token\n+    else Server timeout\n+        CS->>CS: Timeout elapsed -> cancel token\n+    end\n+\n+    LP->>LP: Token cancelled -> abort HTTP request\n+    LP->>OG: Close connection (hard cancel)\n+    OG->>OAI: TCP RST / connection closed\n+    CS->>CS: Persist partial response (usage may be unknown)\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Cancellation propagates end-to-end via a shared `CancellationToken`. When triggered, `llm_provider` performs a hard cancel - aborting the outbound HTTP connection so the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) stops generating immediately. The partial response is persisted. Because provider usage is typically only delivered on a completed response, `input_tokens`/`output_tokens` may be NULL or approximate for cancelled turns; quota enforcement uses the bounded best-effort debit described in `quota_service`.\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Update\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-thread-summary`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Check summary trigger (msg count > 20 OR tokens > budget OR every 15 user turns)\n+    CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    Note over CS: Background worker executes summary task asynchronously\n+    CS->>DB: Load current thread_summary + old messages batch (10-20 msgs)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses (update summary prompt)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API\n+    OAI-->>OG: Updated summary\n+    OG-->>CS: Updated summary\n+    CS->>DB: Save new thread_summary\n+    CS->>DB: Mark summarized messages as compressed\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Thread summary is updated asynchronously after a chat turn when trigger conditions are met. Summary generation is a background task and MUST be attributed as `requester_type=system` so that its usage is not charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+Summary quality gate (P0): the system MUST detect and mitigate obviously-bad summaries without ML-based evaluation.\n+\n+- After generating a summary, `chat_service` MUST validate the candidate summary text.\n+- If summary length < `X` OR entropy < `Y`, `chat_service` MUST attempt regeneration.\n+- If regeneration fails quality checks or the provider call fails, `chat_service` MUST fall back by keeping the previous summary unchanged and MUST NOT mark the message batch as compressed.\n+\n+`X` and `Y` are configurable thresholds. Entropy is a deterministic proxy computed as normalized token entropy over whitespace-delimited tokens:\n+\n+`H_norm = (-sum(p_i * log2(p_i))) / log2(N)` where `p_i` is the empirical frequency of token `i` and `N` is the number of distinct tokens.\n+\n+Observability:\n+\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}` for each regeneration attempt (`reason` from a bounded allowlist such as `too_short|low_entropy|provider_error|invalid_format`).\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total` when the fallback behavior above is used.\n+\n+### 3.7 Database Schemas & Tables\n+\n+#### Table: chats\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chats`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Chat identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | Owning user |\n+| title | VARCHAR(255) | Chat title (user-set or auto-generated) |\n+| is_temporary | BOOLEAN | If true, auto-deleted after 24h |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last activity time |\n+| deleted_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Soft delete timestamp (nullable) |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `created_at`\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, updated_at DESC)` for listing chats\n+\n+#### Table: messages\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-messages`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Message identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key (nullable). Used for completed replay and traceability. Running state is tracked in `chat_turns`. |\n+| role | VARCHAR(16) | `user`, `assistant`, or `system` |\n+| content | TEXT | Message content |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| request_kind | VARCHAR(16) | `chat`, `summary`, or `doc_summary` (nullable) |\n+| features_used | JSONB | Feature flags and counters (nullable) |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Actual input tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Actual output tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| model | VARCHAR(64) | Actual model used for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| is_compressed | BOOLEAN | True if included in a thread summary |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `chat_id`, `role`, `content`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE. UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)` WHERE `request_id IS NOT NULL`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(chat_id, created_at)` for loading recent messages\n+\n+#### Table: chat_turns\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chat-turns`\n+\n+Tracks idempotency and in-progress generation state for `request_id`. This avoids ambiguous interpretation of `messages.request_id` when a generation is still running.\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Turn identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key |\n+| requester_type | VARCHAR(16) | `user` or `system` |\n+| requester_user_id | UUID | User ID when requester_type=`user` (nullable for system) |\n+| state | VARCHAR(16) | `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier (nullable until request starts) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID (nullable) |\n+| assistant_message_id | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID (nullable until completed) |\n+| error_code | VARCHAR(64) | Terminal error code (nullable) |\n+| started_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Start time |\n+| completed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Completion time (nullable) |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+A `chat_turns` row MUST be created before starting the outbound provider request; initial state is `running`.\n+\n+State machine:\n+- Allowed transitions: `running` -> `completed` \\| `failed` \\| `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states MUST be immutable\n+- At most one `running` turn per `(chat_id, request_id)`\n+\n+#### Table: attachments\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-attachments`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Attachment identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| filename | VARCHAR(255) | Original filename |\n+| content_type | VARCHAR(128) | MIME type |\n+| size_bytes | BIGINT | File size |\n+| provider_file_id | VARCHAR(128) | LLM provider file ID - OpenAI `file-*` or Azure OpenAI `assistant-*` (nullable until upload completes) |\n+| status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `ready`, `failed` |\n+| doc_summary | TEXT | LLM-generated document summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_model | VARCHAR(64) | Model used to generate the summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When the summary was last generated (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `in_progress`, `done`, `failed` (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_attempts | INTEGER | Cleanup retry attempts (default 0) |\n+| last_cleanup_error | TEXT | Last cleanup error (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When cleanup state was last updated (nullable) |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Upload time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `chat_id`, `filename`, `status`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: thread_summaries\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-thread-summaries`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Summary identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id, UNIQUE) |\n+| summary_text | TEXT | Compressed conversation summary |\n+| summarized_up_to | UUID | Last message ID included in this summary |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count of summary |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `chat_id`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: tenant_vector_stores\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-vector-stores`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant identifier |\n+| vector_store_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider vector store ID (OpenAI `vs_*` or Azure OpenAI equivalent) |\n+| provider | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier: `openai` or `azure_openai` |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `tenant_id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `vector_store_id`, `provider`, `created_at`. One vector store per tenant.\n+\n+Creation protocol (P0): `chat_service` uses a get-or-create flow with database uniqueness as the race arbiter.\n+\n+1. Attempt to read the row by `tenant_id`.\n+2. If not present, create the vector store via OAGW and attempt INSERT.\n+3. If INSERT fails due to unique violation (concurrent creator), re-read the row and use the existing `vector_store_id`. Best-effort delete the newly created provider vector store to avoid orphans.\n+\n+ **P0**: run a periodic reconcile/orphan reaper job (for example nightly) to reconcile provider state with `tenant_vector_stores`:\n+ - If a provider vector store exists but is not referenced in DB -> delete it.\n+ - If a DB row exists but the provider vector store is missing -> recreate the vector store and update the DB row.\n+\n+#### Table: quota_usage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-quota-usage`\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-quota-usage-accounting`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Record identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | User |\n+| period_type | VARCHAR(16) | `daily` or `monthly` |\n+| period_start | DATE | Start of the period |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Total input tokens consumed |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Total output tokens consumed |\n+| file_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of file search tool calls |\n+| web_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of web search calls (P1+) |\n+| rag_retrieval_calls | INTEGER | Number of internal RAG retrieval calls (P1+) |\n+| premium_model_calls | INTEGER | Calls to premium models |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+Commit semantics: quota updates MUST be atomic per period record. Implementations SHOULD use a transaction with row locking or a single UPDATE statement to avoid race conditions under parallel streams.\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` for quota lookups\n+\n+#### Projection Table: tenant_closure\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-closure-ref`\n+\n+Mini Chat does NOT require the `tenant_closure` local projection table for chat content access in P0. Chat content is owner-only and requires exact `owner_tenant_id` + `user_id` predicates.\n+\n+The `tenant_closure` projection table exists in the platform authorization model for modules that use hierarchical tenant scoping, but it is unused for Mini Chat content operations.\n+\n+Schema is defined in the [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#table-schemas-local-projections).\n+\n+**P2+ note**: When chat sharing (projects) is introduced, `resource_group_membership` and optionally `resource_group_closure` tables will also be required.\n+\n+### 3.8 Authorization (PEP)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-authz-pep`\n+\n+Mini Chat acts as a Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) per the platform's PDP/PEP authorization model defined in [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). The `chat_service` builds evaluation requests, sends them to the AuthZ Resolver (PDP), and compiles returned constraints into SQL WHERE clauses.\n+\n+Policy (P0): chat content is owner-only. For all content operations, authorization MUST enforce:\n+\n+- `owner_tenant_id == subject.tenant_id`\n+- `user_id == subject.id`\n+\n+Mini Chat does not use tenant hierarchy or subtree-based authorization for content operations.\n+\n+#### Resource Type\n+\n+The authorized resource is **Chat**. Sub-resources (Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary) do not have independent authorization - they are accessed through their parent chat, and the chat's authorization decision covers all child operations.\n+\n+| Attribute | Value |\n+|-----------|-------|\n+| GTS Type ID | `gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~` |\n+| Primary table | `chats` |\n+| Authorization granularity | Chat-level (sub-resources inherit) |\n+\n+#### PEP Configuration\n+\n+**Capabilities** (declared in `context.capabilities`):\n+\n+| Capability | P0 | P2+ | Rationale |\n+|------------|-----|------|-----------|\n+| `tenant_hierarchy` | No | No | Chat content is owner-only; `tenant_closure` is unused for Mini Chat content operations |\n+| `group_membership` | No | Yes | Needed when chat sharing via projects is introduced |\n+| `group_hierarchy` | No | Maybe | Needed if projects have nested hierarchy |\n+\n+**Supported properties** (declared in `context.supported_properties`):\n+\n+| Resource Property | SQL Column | Description |\n+|-------------------|------------|-------------|\n+| `owner_tenant_id` | `chats.tenant_id` | Owning tenant |\n+| `user_id` | `chats.user_id` | Owning user |\n+| `id` | `chats.id` | Chat identifier |\n+\n+#### Per-Operation Authorization Matrix\n+\n+| Endpoint | Action | `resource.id` | `require_constraints` | Expected P0 Predicates |\n+|----------|--------|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n+| `POST /v1/chats` | `create` | absent | `false` | decision only (no constraints) |\n+| `GET /v1/chats` | `list` | absent | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}` | `read` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `DELETE /v1/chats/{id}` | `delete` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | `update` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | `send_message` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | `upload` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | `read_turn` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+\n+**Notes**:\n+- `send_message` and `upload` are actions on the Chat resource, not on Message or Attachment resources. The `resource.id` is the chat's ID.\n+- For streaming (`send_message`), authorization is evaluated once at SSE connection establishment. The entire streaming session operates under the initial authorization decision. No per-message re-authorization.\n+- For `create`, the PEP passes `resource.properties.owner_tenant_id` and `resource.properties.user_id` from the SecurityContext. The PDP validates permission without returning constraints.\n+\n+#### Evaluation Request/Response Examples\n+\n+**Example 1: List Chats** (`GET /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"list\" },\n+  \"resource\": { \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\" },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response (P0 - user-owned chats only):\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+ORDER BY updated_at DESC\n+```\n+\n+**Example 2: Get Chat** (`GET /v1/chats/{id}`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"read\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"chat-456\"\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE id = 'chat-456'\n+  AND tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+```\n+\n+Result: 1 row -> return chat; 0 rows -> 404 Not Found (hides existence from unauthorized users).\n+\n+**Example 3: Create Chat** (`POST /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"create\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"properties\": {\n+      \"owner_tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\",\n+      \"user_id\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+    }\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": false,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{ \"decision\": true }\n+```\n+\n+PEP proceeds with INSERT. No constraints needed for create.\n+\n+**Example 4: Send Message** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`)\n+\n+Same authorization flow as Example 2 (Get Chat), but with `\"action\": { \"name\": \"send_message\" }` and `\"resource.id\"` set to the chat ID. Authorization is evaluated once before the SSE stream is established. The constraints are applied to the query that loads the chat and its messages from `chat_store`.\n+\n+#### Fail-Closed Behavior\n+\n+Mini Chat follows the platform's fail-closed rules (see [Authorization Design - Fail-Closed Rules](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#fail-closed-rules)):\n+\n+| Condition | PEP Action |\n+|-----------|------------|\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` present | 404 Not Found (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` absent | 403 Forbidden (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| PDP unreachable / timeout | 403 Forbidden (fail-closed) |\n+| `decision: true` + no constraints + `require_constraints: true` | 403 Forbidden |\n+| Unknown predicate type in constraints | Treat constraint as false; if all constraints false -> 403 |\n+| Unknown `resource_property` in predicate | Treat constraint as false; log error (PDP contract violation) |\n+| Empty `constraints: []` | 403 Forbidden |\n+\n+#### Token Scopes\n+\n+Mini Chat recognizes the following token scopes for third-party application narrowing:\n+\n+| Scope | Permits |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| `ai:chat` | All chat operations (umbrella scope) |\n+| `ai:chat:read` | `list`, `read` actions only |\n+| `ai:chat:write` | `create`, `update`, `delete`, `send_message`, `upload` actions |\n+\n+First-party applications (UI) use `token_scopes: [\"*\"]`. Third-party integrations receive narrowed scopes. Scope enforcement is handled by the PDP - the PEP includes `token_scopes` in the evaluation request context.\n+\n+#### P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\n+\n+When Projects / chat sharing is introduced (P2+), the authorization model extends naturally:\n+\n+1. Add `group_membership` capability (and optionally `group_hierarchy`).\n+2. Maintain `resource_group_membership` projection table mapping chat IDs to project group IDs.\n+3. The PDP returns additional access paths via OR'd constraints - e.g., one constraint for owned chats (`user_id` predicate), another for shared-via-project chats (`in_group` predicate).\n+4. `supported_properties` remains unchanged (the `id` property is used for group membership joins).\n+\n+No changes to the PEP flow or constraint compilation logic are needed. The PDP's response structure naturally handles multiple access paths through OR'd constraints.\n+\n+## 4. Additional Context\n+\n+### P0 Scope Boundaries\n+\n+**Included in P0**:\n+- Single-tenant vector store per tenant (not per workspace)\n+- Thread summary as only compression mechanism\n+- On-upload document summary via File Search (Variant 1 from draft)\n+- Quota enforcement: daily + monthly per user\n+- Temporary chats with 24h scheduled cleanup\n+- File Search per-message call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2 tool calls per message)\n+\n+**Deferred to P2+**:\n+- Projects / chat sharing\n+- Full-text search across chats\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are both supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies (beyond simple limits)\n+- Per-workspace vector stores\n+\n+### Data Classification and Retention (P0)\n+\n+Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data. Mini Chat treats messages and summaries as customer content and applies data minimization and retention controls.\n+\n+**Data classes**:\n+- Chat content: `messages.content`, `thread_summaries.summary_text`, `attachments.doc_summary`\n+- Operational metadata: IDs, timestamps, provider correlation IDs, quotas\n+- Audit events: structured records emitted to platform `audit_service`\n+\n+**Retention**:\n+- Temporary chats are scheduled for cleanup after 24h and MUST be deleted within 25h (PRD SLA).\n+- Non-temporary chats are retained until explicit deletion by default, subject to operator-configured retention policies.\n+- Soft-deleted chats (`deleted_at` set) are hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+**Audit content handling (P0)**:\n+- Audit events include the minimal content required for security and incident response.\n+- `chat_service` MUST redact secret patterns (tokens, keys, credentials) before sending content to `audit_service`.\n+- Redaction MUST be testable and based on a bounded allowlist of rule classes.\n+\n+| Rule class | Example (non-exhaustive) | Action |\n+|-----------|---------------------------|--------|\n+| `bearer_token` | `Authorization: Bearer eyJ...` | Replace token with `Bearer [REDACTED]` |\n+| `jwt` | `eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJS...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_JWT]` |\n+| `openai_api_key` | `sk-...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]` |\n+| `aws_access_key_id` | `AKIA...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]` |\n+| `azure_api_key` | `api-key: ...`, `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: ...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]` |\n+| `api_key_fields` | `api_key=...`, `x-api-key=...`, `client_secret=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `password_fields` | `password=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `pem_private_key` | `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` | Replace block with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]` |\n+| `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+- Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.\n+- Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a maximum of 8 KiB per field (append `…[TRUNCATED]`).\n+- Full DLP-based content redaction is deferred to P2.\n+\n+### Context Plan Assembly Rules\n+\n+On each user message, `chat_service` assembles a `ContextPlan` in this order:\n+\n+1. **System prompt** - fixed instructions for the assistant. The system prompt is configuration and is not persisted as a `messages` row. For debugging, the prompt version SHOULD be recorded on the assistant message (for example in `features_used` or provider metadata).\n+2. **Thread summary** - if exists, replaces older history\n+3. **Document summaries** - short descriptions of attached documents\n+4. **Recent messages** - last 6-10 messages not covered by summary\n+5. **User message** - current turn\n+\n+If the total exceeds the input token budget, truncation happens in reverse priority: old messages first, then doc summaries, then retrieval excerpts. Summary and system prompt are never truncated.\n+\n+### File Search Trigger Heuristics\n+\n+File Search is invoked when:\n+- User explicitly references documents (\"in the file\", \"in section\", \"according to the document\")\n+- Documents are attached and the query likely relates to them\n+- User requests citations or sources\n+\n+Limits: per-message file_search tool call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2); max calls per day/user tracked in `quota_usage`.\n+\n+### Three-Tier Rate Limiting & Throttling\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-throttling-tiers`\n+\n+Rate limiting and quota enforcement are split into three tiers with strict ownership boundaries. These tiers MUST NOT be mixed - each has a single owner and distinct responsibility.\n+\n+| Tier | Owner | What It Controls | Examples                                                                                              |\n+|------|-------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Product quota** | `quota_service` (in `chat_service`) | Per-user daily/monthly token budgets, premium/standard model allocation, file_search call limits, auto-downgrade | \"Premium calls exhausted -> downgrade to gpt-5-mini\"; \"Daily limit reached -> 429 `quota_exceeded`\" |\n+| **Platform rate limit** | `api_gateway` middleware | Per-user/per-IP request rate, concurrent stream caps, abuse protection | \"20 rps per user\"; \"Max 5 concurrent SSE streams\"                                                     |\n+| **Provider rate limit** | OAGW | Provider 429 handling, `Retry-After` respect, circuit breaker, global concurrency cap | \"OpenAI 429 -> wait `Retry-After` -> retry once -> propagate 429 upstream\"                            |\n+\n+**Key rules**:\n+- Product quota decisions happen BEFORE the request reaches OAGW. If quota is exhausted, the request never leaves `chat_service`.\n+- OAGW does NOT know about tenants, users, licenses, or premium status. It handles only provider-level concerns (retry, circuit breaker, concurrency cap).\n+- Provider 429 from OAGW is propagated to `chat_service`, which maps it to `rate_limited` (429) for the client with a meaningful error message.\n+- Mid-stream quota abort is NOT supported - quota is checked at preflight only. Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+**Quota counting flow**:\n+\n+```\n+Preflight (reserve) (before LLM call):\n+  estimate = tokens(ContextPlan) + max_output_tokens\n+  if estimate > remaining_quota -> downgrade model or reject\n+\n+Commit (after done event):\n+  actual = response.usage.input_tokens + response.usage.output_tokens\n+  quota_usage += actual\n+  (if actual > estimate -> debit overshoot, never cancel completed response)\n+```\n+\n+### Provider Request Metadata\n+\n+Every request sent to the LLM provider via `llm_provider` MUST include two identification mechanisms. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI support these fields with identical semantics.\n+\n+**`user` field** - composite tenant+user identifier for provider usage monitoring and abuse detection:\n+\n+```\n+\"user\": \"{tenant_id}:{user_id}\"\n+```","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add language tag to user field example.**\n\nThe fenced code block at line 1284 showing the provider `user` field format should use a language tag (`json` or `text`) to satisfy MD040.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```json\n \"user\": \"{tenant_id}:{user_id}\"\n ```\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 markdownlint-cli2 (0.20.0)</summary>\n\n[warning] 1284-1284: Fenced code blocks should have a language specified\n\n(MD040, fenced-code-language)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md` around lines 1284 - 1286, The fenced code\nblock showing the provider `user` field format currently lacks a language tag;\nupdate the fence for the snippet containing `\"user\": \"{tenant_id}:{user_id}\"` to\ninclude a language (e.g., ```json) so the block is tagged (satisfies MD040),\nleaving the content unchanged and keeping the quotes exactly as shown.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @MikeFalcon77\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:23Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:26Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572940","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572940"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572940"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572940/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1284,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1286,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1286,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572946","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572946,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHUS","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Reference deferred group chats FR in DESIGN.md scope boundaries.**\n\nThe PRD functional requirement `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats` (line 110) is not referenced in DESIGN.md, causing a pipeline validation failure. Since this is a P2+ deferred feature, add an explicit reference to this FR ID in the DESIGN.md \"P0 Scope Boundaries\" section (around line 1175) or the \"P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\" section (around line 1162).\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Suggested addition to DESIGN.md</summary>\n\nIn the \"Deferred to P2+\" section, add:\n\n```markdown\n**Deferred to P2+**:\n- Projects / chat sharing (`cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats` - PRD)\n- Full-text search across chats\n- ...\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 GitHub Actions: Cypilot</summary>\n\n[error] 110-110: Constraints: ID not referenced from required artifact kind (cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md` around lines 110 - 112, Add a reference to the\ndeferred functional requirement ID cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats from\nmodules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md into DESIGN.md’s deferred/P2+ area so the pipeline\nvalidation can find it; specifically, open DESIGN.md and under the \"P0 Scope\nBoundaries\" section (or preferably the \"P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\" /\n\"Deferred to P2+\" subsection) add a bullet entry naming the feature and the FR\nID (e.g., Projects / chat sharing (cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats - PRD)) so\nthe PRD FR is explicitly referenced.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:24Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:09Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572946","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572946"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572946"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572946/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":110,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":112,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":112,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572951","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572951,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHUX","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Add observability NFR ID to DESIGN.md NFR Allocation or Metrics section.**\n\nThe PRD NFR `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability` (line 353) defines the operational contract including Prometheus metrics, SLOs, and support signals. This NFR is not referenced in DESIGN.md, causing a pipeline validation failure. Add this NFR ID to:\n\n1. The NFR Allocation table (DESIGN.md section 1.2, around line 36), OR\n2. The Metrics section introduction (DESIGN.md section 4, around line 1329)\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Suggested addition to DESIGN.md NFR Allocation table</summary>\n\n```markdown\n| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability` | Operational contract: metrics, SLOs, support signals | `chat_service`, OAGW, metrics | Prometheus series, Turn Status API, structured logs, distributed tracing | Metrics dashboard validation; support workflow verification |\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 GitHub Actions: Cypilot</summary>\n\n[error] 353-353: Constraints: ID not referenced from required artifact kind (cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md` around lines 353 - 461, The PRD defines NFR ID\ncpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability but DESIGN.md does not\nreference it, causing validation failure; add this NFR ID to DESIGN.md either by\ninserting a row for `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability` into the\nNFR Allocation table (section 1.2 / \"NFR Allocation\") with a short summary,\nallocated components (e.g., chat_service, OAGW, metrics), design response and\nverification approach, or by appending the NFR ID and one-line mapping into the\nMetrics section introduction (section 4 / \"Metrics\") so the NFR is discoverable\nby the pipeline.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:24Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:08Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572951","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572951"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572951"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572951/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":535,"original_start_line":353,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":665,"original_line":461,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":461,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572953","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572953,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHUZ","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Add REST API interface ID to DESIGN.md API Contracts section.**\n\nThe PRD public interface `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api` (line 488) is not referenced in DESIGN.md, causing a pipeline validation failure. Add this ID to the DESIGN.md \"API Contracts\" section introduction (section 3.3, around line 262).\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Suggested addition to DESIGN.md</summary>\n\nAt the beginning of section 3.3:\n\n```markdown\n### 3.3 API Contracts\n\n**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n\n**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 GitHub Actions: Cypilot</summary>\n\n[error] 488-488: Constraints: ID not referenced from required artifact kind (cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md` around lines 488 - 494, Add the public\ninterface ID `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api` to the start of the \"3.3 API\nContracts\" section in DESIGN.md by inserting the suggested intro block (include\n\"**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\" and \"**Technology**:\nREST/OpenAPI, SSE\") so the PRD public interface is referenced in the DESIGN API\nContracts section.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @MikeFalcon77\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:24Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:56:38Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572953","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572953"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572953"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572953/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":488,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":494,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":494,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572957","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572957,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHUd","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+\n+#### Turn Status (read-only) API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status`\n+\n+Support and UX recovery flows SHOULD be able to query authoritative turn state backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Add Turn Status API interface ID to DESIGN.md.**\n\nThe PRD interface `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status` (line 497) is not referenced in DESIGN.md, causing a pipeline validation failure. Add this ID to the \"Turn Status API\" subsection in DESIGN.md (around line 311).\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Suggested addition to DESIGN.md</summary>\n\n```diff\n #### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n \n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status`\n+\n To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 GitHub Actions: Cypilot</summary>\n\n[error] 497-497: Constraints: ID not referenced from required artifact kind (cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md` around lines 497 - 509, The DESIGN.md is\nmissing the PRD interface ID used in PRD.md; add the interface identifier\ncpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status into the \"Turn Status API\" subsection of\nDESIGN.md (near the existing Turn Status API section) so the design doc\nreferences the PRD endpoint GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id} and its\nresponse fields (`chat_id`, `request_id`, `state`, `updated_at`), ensuring the\nexact ID string is included in the subsection list or metadata to satisfy\npipeline validation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:24Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:13Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572957","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572957"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572957"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572957/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":731,"original_start_line":497,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":744,"original_line":509,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":509,"position":744,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572960","pull_request_review_id":3808789521,"id":2812572960,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6npHUg","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+\n+#### Turn Status (read-only) API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status`\n+\n+Support and UX recovery flows SHOULD be able to query authoritative turn state backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### SSE Streaming Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library\n+**Protocol/Format**: Server-Sent Events (SSE) over HTTP\n+**Compatibility**: Event types (`delta`, `tool`, `citations`, `done`, `error`, `ping`) and their payload schemas are stable within a major API version.\n+\n+**Ordering**: `ping* (delta|tool|citations)* (done|error)`.\n+\n+Provider identifiers (e.g., `file_id` in `citations`) are display-only metadata; clients MUST NOT send them back to any API.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Add SSE streaming contract ID to DESIGN.md.**\n\nThe PRD contract `cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming` (line 514) is not referenced in DESIGN.md, causing a pipeline validation failure. Add this ID to the \"Streaming Contract\" subsection in DESIGN.md (around line 279).\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Suggested addition to DESIGN.md</summary>\n\n```diff\n **Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n \n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming`\n+\n The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Tools</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 GitHub Actions: Cypilot</summary>\n\n[error] 514-514: Constraints: ID not referenced from required artifact kind (cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md` around lines 514 - 523, Add the missing SSE\nstreaming contract ID cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming to the \"Streaming\nContract\" subsection in DESIGN.md (near the existing Streaming Contract entries\naround the previous subsection lines), ensuring the ID is listed alongside the\nother contract identifiers and formatted consistently with the surrounding\nentries so the pipeline validation recognizes it; look for the \"Streaming\nContract\" heading in DESIGN.md and append or insert the exact string\n`cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming`.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- fingerprinting:phantom:triton:puma -->\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->\n\n✅ Confirmed as addressed by @MikeFalcon77\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T13:59:24Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:54:08Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572960","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572960"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2812572960"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2812572960/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":758,"original_start_line":514,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":793,"original_line":523,"side":"RIGHT","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":523,"position":793,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813019890","pull_request_review_id":3809300591,"id":2813019890,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq0by","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:41Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:41Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813019890","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813019890"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813019890"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813019890/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":238,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":240,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572916,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":240,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020596","pull_request_review_id":3809301327,"id":2813020596,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq0m0","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:50Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:50Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813020596","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020596"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813020596"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020596/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":340,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":440,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572922,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":440,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020711","pull_request_review_id":3809301451,"id":2813020711,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq0on","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```\n+ping*  (delta | tool | citations)*  (done | error)\n+```\n+\n+- Zero or more `ping` events may appear at any point.\n+- `delta`, `tool`, and `citations` events may interleave in any order.\n+- Exactly one terminal event (`done` or `error`) ends the stream.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-event-translation\"></a>\n+#### Provider Event Translation\n+\n+Provider-specific streaming events are internal to `llm_provider` and `chat_service`. They are never forwarded to clients. The translation layer maps provider events to the stable SSE protocol defined above.\n+\n+| Provider Event | Stable SSE Event | Notes |\n+|----------------|-----------------|-------|\n+| `response.output_text.delta` | `event: delta` (`type: \"text\"`) | Text content mapped 1:1. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.searching` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"start\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | Emitted when file_search tool is invoked. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.completed` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"done\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | `details` populated from search results metadata. |\n+| File search annotations in response | `event: citations` | Extracted from provider annotations, mapped to `items[]` schema. When provider annotations include ranges, `items[].span` SHOULD be populated as character offsets into the final assistant text. |\n+| `response.completed` | `event: done` | `usage` from `response.usage`; `provider.response_id` from `response.id`. |\n+| Provider HTTP error / disconnect | `event: error` (`code: \"provider_error\"` or `\"provider_timeout\"`) | Error details sanitized; provider internals not exposed. |\n+| Provider 429 | `event: error` (`code: \"rate_limited\"`) | After OAGW retry exhaustion. |\n+\n+This mapping is intentionally provider-agnostic in the stable contract. If the provider changes its event format or a new provider is added, only the translation layer in `llm_provider` is updated. The client contract remains unchanged.\n+\n+**Error Codes**:\n+\n+| Code | HTTP Status | Description |\n+|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `feature_not_licensed` | 403 | Tenant lacks `ai_chat` feature |\n+| `insufficient_permissions` | 403 | Subject lacks permission for the requested action (AuthZ Resolver denied) |\n+| `chat_not_found` | 404 | Chat does not exist or not accessible under current authorization constraints |\n+| `quota_exceeded` | 429 | User exceeded daily/monthly usage limit |\n+| `rate_limited` | 429 | Too many requests in time window |\n+| `file_too_large` | 413 | Uploaded file exceeds size limit |\n+| `unsupported_file_type` | 415 | File type not supported for upload |\n+| `provider_error` | 502 | LLM provider returned an error |\n+| `provider_timeout` | 504 | LLM provider request timed out |\n+\n+### 3.4 Internal Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency Module | Interface Used | Purpose |\n+|-------------------|----------------|---------|\n+| api_gateway (platform) | Axum router / middleware | HTTP request handling, SSE transport |\n+| authn (platform) | Middleware (JWT/opaque token) | Extract `user_id` + `tenant_id` from request |\n+| license_manager (platform) | Middleware | Check tenant has `ai_chat` feature; reject with 403 if not |\n+| authz_resolver (platform) | Access evaluation API (`/access/v1/evaluation`) | Obtain authorization decisions + SQL-compilable constraints for chat operations |\n+| audit_service (platform) | Event emitter | Receive structured audit events (prompts, responses, usage, policy decisions) |\n+| outbound_gateway (platform) | Internal HTTP | Egress to LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) with credential injection |\n+\n+**Dependency Rules**:\n+- `chat_service` never calls the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) directly; all external calls go through OAGW\n+- `SecurityContext` (user_id, tenant_id) propagated through all in-process calls\n+- `license_manager` runs as middleware before `chat_service` is invoked\n+- `chat_service` calls `authz_resolver` before every database query; on PDP denial or PDP unreachable, fail-closed (deny access)\n+- `chat_service` emits audit events to `audit_service` after each turn; mini-chat does not store audit data locally\n+\n+### 3.5 External Dependencies\n+\n+#### LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\n+\n+Both providers expose a compatible API surface. OAGW routes requests to the configured provider and injects credentials accordingly.\n+\n+| API | Purpose | OAGW Route | OpenAI Endpoint | Azure OpenAI Endpoint |\n+|-----|---------|------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| Responses API (streaming) | Chat completion with tool support | `POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Responses API (non-streaming) | Thread summary generation, doc summary | `POST /outbound/llm/responses` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Files API | Upload user documents | `POST /outbound/llm/files` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/files` |\n+| Vector Stores API | Manage per-tenant vector stores, add/remove files | `POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/vector_stores/*` |\n+| File Search (tool) | Retrieve document excerpts during chat | - (invoked as tool within Responses API call) | `file_search` tool | `file_search` tool (identical contract) |\n+\n+Note: Azure OpenAI path variants differ by rollout; OAGW owns the exact path mapping for each API.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-api-mapping\"></a>\n+**Provider API Mapping** - authentication and endpoint differences:\n+\n+| Aspect | OpenAI | Azure OpenAI |\n+|--------|--------|--------------|\n+| **Base URL** | `https://api.openai.com/v1` | `https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1` |\n+| **Authentication** | `Authorization: Bearer {api_key}` | `api-key: {key}` header or Entra ID bearer token |\n+| **API version** | Not required | Azure may require an `api-version` query parameter depending on feature/rollout; OAGW owns this provider-specific detail |\n+| **File upload `purpose`** | `assistants` (P0) | `assistants` only (`user_data` not supported) |\n+| **Vector stores per `file_search`** | Multiple | **One** (sufficient for P0: one store per tenant) |\n+| **SSE format** | `event:` + `data:` lines, structured events | Identical format |\n+| **`user` field** | Supported | Supported (feeds into Azure abuse monitoring) |\n+| **`metadata` object** | Supported | Supported |\n+\n+OAGW MUST inject the required `api-version` query parameter when calling Azure OpenAI endpoints.\n+\n+**OAGW throttling scope**: OAGW handles provider-side rate limiting only - retry on provider 429 (with `Retry-After` respect, max 1 retry), circuit breaker when provider error rate exceeds threshold, and global concurrency cap as an SRE safety valve. Product-level quota enforcement (per-user, per-tenant, model downgrade) is NOT an OAGW concern - it is handled entirely by `chat_service` / `quota_service` before any outbound call (see constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+#### PostgreSQL\n+\n+| Usage | Purpose |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| Primary datastore | Chats, messages, attachments, summaries, quota counters, tenant vector store mappings |\n+\n+### 3.6 Interactions & Sequences\n+\n+#### Send Message with Streaming Response\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-send-message`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant AuthZ as authz_resolver (PDP)\n+    participant CS as chat_service (PEP)\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream\n+    AG->>AG: AuthN + license_manager\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(chat_id, user_msg, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>AuthZ: Evaluate(subject, action: \"send_message\", resource: {type: chat, id: chat_id})\n+    AuthZ-->>CS: decision + constraints\n+\n+    alt PDP unreachable / timeout\n+        CS-->>AG: 403 Forbidden\n+        AG-->>UI: 403\n+    else decision = false\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Load chat, recent messages, thread_summary, attachments, tenant vector_store_id (with constraints in WHERE)\n+\n+    alt 0 rows returned\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Build ContextPlan (system prompt + summary + doc summaries + recent msgs + user msg)\n+    CS->>CS: Preflight (reserve) quota check (estimate tokens from ContextPlan + max_output -> allow / downgrade / reject)\n+\n+    alt quota exhausted\n+        CS-->>AG: 429 quota_exceeded\n+        AG-->>UI: SSE error event\n+    end\n+\n+    Note over CS, OAI: Single provider call per user turn. file_search is enabled as a tool within the same streaming Responses API request.\n+\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream (tools include file_search, store=tenant_store)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API (streaming, tool calling enabled)\n+    OAI-->>OG: SSE tokens\n+    OG-->>CS: Token stream\n+    CS-->>AG: Token stream\n+    AG-->>UI: SSE tokens\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Persist user msg + assistant msg + usage\n+    CS->>CS: Commit actual usage to quota_service (debit input_tokens + output_tokens)\n+\n+    participant AS as audit_service\n+    CS->>AS: Emit audit event (usage, policy decisions)\n+\n+    opt Thread summary update triggered (background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Full lifecycle of a user message - from authorization through streaming LLM response to persistence and optional thread compression. Authorization is evaluated before any database access. The PEP sends an evaluation request to the AuthZ Resolver with the chat's resource type and ID; the returned constraints are applied to the DB query's WHERE clause. If the PDP is unreachable, the request is rejected immediately (fail-closed, 403). If the PDP denies access for an operation with a concrete `chat_id`, the PEP returns 404 to avoid disclosing resource existence. If the constrained query returns 0 rows, the PEP returns 404.\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-file-upload`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments (multipart)\n+    AG->>CS: UploadAttachment(chat_id, file, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>DB: Insert attachment metadata (status: pending)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/files (upload)\n+    OG->>OAI: Files API upload\n+    OAI-->>OG: file_id\n+    OG-->>CS: file_id\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/{tenant_store}/files (add file)\n+    OG->>OAI: Add file to vector store\n+    OAI-->>OG: OK\n+    OG-->>CS: OK\n+    CS->>DB: Update attachment (status: ready, provider_file_id)\n+\n+    opt Generate doc summary (Variant 1, background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue doc summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+\n+    CS-->>AG: attachment_id, status\n+    AG-->>UI: 201 Created\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: File upload flow - the file is uploaded to the LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) via OAGW, added to the tenant's vector store, optionally summarized, and metadata is persisted locally.\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-cancellation`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant LP as llm_provider\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: SSE connection established\n+    AG->>AG: Create CancellationToken\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(..., cancel_token)\n+    CS->>LP: stream_response(..., cancel_token)\n+    LP->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream\n+    OG->>OAI: Streaming in progress\n+\n+    alt UI disconnect\n+        UI--xAG: Client disconnects / stop button\n+        AG->>AG: Detect broken SSE write -> cancel token\n+    else Server timeout\n+        CS->>CS: Timeout elapsed -> cancel token\n+    end\n+\n+    LP->>LP: Token cancelled -> abort HTTP request\n+    LP->>OG: Close connection (hard cancel)\n+    OG->>OAI: TCP RST / connection closed\n+    CS->>CS: Persist partial response (usage may be unknown)\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Cancellation propagates end-to-end via a shared `CancellationToken`. When triggered, `llm_provider` performs a hard cancel - aborting the outbound HTTP connection so the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) stops generating immediately. The partial response is persisted. Because provider usage is typically only delivered on a completed response, `input_tokens`/`output_tokens` may be NULL or approximate for cancelled turns; quota enforcement uses the bounded best-effort debit described in `quota_service`.\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Update\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-thread-summary`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Check summary trigger (msg count > 20 OR tokens > budget OR every 15 user turns)\n+    CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    Note over CS: Background worker executes summary task asynchronously\n+    CS->>DB: Load current thread_summary + old messages batch (10-20 msgs)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses (update summary prompt)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API\n+    OAI-->>OG: Updated summary\n+    OG-->>CS: Updated summary\n+    CS->>DB: Save new thread_summary\n+    CS->>DB: Mark summarized messages as compressed\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Thread summary is updated asynchronously after a chat turn when trigger conditions are met. Summary generation is a background task and MUST be attributed as `requester_type=system` so that its usage is not charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+Summary quality gate (P0): the system MUST detect and mitigate obviously-bad summaries without ML-based evaluation.\n+\n+- After generating a summary, `chat_service` MUST validate the candidate summary text.\n+- If summary length < `X` OR entropy < `Y`, `chat_service` MUST attempt regeneration.\n+- If regeneration fails quality checks or the provider call fails, `chat_service` MUST fall back by keeping the previous summary unchanged and MUST NOT mark the message batch as compressed.\n+\n+`X` and `Y` are configurable thresholds. Entropy is a deterministic proxy computed as normalized token entropy over whitespace-delimited tokens:\n+\n+`H_norm = (-sum(p_i * log2(p_i))) / log2(N)` where `p_i` is the empirical frequency of token `i` and `N` is the number of distinct tokens.\n+\n+Observability:\n+\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}` for each regeneration attempt (`reason` from a bounded allowlist such as `too_short|low_entropy|provider_error|invalid_format`).\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total` when the fallback behavior above is used.\n+\n+### 3.7 Database Schemas & Tables\n+\n+#### Table: chats\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chats`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Chat identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | Owning user |\n+| title | VARCHAR(255) | Chat title (user-set or auto-generated) |\n+| is_temporary | BOOLEAN | If true, auto-deleted after 24h |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last activity time |\n+| deleted_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Soft delete timestamp (nullable) |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `created_at`\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, updated_at DESC)` for listing chats\n+\n+#### Table: messages\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-messages`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Message identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key (nullable). Used for completed replay and traceability. Running state is tracked in `chat_turns`. |\n+| role | VARCHAR(16) | `user`, `assistant`, or `system` |\n+| content | TEXT | Message content |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| request_kind | VARCHAR(16) | `chat`, `summary`, or `doc_summary` (nullable) |\n+| features_used | JSONB | Feature flags and counters (nullable) |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Actual input tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Actual output tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| model | VARCHAR(64) | Actual model used for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| is_compressed | BOOLEAN | True if included in a thread summary |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `chat_id`, `role`, `content`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE. UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)` WHERE `request_id IS NOT NULL`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(chat_id, created_at)` for loading recent messages\n+\n+#### Table: chat_turns\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chat-turns`\n+\n+Tracks idempotency and in-progress generation state for `request_id`. This avoids ambiguous interpretation of `messages.request_id` when a generation is still running.\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Turn identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key |\n+| requester_type | VARCHAR(16) | `user` or `system` |\n+| requester_user_id | UUID | User ID when requester_type=`user` (nullable for system) |\n+| state | VARCHAR(16) | `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier (nullable until request starts) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID (nullable) |\n+| assistant_message_id | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID (nullable until completed) |\n+| error_code | VARCHAR(64) | Terminal error code (nullable) |\n+| started_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Start time |\n+| completed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Completion time (nullable) |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+A `chat_turns` row MUST be created before starting the outbound provider request; initial state is `running`.\n+\n+State machine:\n+- Allowed transitions: `running` -> `completed` \\| `failed` \\| `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states MUST be immutable\n+- At most one `running` turn per `(chat_id, request_id)`\n+\n+#### Table: attachments\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-attachments`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Attachment identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| filename | VARCHAR(255) | Original filename |\n+| content_type | VARCHAR(128) | MIME type |\n+| size_bytes | BIGINT | File size |\n+| provider_file_id | VARCHAR(128) | LLM provider file ID - OpenAI `file-*` or Azure OpenAI `assistant-*` (nullable until upload completes) |\n+| status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `ready`, `failed` |\n+| doc_summary | TEXT | LLM-generated document summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_model | VARCHAR(64) | Model used to generate the summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When the summary was last generated (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `in_progress`, `done`, `failed` (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_attempts | INTEGER | Cleanup retry attempts (default 0) |\n+| last_cleanup_error | TEXT | Last cleanup error (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When cleanup state was last updated (nullable) |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Upload time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `chat_id`, `filename`, `status`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: thread_summaries\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-thread-summaries`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Summary identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id, UNIQUE) |\n+| summary_text | TEXT | Compressed conversation summary |\n+| summarized_up_to | UUID | Last message ID included in this summary |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count of summary |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `chat_id`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: tenant_vector_stores\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-vector-stores`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant identifier |\n+| vector_store_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider vector store ID (OpenAI `vs_*` or Azure OpenAI equivalent) |\n+| provider | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier: `openai` or `azure_openai` |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `tenant_id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `vector_store_id`, `provider`, `created_at`. One vector store per tenant.\n+\n+Creation protocol (P0): `chat_service` uses a get-or-create flow with database uniqueness as the race arbiter.\n+\n+1. Attempt to read the row by `tenant_id`.\n+2. If not present, create the vector store via OAGW and attempt INSERT.\n+3. If INSERT fails due to unique violation (concurrent creator), re-read the row and use the existing `vector_store_id`. Best-effort delete the newly created provider vector store to avoid orphans.\n+\n+ **P0**: run a periodic reconcile/orphan reaper job (for example nightly) to reconcile provider state with `tenant_vector_stores`:\n+ - If a provider vector store exists but is not referenced in DB -> delete it.\n+ - If a DB row exists but the provider vector store is missing -> recreate the vector store and update the DB row.\n+\n+#### Table: quota_usage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-quota-usage`\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-quota-usage-accounting`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Record identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | User |\n+| period_type | VARCHAR(16) | `daily` or `monthly` |\n+| period_start | DATE | Start of the period |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Total input tokens consumed |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Total output tokens consumed |\n+| file_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of file search tool calls |\n+| web_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of web search calls (P1+) |\n+| rag_retrieval_calls | INTEGER | Number of internal RAG retrieval calls (P1+) |\n+| premium_model_calls | INTEGER | Calls to premium models |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+Commit semantics: quota updates MUST be atomic per period record. Implementations SHOULD use a transaction with row locking or a single UPDATE statement to avoid race conditions under parallel streams.\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` for quota lookups\n+\n+#### Projection Table: tenant_closure\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-closure-ref`\n+\n+Mini Chat does NOT require the `tenant_closure` local projection table for chat content access in P0. Chat content is owner-only and requires exact `owner_tenant_id` + `user_id` predicates.\n+\n+The `tenant_closure` projection table exists in the platform authorization model for modules that use hierarchical tenant scoping, but it is unused for Mini Chat content operations.\n+\n+Schema is defined in the [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#table-schemas-local-projections).\n+\n+**P2+ note**: When chat sharing (projects) is introduced, `resource_group_membership` and optionally `resource_group_closure` tables will also be required.\n+\n+### 3.8 Authorization (PEP)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-authz-pep`\n+\n+Mini Chat acts as a Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) per the platform's PDP/PEP authorization model defined in [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). The `chat_service` builds evaluation requests, sends them to the AuthZ Resolver (PDP), and compiles returned constraints into SQL WHERE clauses.\n+\n+Policy (P0): chat content is owner-only. For all content operations, authorization MUST enforce:\n+\n+- `owner_tenant_id == subject.tenant_id`\n+- `user_id == subject.id`\n+\n+Mini Chat does not use tenant hierarchy or subtree-based authorization for content operations.\n+\n+#### Resource Type\n+\n+The authorized resource is **Chat**. Sub-resources (Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary) do not have independent authorization - they are accessed through their parent chat, and the chat's authorization decision covers all child operations.\n+\n+| Attribute | Value |\n+|-----------|-------|\n+| GTS Type ID | `gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~` |\n+| Primary table | `chats` |\n+| Authorization granularity | Chat-level (sub-resources inherit) |\n+\n+#### PEP Configuration\n+\n+**Capabilities** (declared in `context.capabilities`):\n+\n+| Capability | P0 | P2+ | Rationale |\n+|------------|-----|------|-----------|\n+| `tenant_hierarchy` | No | No | Chat content is owner-only; `tenant_closure` is unused for Mini Chat content operations |\n+| `group_membership` | No | Yes | Needed when chat sharing via projects is introduced |\n+| `group_hierarchy` | No | Maybe | Needed if projects have nested hierarchy |\n+\n+**Supported properties** (declared in `context.supported_properties`):\n+\n+| Resource Property | SQL Column | Description |\n+|-------------------|------------|-------------|\n+| `owner_tenant_id` | `chats.tenant_id` | Owning tenant |\n+| `user_id` | `chats.user_id` | Owning user |\n+| `id` | `chats.id` | Chat identifier |\n+\n+#### Per-Operation Authorization Matrix\n+\n+| Endpoint | Action | `resource.id` | `require_constraints` | Expected P0 Predicates |\n+|----------|--------|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n+| `POST /v1/chats` | `create` | absent | `false` | decision only (no constraints) |\n+| `GET /v1/chats` | `list` | absent | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}` | `read` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `DELETE /v1/chats/{id}` | `delete` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | `update` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | `send_message` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | `upload` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | `read_turn` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+\n+**Notes**:\n+- `send_message` and `upload` are actions on the Chat resource, not on Message or Attachment resources. The `resource.id` is the chat's ID.\n+- For streaming (`send_message`), authorization is evaluated once at SSE connection establishment. The entire streaming session operates under the initial authorization decision. No per-message re-authorization.\n+- For `create`, the PEP passes `resource.properties.owner_tenant_id` and `resource.properties.user_id` from the SecurityContext. The PDP validates permission without returning constraints.\n+\n+#### Evaluation Request/Response Examples\n+\n+**Example 1: List Chats** (`GET /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"list\" },\n+  \"resource\": { \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\" },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response (P0 - user-owned chats only):\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+ORDER BY updated_at DESC\n+```\n+\n+**Example 2: Get Chat** (`GET /v1/chats/{id}`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"read\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"chat-456\"\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE id = 'chat-456'\n+  AND tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+```\n+\n+Result: 1 row -> return chat; 0 rows -> 404 Not Found (hides existence from unauthorized users).\n+\n+**Example 3: Create Chat** (`POST /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"create\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"properties\": {\n+      \"owner_tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\",\n+      \"user_id\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+    }\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": false,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{ \"decision\": true }\n+```\n+\n+PEP proceeds with INSERT. No constraints needed for create.\n+\n+**Example 4: Send Message** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`)\n+\n+Same authorization flow as Example 2 (Get Chat), but with `\"action\": { \"name\": \"send_message\" }` and `\"resource.id\"` set to the chat ID. Authorization is evaluated once before the SSE stream is established. The constraints are applied to the query that loads the chat and its messages from `chat_store`.\n+\n+#### Fail-Closed Behavior\n+\n+Mini Chat follows the platform's fail-closed rules (see [Authorization Design - Fail-Closed Rules](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#fail-closed-rules)):\n+\n+| Condition | PEP Action |\n+|-----------|------------|\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` present | 404 Not Found (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` absent | 403 Forbidden (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| PDP unreachable / timeout | 403 Forbidden (fail-closed) |\n+| `decision: true` + no constraints + `require_constraints: true` | 403 Forbidden |\n+| Unknown predicate type in constraints | Treat constraint as false; if all constraints false -> 403 |\n+| Unknown `resource_property` in predicate | Treat constraint as false; log error (PDP contract violation) |\n+| Empty `constraints: []` | 403 Forbidden |\n+\n+#### Token Scopes\n+\n+Mini Chat recognizes the following token scopes for third-party application narrowing:\n+\n+| Scope | Permits |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| `ai:chat` | All chat operations (umbrella scope) |\n+| `ai:chat:read` | `list`, `read` actions only |\n+| `ai:chat:write` | `create`, `update`, `delete`, `send_message`, `upload` actions |\n+\n+First-party applications (UI) use `token_scopes: [\"*\"]`. Third-party integrations receive narrowed scopes. Scope enforcement is handled by the PDP - the PEP includes `token_scopes` in the evaluation request context.\n+\n+#### P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\n+\n+When Projects / chat sharing is introduced (P2+), the authorization model extends naturally:\n+\n+1. Add `group_membership` capability (and optionally `group_hierarchy`).\n+2. Maintain `resource_group_membership` projection table mapping chat IDs to project group IDs.\n+3. The PDP returns additional access paths via OR'd constraints - e.g., one constraint for owned chats (`user_id` predicate), another for shared-via-project chats (`in_group` predicate).\n+4. `supported_properties` remains unchanged (the `id` property is used for group membership joins).\n+\n+No changes to the PEP flow or constraint compilation logic are needed. The PDP's response structure naturally handles multiple access paths through OR'd constraints.\n+\n+## 4. Additional Context\n+\n+### P0 Scope Boundaries\n+\n+**Included in P0**:\n+- Single-tenant vector store per tenant (not per workspace)\n+- Thread summary as only compression mechanism\n+- On-upload document summary via File Search (Variant 1 from draft)\n+- Quota enforcement: daily + monthly per user\n+- Temporary chats with 24h scheduled cleanup\n+- File Search per-message call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2 tool calls per message)\n+\n+**Deferred to P2+**:\n+- Projects / chat sharing\n+- Full-text search across chats\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are both supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies (beyond simple limits)\n+- Per-workspace vector stores\n+\n+### Data Classification and Retention (P0)\n+\n+Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data. Mini Chat treats messages and summaries as customer content and applies data minimization and retention controls.\n+\n+**Data classes**:\n+- Chat content: `messages.content`, `thread_summaries.summary_text`, `attachments.doc_summary`\n+- Operational metadata: IDs, timestamps, provider correlation IDs, quotas\n+- Audit events: structured records emitted to platform `audit_service`\n+\n+**Retention**:\n+- Temporary chats are scheduled for cleanup after 24h and MUST be deleted within 25h (PRD SLA).\n+- Non-temporary chats are retained until explicit deletion by default, subject to operator-configured retention policies.\n+- Soft-deleted chats (`deleted_at` set) are hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+**Audit content handling (P0)**:\n+- Audit events include the minimal content required for security and incident response.\n+- `chat_service` MUST redact secret patterns (tokens, keys, credentials) before sending content to `audit_service`.\n+- Redaction MUST be testable and based on a bounded allowlist of rule classes.\n+\n+| Rule class | Example (non-exhaustive) | Action |\n+|-----------|---------------------------|--------|\n+| `bearer_token` | `Authorization: Bearer eyJ...` | Replace token with `Bearer [REDACTED]` |\n+| `jwt` | `eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJS...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_JWT]` |\n+| `openai_api_key` | `sk-...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]` |\n+| `aws_access_key_id` | `AKIA...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]` |\n+| `azure_api_key` | `api-key: ...`, `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: ...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]` |\n+| `api_key_fields` | `api_key=...`, `x-api-key=...`, `client_secret=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `password_fields` | `password=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `pem_private_key` | `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` | Replace block with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]` |\n+| `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+- Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:51Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:51Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813020711","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020711"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813020711"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020711/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1211,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1222,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572927,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":1222,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020830","pull_request_review_id":3809301560,"id":2813020830,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq0qe","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```\n+ping*  (delta | tool | citations)*  (done | error)\n+```\n+\n+- Zero or more `ping` events may appear at any point.\n+- `delta`, `tool`, and `citations` events may interleave in any order.\n+- Exactly one terminal event (`done` or `error`) ends the stream.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-event-translation\"></a>\n+#### Provider Event Translation\n+\n+Provider-specific streaming events are internal to `llm_provider` and `chat_service`. They are never forwarded to clients. The translation layer maps provider events to the stable SSE protocol defined above.\n+\n+| Provider Event | Stable SSE Event | Notes |\n+|----------------|-----------------|-------|\n+| `response.output_text.delta` | `event: delta` (`type: \"text\"`) | Text content mapped 1:1. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.searching` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"start\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | Emitted when file_search tool is invoked. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.completed` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"done\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | `details` populated from search results metadata. |\n+| File search annotations in response | `event: citations` | Extracted from provider annotations, mapped to `items[]` schema. When provider annotations include ranges, `items[].span` SHOULD be populated as character offsets into the final assistant text. |\n+| `response.completed` | `event: done` | `usage` from `response.usage`; `provider.response_id` from `response.id`. |\n+| Provider HTTP error / disconnect | `event: error` (`code: \"provider_error\"` or `\"provider_timeout\"`) | Error details sanitized; provider internals not exposed. |\n+| Provider 429 | `event: error` (`code: \"rate_limited\"`) | After OAGW retry exhaustion. |\n+\n+This mapping is intentionally provider-agnostic in the stable contract. If the provider changes its event format or a new provider is added, only the translation layer in `llm_provider` is updated. The client contract remains unchanged.\n+\n+**Error Codes**:\n+\n+| Code | HTTP Status | Description |\n+|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `feature_not_licensed` | 403 | Tenant lacks `ai_chat` feature |\n+| `insufficient_permissions` | 403 | Subject lacks permission for the requested action (AuthZ Resolver denied) |\n+| `chat_not_found` | 404 | Chat does not exist or not accessible under current authorization constraints |\n+| `quota_exceeded` | 429 | User exceeded daily/monthly usage limit |\n+| `rate_limited` | 429 | Too many requests in time window |\n+| `file_too_large` | 413 | Uploaded file exceeds size limit |\n+| `unsupported_file_type` | 415 | File type not supported for upload |\n+| `provider_error` | 502 | LLM provider returned an error |\n+| `provider_timeout` | 504 | LLM provider request timed out |\n+\n+### 3.4 Internal Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency Module | Interface Used | Purpose |\n+|-------------------|----------------|---------|\n+| api_gateway (platform) | Axum router / middleware | HTTP request handling, SSE transport |\n+| authn (platform) | Middleware (JWT/opaque token) | Extract `user_id` + `tenant_id` from request |\n+| license_manager (platform) | Middleware | Check tenant has `ai_chat` feature; reject with 403 if not |\n+| authz_resolver (platform) | Access evaluation API (`/access/v1/evaluation`) | Obtain authorization decisions + SQL-compilable constraints for chat operations |\n+| audit_service (platform) | Event emitter | Receive structured audit events (prompts, responses, usage, policy decisions) |\n+| outbound_gateway (platform) | Internal HTTP | Egress to LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) with credential injection |\n+\n+**Dependency Rules**:\n+- `chat_service` never calls the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) directly; all external calls go through OAGW\n+- `SecurityContext` (user_id, tenant_id) propagated through all in-process calls\n+- `license_manager` runs as middleware before `chat_service` is invoked\n+- `chat_service` calls `authz_resolver` before every database query; on PDP denial or PDP unreachable, fail-closed (deny access)\n+- `chat_service` emits audit events to `audit_service` after each turn; mini-chat does not store audit data locally\n+\n+### 3.5 External Dependencies\n+\n+#### LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\n+\n+Both providers expose a compatible API surface. OAGW routes requests to the configured provider and injects credentials accordingly.\n+\n+| API | Purpose | OAGW Route | OpenAI Endpoint | Azure OpenAI Endpoint |\n+|-----|---------|------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| Responses API (streaming) | Chat completion with tool support | `POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Responses API (non-streaming) | Thread summary generation, doc summary | `POST /outbound/llm/responses` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Files API | Upload user documents | `POST /outbound/llm/files` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/files` |\n+| Vector Stores API | Manage per-tenant vector stores, add/remove files | `POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/vector_stores/*` |\n+| File Search (tool) | Retrieve document excerpts during chat | - (invoked as tool within Responses API call) | `file_search` tool | `file_search` tool (identical contract) |\n+\n+Note: Azure OpenAI path variants differ by rollout; OAGW owns the exact path mapping for each API.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-api-mapping\"></a>\n+**Provider API Mapping** - authentication and endpoint differences:\n+\n+| Aspect | OpenAI | Azure OpenAI |\n+|--------|--------|--------------|\n+| **Base URL** | `https://api.openai.com/v1` | `https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1` |\n+| **Authentication** | `Authorization: Bearer {api_key}` | `api-key: {key}` header or Entra ID bearer token |\n+| **API version** | Not required | Azure may require an `api-version` query parameter depending on feature/rollout; OAGW owns this provider-specific detail |\n+| **File upload `purpose`** | `assistants` (P0) | `assistants` only (`user_data` not supported) |\n+| **Vector stores per `file_search`** | Multiple | **One** (sufficient for P0: one store per tenant) |\n+| **SSE format** | `event:` + `data:` lines, structured events | Identical format |\n+| **`user` field** | Supported | Supported (feeds into Azure abuse monitoring) |\n+| **`metadata` object** | Supported | Supported |\n+\n+OAGW MUST inject the required `api-version` query parameter when calling Azure OpenAI endpoints.\n+\n+**OAGW throttling scope**: OAGW handles provider-side rate limiting only - retry on provider 429 (with `Retry-After` respect, max 1 retry), circuit breaker when provider error rate exceeds threshold, and global concurrency cap as an SRE safety valve. Product-level quota enforcement (per-user, per-tenant, model downgrade) is NOT an OAGW concern - it is handled entirely by `chat_service` / `quota_service` before any outbound call (see constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+#### PostgreSQL\n+\n+| Usage | Purpose |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| Primary datastore | Chats, messages, attachments, summaries, quota counters, tenant vector store mappings |\n+\n+### 3.6 Interactions & Sequences\n+\n+#### Send Message with Streaming Response\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-send-message`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant AuthZ as authz_resolver (PDP)\n+    participant CS as chat_service (PEP)\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream\n+    AG->>AG: AuthN + license_manager\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(chat_id, user_msg, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>AuthZ: Evaluate(subject, action: \"send_message\", resource: {type: chat, id: chat_id})\n+    AuthZ-->>CS: decision + constraints\n+\n+    alt PDP unreachable / timeout\n+        CS-->>AG: 403 Forbidden\n+        AG-->>UI: 403\n+    else decision = false\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Load chat, recent messages, thread_summary, attachments, tenant vector_store_id (with constraints in WHERE)\n+\n+    alt 0 rows returned\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Build ContextPlan (system prompt + summary + doc summaries + recent msgs + user msg)\n+    CS->>CS: Preflight (reserve) quota check (estimate tokens from ContextPlan + max_output -> allow / downgrade / reject)\n+\n+    alt quota exhausted\n+        CS-->>AG: 429 quota_exceeded\n+        AG-->>UI: SSE error event\n+    end\n+\n+    Note over CS, OAI: Single provider call per user turn. file_search is enabled as a tool within the same streaming Responses API request.\n+\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream (tools include file_search, store=tenant_store)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API (streaming, tool calling enabled)\n+    OAI-->>OG: SSE tokens\n+    OG-->>CS: Token stream\n+    CS-->>AG: Token stream\n+    AG-->>UI: SSE tokens\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Persist user msg + assistant msg + usage\n+    CS->>CS: Commit actual usage to quota_service (debit input_tokens + output_tokens)\n+\n+    participant AS as audit_service\n+    CS->>AS: Emit audit event (usage, policy decisions)\n+\n+    opt Thread summary update triggered (background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Full lifecycle of a user message - from authorization through streaming LLM response to persistence and optional thread compression. Authorization is evaluated before any database access. The PEP sends an evaluation request to the AuthZ Resolver with the chat's resource type and ID; the returned constraints are applied to the DB query's WHERE clause. If the PDP is unreachable, the request is rejected immediately (fail-closed, 403). If the PDP denies access for an operation with a concrete `chat_id`, the PEP returns 404 to avoid disclosing resource existence. If the constrained query returns 0 rows, the PEP returns 404.\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-file-upload`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments (multipart)\n+    AG->>CS: UploadAttachment(chat_id, file, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>DB: Insert attachment metadata (status: pending)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/files (upload)\n+    OG->>OAI: Files API upload\n+    OAI-->>OG: file_id\n+    OG-->>CS: file_id\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/{tenant_store}/files (add file)\n+    OG->>OAI: Add file to vector store\n+    OAI-->>OG: OK\n+    OG-->>CS: OK\n+    CS->>DB: Update attachment (status: ready, provider_file_id)\n+\n+    opt Generate doc summary (Variant 1, background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue doc summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+\n+    CS-->>AG: attachment_id, status\n+    AG-->>UI: 201 Created\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: File upload flow - the file is uploaded to the LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) via OAGW, added to the tenant's vector store, optionally summarized, and metadata is persisted locally.\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-cancellation`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant LP as llm_provider\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: SSE connection established\n+    AG->>AG: Create CancellationToken\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(..., cancel_token)\n+    CS->>LP: stream_response(..., cancel_token)\n+    LP->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream\n+    OG->>OAI: Streaming in progress\n+\n+    alt UI disconnect\n+        UI--xAG: Client disconnects / stop button\n+        AG->>AG: Detect broken SSE write -> cancel token\n+    else Server timeout\n+        CS->>CS: Timeout elapsed -> cancel token\n+    end\n+\n+    LP->>LP: Token cancelled -> abort HTTP request\n+    LP->>OG: Close connection (hard cancel)\n+    OG->>OAI: TCP RST / connection closed\n+    CS->>CS: Persist partial response (usage may be unknown)\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Cancellation propagates end-to-end via a shared `CancellationToken`. When triggered, `llm_provider` performs a hard cancel - aborting the outbound HTTP connection so the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) stops generating immediately. The partial response is persisted. Because provider usage is typically only delivered on a completed response, `input_tokens`/`output_tokens` may be NULL or approximate for cancelled turns; quota enforcement uses the bounded best-effort debit described in `quota_service`.\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Update\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-thread-summary`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Check summary trigger (msg count > 20 OR tokens > budget OR every 15 user turns)\n+    CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    Note over CS: Background worker executes summary task asynchronously\n+    CS->>DB: Load current thread_summary + old messages batch (10-20 msgs)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses (update summary prompt)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API\n+    OAI-->>OG: Updated summary\n+    OG-->>CS: Updated summary\n+    CS->>DB: Save new thread_summary\n+    CS->>DB: Mark summarized messages as compressed\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Thread summary is updated asynchronously after a chat turn when trigger conditions are met. Summary generation is a background task and MUST be attributed as `requester_type=system` so that its usage is not charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+Summary quality gate (P0): the system MUST detect and mitigate obviously-bad summaries without ML-based evaluation.\n+\n+- After generating a summary, `chat_service` MUST validate the candidate summary text.\n+- If summary length < `X` OR entropy < `Y`, `chat_service` MUST attempt regeneration.\n+- If regeneration fails quality checks or the provider call fails, `chat_service` MUST fall back by keeping the previous summary unchanged and MUST NOT mark the message batch as compressed.\n+\n+`X` and `Y` are configurable thresholds. Entropy is a deterministic proxy computed as normalized token entropy over whitespace-delimited tokens:\n+\n+`H_norm = (-sum(p_i * log2(p_i))) / log2(N)` where `p_i` is the empirical frequency of token `i` and `N` is the number of distinct tokens.\n+\n+Observability:\n+\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}` for each regeneration attempt (`reason` from a bounded allowlist such as `too_short|low_entropy|provider_error|invalid_format`).\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total` when the fallback behavior above is used.\n+\n+### 3.7 Database Schemas & Tables\n+\n+#### Table: chats\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chats`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Chat identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | Owning user |\n+| title | VARCHAR(255) | Chat title (user-set or auto-generated) |\n+| is_temporary | BOOLEAN | If true, auto-deleted after 24h |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last activity time |\n+| deleted_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Soft delete timestamp (nullable) |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `created_at`\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, updated_at DESC)` for listing chats\n+\n+#### Table: messages\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-messages`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Message identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key (nullable). Used for completed replay and traceability. Running state is tracked in `chat_turns`. |\n+| role | VARCHAR(16) | `user`, `assistant`, or `system` |\n+| content | TEXT | Message content |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| request_kind | VARCHAR(16) | `chat`, `summary`, or `doc_summary` (nullable) |\n+| features_used | JSONB | Feature flags and counters (nullable) |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Actual input tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Actual output tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| model | VARCHAR(64) | Actual model used for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| is_compressed | BOOLEAN | True if included in a thread summary |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `chat_id`, `role`, `content`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE. UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)` WHERE `request_id IS NOT NULL`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(chat_id, created_at)` for loading recent messages\n+\n+#### Table: chat_turns\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chat-turns`\n+\n+Tracks idempotency and in-progress generation state for `request_id`. This avoids ambiguous interpretation of `messages.request_id` when a generation is still running.\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Turn identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key |\n+| requester_type | VARCHAR(16) | `user` or `system` |\n+| requester_user_id | UUID | User ID when requester_type=`user` (nullable for system) |\n+| state | VARCHAR(16) | `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier (nullable until request starts) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID (nullable) |\n+| assistant_message_id | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID (nullable until completed) |\n+| error_code | VARCHAR(64) | Terminal error code (nullable) |\n+| started_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Start time |\n+| completed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Completion time (nullable) |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+A `chat_turns` row MUST be created before starting the outbound provider request; initial state is `running`.\n+\n+State machine:\n+- Allowed transitions: `running` -> `completed` \\| `failed` \\| `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states MUST be immutable\n+- At most one `running` turn per `(chat_id, request_id)`\n+\n+#### Table: attachments\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-attachments`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Attachment identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| filename | VARCHAR(255) | Original filename |\n+| content_type | VARCHAR(128) | MIME type |\n+| size_bytes | BIGINT | File size |\n+| provider_file_id | VARCHAR(128) | LLM provider file ID - OpenAI `file-*` or Azure OpenAI `assistant-*` (nullable until upload completes) |\n+| status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `ready`, `failed` |\n+| doc_summary | TEXT | LLM-generated document summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_model | VARCHAR(64) | Model used to generate the summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When the summary was last generated (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `in_progress`, `done`, `failed` (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_attempts | INTEGER | Cleanup retry attempts (default 0) |\n+| last_cleanup_error | TEXT | Last cleanup error (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When cleanup state was last updated (nullable) |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Upload time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `chat_id`, `filename`, `status`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: thread_summaries\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-thread-summaries`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Summary identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id, UNIQUE) |\n+| summary_text | TEXT | Compressed conversation summary |\n+| summarized_up_to | UUID | Last message ID included in this summary |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count of summary |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `chat_id`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: tenant_vector_stores\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-vector-stores`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant identifier |\n+| vector_store_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider vector store ID (OpenAI `vs_*` or Azure OpenAI equivalent) |\n+| provider | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier: `openai` or `azure_openai` |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `tenant_id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `vector_store_id`, `provider`, `created_at`. One vector store per tenant.\n+\n+Creation protocol (P0): `chat_service` uses a get-or-create flow with database uniqueness as the race arbiter.\n+\n+1. Attempt to read the row by `tenant_id`.\n+2. If not present, create the vector store via OAGW and attempt INSERT.\n+3. If INSERT fails due to unique violation (concurrent creator), re-read the row and use the existing `vector_store_id`. Best-effort delete the newly created provider vector store to avoid orphans.\n+\n+ **P0**: run a periodic reconcile/orphan reaper job (for example nightly) to reconcile provider state with `tenant_vector_stores`:\n+ - If a provider vector store exists but is not referenced in DB -> delete it.\n+ - If a DB row exists but the provider vector store is missing -> recreate the vector store and update the DB row.\n+\n+#### Table: quota_usage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-quota-usage`\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-quota-usage-accounting`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Record identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | User |\n+| period_type | VARCHAR(16) | `daily` or `monthly` |\n+| period_start | DATE | Start of the period |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Total input tokens consumed |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Total output tokens consumed |\n+| file_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of file search tool calls |\n+| web_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of web search calls (P1+) |\n+| rag_retrieval_calls | INTEGER | Number of internal RAG retrieval calls (P1+) |\n+| premium_model_calls | INTEGER | Calls to premium models |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+Commit semantics: quota updates MUST be atomic per period record. Implementations SHOULD use a transaction with row locking or a single UPDATE statement to avoid race conditions under parallel streams.\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` for quota lookups\n+\n+#### Projection Table: tenant_closure\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-closure-ref`\n+\n+Mini Chat does NOT require the `tenant_closure` local projection table for chat content access in P0. Chat content is owner-only and requires exact `owner_tenant_id` + `user_id` predicates.\n+\n+The `tenant_closure` projection table exists in the platform authorization model for modules that use hierarchical tenant scoping, but it is unused for Mini Chat content operations.\n+\n+Schema is defined in the [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#table-schemas-local-projections).\n+\n+**P2+ note**: When chat sharing (projects) is introduced, `resource_group_membership` and optionally `resource_group_closure` tables will also be required.\n+\n+### 3.8 Authorization (PEP)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-authz-pep`\n+\n+Mini Chat acts as a Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) per the platform's PDP/PEP authorization model defined in [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). The `chat_service` builds evaluation requests, sends them to the AuthZ Resolver (PDP), and compiles returned constraints into SQL WHERE clauses.\n+\n+Policy (P0): chat content is owner-only. For all content operations, authorization MUST enforce:\n+\n+- `owner_tenant_id == subject.tenant_id`\n+- `user_id == subject.id`\n+\n+Mini Chat does not use tenant hierarchy or subtree-based authorization for content operations.\n+\n+#### Resource Type\n+\n+The authorized resource is **Chat**. Sub-resources (Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary) do not have independent authorization - they are accessed through their parent chat, and the chat's authorization decision covers all child operations.\n+\n+| Attribute | Value |\n+|-----------|-------|\n+| GTS Type ID | `gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~` |\n+| Primary table | `chats` |\n+| Authorization granularity | Chat-level (sub-resources inherit) |\n+\n+#### PEP Configuration\n+\n+**Capabilities** (declared in `context.capabilities`):\n+\n+| Capability | P0 | P2+ | Rationale |\n+|------------|-----|------|-----------|\n+| `tenant_hierarchy` | No | No | Chat content is owner-only; `tenant_closure` is unused for Mini Chat content operations |\n+| `group_membership` | No | Yes | Needed when chat sharing via projects is introduced |\n+| `group_hierarchy` | No | Maybe | Needed if projects have nested hierarchy |\n+\n+**Supported properties** (declared in `context.supported_properties`):\n+\n+| Resource Property | SQL Column | Description |\n+|-------------------|------------|-------------|\n+| `owner_tenant_id` | `chats.tenant_id` | Owning tenant |\n+| `user_id` | `chats.user_id` | Owning user |\n+| `id` | `chats.id` | Chat identifier |\n+\n+#### Per-Operation Authorization Matrix\n+\n+| Endpoint | Action | `resource.id` | `require_constraints` | Expected P0 Predicates |\n+|----------|--------|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n+| `POST /v1/chats` | `create` | absent | `false` | decision only (no constraints) |\n+| `GET /v1/chats` | `list` | absent | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}` | `read` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `DELETE /v1/chats/{id}` | `delete` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | `update` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | `send_message` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | `upload` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | `read_turn` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+\n+**Notes**:\n+- `send_message` and `upload` are actions on the Chat resource, not on Message or Attachment resources. The `resource.id` is the chat's ID.\n+- For streaming (`send_message`), authorization is evaluated once at SSE connection establishment. The entire streaming session operates under the initial authorization decision. No per-message re-authorization.\n+- For `create`, the PEP passes `resource.properties.owner_tenant_id` and `resource.properties.user_id` from the SecurityContext. The PDP validates permission without returning constraints.\n+\n+#### Evaluation Request/Response Examples\n+\n+**Example 1: List Chats** (`GET /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"list\" },\n+  \"resource\": { \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\" },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response (P0 - user-owned chats only):\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+ORDER BY updated_at DESC\n+```\n+\n+**Example 2: Get Chat** (`GET /v1/chats/{id}`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"read\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"chat-456\"\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE id = 'chat-456'\n+  AND tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+```\n+\n+Result: 1 row -> return chat; 0 rows -> 404 Not Found (hides existence from unauthorized users).\n+\n+**Example 3: Create Chat** (`POST /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"create\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"properties\": {\n+      \"owner_tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\",\n+      \"user_id\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+    }\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": false,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{ \"decision\": true }\n+```\n+\n+PEP proceeds with INSERT. No constraints needed for create.\n+\n+**Example 4: Send Message** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`)\n+\n+Same authorization flow as Example 2 (Get Chat), but with `\"action\": { \"name\": \"send_message\" }` and `\"resource.id\"` set to the chat ID. Authorization is evaluated once before the SSE stream is established. The constraints are applied to the query that loads the chat and its messages from `chat_store`.\n+\n+#### Fail-Closed Behavior\n+\n+Mini Chat follows the platform's fail-closed rules (see [Authorization Design - Fail-Closed Rules](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#fail-closed-rules)):\n+\n+| Condition | PEP Action |\n+|-----------|------------|\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` present | 404 Not Found (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` absent | 403 Forbidden (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| PDP unreachable / timeout | 403 Forbidden (fail-closed) |\n+| `decision: true` + no constraints + `require_constraints: true` | 403 Forbidden |\n+| Unknown predicate type in constraints | Treat constraint as false; if all constraints false -> 403 |\n+| Unknown `resource_property` in predicate | Treat constraint as false; log error (PDP contract violation) |\n+| Empty `constraints: []` | 403 Forbidden |\n+\n+#### Token Scopes\n+\n+Mini Chat recognizes the following token scopes for third-party application narrowing:\n+\n+| Scope | Permits |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| `ai:chat` | All chat operations (umbrella scope) |\n+| `ai:chat:read` | `list`, `read` actions only |\n+| `ai:chat:write` | `create`, `update`, `delete`, `send_message`, `upload` actions |\n+\n+First-party applications (UI) use `token_scopes: [\"*\"]`. Third-party integrations receive narrowed scopes. Scope enforcement is handled by the PDP - the PEP includes `token_scopes` in the evaluation request context.\n+\n+#### P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\n+\n+When Projects / chat sharing is introduced (P2+), the authorization model extends naturally:\n+\n+1. Add `group_membership` capability (and optionally `group_hierarchy`).\n+2. Maintain `resource_group_membership` projection table mapping chat IDs to project group IDs.\n+3. The PDP returns additional access paths via OR'd constraints - e.g., one constraint for owned chats (`user_id` predicate), another for shared-via-project chats (`in_group` predicate).\n+4. `supported_properties` remains unchanged (the `id` property is used for group membership joins).\n+\n+No changes to the PEP flow or constraint compilation logic are needed. The PDP's response structure naturally handles multiple access paths through OR'd constraints.\n+\n+## 4. Additional Context\n+\n+### P0 Scope Boundaries\n+\n+**Included in P0**:\n+- Single-tenant vector store per tenant (not per workspace)\n+- Thread summary as only compression mechanism\n+- On-upload document summary via File Search (Variant 1 from draft)\n+- Quota enforcement: daily + monthly per user\n+- Temporary chats with 24h scheduled cleanup\n+- File Search per-message call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2 tool calls per message)\n+\n+**Deferred to P2+**:\n+- Projects / chat sharing\n+- Full-text search across chats\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are both supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies (beyond simple limits)\n+- Per-workspace vector stores\n+\n+### Data Classification and Retention (P0)\n+\n+Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data. Mini Chat treats messages and summaries as customer content and applies data minimization and retention controls.\n+\n+**Data classes**:\n+- Chat content: `messages.content`, `thread_summaries.summary_text`, `attachments.doc_summary`\n+- Operational metadata: IDs, timestamps, provider correlation IDs, quotas\n+- Audit events: structured records emitted to platform `audit_service`\n+\n+**Retention**:\n+- Temporary chats are scheduled for cleanup after 24h and MUST be deleted within 25h (PRD SLA).\n+- Non-temporary chats are retained until explicit deletion by default, subject to operator-configured retention policies.\n+- Soft-deleted chats (`deleted_at` set) are hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+**Audit content handling (P0)**:\n+- Audit events include the minimal content required for security and incident response.\n+- `chat_service` MUST redact secret patterns (tokens, keys, credentials) before sending content to `audit_service`.\n+- Redaction MUST be testable and based on a bounded allowlist of rule classes.\n+\n+| Rule class | Example (non-exhaustive) | Action |\n+|-----------|---------------------------|--------|\n+| `bearer_token` | `Authorization: Bearer eyJ...` | Replace token with `Bearer [REDACTED]` |\n+| `jwt` | `eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJS...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_JWT]` |\n+| `openai_api_key` | `sk-...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]` |\n+| `aws_access_key_id` | `AKIA...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]` |\n+| `azure_api_key` | `api-key: ...`, `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: ...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]` |\n+| `api_key_fields` | `api_key=...`, `x-api-key=...`, `client_secret=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `password_fields` | `password=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `pem_private_key` | `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` | Replace block with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]` |\n+| `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+- Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.\n+- Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a maximum of 8 KiB per field (append `…[TRUNCATED]`).\n+- Full DLP-based content redaction is deferred to P2.\n+\n+### Context Plan Assembly Rules\n+\n+On each user message, `chat_service` assembles a `ContextPlan` in this order:\n+\n+1. **System prompt** - fixed instructions for the assistant. The system prompt is configuration and is not persisted as a `messages` row. For debugging, the prompt version SHOULD be recorded on the assistant message (for example in `features_used` or provider metadata).\n+2. **Thread summary** - if exists, replaces older history\n+3. **Document summaries** - short descriptions of attached documents\n+4. **Recent messages** - last 6-10 messages not covered by summary\n+5. **User message** - current turn\n+\n+If the total exceeds the input token budget, truncation happens in reverse priority: old messages first, then doc summaries, then retrieval excerpts. Summary and system prompt are never truncated.\n+\n+### File Search Trigger Heuristics\n+\n+File Search is invoked when:\n+- User explicitly references documents (\"in the file\", \"in section\", \"according to the document\")\n+- Documents are attached and the query likely relates to them\n+- User requests citations or sources\n+\n+Limits: per-message file_search tool call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2); max calls per day/user tracked in `quota_usage`.\n+\n+### Three-Tier Rate Limiting & Throttling\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-throttling-tiers`\n+\n+Rate limiting and quota enforcement are split into three tiers with strict ownership boundaries. These tiers MUST NOT be mixed - each has a single owner and distinct responsibility.\n+\n+| Tier | Owner | What It Controls | Examples                                                                                              |\n+|------|-------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Product quota** | `quota_service` (in `chat_service`) | Per-user daily/monthly token budgets, premium/standard model allocation, file_search call limits, auto-downgrade | \"Premium calls exhausted -> downgrade to gpt-5-mini\"; \"Daily limit reached -> 429 `quota_exceeded`\" |\n+| **Platform rate limit** | `api_gateway` middleware | Per-user/per-IP request rate, concurrent stream caps, abuse protection | \"20 rps per user\"; \"Max 5 concurrent SSE streams\"                                                     |\n+| **Provider rate limit** | OAGW | Provider 429 handling, `Retry-After` respect, circuit breaker, global concurrency cap | \"OpenAI 429 -> wait `Retry-After` -> retry once -> propagate 429 upstream\"                            |\n+\n+**Key rules**:\n+- Product quota decisions happen BEFORE the request reaches OAGW. If quota is exhausted, the request never leaves `chat_service`.\n+- OAGW does NOT know about tenants, users, licenses, or premium status. It handles only provider-level concerns (retry, circuit breaker, concurrency cap).\n+- Provider 429 from OAGW is propagated to `chat_service`, which maps it to `rate_limited` (429) for the client with a meaningful error message.\n+- Mid-stream quota abort is NOT supported - quota is checked at preflight only. Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+**Quota counting flow**:\n+\n+```\n+Preflight (reserve) (before LLM call):\n+  estimate = tokens(ContextPlan) + max_output_tokens\n+  if estimate > remaining_quota -> downgrade model or reject\n+\n+Commit (after done event):\n+  actual = response.usage.input_tokens + response.usage.output_tokens\n+  quota_usage += actual\n+  (if actual > estimate -> debit overshoot, never cancel completed response)\n+```\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:53Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:53Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813020830","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020830"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813020830"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020830/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1267,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1277,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572932,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":1277,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020931","pull_request_review_id":3809301649,"id":2813020931,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq0sD","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```\n+ping*  (delta | tool | citations)*  (done | error)\n+```\n+\n+- Zero or more `ping` events may appear at any point.\n+- `delta`, `tool`, and `citations` events may interleave in any order.\n+- Exactly one terminal event (`done` or `error`) ends the stream.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-event-translation\"></a>\n+#### Provider Event Translation\n+\n+Provider-specific streaming events are internal to `llm_provider` and `chat_service`. They are never forwarded to clients. The translation layer maps provider events to the stable SSE protocol defined above.\n+\n+| Provider Event | Stable SSE Event | Notes |\n+|----------------|-----------------|-------|\n+| `response.output_text.delta` | `event: delta` (`type: \"text\"`) | Text content mapped 1:1. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.searching` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"start\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | Emitted when file_search tool is invoked. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.completed` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"done\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | `details` populated from search results metadata. |\n+| File search annotations in response | `event: citations` | Extracted from provider annotations, mapped to `items[]` schema. When provider annotations include ranges, `items[].span` SHOULD be populated as character offsets into the final assistant text. |\n+| `response.completed` | `event: done` | `usage` from `response.usage`; `provider.response_id` from `response.id`. |\n+| Provider HTTP error / disconnect | `event: error` (`code: \"provider_error\"` or `\"provider_timeout\"`) | Error details sanitized; provider internals not exposed. |\n+| Provider 429 | `event: error` (`code: \"rate_limited\"`) | After OAGW retry exhaustion. |\n+\n+This mapping is intentionally provider-agnostic in the stable contract. If the provider changes its event format or a new provider is added, only the translation layer in `llm_provider` is updated. The client contract remains unchanged.\n+\n+**Error Codes**:\n+\n+| Code | HTTP Status | Description |\n+|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `feature_not_licensed` | 403 | Tenant lacks `ai_chat` feature |\n+| `insufficient_permissions` | 403 | Subject lacks permission for the requested action (AuthZ Resolver denied) |\n+| `chat_not_found` | 404 | Chat does not exist or not accessible under current authorization constraints |\n+| `quota_exceeded` | 429 | User exceeded daily/monthly usage limit |\n+| `rate_limited` | 429 | Too many requests in time window |\n+| `file_too_large` | 413 | Uploaded file exceeds size limit |\n+| `unsupported_file_type` | 415 | File type not supported for upload |\n+| `provider_error` | 502 | LLM provider returned an error |\n+| `provider_timeout` | 504 | LLM provider request timed out |\n+\n+### 3.4 Internal Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency Module | Interface Used | Purpose |\n+|-------------------|----------------|---------|\n+| api_gateway (platform) | Axum router / middleware | HTTP request handling, SSE transport |\n+| authn (platform) | Middleware (JWT/opaque token) | Extract `user_id` + `tenant_id` from request |\n+| license_manager (platform) | Middleware | Check tenant has `ai_chat` feature; reject with 403 if not |\n+| authz_resolver (platform) | Access evaluation API (`/access/v1/evaluation`) | Obtain authorization decisions + SQL-compilable constraints for chat operations |\n+| audit_service (platform) | Event emitter | Receive structured audit events (prompts, responses, usage, policy decisions) |\n+| outbound_gateway (platform) | Internal HTTP | Egress to LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) with credential injection |\n+\n+**Dependency Rules**:\n+- `chat_service` never calls the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) directly; all external calls go through OAGW\n+- `SecurityContext` (user_id, tenant_id) propagated through all in-process calls\n+- `license_manager` runs as middleware before `chat_service` is invoked\n+- `chat_service` calls `authz_resolver` before every database query; on PDP denial or PDP unreachable, fail-closed (deny access)\n+- `chat_service` emits audit events to `audit_service` after each turn; mini-chat does not store audit data locally\n+\n+### 3.5 External Dependencies\n+\n+#### LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\n+\n+Both providers expose a compatible API surface. OAGW routes requests to the configured provider and injects credentials accordingly.\n+\n+| API | Purpose | OAGW Route | OpenAI Endpoint | Azure OpenAI Endpoint |\n+|-----|---------|------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| Responses API (streaming) | Chat completion with tool support | `POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Responses API (non-streaming) | Thread summary generation, doc summary | `POST /outbound/llm/responses` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Files API | Upload user documents | `POST /outbound/llm/files` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/files` |\n+| Vector Stores API | Manage per-tenant vector stores, add/remove files | `POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/vector_stores/*` |\n+| File Search (tool) | Retrieve document excerpts during chat | - (invoked as tool within Responses API call) | `file_search` tool | `file_search` tool (identical contract) |\n+\n+Note: Azure OpenAI path variants differ by rollout; OAGW owns the exact path mapping for each API.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-api-mapping\"></a>\n+**Provider API Mapping** - authentication and endpoint differences:\n+\n+| Aspect | OpenAI | Azure OpenAI |\n+|--------|--------|--------------|\n+| **Base URL** | `https://api.openai.com/v1` | `https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1` |\n+| **Authentication** | `Authorization: Bearer {api_key}` | `api-key: {key}` header or Entra ID bearer token |\n+| **API version** | Not required | Azure may require an `api-version` query parameter depending on feature/rollout; OAGW owns this provider-specific detail |\n+| **File upload `purpose`** | `assistants` (P0) | `assistants` only (`user_data` not supported) |\n+| **Vector stores per `file_search`** | Multiple | **One** (sufficient for P0: one store per tenant) |\n+| **SSE format** | `event:` + `data:` lines, structured events | Identical format |\n+| **`user` field** | Supported | Supported (feeds into Azure abuse monitoring) |\n+| **`metadata` object** | Supported | Supported |\n+\n+OAGW MUST inject the required `api-version` query parameter when calling Azure OpenAI endpoints.\n+\n+**OAGW throttling scope**: OAGW handles provider-side rate limiting only - retry on provider 429 (with `Retry-After` respect, max 1 retry), circuit breaker when provider error rate exceeds threshold, and global concurrency cap as an SRE safety valve. Product-level quota enforcement (per-user, per-tenant, model downgrade) is NOT an OAGW concern - it is handled entirely by `chat_service` / `quota_service` before any outbound call (see constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+#### PostgreSQL\n+\n+| Usage | Purpose |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| Primary datastore | Chats, messages, attachments, summaries, quota counters, tenant vector store mappings |\n+\n+### 3.6 Interactions & Sequences\n+\n+#### Send Message with Streaming Response\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-send-message`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant AuthZ as authz_resolver (PDP)\n+    participant CS as chat_service (PEP)\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream\n+    AG->>AG: AuthN + license_manager\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(chat_id, user_msg, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>AuthZ: Evaluate(subject, action: \"send_message\", resource: {type: chat, id: chat_id})\n+    AuthZ-->>CS: decision + constraints\n+\n+    alt PDP unreachable / timeout\n+        CS-->>AG: 403 Forbidden\n+        AG-->>UI: 403\n+    else decision = false\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Load chat, recent messages, thread_summary, attachments, tenant vector_store_id (with constraints in WHERE)\n+\n+    alt 0 rows returned\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Build ContextPlan (system prompt + summary + doc summaries + recent msgs + user msg)\n+    CS->>CS: Preflight (reserve) quota check (estimate tokens from ContextPlan + max_output -> allow / downgrade / reject)\n+\n+    alt quota exhausted\n+        CS-->>AG: 429 quota_exceeded\n+        AG-->>UI: SSE error event\n+    end\n+\n+    Note over CS, OAI: Single provider call per user turn. file_search is enabled as a tool within the same streaming Responses API request.\n+\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream (tools include file_search, store=tenant_store)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API (streaming, tool calling enabled)\n+    OAI-->>OG: SSE tokens\n+    OG-->>CS: Token stream\n+    CS-->>AG: Token stream\n+    AG-->>UI: SSE tokens\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Persist user msg + assistant msg + usage\n+    CS->>CS: Commit actual usage to quota_service (debit input_tokens + output_tokens)\n+\n+    participant AS as audit_service\n+    CS->>AS: Emit audit event (usage, policy decisions)\n+\n+    opt Thread summary update triggered (background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Full lifecycle of a user message - from authorization through streaming LLM response to persistence and optional thread compression. Authorization is evaluated before any database access. The PEP sends an evaluation request to the AuthZ Resolver with the chat's resource type and ID; the returned constraints are applied to the DB query's WHERE clause. If the PDP is unreachable, the request is rejected immediately (fail-closed, 403). If the PDP denies access for an operation with a concrete `chat_id`, the PEP returns 404 to avoid disclosing resource existence. If the constrained query returns 0 rows, the PEP returns 404.\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-file-upload`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments (multipart)\n+    AG->>CS: UploadAttachment(chat_id, file, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>DB: Insert attachment metadata (status: pending)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/files (upload)\n+    OG->>OAI: Files API upload\n+    OAI-->>OG: file_id\n+    OG-->>CS: file_id\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/{tenant_store}/files (add file)\n+    OG->>OAI: Add file to vector store\n+    OAI-->>OG: OK\n+    OG-->>CS: OK\n+    CS->>DB: Update attachment (status: ready, provider_file_id)\n+\n+    opt Generate doc summary (Variant 1, background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue doc summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+\n+    CS-->>AG: attachment_id, status\n+    AG-->>UI: 201 Created\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: File upload flow - the file is uploaded to the LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) via OAGW, added to the tenant's vector store, optionally summarized, and metadata is persisted locally.\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-cancellation`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant LP as llm_provider\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: SSE connection established\n+    AG->>AG: Create CancellationToken\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(..., cancel_token)\n+    CS->>LP: stream_response(..., cancel_token)\n+    LP->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream\n+    OG->>OAI: Streaming in progress\n+\n+    alt UI disconnect\n+        UI--xAG: Client disconnects / stop button\n+        AG->>AG: Detect broken SSE write -> cancel token\n+    else Server timeout\n+        CS->>CS: Timeout elapsed -> cancel token\n+    end\n+\n+    LP->>LP: Token cancelled -> abort HTTP request\n+    LP->>OG: Close connection (hard cancel)\n+    OG->>OAI: TCP RST / connection closed\n+    CS->>CS: Persist partial response (usage may be unknown)\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Cancellation propagates end-to-end via a shared `CancellationToken`. When triggered, `llm_provider` performs a hard cancel - aborting the outbound HTTP connection so the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) stops generating immediately. The partial response is persisted. Because provider usage is typically only delivered on a completed response, `input_tokens`/`output_tokens` may be NULL or approximate for cancelled turns; quota enforcement uses the bounded best-effort debit described in `quota_service`.\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Update\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-thread-summary`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Check summary trigger (msg count > 20 OR tokens > budget OR every 15 user turns)\n+    CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    Note over CS: Background worker executes summary task asynchronously\n+    CS->>DB: Load current thread_summary + old messages batch (10-20 msgs)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses (update summary prompt)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API\n+    OAI-->>OG: Updated summary\n+    OG-->>CS: Updated summary\n+    CS->>DB: Save new thread_summary\n+    CS->>DB: Mark summarized messages as compressed\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Thread summary is updated asynchronously after a chat turn when trigger conditions are met. Summary generation is a background task and MUST be attributed as `requester_type=system` so that its usage is not charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+Summary quality gate (P0): the system MUST detect and mitigate obviously-bad summaries without ML-based evaluation.\n+\n+- After generating a summary, `chat_service` MUST validate the candidate summary text.\n+- If summary length < `X` OR entropy < `Y`, `chat_service` MUST attempt regeneration.\n+- If regeneration fails quality checks or the provider call fails, `chat_service` MUST fall back by keeping the previous summary unchanged and MUST NOT mark the message batch as compressed.\n+\n+`X` and `Y` are configurable thresholds. Entropy is a deterministic proxy computed as normalized token entropy over whitespace-delimited tokens:\n+\n+`H_norm = (-sum(p_i * log2(p_i))) / log2(N)` where `p_i` is the empirical frequency of token `i` and `N` is the number of distinct tokens.\n+\n+Observability:\n+\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}` for each regeneration attempt (`reason` from a bounded allowlist such as `too_short|low_entropy|provider_error|invalid_format`).\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total` when the fallback behavior above is used.\n+\n+### 3.7 Database Schemas & Tables\n+\n+#### Table: chats\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chats`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Chat identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | Owning user |\n+| title | VARCHAR(255) | Chat title (user-set or auto-generated) |\n+| is_temporary | BOOLEAN | If true, auto-deleted after 24h |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last activity time |\n+| deleted_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Soft delete timestamp (nullable) |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `created_at`\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, updated_at DESC)` for listing chats\n+\n+#### Table: messages\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-messages`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Message identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key (nullable). Used for completed replay and traceability. Running state is tracked in `chat_turns`. |\n+| role | VARCHAR(16) | `user`, `assistant`, or `system` |\n+| content | TEXT | Message content |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| request_kind | VARCHAR(16) | `chat`, `summary`, or `doc_summary` (nullable) |\n+| features_used | JSONB | Feature flags and counters (nullable) |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Actual input tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Actual output tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| model | VARCHAR(64) | Actual model used for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| is_compressed | BOOLEAN | True if included in a thread summary |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `chat_id`, `role`, `content`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE. UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)` WHERE `request_id IS NOT NULL`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(chat_id, created_at)` for loading recent messages\n+\n+#### Table: chat_turns\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chat-turns`\n+\n+Tracks idempotency and in-progress generation state for `request_id`. This avoids ambiguous interpretation of `messages.request_id` when a generation is still running.\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Turn identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key |\n+| requester_type | VARCHAR(16) | `user` or `system` |\n+| requester_user_id | UUID | User ID when requester_type=`user` (nullable for system) |\n+| state | VARCHAR(16) | `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier (nullable until request starts) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID (nullable) |\n+| assistant_message_id | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID (nullable until completed) |\n+| error_code | VARCHAR(64) | Terminal error code (nullable) |\n+| started_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Start time |\n+| completed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Completion time (nullable) |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+A `chat_turns` row MUST be created before starting the outbound provider request; initial state is `running`.\n+\n+State machine:\n+- Allowed transitions: `running` -> `completed` \\| `failed` \\| `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states MUST be immutable\n+- At most one `running` turn per `(chat_id, request_id)`\n+\n+#### Table: attachments\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-attachments`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Attachment identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| filename | VARCHAR(255) | Original filename |\n+| content_type | VARCHAR(128) | MIME type |\n+| size_bytes | BIGINT | File size |\n+| provider_file_id | VARCHAR(128) | LLM provider file ID - OpenAI `file-*` or Azure OpenAI `assistant-*` (nullable until upload completes) |\n+| status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `ready`, `failed` |\n+| doc_summary | TEXT | LLM-generated document summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_model | VARCHAR(64) | Model used to generate the summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When the summary was last generated (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `in_progress`, `done`, `failed` (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_attempts | INTEGER | Cleanup retry attempts (default 0) |\n+| last_cleanup_error | TEXT | Last cleanup error (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When cleanup state was last updated (nullable) |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Upload time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `chat_id`, `filename`, `status`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: thread_summaries\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-thread-summaries`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Summary identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id, UNIQUE) |\n+| summary_text | TEXT | Compressed conversation summary |\n+| summarized_up_to | UUID | Last message ID included in this summary |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count of summary |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `chat_id`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: tenant_vector_stores\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-vector-stores`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant identifier |\n+| vector_store_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider vector store ID (OpenAI `vs_*` or Azure OpenAI equivalent) |\n+| provider | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier: `openai` or `azure_openai` |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `tenant_id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `vector_store_id`, `provider`, `created_at`. One vector store per tenant.\n+\n+Creation protocol (P0): `chat_service` uses a get-or-create flow with database uniqueness as the race arbiter.\n+\n+1. Attempt to read the row by `tenant_id`.\n+2. If not present, create the vector store via OAGW and attempt INSERT.\n+3. If INSERT fails due to unique violation (concurrent creator), re-read the row and use the existing `vector_store_id`. Best-effort delete the newly created provider vector store to avoid orphans.\n+\n+ **P0**: run a periodic reconcile/orphan reaper job (for example nightly) to reconcile provider state with `tenant_vector_stores`:\n+ - If a provider vector store exists but is not referenced in DB -> delete it.\n+ - If a DB row exists but the provider vector store is missing -> recreate the vector store and update the DB row.\n+\n+#### Table: quota_usage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-quota-usage`\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-quota-usage-accounting`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Record identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | User |\n+| period_type | VARCHAR(16) | `daily` or `monthly` |\n+| period_start | DATE | Start of the period |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Total input tokens consumed |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Total output tokens consumed |\n+| file_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of file search tool calls |\n+| web_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of web search calls (P1+) |\n+| rag_retrieval_calls | INTEGER | Number of internal RAG retrieval calls (P1+) |\n+| premium_model_calls | INTEGER | Calls to premium models |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+Commit semantics: quota updates MUST be atomic per period record. Implementations SHOULD use a transaction with row locking or a single UPDATE statement to avoid race conditions under parallel streams.\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` for quota lookups\n+\n+#### Projection Table: tenant_closure\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-closure-ref`\n+\n+Mini Chat does NOT require the `tenant_closure` local projection table for chat content access in P0. Chat content is owner-only and requires exact `owner_tenant_id` + `user_id` predicates.\n+\n+The `tenant_closure` projection table exists in the platform authorization model for modules that use hierarchical tenant scoping, but it is unused for Mini Chat content operations.\n+\n+Schema is defined in the [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#table-schemas-local-projections).\n+\n+**P2+ note**: When chat sharing (projects) is introduced, `resource_group_membership` and optionally `resource_group_closure` tables will also be required.\n+\n+### 3.8 Authorization (PEP)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-authz-pep`\n+\n+Mini Chat acts as a Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) per the platform's PDP/PEP authorization model defined in [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). The `chat_service` builds evaluation requests, sends them to the AuthZ Resolver (PDP), and compiles returned constraints into SQL WHERE clauses.\n+\n+Policy (P0): chat content is owner-only. For all content operations, authorization MUST enforce:\n+\n+- `owner_tenant_id == subject.tenant_id`\n+- `user_id == subject.id`\n+\n+Mini Chat does not use tenant hierarchy or subtree-based authorization for content operations.\n+\n+#### Resource Type\n+\n+The authorized resource is **Chat**. Sub-resources (Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary) do not have independent authorization - they are accessed through their parent chat, and the chat's authorization decision covers all child operations.\n+\n+| Attribute | Value |\n+|-----------|-------|\n+| GTS Type ID | `gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~` |\n+| Primary table | `chats` |\n+| Authorization granularity | Chat-level (sub-resources inherit) |\n+\n+#### PEP Configuration\n+\n+**Capabilities** (declared in `context.capabilities`):\n+\n+| Capability | P0 | P2+ | Rationale |\n+|------------|-----|------|-----------|\n+| `tenant_hierarchy` | No | No | Chat content is owner-only; `tenant_closure` is unused for Mini Chat content operations |\n+| `group_membership` | No | Yes | Needed when chat sharing via projects is introduced |\n+| `group_hierarchy` | No | Maybe | Needed if projects have nested hierarchy |\n+\n+**Supported properties** (declared in `context.supported_properties`):\n+\n+| Resource Property | SQL Column | Description |\n+|-------------------|------------|-------------|\n+| `owner_tenant_id` | `chats.tenant_id` | Owning tenant |\n+| `user_id` | `chats.user_id` | Owning user |\n+| `id` | `chats.id` | Chat identifier |\n+\n+#### Per-Operation Authorization Matrix\n+\n+| Endpoint | Action | `resource.id` | `require_constraints` | Expected P0 Predicates |\n+|----------|--------|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n+| `POST /v1/chats` | `create` | absent | `false` | decision only (no constraints) |\n+| `GET /v1/chats` | `list` | absent | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}` | `read` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `DELETE /v1/chats/{id}` | `delete` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | `update` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | `send_message` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | `upload` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | `read_turn` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+\n+**Notes**:\n+- `send_message` and `upload` are actions on the Chat resource, not on Message or Attachment resources. The `resource.id` is the chat's ID.\n+- For streaming (`send_message`), authorization is evaluated once at SSE connection establishment. The entire streaming session operates under the initial authorization decision. No per-message re-authorization.\n+- For `create`, the PEP passes `resource.properties.owner_tenant_id` and `resource.properties.user_id` from the SecurityContext. The PDP validates permission without returning constraints.\n+\n+#### Evaluation Request/Response Examples\n+\n+**Example 1: List Chats** (`GET /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"list\" },\n+  \"resource\": { \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\" },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response (P0 - user-owned chats only):\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+ORDER BY updated_at DESC\n+```\n+\n+**Example 2: Get Chat** (`GET /v1/chats/{id}`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"read\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"chat-456\"\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE id = 'chat-456'\n+  AND tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+```\n+\n+Result: 1 row -> return chat; 0 rows -> 404 Not Found (hides existence from unauthorized users).\n+\n+**Example 3: Create Chat** (`POST /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"create\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"properties\": {\n+      \"owner_tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\",\n+      \"user_id\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+    }\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": false,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{ \"decision\": true }\n+```\n+\n+PEP proceeds with INSERT. No constraints needed for create.\n+\n+**Example 4: Send Message** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`)\n+\n+Same authorization flow as Example 2 (Get Chat), but with `\"action\": { \"name\": \"send_message\" }` and `\"resource.id\"` set to the chat ID. Authorization is evaluated once before the SSE stream is established. The constraints are applied to the query that loads the chat and its messages from `chat_store`.\n+\n+#### Fail-Closed Behavior\n+\n+Mini Chat follows the platform's fail-closed rules (see [Authorization Design - Fail-Closed Rules](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#fail-closed-rules)):\n+\n+| Condition | PEP Action |\n+|-----------|------------|\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` present | 404 Not Found (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` absent | 403 Forbidden (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| PDP unreachable / timeout | 403 Forbidden (fail-closed) |\n+| `decision: true` + no constraints + `require_constraints: true` | 403 Forbidden |\n+| Unknown predicate type in constraints | Treat constraint as false; if all constraints false -> 403 |\n+| Unknown `resource_property` in predicate | Treat constraint as false; log error (PDP contract violation) |\n+| Empty `constraints: []` | 403 Forbidden |\n+\n+#### Token Scopes\n+\n+Mini Chat recognizes the following token scopes for third-party application narrowing:\n+\n+| Scope | Permits |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| `ai:chat` | All chat operations (umbrella scope) |\n+| `ai:chat:read` | `list`, `read` actions only |\n+| `ai:chat:write` | `create`, `update`, `delete`, `send_message`, `upload` actions |\n+\n+First-party applications (UI) use `token_scopes: [\"*\"]`. Third-party integrations receive narrowed scopes. Scope enforcement is handled by the PDP - the PEP includes `token_scopes` in the evaluation request context.\n+\n+#### P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\n+\n+When Projects / chat sharing is introduced (P2+), the authorization model extends naturally:\n+\n+1. Add `group_membership` capability (and optionally `group_hierarchy`).\n+2. Maintain `resource_group_membership` projection table mapping chat IDs to project group IDs.\n+3. The PDP returns additional access paths via OR'd constraints - e.g., one constraint for owned chats (`user_id` predicate), another for shared-via-project chats (`in_group` predicate).\n+4. `supported_properties` remains unchanged (the `id` property is used for group membership joins).\n+\n+No changes to the PEP flow or constraint compilation logic are needed. The PDP's response structure naturally handles multiple access paths through OR'd constraints.\n+\n+## 4. Additional Context\n+\n+### P0 Scope Boundaries\n+\n+**Included in P0**:\n+- Single-tenant vector store per tenant (not per workspace)\n+- Thread summary as only compression mechanism\n+- On-upload document summary via File Search (Variant 1 from draft)\n+- Quota enforcement: daily + monthly per user\n+- Temporary chats with 24h scheduled cleanup\n+- File Search per-message call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2 tool calls per message)\n+\n+**Deferred to P2+**:\n+- Projects / chat sharing\n+- Full-text search across chats\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are both supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies (beyond simple limits)\n+- Per-workspace vector stores\n+\n+### Data Classification and Retention (P0)\n+\n+Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data. Mini Chat treats messages and summaries as customer content and applies data minimization and retention controls.\n+\n+**Data classes**:\n+- Chat content: `messages.content`, `thread_summaries.summary_text`, `attachments.doc_summary`\n+- Operational metadata: IDs, timestamps, provider correlation IDs, quotas\n+- Audit events: structured records emitted to platform `audit_service`\n+\n+**Retention**:\n+- Temporary chats are scheduled for cleanup after 24h and MUST be deleted within 25h (PRD SLA).\n+- Non-temporary chats are retained until explicit deletion by default, subject to operator-configured retention policies.\n+- Soft-deleted chats (`deleted_at` set) are hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+**Audit content handling (P0)**:\n+- Audit events include the minimal content required for security and incident response.\n+- `chat_service` MUST redact secret patterns (tokens, keys, credentials) before sending content to `audit_service`.\n+- Redaction MUST be testable and based on a bounded allowlist of rule classes.\n+\n+| Rule class | Example (non-exhaustive) | Action |\n+|-----------|---------------------------|--------|\n+| `bearer_token` | `Authorization: Bearer eyJ...` | Replace token with `Bearer [REDACTED]` |\n+| `jwt` | `eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJS...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_JWT]` |\n+| `openai_api_key` | `sk-...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]` |\n+| `aws_access_key_id` | `AKIA...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]` |\n+| `azure_api_key` | `api-key: ...`, `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: ...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]` |\n+| `api_key_fields` | `api_key=...`, `x-api-key=...`, `client_secret=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `password_fields` | `password=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `pem_private_key` | `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` | Replace block with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]` |\n+| `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+- Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.\n+- Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a maximum of 8 KiB per field (append `…[TRUNCATED]`).\n+- Full DLP-based content redaction is deferred to P2.\n+\n+### Context Plan Assembly Rules\n+\n+On each user message, `chat_service` assembles a `ContextPlan` in this order:\n+\n+1. **System prompt** - fixed instructions for the assistant. The system prompt is configuration and is not persisted as a `messages` row. For debugging, the prompt version SHOULD be recorded on the assistant message (for example in `features_used` or provider metadata).\n+2. **Thread summary** - if exists, replaces older history\n+3. **Document summaries** - short descriptions of attached documents\n+4. **Recent messages** - last 6-10 messages not covered by summary\n+5. **User message** - current turn\n+\n+If the total exceeds the input token budget, truncation happens in reverse priority: old messages first, then doc summaries, then retrieval excerpts. Summary and system prompt are never truncated.\n+\n+### File Search Trigger Heuristics\n+\n+File Search is invoked when:\n+- User explicitly references documents (\"in the file\", \"in section\", \"according to the document\")\n+- Documents are attached and the query likely relates to them\n+- User requests citations or sources\n+\n+Limits: per-message file_search tool call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2); max calls per day/user tracked in `quota_usage`.\n+\n+### Three-Tier Rate Limiting & Throttling\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-throttling-tiers`\n+\n+Rate limiting and quota enforcement are split into three tiers with strict ownership boundaries. These tiers MUST NOT be mixed - each has a single owner and distinct responsibility.\n+\n+| Tier | Owner | What It Controls | Examples                                                                                              |\n+|------|-------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Product quota** | `quota_service` (in `chat_service`) | Per-user daily/monthly token budgets, premium/standard model allocation, file_search call limits, auto-downgrade | \"Premium calls exhausted -> downgrade to gpt-5-mini\"; \"Daily limit reached -> 429 `quota_exceeded`\" |\n+| **Platform rate limit** | `api_gateway` middleware | Per-user/per-IP request rate, concurrent stream caps, abuse protection | \"20 rps per user\"; \"Max 5 concurrent SSE streams\"                                                     |\n+| **Provider rate limit** | OAGW | Provider 429 handling, `Retry-After` respect, circuit breaker, global concurrency cap | \"OpenAI 429 -> wait `Retry-After` -> retry once -> propagate 429 upstream\"                            |\n+\n+**Key rules**:\n+- Product quota decisions happen BEFORE the request reaches OAGW. If quota is exhausted, the request never leaves `chat_service`.\n+- OAGW does NOT know about tenants, users, licenses, or premium status. It handles only provider-level concerns (retry, circuit breaker, concurrency cap).\n+- Provider 429 from OAGW is propagated to `chat_service`, which maps it to `rate_limited` (429) for the client with a meaningful error message.\n+- Mid-stream quota abort is NOT supported - quota is checked at preflight only. Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+**Quota counting flow**:\n+\n+```\n+Preflight (reserve) (before LLM call):\n+  estimate = tokens(ContextPlan) + max_output_tokens\n+  if estimate > remaining_quota -> downgrade model or reject\n+\n+Commit (after done event):\n+  actual = response.usage.input_tokens + response.usage.output_tokens\n+  quota_usage += actual\n+  (if actual > estimate -> debit overshoot, never cancel completed response)\n+```\n+\n+### Provider Request Metadata\n+\n+Every request sent to the LLM provider via `llm_provider` MUST include two identification mechanisms. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI support these fields with identical semantics.\n+\n+**`user` field** - composite tenant+user identifier for provider usage monitoring and abuse detection:\n+\n+```\n+\"user\": \"{tenant_id}:{user_id}\"\n+```","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:54Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:54Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813020931","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020931"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813020931"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813020931/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1284,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1286,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572940,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":1286,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021049","pull_request_review_id":3809301774,"id":2813021049,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq0t5","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,129 @@\n+---\n+status: accepted\n+date: 2026-02-14\n+decision-makers: Mike Yastrebtsov, Alex Henry\n+---\n+\n+<!--\n+=============================================================================\n+ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD (ADR) - based on MADR format\n+=============================================================================\n+PURPOSE: Capture WHY a significant technical decision was made - context,\n+options considered, trade-offs, and consequences.\n+\n+SCOPE:\n+✓ Context and problem statement\n+✓ Decision drivers (constraints, quality attributes)\n+✓ Options considered with pros/cons\n+✓ Chosen option with justification\n+✓ Consequences (good and bad)\n+\n+NOT IN THIS DOCUMENT (see other templates):\n+✗ Requirements -> PRD.md\n+✗ Full architecture description -> DESIGN.md\n+✗ Implementation details -> features/\n+\n+RULES:\n+- ADRs represent actual decision dilemma and decision state\n+- DESIGN is the primary artifact (\"what\"); ADRs annotate DESIGN with rationale (\"why\")\n+- Avoid \"everything is a decision\"; write ADRs only when the rationale needs to be explained and recorded for traceability\n+- Decision history is in git, not in documents\n+- Use single ADR per decision\n+\n+STANDARDS ALIGNMENT:\n+- MADR (Markdown Any Decision Records)\n+- IEEE 42010 (architecture decisions as first-class elements)\n+- ISO/IEC 15288 / 12207 (decision analysis process)\n+  ==============================================================================\n+  -->\n+\n+# Group chat ownership and usage attribution model (tenant-owned, sender-attributed)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution`","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/0003-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Fixed — ADR-0003 added to DESIGN.md traceability section.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:56Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:56Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021049","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021049"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021049"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021049/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":42,"original_line":42,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572909,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":42,"position":42,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021310","pull_request_review_id":3809302043,"id":2813021310,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq0x-","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"These IDs are defined in ADR files (modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/), not in DESIGN.md. This is intentional — ADR documents cover deferred features and architectural decisions that are out of P0 DESIGN scope.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:59Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:38:59Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021310","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021310"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021310"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021310/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":110,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":112,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572946,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":112,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021419","pull_request_review_id":3809302150,"id":2813021419,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq0zr","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"These IDs are defined in ADR files (modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/), not in DESIGN.md. This is intentional — ADR documents cover deferred features and architectural decisions that are out of P0 DESIGN scope.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:00Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:00Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021419","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021419"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021419"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021419/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":535,"original_start_line":353,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":665,"original_line":461,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572951,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":461,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021539","pull_request_review_id":3809302296,"id":2813021539,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq01j","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"These IDs are defined in ADR files (modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/), not in DESIGN.md. This is intentional — ADR documents cover deferred features and architectural decisions that are out of P0 DESIGN scope.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:02Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:02Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021539","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021539"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021539"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021539/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":488,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":494,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572953,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":494,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021690","pull_request_review_id":3809302428,"id":2813021690,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq036","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+\n+#### Turn Status (read-only) API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status`\n+\n+Support and UX recovery flows SHOULD be able to query authoritative turn state backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"These IDs are defined in ADR files (modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/), not in DESIGN.md. This is intentional — ADR documents cover deferred features and architectural decisions that are out of P0 DESIGN scope.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:03Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:04Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021690","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021690"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021690"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021690/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":731,"original_start_line":497,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":744,"original_line":509,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572957,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":509,"position":744,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021792","pull_request_review_id":3809302537,"id":2813021792,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq05g","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+\n+#### Turn Status (read-only) API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status`\n+\n+Support and UX recovery flows SHOULD be able to query authoritative turn state backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### SSE Streaming Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library\n+**Protocol/Format**: Server-Sent Events (SSE) over HTTP\n+**Compatibility**: Event types (`delta`, `tool`, `citations`, `done`, `error`, `ping`) and their payload schemas are stable within a major API version.\n+\n+**Ordering**: `ping* (delta|tool|citations)* (done|error)`.\n+\n+Provider identifiers (e.g., `file_id` in `citations`) are display-only metadata; clients MUST NOT send them back to any API.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"These IDs are defined in ADR files (modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/), not in DESIGN.md. This is intentional — ADR documents cover deferred features and architectural decisions that are out of P0 DESIGN scope.","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:05Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:05Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021792","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021792"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813021792"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813021792/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":758,"original_start_line":514,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":793,"original_line":523,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572960,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":523,"position":793,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813022289","pull_request_review_id":3809303012,"id":2813022289,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq1BR","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@MikeFalcon77`, thank you for addressing the markdown formatting issue! The list structure should now be consistent with the rest of the document.\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:11Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:11Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813022289","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813022289"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813022289"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813022289/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":238,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":240,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572916,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":240,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813022945","pull_request_review_id":3809303582,"id":2813022945,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq1Lh","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@MikeFalcon77`, thank you for addressing this! \n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : Use `## ADDED|MODIFIED|REMOVED|RENAMED Requirements` headers in delta spec files with at least one `#### Scenario:` per requirement\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:19Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813022945","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813022945"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813022945"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813022945/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":340,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":440,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572922,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":440,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813022947","pull_request_review_id":3809303580,"id":2813022947,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq1Lj","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```\n+ping*  (delta | tool | citations)*  (done | error)\n+```\n+\n+- Zero or more `ping` events may appear at any point.\n+- `delta`, `tool`, and `citations` events may interleave in any order.\n+- Exactly one terminal event (`done` or `error`) ends the stream.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-event-translation\"></a>\n+#### Provider Event Translation\n+\n+Provider-specific streaming events are internal to `llm_provider` and `chat_service`. They are never forwarded to clients. The translation layer maps provider events to the stable SSE protocol defined above.\n+\n+| Provider Event | Stable SSE Event | Notes |\n+|----------------|-----------------|-------|\n+| `response.output_text.delta` | `event: delta` (`type: \"text\"`) | Text content mapped 1:1. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.searching` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"start\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | Emitted when file_search tool is invoked. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.completed` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"done\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | `details` populated from search results metadata. |\n+| File search annotations in response | `event: citations` | Extracted from provider annotations, mapped to `items[]` schema. When provider annotations include ranges, `items[].span` SHOULD be populated as character offsets into the final assistant text. |\n+| `response.completed` | `event: done` | `usage` from `response.usage`; `provider.response_id` from `response.id`. |\n+| Provider HTTP error / disconnect | `event: error` (`code: \"provider_error\"` or `\"provider_timeout\"`) | Error details sanitized; provider internals not exposed. |\n+| Provider 429 | `event: error` (`code: \"rate_limited\"`) | After OAGW retry exhaustion. |\n+\n+This mapping is intentionally provider-agnostic in the stable contract. If the provider changes its event format or a new provider is added, only the translation layer in `llm_provider` is updated. The client contract remains unchanged.\n+\n+**Error Codes**:\n+\n+| Code | HTTP Status | Description |\n+|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `feature_not_licensed` | 403 | Tenant lacks `ai_chat` feature |\n+| `insufficient_permissions` | 403 | Subject lacks permission for the requested action (AuthZ Resolver denied) |\n+| `chat_not_found` | 404 | Chat does not exist or not accessible under current authorization constraints |\n+| `quota_exceeded` | 429 | User exceeded daily/monthly usage limit |\n+| `rate_limited` | 429 | Too many requests in time window |\n+| `file_too_large` | 413 | Uploaded file exceeds size limit |\n+| `unsupported_file_type` | 415 | File type not supported for upload |\n+| `provider_error` | 502 | LLM provider returned an error |\n+| `provider_timeout` | 504 | LLM provider request timed out |\n+\n+### 3.4 Internal Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency Module | Interface Used | Purpose |\n+|-------------------|----------------|---------|\n+| api_gateway (platform) | Axum router / middleware | HTTP request handling, SSE transport |\n+| authn (platform) | Middleware (JWT/opaque token) | Extract `user_id` + `tenant_id` from request |\n+| license_manager (platform) | Middleware | Check tenant has `ai_chat` feature; reject with 403 if not |\n+| authz_resolver (platform) | Access evaluation API (`/access/v1/evaluation`) | Obtain authorization decisions + SQL-compilable constraints for chat operations |\n+| audit_service (platform) | Event emitter | Receive structured audit events (prompts, responses, usage, policy decisions) |\n+| outbound_gateway (platform) | Internal HTTP | Egress to LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) with credential injection |\n+\n+**Dependency Rules**:\n+- `chat_service` never calls the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) directly; all external calls go through OAGW\n+- `SecurityContext` (user_id, tenant_id) propagated through all in-process calls\n+- `license_manager` runs as middleware before `chat_service` is invoked\n+- `chat_service` calls `authz_resolver` before every database query; on PDP denial or PDP unreachable, fail-closed (deny access)\n+- `chat_service` emits audit events to `audit_service` after each turn; mini-chat does not store audit data locally\n+\n+### 3.5 External Dependencies\n+\n+#### LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\n+\n+Both providers expose a compatible API surface. OAGW routes requests to the configured provider and injects credentials accordingly.\n+\n+| API | Purpose | OAGW Route | OpenAI Endpoint | Azure OpenAI Endpoint |\n+|-----|---------|------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| Responses API (streaming) | Chat completion with tool support | `POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Responses API (non-streaming) | Thread summary generation, doc summary | `POST /outbound/llm/responses` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Files API | Upload user documents | `POST /outbound/llm/files` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/files` |\n+| Vector Stores API | Manage per-tenant vector stores, add/remove files | `POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/vector_stores/*` |\n+| File Search (tool) | Retrieve document excerpts during chat | - (invoked as tool within Responses API call) | `file_search` tool | `file_search` tool (identical contract) |\n+\n+Note: Azure OpenAI path variants differ by rollout; OAGW owns the exact path mapping for each API.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-api-mapping\"></a>\n+**Provider API Mapping** - authentication and endpoint differences:\n+\n+| Aspect | OpenAI | Azure OpenAI |\n+|--------|--------|--------------|\n+| **Base URL** | `https://api.openai.com/v1` | `https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1` |\n+| **Authentication** | `Authorization: Bearer {api_key}` | `api-key: {key}` header or Entra ID bearer token |\n+| **API version** | Not required | Azure may require an `api-version` query parameter depending on feature/rollout; OAGW owns this provider-specific detail |\n+| **File upload `purpose`** | `assistants` (P0) | `assistants` only (`user_data` not supported) |\n+| **Vector stores per `file_search`** | Multiple | **One** (sufficient for P0: one store per tenant) |\n+| **SSE format** | `event:` + `data:` lines, structured events | Identical format |\n+| **`user` field** | Supported | Supported (feeds into Azure abuse monitoring) |\n+| **`metadata` object** | Supported | Supported |\n+\n+OAGW MUST inject the required `api-version` query parameter when calling Azure OpenAI endpoints.\n+\n+**OAGW throttling scope**: OAGW handles provider-side rate limiting only - retry on provider 429 (with `Retry-After` respect, max 1 retry), circuit breaker when provider error rate exceeds threshold, and global concurrency cap as an SRE safety valve. Product-level quota enforcement (per-user, per-tenant, model downgrade) is NOT an OAGW concern - it is handled entirely by `chat_service` / `quota_service` before any outbound call (see constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+#### PostgreSQL\n+\n+| Usage | Purpose |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| Primary datastore | Chats, messages, attachments, summaries, quota counters, tenant vector store mappings |\n+\n+### 3.6 Interactions & Sequences\n+\n+#### Send Message with Streaming Response\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-send-message`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant AuthZ as authz_resolver (PDP)\n+    participant CS as chat_service (PEP)\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream\n+    AG->>AG: AuthN + license_manager\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(chat_id, user_msg, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>AuthZ: Evaluate(subject, action: \"send_message\", resource: {type: chat, id: chat_id})\n+    AuthZ-->>CS: decision + constraints\n+\n+    alt PDP unreachable / timeout\n+        CS-->>AG: 403 Forbidden\n+        AG-->>UI: 403\n+    else decision = false\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Load chat, recent messages, thread_summary, attachments, tenant vector_store_id (with constraints in WHERE)\n+\n+    alt 0 rows returned\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Build ContextPlan (system prompt + summary + doc summaries + recent msgs + user msg)\n+    CS->>CS: Preflight (reserve) quota check (estimate tokens from ContextPlan + max_output -> allow / downgrade / reject)\n+\n+    alt quota exhausted\n+        CS-->>AG: 429 quota_exceeded\n+        AG-->>UI: SSE error event\n+    end\n+\n+    Note over CS, OAI: Single provider call per user turn. file_search is enabled as a tool within the same streaming Responses API request.\n+\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream (tools include file_search, store=tenant_store)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API (streaming, tool calling enabled)\n+    OAI-->>OG: SSE tokens\n+    OG-->>CS: Token stream\n+    CS-->>AG: Token stream\n+    AG-->>UI: SSE tokens\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Persist user msg + assistant msg + usage\n+    CS->>CS: Commit actual usage to quota_service (debit input_tokens + output_tokens)\n+\n+    participant AS as audit_service\n+    CS->>AS: Emit audit event (usage, policy decisions)\n+\n+    opt Thread summary update triggered (background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Full lifecycle of a user message - from authorization through streaming LLM response to persistence and optional thread compression. Authorization is evaluated before any database access. The PEP sends an evaluation request to the AuthZ Resolver with the chat's resource type and ID; the returned constraints are applied to the DB query's WHERE clause. If the PDP is unreachable, the request is rejected immediately (fail-closed, 403). If the PDP denies access for an operation with a concrete `chat_id`, the PEP returns 404 to avoid disclosing resource existence. If the constrained query returns 0 rows, the PEP returns 404.\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-file-upload`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments (multipart)\n+    AG->>CS: UploadAttachment(chat_id, file, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>DB: Insert attachment metadata (status: pending)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/files (upload)\n+    OG->>OAI: Files API upload\n+    OAI-->>OG: file_id\n+    OG-->>CS: file_id\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/{tenant_store}/files (add file)\n+    OG->>OAI: Add file to vector store\n+    OAI-->>OG: OK\n+    OG-->>CS: OK\n+    CS->>DB: Update attachment (status: ready, provider_file_id)\n+\n+    opt Generate doc summary (Variant 1, background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue doc summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+\n+    CS-->>AG: attachment_id, status\n+    AG-->>UI: 201 Created\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: File upload flow - the file is uploaded to the LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) via OAGW, added to the tenant's vector store, optionally summarized, and metadata is persisted locally.\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-cancellation`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant LP as llm_provider\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: SSE connection established\n+    AG->>AG: Create CancellationToken\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(..., cancel_token)\n+    CS->>LP: stream_response(..., cancel_token)\n+    LP->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream\n+    OG->>OAI: Streaming in progress\n+\n+    alt UI disconnect\n+        UI--xAG: Client disconnects / stop button\n+        AG->>AG: Detect broken SSE write -> cancel token\n+    else Server timeout\n+        CS->>CS: Timeout elapsed -> cancel token\n+    end\n+\n+    LP->>LP: Token cancelled -> abort HTTP request\n+    LP->>OG: Close connection (hard cancel)\n+    OG->>OAI: TCP RST / connection closed\n+    CS->>CS: Persist partial response (usage may be unknown)\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Cancellation propagates end-to-end via a shared `CancellationToken`. When triggered, `llm_provider` performs a hard cancel - aborting the outbound HTTP connection so the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) stops generating immediately. The partial response is persisted. Because provider usage is typically only delivered on a completed response, `input_tokens`/`output_tokens` may be NULL or approximate for cancelled turns; quota enforcement uses the bounded best-effort debit described in `quota_service`.\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Update\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-thread-summary`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Check summary trigger (msg count > 20 OR tokens > budget OR every 15 user turns)\n+    CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    Note over CS: Background worker executes summary task asynchronously\n+    CS->>DB: Load current thread_summary + old messages batch (10-20 msgs)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses (update summary prompt)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API\n+    OAI-->>OG: Updated summary\n+    OG-->>CS: Updated summary\n+    CS->>DB: Save new thread_summary\n+    CS->>DB: Mark summarized messages as compressed\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Thread summary is updated asynchronously after a chat turn when trigger conditions are met. Summary generation is a background task and MUST be attributed as `requester_type=system` so that its usage is not charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+Summary quality gate (P0): the system MUST detect and mitigate obviously-bad summaries without ML-based evaluation.\n+\n+- After generating a summary, `chat_service` MUST validate the candidate summary text.\n+- If summary length < `X` OR entropy < `Y`, `chat_service` MUST attempt regeneration.\n+- If regeneration fails quality checks or the provider call fails, `chat_service` MUST fall back by keeping the previous summary unchanged and MUST NOT mark the message batch as compressed.\n+\n+`X` and `Y` are configurable thresholds. Entropy is a deterministic proxy computed as normalized token entropy over whitespace-delimited tokens:\n+\n+`H_norm = (-sum(p_i * log2(p_i))) / log2(N)` where `p_i` is the empirical frequency of token `i` and `N` is the number of distinct tokens.\n+\n+Observability:\n+\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}` for each regeneration attempt (`reason` from a bounded allowlist such as `too_short|low_entropy|provider_error|invalid_format`).\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total` when the fallback behavior above is used.\n+\n+### 3.7 Database Schemas & Tables\n+\n+#### Table: chats\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chats`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Chat identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | Owning user |\n+| title | VARCHAR(255) | Chat title (user-set or auto-generated) |\n+| is_temporary | BOOLEAN | If true, auto-deleted after 24h |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last activity time |\n+| deleted_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Soft delete timestamp (nullable) |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `created_at`\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, updated_at DESC)` for listing chats\n+\n+#### Table: messages\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-messages`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Message identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key (nullable). Used for completed replay and traceability. Running state is tracked in `chat_turns`. |\n+| role | VARCHAR(16) | `user`, `assistant`, or `system` |\n+| content | TEXT | Message content |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| request_kind | VARCHAR(16) | `chat`, `summary`, or `doc_summary` (nullable) |\n+| features_used | JSONB | Feature flags and counters (nullable) |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Actual input tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Actual output tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| model | VARCHAR(64) | Actual model used for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| is_compressed | BOOLEAN | True if included in a thread summary |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `chat_id`, `role`, `content`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE. UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)` WHERE `request_id IS NOT NULL`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(chat_id, created_at)` for loading recent messages\n+\n+#### Table: chat_turns\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chat-turns`\n+\n+Tracks idempotency and in-progress generation state for `request_id`. This avoids ambiguous interpretation of `messages.request_id` when a generation is still running.\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Turn identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key |\n+| requester_type | VARCHAR(16) | `user` or `system` |\n+| requester_user_id | UUID | User ID when requester_type=`user` (nullable for system) |\n+| state | VARCHAR(16) | `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier (nullable until request starts) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID (nullable) |\n+| assistant_message_id | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID (nullable until completed) |\n+| error_code | VARCHAR(64) | Terminal error code (nullable) |\n+| started_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Start time |\n+| completed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Completion time (nullable) |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+A `chat_turns` row MUST be created before starting the outbound provider request; initial state is `running`.\n+\n+State machine:\n+- Allowed transitions: `running` -> `completed` \\| `failed` \\| `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states MUST be immutable\n+- At most one `running` turn per `(chat_id, request_id)`\n+\n+#### Table: attachments\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-attachments`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Attachment identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| filename | VARCHAR(255) | Original filename |\n+| content_type | VARCHAR(128) | MIME type |\n+| size_bytes | BIGINT | File size |\n+| provider_file_id | VARCHAR(128) | LLM provider file ID - OpenAI `file-*` or Azure OpenAI `assistant-*` (nullable until upload completes) |\n+| status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `ready`, `failed` |\n+| doc_summary | TEXT | LLM-generated document summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_model | VARCHAR(64) | Model used to generate the summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When the summary was last generated (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `in_progress`, `done`, `failed` (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_attempts | INTEGER | Cleanup retry attempts (default 0) |\n+| last_cleanup_error | TEXT | Last cleanup error (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When cleanup state was last updated (nullable) |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Upload time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `chat_id`, `filename`, `status`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: thread_summaries\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-thread-summaries`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Summary identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id, UNIQUE) |\n+| summary_text | TEXT | Compressed conversation summary |\n+| summarized_up_to | UUID | Last message ID included in this summary |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count of summary |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `chat_id`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: tenant_vector_stores\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-vector-stores`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant identifier |\n+| vector_store_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider vector store ID (OpenAI `vs_*` or Azure OpenAI equivalent) |\n+| provider | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier: `openai` or `azure_openai` |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `tenant_id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `vector_store_id`, `provider`, `created_at`. One vector store per tenant.\n+\n+Creation protocol (P0): `chat_service` uses a get-or-create flow with database uniqueness as the race arbiter.\n+\n+1. Attempt to read the row by `tenant_id`.\n+2. If not present, create the vector store via OAGW and attempt INSERT.\n+3. If INSERT fails due to unique violation (concurrent creator), re-read the row and use the existing `vector_store_id`. Best-effort delete the newly created provider vector store to avoid orphans.\n+\n+ **P0**: run a periodic reconcile/orphan reaper job (for example nightly) to reconcile provider state with `tenant_vector_stores`:\n+ - If a provider vector store exists but is not referenced in DB -> delete it.\n+ - If a DB row exists but the provider vector store is missing -> recreate the vector store and update the DB row.\n+\n+#### Table: quota_usage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-quota-usage`\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-quota-usage-accounting`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Record identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | User |\n+| period_type | VARCHAR(16) | `daily` or `monthly` |\n+| period_start | DATE | Start of the period |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Total input tokens consumed |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Total output tokens consumed |\n+| file_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of file search tool calls |\n+| web_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of web search calls (P1+) |\n+| rag_retrieval_calls | INTEGER | Number of internal RAG retrieval calls (P1+) |\n+| premium_model_calls | INTEGER | Calls to premium models |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+Commit semantics: quota updates MUST be atomic per period record. Implementations SHOULD use a transaction with row locking or a single UPDATE statement to avoid race conditions under parallel streams.\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` for quota lookups\n+\n+#### Projection Table: tenant_closure\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-closure-ref`\n+\n+Mini Chat does NOT require the `tenant_closure` local projection table for chat content access in P0. Chat content is owner-only and requires exact `owner_tenant_id` + `user_id` predicates.\n+\n+The `tenant_closure` projection table exists in the platform authorization model for modules that use hierarchical tenant scoping, but it is unused for Mini Chat content operations.\n+\n+Schema is defined in the [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#table-schemas-local-projections).\n+\n+**P2+ note**: When chat sharing (projects) is introduced, `resource_group_membership` and optionally `resource_group_closure` tables will also be required.\n+\n+### 3.8 Authorization (PEP)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-authz-pep`\n+\n+Mini Chat acts as a Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) per the platform's PDP/PEP authorization model defined in [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). The `chat_service` builds evaluation requests, sends them to the AuthZ Resolver (PDP), and compiles returned constraints into SQL WHERE clauses.\n+\n+Policy (P0): chat content is owner-only. For all content operations, authorization MUST enforce:\n+\n+- `owner_tenant_id == subject.tenant_id`\n+- `user_id == subject.id`\n+\n+Mini Chat does not use tenant hierarchy or subtree-based authorization for content operations.\n+\n+#### Resource Type\n+\n+The authorized resource is **Chat**. Sub-resources (Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary) do not have independent authorization - they are accessed through their parent chat, and the chat's authorization decision covers all child operations.\n+\n+| Attribute | Value |\n+|-----------|-------|\n+| GTS Type ID | `gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~` |\n+| Primary table | `chats` |\n+| Authorization granularity | Chat-level (sub-resources inherit) |\n+\n+#### PEP Configuration\n+\n+**Capabilities** (declared in `context.capabilities`):\n+\n+| Capability | P0 | P2+ | Rationale |\n+|------------|-----|------|-----------|\n+| `tenant_hierarchy` | No | No | Chat content is owner-only; `tenant_closure` is unused for Mini Chat content operations |\n+| `group_membership` | No | Yes | Needed when chat sharing via projects is introduced |\n+| `group_hierarchy` | No | Maybe | Needed if projects have nested hierarchy |\n+\n+**Supported properties** (declared in `context.supported_properties`):\n+\n+| Resource Property | SQL Column | Description |\n+|-------------------|------------|-------------|\n+| `owner_tenant_id` | `chats.tenant_id` | Owning tenant |\n+| `user_id` | `chats.user_id` | Owning user |\n+| `id` | `chats.id` | Chat identifier |\n+\n+#### Per-Operation Authorization Matrix\n+\n+| Endpoint | Action | `resource.id` | `require_constraints` | Expected P0 Predicates |\n+|----------|--------|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n+| `POST /v1/chats` | `create` | absent | `false` | decision only (no constraints) |\n+| `GET /v1/chats` | `list` | absent | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}` | `read` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `DELETE /v1/chats/{id}` | `delete` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | `update` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | `send_message` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | `upload` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | `read_turn` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+\n+**Notes**:\n+- `send_message` and `upload` are actions on the Chat resource, not on Message or Attachment resources. The `resource.id` is the chat's ID.\n+- For streaming (`send_message`), authorization is evaluated once at SSE connection establishment. The entire streaming session operates under the initial authorization decision. No per-message re-authorization.\n+- For `create`, the PEP passes `resource.properties.owner_tenant_id` and `resource.properties.user_id` from the SecurityContext. The PDP validates permission without returning constraints.\n+\n+#### Evaluation Request/Response Examples\n+\n+**Example 1: List Chats** (`GET /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"list\" },\n+  \"resource\": { \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\" },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response (P0 - user-owned chats only):\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+ORDER BY updated_at DESC\n+```\n+\n+**Example 2: Get Chat** (`GET /v1/chats/{id}`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"read\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"chat-456\"\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE id = 'chat-456'\n+  AND tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+```\n+\n+Result: 1 row -> return chat; 0 rows -> 404 Not Found (hides existence from unauthorized users).\n+\n+**Example 3: Create Chat** (`POST /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"create\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"properties\": {\n+      \"owner_tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\",\n+      \"user_id\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+    }\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": false,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{ \"decision\": true }\n+```\n+\n+PEP proceeds with INSERT. No constraints needed for create.\n+\n+**Example 4: Send Message** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`)\n+\n+Same authorization flow as Example 2 (Get Chat), but with `\"action\": { \"name\": \"send_message\" }` and `\"resource.id\"` set to the chat ID. Authorization is evaluated once before the SSE stream is established. The constraints are applied to the query that loads the chat and its messages from `chat_store`.\n+\n+#### Fail-Closed Behavior\n+\n+Mini Chat follows the platform's fail-closed rules (see [Authorization Design - Fail-Closed Rules](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#fail-closed-rules)):\n+\n+| Condition | PEP Action |\n+|-----------|------------|\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` present | 404 Not Found (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` absent | 403 Forbidden (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| PDP unreachable / timeout | 403 Forbidden (fail-closed) |\n+| `decision: true` + no constraints + `require_constraints: true` | 403 Forbidden |\n+| Unknown predicate type in constraints | Treat constraint as false; if all constraints false -> 403 |\n+| Unknown `resource_property` in predicate | Treat constraint as false; log error (PDP contract violation) |\n+| Empty `constraints: []` | 403 Forbidden |\n+\n+#### Token Scopes\n+\n+Mini Chat recognizes the following token scopes for third-party application narrowing:\n+\n+| Scope | Permits |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| `ai:chat` | All chat operations (umbrella scope) |\n+| `ai:chat:read` | `list`, `read` actions only |\n+| `ai:chat:write` | `create`, `update`, `delete`, `send_message`, `upload` actions |\n+\n+First-party applications (UI) use `token_scopes: [\"*\"]`. Third-party integrations receive narrowed scopes. Scope enforcement is handled by the PDP - the PEP includes `token_scopes` in the evaluation request context.\n+\n+#### P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\n+\n+When Projects / chat sharing is introduced (P2+), the authorization model extends naturally:\n+\n+1. Add `group_membership` capability (and optionally `group_hierarchy`).\n+2. Maintain `resource_group_membership` projection table mapping chat IDs to project group IDs.\n+3. The PDP returns additional access paths via OR'd constraints - e.g., one constraint for owned chats (`user_id` predicate), another for shared-via-project chats (`in_group` predicate).\n+4. `supported_properties` remains unchanged (the `id` property is used for group membership joins).\n+\n+No changes to the PEP flow or constraint compilation logic are needed. The PDP's response structure naturally handles multiple access paths through OR'd constraints.\n+\n+## 4. Additional Context\n+\n+### P0 Scope Boundaries\n+\n+**Included in P0**:\n+- Single-tenant vector store per tenant (not per workspace)\n+- Thread summary as only compression mechanism\n+- On-upload document summary via File Search (Variant 1 from draft)\n+- Quota enforcement: daily + monthly per user\n+- Temporary chats with 24h scheduled cleanup\n+- File Search per-message call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2 tool calls per message)\n+\n+**Deferred to P2+**:\n+- Projects / chat sharing\n+- Full-text search across chats\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are both supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies (beyond simple limits)\n+- Per-workspace vector stores\n+\n+### Data Classification and Retention (P0)\n+\n+Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data. Mini Chat treats messages and summaries as customer content and applies data minimization and retention controls.\n+\n+**Data classes**:\n+- Chat content: `messages.content`, `thread_summaries.summary_text`, `attachments.doc_summary`\n+- Operational metadata: IDs, timestamps, provider correlation IDs, quotas\n+- Audit events: structured records emitted to platform `audit_service`\n+\n+**Retention**:\n+- Temporary chats are scheduled for cleanup after 24h and MUST be deleted within 25h (PRD SLA).\n+- Non-temporary chats are retained until explicit deletion by default, subject to operator-configured retention policies.\n+- Soft-deleted chats (`deleted_at` set) are hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+**Audit content handling (P0)**:\n+- Audit events include the minimal content required for security and incident response.\n+- `chat_service` MUST redact secret patterns (tokens, keys, credentials) before sending content to `audit_service`.\n+- Redaction MUST be testable and based on a bounded allowlist of rule classes.\n+\n+| Rule class | Example (non-exhaustive) | Action |\n+|-----------|---------------------------|--------|\n+| `bearer_token` | `Authorization: Bearer eyJ...` | Replace token with `Bearer [REDACTED]` |\n+| `jwt` | `eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJS...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_JWT]` |\n+| `openai_api_key` | `sk-...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]` |\n+| `aws_access_key_id` | `AKIA...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]` |\n+| `azure_api_key` | `api-key: ...`, `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: ...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]` |\n+| `api_key_fields` | `api_key=...`, `x-api-key=...`, `client_secret=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `password_fields` | `password=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `pem_private_key` | `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` | Replace block with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]` |\n+| `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+- Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@MikeFalcon77`, thank you for fixing the markdown formatting issue!\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:19Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813022947","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813022947"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813022947"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813022947/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1211,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1222,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572927,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1222,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813023153","pull_request_review_id":3809303768,"id":2813023153,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq1Ox","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```\n+ping*  (delta | tool | citations)*  (done | error)\n+```\n+\n+- Zero or more `ping` events may appear at any point.\n+- `delta`, `tool`, and `citations` events may interleave in any order.\n+- Exactly one terminal event (`done` or `error`) ends the stream.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-event-translation\"></a>\n+#### Provider Event Translation\n+\n+Provider-specific streaming events are internal to `llm_provider` and `chat_service`. They are never forwarded to clients. The translation layer maps provider events to the stable SSE protocol defined above.\n+\n+| Provider Event | Stable SSE Event | Notes |\n+|----------------|-----------------|-------|\n+| `response.output_text.delta` | `event: delta` (`type: \"text\"`) | Text content mapped 1:1. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.searching` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"start\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | Emitted when file_search tool is invoked. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.completed` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"done\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | `details` populated from search results metadata. |\n+| File search annotations in response | `event: citations` | Extracted from provider annotations, mapped to `items[]` schema. When provider annotations include ranges, `items[].span` SHOULD be populated as character offsets into the final assistant text. |\n+| `response.completed` | `event: done` | `usage` from `response.usage`; `provider.response_id` from `response.id`. |\n+| Provider HTTP error / disconnect | `event: error` (`code: \"provider_error\"` or `\"provider_timeout\"`) | Error details sanitized; provider internals not exposed. |\n+| Provider 429 | `event: error` (`code: \"rate_limited\"`) | After OAGW retry exhaustion. |\n+\n+This mapping is intentionally provider-agnostic in the stable contract. If the provider changes its event format or a new provider is added, only the translation layer in `llm_provider` is updated. The client contract remains unchanged.\n+\n+**Error Codes**:\n+\n+| Code | HTTP Status | Description |\n+|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `feature_not_licensed` | 403 | Tenant lacks `ai_chat` feature |\n+| `insufficient_permissions` | 403 | Subject lacks permission for the requested action (AuthZ Resolver denied) |\n+| `chat_not_found` | 404 | Chat does not exist or not accessible under current authorization constraints |\n+| `quota_exceeded` | 429 | User exceeded daily/monthly usage limit |\n+| `rate_limited` | 429 | Too many requests in time window |\n+| `file_too_large` | 413 | Uploaded file exceeds size limit |\n+| `unsupported_file_type` | 415 | File type not supported for upload |\n+| `provider_error` | 502 | LLM provider returned an error |\n+| `provider_timeout` | 504 | LLM provider request timed out |\n+\n+### 3.4 Internal Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency Module | Interface Used | Purpose |\n+|-------------------|----------------|---------|\n+| api_gateway (platform) | Axum router / middleware | HTTP request handling, SSE transport |\n+| authn (platform) | Middleware (JWT/opaque token) | Extract `user_id` + `tenant_id` from request |\n+| license_manager (platform) | Middleware | Check tenant has `ai_chat` feature; reject with 403 if not |\n+| authz_resolver (platform) | Access evaluation API (`/access/v1/evaluation`) | Obtain authorization decisions + SQL-compilable constraints for chat operations |\n+| audit_service (platform) | Event emitter | Receive structured audit events (prompts, responses, usage, policy decisions) |\n+| outbound_gateway (platform) | Internal HTTP | Egress to LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) with credential injection |\n+\n+**Dependency Rules**:\n+- `chat_service` never calls the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) directly; all external calls go through OAGW\n+- `SecurityContext` (user_id, tenant_id) propagated through all in-process calls\n+- `license_manager` runs as middleware before `chat_service` is invoked\n+- `chat_service` calls `authz_resolver` before every database query; on PDP denial or PDP unreachable, fail-closed (deny access)\n+- `chat_service` emits audit events to `audit_service` after each turn; mini-chat does not store audit data locally\n+\n+### 3.5 External Dependencies\n+\n+#### LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\n+\n+Both providers expose a compatible API surface. OAGW routes requests to the configured provider and injects credentials accordingly.\n+\n+| API | Purpose | OAGW Route | OpenAI Endpoint | Azure OpenAI Endpoint |\n+|-----|---------|------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| Responses API (streaming) | Chat completion with tool support | `POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Responses API (non-streaming) | Thread summary generation, doc summary | `POST /outbound/llm/responses` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Files API | Upload user documents | `POST /outbound/llm/files` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/files` |\n+| Vector Stores API | Manage per-tenant vector stores, add/remove files | `POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/vector_stores/*` |\n+| File Search (tool) | Retrieve document excerpts during chat | - (invoked as tool within Responses API call) | `file_search` tool | `file_search` tool (identical contract) |\n+\n+Note: Azure OpenAI path variants differ by rollout; OAGW owns the exact path mapping for each API.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-api-mapping\"></a>\n+**Provider API Mapping** - authentication and endpoint differences:\n+\n+| Aspect | OpenAI | Azure OpenAI |\n+|--------|--------|--------------|\n+| **Base URL** | `https://api.openai.com/v1` | `https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1` |\n+| **Authentication** | `Authorization: Bearer {api_key}` | `api-key: {key}` header or Entra ID bearer token |\n+| **API version** | Not required | Azure may require an `api-version` query parameter depending on feature/rollout; OAGW owns this provider-specific detail |\n+| **File upload `purpose`** | `assistants` (P0) | `assistants` only (`user_data` not supported) |\n+| **Vector stores per `file_search`** | Multiple | **One** (sufficient for P0: one store per tenant) |\n+| **SSE format** | `event:` + `data:` lines, structured events | Identical format |\n+| **`user` field** | Supported | Supported (feeds into Azure abuse monitoring) |\n+| **`metadata` object** | Supported | Supported |\n+\n+OAGW MUST inject the required `api-version` query parameter when calling Azure OpenAI endpoints.\n+\n+**OAGW throttling scope**: OAGW handles provider-side rate limiting only - retry on provider 429 (with `Retry-After` respect, max 1 retry), circuit breaker when provider error rate exceeds threshold, and global concurrency cap as an SRE safety valve. Product-level quota enforcement (per-user, per-tenant, model downgrade) is NOT an OAGW concern - it is handled entirely by `chat_service` / `quota_service` before any outbound call (see constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+#### PostgreSQL\n+\n+| Usage | Purpose |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| Primary datastore | Chats, messages, attachments, summaries, quota counters, tenant vector store mappings |\n+\n+### 3.6 Interactions & Sequences\n+\n+#### Send Message with Streaming Response\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-send-message`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant AuthZ as authz_resolver (PDP)\n+    participant CS as chat_service (PEP)\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream\n+    AG->>AG: AuthN + license_manager\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(chat_id, user_msg, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>AuthZ: Evaluate(subject, action: \"send_message\", resource: {type: chat, id: chat_id})\n+    AuthZ-->>CS: decision + constraints\n+\n+    alt PDP unreachable / timeout\n+        CS-->>AG: 403 Forbidden\n+        AG-->>UI: 403\n+    else decision = false\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Load chat, recent messages, thread_summary, attachments, tenant vector_store_id (with constraints in WHERE)\n+\n+    alt 0 rows returned\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Build ContextPlan (system prompt + summary + doc summaries + recent msgs + user msg)\n+    CS->>CS: Preflight (reserve) quota check (estimate tokens from ContextPlan + max_output -> allow / downgrade / reject)\n+\n+    alt quota exhausted\n+        CS-->>AG: 429 quota_exceeded\n+        AG-->>UI: SSE error event\n+    end\n+\n+    Note over CS, OAI: Single provider call per user turn. file_search is enabled as a tool within the same streaming Responses API request.\n+\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream (tools include file_search, store=tenant_store)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API (streaming, tool calling enabled)\n+    OAI-->>OG: SSE tokens\n+    OG-->>CS: Token stream\n+    CS-->>AG: Token stream\n+    AG-->>UI: SSE tokens\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Persist user msg + assistant msg + usage\n+    CS->>CS: Commit actual usage to quota_service (debit input_tokens + output_tokens)\n+\n+    participant AS as audit_service\n+    CS->>AS: Emit audit event (usage, policy decisions)\n+\n+    opt Thread summary update triggered (background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Full lifecycle of a user message - from authorization through streaming LLM response to persistence and optional thread compression. Authorization is evaluated before any database access. The PEP sends an evaluation request to the AuthZ Resolver with the chat's resource type and ID; the returned constraints are applied to the DB query's WHERE clause. If the PDP is unreachable, the request is rejected immediately (fail-closed, 403). If the PDP denies access for an operation with a concrete `chat_id`, the PEP returns 404 to avoid disclosing resource existence. If the constrained query returns 0 rows, the PEP returns 404.\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-file-upload`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments (multipart)\n+    AG->>CS: UploadAttachment(chat_id, file, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>DB: Insert attachment metadata (status: pending)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/files (upload)\n+    OG->>OAI: Files API upload\n+    OAI-->>OG: file_id\n+    OG-->>CS: file_id\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/{tenant_store}/files (add file)\n+    OG->>OAI: Add file to vector store\n+    OAI-->>OG: OK\n+    OG-->>CS: OK\n+    CS->>DB: Update attachment (status: ready, provider_file_id)\n+\n+    opt Generate doc summary (Variant 1, background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue doc summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+\n+    CS-->>AG: attachment_id, status\n+    AG-->>UI: 201 Created\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: File upload flow - the file is uploaded to the LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) via OAGW, added to the tenant's vector store, optionally summarized, and metadata is persisted locally.\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-cancellation`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant LP as llm_provider\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: SSE connection established\n+    AG->>AG: Create CancellationToken\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(..., cancel_token)\n+    CS->>LP: stream_response(..., cancel_token)\n+    LP->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream\n+    OG->>OAI: Streaming in progress\n+\n+    alt UI disconnect\n+        UI--xAG: Client disconnects / stop button\n+        AG->>AG: Detect broken SSE write -> cancel token\n+    else Server timeout\n+        CS->>CS: Timeout elapsed -> cancel token\n+    end\n+\n+    LP->>LP: Token cancelled -> abort HTTP request\n+    LP->>OG: Close connection (hard cancel)\n+    OG->>OAI: TCP RST / connection closed\n+    CS->>CS: Persist partial response (usage may be unknown)\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Cancellation propagates end-to-end via a shared `CancellationToken`. When triggered, `llm_provider` performs a hard cancel - aborting the outbound HTTP connection so the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) stops generating immediately. The partial response is persisted. Because provider usage is typically only delivered on a completed response, `input_tokens`/`output_tokens` may be NULL or approximate for cancelled turns; quota enforcement uses the bounded best-effort debit described in `quota_service`.\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Update\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-thread-summary`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Check summary trigger (msg count > 20 OR tokens > budget OR every 15 user turns)\n+    CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    Note over CS: Background worker executes summary task asynchronously\n+    CS->>DB: Load current thread_summary + old messages batch (10-20 msgs)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses (update summary prompt)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API\n+    OAI-->>OG: Updated summary\n+    OG-->>CS: Updated summary\n+    CS->>DB: Save new thread_summary\n+    CS->>DB: Mark summarized messages as compressed\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Thread summary is updated asynchronously after a chat turn when trigger conditions are met. Summary generation is a background task and MUST be attributed as `requester_type=system` so that its usage is not charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+Summary quality gate (P0): the system MUST detect and mitigate obviously-bad summaries without ML-based evaluation.\n+\n+- After generating a summary, `chat_service` MUST validate the candidate summary text.\n+- If summary length < `X` OR entropy < `Y`, `chat_service` MUST attempt regeneration.\n+- If regeneration fails quality checks or the provider call fails, `chat_service` MUST fall back by keeping the previous summary unchanged and MUST NOT mark the message batch as compressed.\n+\n+`X` and `Y` are configurable thresholds. Entropy is a deterministic proxy computed as normalized token entropy over whitespace-delimited tokens:\n+\n+`H_norm = (-sum(p_i * log2(p_i))) / log2(N)` where `p_i` is the empirical frequency of token `i` and `N` is the number of distinct tokens.\n+\n+Observability:\n+\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}` for each regeneration attempt (`reason` from a bounded allowlist such as `too_short|low_entropy|provider_error|invalid_format`).\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total` when the fallback behavior above is used.\n+\n+### 3.7 Database Schemas & Tables\n+\n+#### Table: chats\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chats`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Chat identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | Owning user |\n+| title | VARCHAR(255) | Chat title (user-set or auto-generated) |\n+| is_temporary | BOOLEAN | If true, auto-deleted after 24h |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last activity time |\n+| deleted_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Soft delete timestamp (nullable) |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `created_at`\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, updated_at DESC)` for listing chats\n+\n+#### Table: messages\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-messages`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Message identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key (nullable). Used for completed replay and traceability. Running state is tracked in `chat_turns`. |\n+| role | VARCHAR(16) | `user`, `assistant`, or `system` |\n+| content | TEXT | Message content |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| request_kind | VARCHAR(16) | `chat`, `summary`, or `doc_summary` (nullable) |\n+| features_used | JSONB | Feature flags and counters (nullable) |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Actual input tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Actual output tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| model | VARCHAR(64) | Actual model used for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| is_compressed | BOOLEAN | True if included in a thread summary |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `chat_id`, `role`, `content`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE. UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)` WHERE `request_id IS NOT NULL`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(chat_id, created_at)` for loading recent messages\n+\n+#### Table: chat_turns\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chat-turns`\n+\n+Tracks idempotency and in-progress generation state for `request_id`. This avoids ambiguous interpretation of `messages.request_id` when a generation is still running.\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Turn identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key |\n+| requester_type | VARCHAR(16) | `user` or `system` |\n+| requester_user_id | UUID | User ID when requester_type=`user` (nullable for system) |\n+| state | VARCHAR(16) | `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier (nullable until request starts) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID (nullable) |\n+| assistant_message_id | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID (nullable until completed) |\n+| error_code | VARCHAR(64) | Terminal error code (nullable) |\n+| started_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Start time |\n+| completed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Completion time (nullable) |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+A `chat_turns` row MUST be created before starting the outbound provider request; initial state is `running`.\n+\n+State machine:\n+- Allowed transitions: `running` -> `completed` \\| `failed` \\| `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states MUST be immutable\n+- At most one `running` turn per `(chat_id, request_id)`\n+\n+#### Table: attachments\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-attachments`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Attachment identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| filename | VARCHAR(255) | Original filename |\n+| content_type | VARCHAR(128) | MIME type |\n+| size_bytes | BIGINT | File size |\n+| provider_file_id | VARCHAR(128) | LLM provider file ID - OpenAI `file-*` or Azure OpenAI `assistant-*` (nullable until upload completes) |\n+| status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `ready`, `failed` |\n+| doc_summary | TEXT | LLM-generated document summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_model | VARCHAR(64) | Model used to generate the summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When the summary was last generated (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `in_progress`, `done`, `failed` (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_attempts | INTEGER | Cleanup retry attempts (default 0) |\n+| last_cleanup_error | TEXT | Last cleanup error (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When cleanup state was last updated (nullable) |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Upload time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `chat_id`, `filename`, `status`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: thread_summaries\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-thread-summaries`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Summary identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id, UNIQUE) |\n+| summary_text | TEXT | Compressed conversation summary |\n+| summarized_up_to | UUID | Last message ID included in this summary |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count of summary |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `chat_id`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: tenant_vector_stores\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-vector-stores`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant identifier |\n+| vector_store_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider vector store ID (OpenAI `vs_*` or Azure OpenAI equivalent) |\n+| provider | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier: `openai` or `azure_openai` |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `tenant_id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `vector_store_id`, `provider`, `created_at`. One vector store per tenant.\n+\n+Creation protocol (P0): `chat_service` uses a get-or-create flow with database uniqueness as the race arbiter.\n+\n+1. Attempt to read the row by `tenant_id`.\n+2. If not present, create the vector store via OAGW and attempt INSERT.\n+3. If INSERT fails due to unique violation (concurrent creator), re-read the row and use the existing `vector_store_id`. Best-effort delete the newly created provider vector store to avoid orphans.\n+\n+ **P0**: run a periodic reconcile/orphan reaper job (for example nightly) to reconcile provider state with `tenant_vector_stores`:\n+ - If a provider vector store exists but is not referenced in DB -> delete it.\n+ - If a DB row exists but the provider vector store is missing -> recreate the vector store and update the DB row.\n+\n+#### Table: quota_usage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-quota-usage`\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-quota-usage-accounting`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Record identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | User |\n+| period_type | VARCHAR(16) | `daily` or `monthly` |\n+| period_start | DATE | Start of the period |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Total input tokens consumed |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Total output tokens consumed |\n+| file_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of file search tool calls |\n+| web_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of web search calls (P1+) |\n+| rag_retrieval_calls | INTEGER | Number of internal RAG retrieval calls (P1+) |\n+| premium_model_calls | INTEGER | Calls to premium models |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+Commit semantics: quota updates MUST be atomic per period record. Implementations SHOULD use a transaction with row locking or a single UPDATE statement to avoid race conditions under parallel streams.\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` for quota lookups\n+\n+#### Projection Table: tenant_closure\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-closure-ref`\n+\n+Mini Chat does NOT require the `tenant_closure` local projection table for chat content access in P0. Chat content is owner-only and requires exact `owner_tenant_id` + `user_id` predicates.\n+\n+The `tenant_closure` projection table exists in the platform authorization model for modules that use hierarchical tenant scoping, but it is unused for Mini Chat content operations.\n+\n+Schema is defined in the [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#table-schemas-local-projections).\n+\n+**P2+ note**: When chat sharing (projects) is introduced, `resource_group_membership` and optionally `resource_group_closure` tables will also be required.\n+\n+### 3.8 Authorization (PEP)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-authz-pep`\n+\n+Mini Chat acts as a Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) per the platform's PDP/PEP authorization model defined in [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). The `chat_service` builds evaluation requests, sends them to the AuthZ Resolver (PDP), and compiles returned constraints into SQL WHERE clauses.\n+\n+Policy (P0): chat content is owner-only. For all content operations, authorization MUST enforce:\n+\n+- `owner_tenant_id == subject.tenant_id`\n+- `user_id == subject.id`\n+\n+Mini Chat does not use tenant hierarchy or subtree-based authorization for content operations.\n+\n+#### Resource Type\n+\n+The authorized resource is **Chat**. Sub-resources (Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary) do not have independent authorization - they are accessed through their parent chat, and the chat's authorization decision covers all child operations.\n+\n+| Attribute | Value |\n+|-----------|-------|\n+| GTS Type ID | `gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~` |\n+| Primary table | `chats` |\n+| Authorization granularity | Chat-level (sub-resources inherit) |\n+\n+#### PEP Configuration\n+\n+**Capabilities** (declared in `context.capabilities`):\n+\n+| Capability | P0 | P2+ | Rationale |\n+|------------|-----|------|-----------|\n+| `tenant_hierarchy` | No | No | Chat content is owner-only; `tenant_closure` is unused for Mini Chat content operations |\n+| `group_membership` | No | Yes | Needed when chat sharing via projects is introduced |\n+| `group_hierarchy` | No | Maybe | Needed if projects have nested hierarchy |\n+\n+**Supported properties** (declared in `context.supported_properties`):\n+\n+| Resource Property | SQL Column | Description |\n+|-------------------|------------|-------------|\n+| `owner_tenant_id` | `chats.tenant_id` | Owning tenant |\n+| `user_id` | `chats.user_id` | Owning user |\n+| `id` | `chats.id` | Chat identifier |\n+\n+#### Per-Operation Authorization Matrix\n+\n+| Endpoint | Action | `resource.id` | `require_constraints` | Expected P0 Predicates |\n+|----------|--------|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n+| `POST /v1/chats` | `create` | absent | `false` | decision only (no constraints) |\n+| `GET /v1/chats` | `list` | absent | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}` | `read` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `DELETE /v1/chats/{id}` | `delete` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | `update` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | `send_message` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | `upload` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | `read_turn` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+\n+**Notes**:\n+- `send_message` and `upload` are actions on the Chat resource, not on Message or Attachment resources. The `resource.id` is the chat's ID.\n+- For streaming (`send_message`), authorization is evaluated once at SSE connection establishment. The entire streaming session operates under the initial authorization decision. No per-message re-authorization.\n+- For `create`, the PEP passes `resource.properties.owner_tenant_id` and `resource.properties.user_id` from the SecurityContext. The PDP validates permission without returning constraints.\n+\n+#### Evaluation Request/Response Examples\n+\n+**Example 1: List Chats** (`GET /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"list\" },\n+  \"resource\": { \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\" },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response (P0 - user-owned chats only):\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+ORDER BY updated_at DESC\n+```\n+\n+**Example 2: Get Chat** (`GET /v1/chats/{id}`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"read\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"chat-456\"\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE id = 'chat-456'\n+  AND tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+```\n+\n+Result: 1 row -> return chat; 0 rows -> 404 Not Found (hides existence from unauthorized users).\n+\n+**Example 3: Create Chat** (`POST /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"create\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"properties\": {\n+      \"owner_tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\",\n+      \"user_id\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+    }\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": false,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{ \"decision\": true }\n+```\n+\n+PEP proceeds with INSERT. No constraints needed for create.\n+\n+**Example 4: Send Message** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`)\n+\n+Same authorization flow as Example 2 (Get Chat), but with `\"action\": { \"name\": \"send_message\" }` and `\"resource.id\"` set to the chat ID. Authorization is evaluated once before the SSE stream is established. The constraints are applied to the query that loads the chat and its messages from `chat_store`.\n+\n+#### Fail-Closed Behavior\n+\n+Mini Chat follows the platform's fail-closed rules (see [Authorization Design - Fail-Closed Rules](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#fail-closed-rules)):\n+\n+| Condition | PEP Action |\n+|-----------|------------|\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` present | 404 Not Found (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` absent | 403 Forbidden (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| PDP unreachable / timeout | 403 Forbidden (fail-closed) |\n+| `decision: true` + no constraints + `require_constraints: true` | 403 Forbidden |\n+| Unknown predicate type in constraints | Treat constraint as false; if all constraints false -> 403 |\n+| Unknown `resource_property` in predicate | Treat constraint as false; log error (PDP contract violation) |\n+| Empty `constraints: []` | 403 Forbidden |\n+\n+#### Token Scopes\n+\n+Mini Chat recognizes the following token scopes for third-party application narrowing:\n+\n+| Scope | Permits |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| `ai:chat` | All chat operations (umbrella scope) |\n+| `ai:chat:read` | `list`, `read` actions only |\n+| `ai:chat:write` | `create`, `update`, `delete`, `send_message`, `upload` actions |\n+\n+First-party applications (UI) use `token_scopes: [\"*\"]`. Third-party integrations receive narrowed scopes. Scope enforcement is handled by the PDP - the PEP includes `token_scopes` in the evaluation request context.\n+\n+#### P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\n+\n+When Projects / chat sharing is introduced (P2+), the authorization model extends naturally:\n+\n+1. Add `group_membership` capability (and optionally `group_hierarchy`).\n+2. Maintain `resource_group_membership` projection table mapping chat IDs to project group IDs.\n+3. The PDP returns additional access paths via OR'd constraints - e.g., one constraint for owned chats (`user_id` predicate), another for shared-via-project chats (`in_group` predicate).\n+4. `supported_properties` remains unchanged (the `id` property is used for group membership joins).\n+\n+No changes to the PEP flow or constraint compilation logic are needed. The PDP's response structure naturally handles multiple access paths through OR'd constraints.\n+\n+## 4. Additional Context\n+\n+### P0 Scope Boundaries\n+\n+**Included in P0**:\n+- Single-tenant vector store per tenant (not per workspace)\n+- Thread summary as only compression mechanism\n+- On-upload document summary via File Search (Variant 1 from draft)\n+- Quota enforcement: daily + monthly per user\n+- Temporary chats with 24h scheduled cleanup\n+- File Search per-message call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2 tool calls per message)\n+\n+**Deferred to P2+**:\n+- Projects / chat sharing\n+- Full-text search across chats\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are both supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies (beyond simple limits)\n+- Per-workspace vector stores\n+\n+### Data Classification and Retention (P0)\n+\n+Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data. Mini Chat treats messages and summaries as customer content and applies data minimization and retention controls.\n+\n+**Data classes**:\n+- Chat content: `messages.content`, `thread_summaries.summary_text`, `attachments.doc_summary`\n+- Operational metadata: IDs, timestamps, provider correlation IDs, quotas\n+- Audit events: structured records emitted to platform `audit_service`\n+\n+**Retention**:\n+- Temporary chats are scheduled for cleanup after 24h and MUST be deleted within 25h (PRD SLA).\n+- Non-temporary chats are retained until explicit deletion by default, subject to operator-configured retention policies.\n+- Soft-deleted chats (`deleted_at` set) are hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+**Audit content handling (P0)**:\n+- Audit events include the minimal content required for security and incident response.\n+- `chat_service` MUST redact secret patterns (tokens, keys, credentials) before sending content to `audit_service`.\n+- Redaction MUST be testable and based on a bounded allowlist of rule classes.\n+\n+| Rule class | Example (non-exhaustive) | Action |\n+|-----------|---------------------------|--------|\n+| `bearer_token` | `Authorization: Bearer eyJ...` | Replace token with `Bearer [REDACTED]` |\n+| `jwt` | `eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJS...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_JWT]` |\n+| `openai_api_key` | `sk-...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]` |\n+| `aws_access_key_id` | `AKIA...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]` |\n+| `azure_api_key` | `api-key: ...`, `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: ...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]` |\n+| `api_key_fields` | `api_key=...`, `x-api-key=...`, `client_secret=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `password_fields` | `password=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `pem_private_key` | `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` | Replace block with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]` |\n+| `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+- Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.\n+- Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a maximum of 8 KiB per field (append `…[TRUNCATED]`).\n+- Full DLP-based content redaction is deferred to P2.\n+\n+### Context Plan Assembly Rules\n+\n+On each user message, `chat_service` assembles a `ContextPlan` in this order:\n+\n+1. **System prompt** - fixed instructions for the assistant. The system prompt is configuration and is not persisted as a `messages` row. For debugging, the prompt version SHOULD be recorded on the assistant message (for example in `features_used` or provider metadata).\n+2. **Thread summary** - if exists, replaces older history\n+3. **Document summaries** - short descriptions of attached documents\n+4. **Recent messages** - last 6-10 messages not covered by summary\n+5. **User message** - current turn\n+\n+If the total exceeds the input token budget, truncation happens in reverse priority: old messages first, then doc summaries, then retrieval excerpts. Summary and system prompt are never truncated.\n+\n+### File Search Trigger Heuristics\n+\n+File Search is invoked when:\n+- User explicitly references documents (\"in the file\", \"in section\", \"according to the document\")\n+- Documents are attached and the query likely relates to them\n+- User requests citations or sources\n+\n+Limits: per-message file_search tool call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2); max calls per day/user tracked in `quota_usage`.\n+\n+### Three-Tier Rate Limiting & Throttling\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-throttling-tiers`\n+\n+Rate limiting and quota enforcement are split into three tiers with strict ownership boundaries. These tiers MUST NOT be mixed - each has a single owner and distinct responsibility.\n+\n+| Tier | Owner | What It Controls | Examples                                                                                              |\n+|------|-------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Product quota** | `quota_service` (in `chat_service`) | Per-user daily/monthly token budgets, premium/standard model allocation, file_search call limits, auto-downgrade | \"Premium calls exhausted -> downgrade to gpt-5-mini\"; \"Daily limit reached -> 429 `quota_exceeded`\" |\n+| **Platform rate limit** | `api_gateway` middleware | Per-user/per-IP request rate, concurrent stream caps, abuse protection | \"20 rps per user\"; \"Max 5 concurrent SSE streams\"                                                     |\n+| **Provider rate limit** | OAGW | Provider 429 handling, `Retry-After` respect, circuit breaker, global concurrency cap | \"OpenAI 429 -> wait `Retry-After` -> retry once -> propagate 429 upstream\"                            |\n+\n+**Key rules**:\n+- Product quota decisions happen BEFORE the request reaches OAGW. If quota is exhausted, the request never leaves `chat_service`.\n+- OAGW does NOT know about tenants, users, licenses, or premium status. It handles only provider-level concerns (retry, circuit breaker, concurrency cap).\n+- Provider 429 from OAGW is propagated to `chat_service`, which maps it to `rate_limited` (429) for the client with a meaningful error message.\n+- Mid-stream quota abort is NOT supported - quota is checked at preflight only. Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+**Quota counting flow**:\n+\n+```\n+Preflight (reserve) (before LLM call):\n+  estimate = tokens(ContextPlan) + max_output_tokens\n+  if estimate > remaining_quota -> downgrade model or reject\n+\n+Commit (after done event):\n+  actual = response.usage.input_tokens + response.usage.output_tokens\n+  quota_usage += actual\n+  (if actual > estimate -> debit overshoot, never cancel completed response)\n+```\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@MikeFalcon77`, thank you for fixing the language tag! 🎉\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n(ﾉ◕ヮ◕)ﾉ*:･ﾟ✧\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:21Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:21Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813023153","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813023153"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813023153"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813023153/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1267,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1277,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572932,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1277,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813023459","pull_request_review_id":3809304075,"id":2813023459,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq1Tj","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,129 @@\n+---\n+status: accepted\n+date: 2026-02-14\n+decision-makers: Mike Yastrebtsov, Alex Henry\n+---\n+\n+<!--\n+=============================================================================\n+ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD (ADR) - based on MADR format\n+=============================================================================\n+PURPOSE: Capture WHY a significant technical decision was made - context,\n+options considered, trade-offs, and consequences.\n+\n+SCOPE:\n+✓ Context and problem statement\n+✓ Decision drivers (constraints, quality attributes)\n+✓ Options considered with pros/cons\n+✓ Chosen option with justification\n+✓ Consequences (good and bad)\n+\n+NOT IN THIS DOCUMENT (see other templates):\n+✗ Requirements -> PRD.md\n+✗ Full architecture description -> DESIGN.md\n+✗ Implementation details -> features/\n+\n+RULES:\n+- ADRs represent actual decision dilemma and decision state\n+- DESIGN is the primary artifact (\"what\"); ADRs annotate DESIGN with rationale (\"why\")\n+- Avoid \"everything is a decision\"; write ADRs only when the rationale needs to be explained and recorded for traceability\n+- Decision history is in git, not in documents\n+- Use single ADR per decision\n+\n+STANDARDS ALIGNMENT:\n+- MADR (Markdown Any Decision Records)\n+- IEEE 42010 (architecture decisions as first-class elements)\n+- ISO/IEC 15288 / 12207 (decision analysis process)\n+  ==============================================================================\n+  -->\n+\n+# Group chat ownership and usage attribution model (tenant-owned, sender-attributed)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution`","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/0003-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@MikeFalcon77`, thank you for adding the ADR-0003 reference to the DESIGN.md traceability section! This ensures the group chat ownership and usage attribution model is discoverable from the design docs.\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/specs/**/spec.md : Use `## ADDED` for new capabilities that stand alone; use `## MODIFIED` for behavior changes to existing requirements (include full requirement content); use `## REMOVED` for deprecated features; use `## RENAMED` for name-only changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : In spec delta files, use ADDED for new orthogonal capabilities, MODIFIED for behavior changes of existing requirements, and RENAMED for name-only changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/**/specs/**/*.md : Use `## ADDED|MODIFIED|REMOVED|RENAMED Requirements` headers in delta spec files with at least one `#### Scenario:` per requirement\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/specs/**/spec.md : In spec delta files under `changes/[change-id]/specs/`, use `## ADDED Requirements`, `## MODIFIED Requirements`, `## REMOVED Requirements`, and `## RENAMED Requirements` sections\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:25Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:25Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813023459","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813023459"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813023459"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813023459/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":42,"original_line":42,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572909,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":42,"position":42,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813023605","pull_request_review_id":3809304229,"id":2813023605,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq1V1","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,1571 @@\n+# Technical Design: Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Architecture Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Architectural Vision\n+\n+Mini Chat provides a multi-tenant AI chat experience with SSE streaming, conversation history, and document-aware question answering. Users interact through a REST/SSE API backed by the Responses API with File Search (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - see [Provider API Mapping](#provider-api-mapping)). The system maintains strict tenant isolation via per-tenant vector stores and enforces cost control through token budgets, usage quotas, and file search limits. Authorization decisions are delegated to the platform's AuthZ Resolver (PDP), which returns query-level constraints compiled to SQL by `chat_service` acting as the Policy Enforcement Point (PEP).\n+\n+The architecture is modular: `chat_service` orchestrates all request processing - context assembly, LLM invocation, streaming relay, and persistence. It owns the full request lifecycle from receiving a user message to persisting the assistant response and usage metrics. External LLM calls route exclusively through the platform's Outbound API Gateway (OAGW), which handles credential injection and egress control. Mini Chat calls the LLM provider directly via OAGW rather than through `cf-llm-gateway`, because it relies on provider-specific features (Responses API, Files API, File Search with vector stores) that the generic gateway does not abstract. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI expose a compatible Responses API surface; OAGW routes to the configured provider and injects the appropriate credentials (API key header for OpenAI, `api-key` header or Entra ID bearer token for Azure OpenAI).\n+\n+Long conversations are managed via thread summaries - a Level 1 compression strategy where older messages are periodically summarized by the LLM, and the summary replaces them in the context window. This keeps token costs bounded while preserving key facts, decisions, and document references.\n+\n+### 1.2 Architecture Drivers\n+\n+#### Functional Drivers\n+\n+| Requirement | Design Response |\n+|-------------|-----------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming` | SSE streaming via `chat_service` -> OAGW -> Responses API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/responses`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/v1/responses`) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history` | `chat_store` (Postgres) persists all messages; recent messages loaded per request |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload` | Upload via OAGW -> Files API (OpenAI: `POST /v1/files`; Azure OpenAI: `POST /openai/files`); metadata in `chat_store`; file added to tenant vector store. P0 uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` for both providers (OpenAI also supports `purpose=\"user_data\"`, but we use `assistants` to keep parity and because the files are used with Vector Stores / File Search). |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search` | File Search tool call scoped to tenant vector store (identical `file_search` tool on both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI Responses API) |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary` | See **File Upload** sequence (\"Generate doc summary\" background variant) and `attachments.doc_summary` schema field |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary` | Periodic LLM-driven summarization of old messages; summary replaces history in context |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud` | REST endpoints for create/list/get/delete chats |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat` | Toggle temporary flag; scheduled cleanup after 24h |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup` | See **Cleanup on Chat Deletion** |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation` | See **Streaming Cancellation** sequence and quota bounded best-effort debit rules in `quota_service` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement` | See `quota_service` component and `quota_usage` table |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget` | See constraint **Context Window Budget** and ContextPlan truncation rules |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate` | See constraint **License Gate** and dependency `license_manager (platform)` |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit` | Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for every AI interaction |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery` | See **Streaming Contract** (Idempotency + reconnect rule) and **Turn Status API** |\n+\n+#### NFR Allocation\n+\n+| NFR ID | NFR Summary | Allocated To | Design Response | Verification Approach |\n+|--------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation` | Tenant data must never leak across tenants | `chat_service`, `chat_store` | Per-tenant vector store; all queries scoped by `tenant_id`; no user-supplied `file_id` or `vector_store_id` in API | Integration tests with multi-tenant scenarios |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment` | Authorization must follow platform PDP/PEP model | `chat_service` (PEP) | AuthZ Resolver evaluates every data-access operation; constraints compiled to SQL WHERE clauses; fail-closed on PDP errors | Integration tests with mock PDP; fail-closed verification tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control` | Predictable and bounded LLM costs | `quota_service`, `chat_service` | Per-user daily/monthly limits; auto-downgrade to base model; file search call limits; token budget per request | Usage metrics dashboard; budget alert tests |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency` | Low time-to-first-token for chat responses | `chat_service`, OAGW | Direct SSE relay without buffering; cancellation propagation on disconnect | TTFT benchmarks under load; **Disconnect test**: open SSE -> receive 1-2 tokens -> disconnect -> assert provider request closed within 200 ms and active-generation counter decrements; **TTFT delta test**: measure `t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider` -> assert platform overhead < 50 ms p99 |\n+| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention` | Temporary chats cleaned up; deleted chats purged from provider | `chat_store`, `chat_service` | Scheduled cleanup job; cascade delete to provider files and vector store entries (OpenAI Files API / Azure OpenAI Files API) | Retention policy compliance tests |\n+\n+### 1.3 Architecture Layers\n+\n+```text\n+┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n+│  Presentation (api_gateway - platform)                │\n+│  REST + SSE endpoints, AuthN middleware               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Application (chat_service - PEP)                     │\n+│  Orchestration, authz evaluation, context planning,   │\n+│  streaming                                            │\n+│  ┌───────────┐  ┌─────────────────────┐               │\n+│  │ quota_svc │  │ authz_resolver (PDP)│               │\n+│  └───────────┘  └─────────────────────┘               │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Domain                                               │\n+│  Chat, Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary,            │\n+│  ContextPlan, QuotaPolicy                             │\n+├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n+│  Infrastructure                                       │\n+│  ┌─────────────┐  ┌───────────────────────────┐       │\n+│  │ chat_store  │  │ llm_provider (lib)        │       │\n+│  │ (Postgres)  │  │ -> OAGW -> OpenAI /       │       │\n+│  │             │  │          Azure OpenAI     │       │\n+│  └─────────────┘  └───────────────────────────┘       │\n+└───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n+```\n+\n+| Layer | Responsibility | Technology |\n+|-------|---------------|------------|\n+| Presentation | Public REST/SSE API, authentication, routing | Axum (platform api_gateway) |\n+| Application | Request orchestration, authorization evaluation (PEP), context assembly, streaming relay, quota checks | Rust (chat_service crate) |\n+| Domain | Business entities and rules | Rust structs |\n+| Infrastructure | Persistence, external LLM communication | Postgres (sqlx), HTTP client (reqwest) via OAGW |\n+\n+## 2. Principles & Constraints\n+\n+### 2.1 Design Principles\n+\n+#### Tenant-Scoped Everything\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-tenant-scoped`\n+\n+Every data access is scoped by constraints issued by the AuthZ Resolver (PDP). At P0, chat content is owner-only: the PDP returns `eq` predicates on `owner_tenant_id` and `user_id` that `chat_service` (PEP) compiles to SQL WHERE clauses. This replaces application-level tenant/user scoping with a formalized constraint model aligned with the platform's [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). Vector stores, file uploads, and quota checks all require tenant context. No API accepts raw `vector_store_id` or `file_id` from the client.\n+\n+#### Owner-Only Chat Content\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-owner-only-content`\n+\n+Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) is accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Parent tenants / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+#### Summary Over History\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-summary-over-history`\n+\n+The system favors compressed summaries over unbounded message history. Old messages are summarized rather than paginated into the LLM context. This bounds token costs and keeps response quality stable for long conversations.\n+\n+#### Streaming-First\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-principle-streaming-first`\n+\n+All LLM responses are streamed. The primary delivery path is SSE from LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) -> OAGW -> chat_service -> api_gateway -> UI. Non-streaming responses are not supported for chat completion. Both providers use an identical SSE event format for the Responses API.\n+\n+### 2.2 Constraints\n+\n+#### OpenAI-Compatible Provider (P0)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-openai-compatible`\n+\n+P0 targets the OpenAI-compatible API surface - either **OpenAI** or **Azure OpenAI** as the LLM provider. Both expose the Responses API, Files API, Vector Stores API, and File Search tool with compatible request/response contracts. The active provider is selected per deployment via OAGW configuration; `llm_provider` does not need a runtime abstraction layer because the API surface is shared. Multi-provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) is deferred.\n+\n+**Provider parity notes** (Azure OpenAI known limitations at time of writing):\n+- Azure supports only **one vector store** per `file_search` tool call (sufficient for P0: one vector store per tenant).\n+- `purpose=\"user_data\"` for file uploads is not supported on Azure; use `purpose=\"assistants\"`.\n+- `vector_stores.search` (client-side manual search) is not exposed on Azure - not used in this design.\n+- New OpenAI features may appear on Azure with a lag of weeks to months.\n+\n+**OpenAI file upload purpose choice (P0)**: although OpenAI supports both `purpose=\"user_data\"` and `purpose=\"assistants\"`, Mini Chat uses `purpose=\"assistants\"` to align with Azure OpenAI and to keep the upload path uniform across providers.\n+\n+#### No Credential Storage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-credentials`\n+\n+Mini Chat never stores or handles API keys. All external calls go through OAGW, which injects credentials from CredStore.\n+\n+#### Context Window Budget\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-context-budget`\n+\n+Every request must fit within fixed `max_input_tokens` and `max_output_tokens` budgets. When context exceeds the budget, the system truncates in order: old messages (not summary), doc summaries, retrieval excerpts. A reserve is always maintained for the response.\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-license-gate`\n+\n+Access requires the `ai_chat` feature on the tenant license, enforced by the platform's `license_manager` middleware. Requests from unlicensed tenants receive HTTP 403.\n+\n+#### No Buffering\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-no-buffering`\n+\n+No layer in the streaming pipeline may collect the full LLM response before relaying it. Every component - `llm_provider`, `chat_service`, `api_gateway` - must read one SSE event and immediately forward it to the next layer. Middleware must not buffer response bodies. `.collect()` on the token stream is prohibited in the hot path.\n+\n+#### Bounded Channels\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-bounded-channels`\n+\n+Internal mpsc channels between `llm_provider` -> `chat_service` -> SSE writer must use bounded buffers (16–64 messages). This provides backpressure: if the consumer is slow, the producer blocks rather than accumulating unbounded memory. Channel capacity is configurable per deployment.\n+\n+#### Quota Before Outbound\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`\n+\n+All product-level quota decisions (block, downgrade, limit) MUST be made in `chat_service` before any request reaches OAGW. OAGW never makes user-level or tenant-level quota decisions - it is transport + credential broker only. Only `chat_service` has the business context needed for quota decisions: tenant, user, license tier, premium/standard status, downgrade rules, file_search call limits. OAGW sees an opaque HTTP request with no business semantics.\n+\n+Model selection and lifecycle rules (P0) are configuration-owned and applied at the `chat_service` boundary:\n+\n+- Model defaults, upgrade policy, and downgrade thresholds MUST be defined in deployment configuration (P0) and are expected to be owned by a platform Settings Service / License Manager layer as the long-term system of record.\n+- `chat_service` / `quota_service` is the enforcement point: it MUST deterministically choose the effective model before the outbound call (allow / downgrade / reject) and MUST surface the chosen model via metrics (`{model}` label) and audit.\n+\n+Global emergency flags / kill switches (P0): operators MUST have a way to immediately reduce cost and risk at runtime via configuration-owned flags.\n+\n+- `disable_premium_models` - if enabled, premium models MUST NOT be used; requests that would have used premium MUST be downgraded to base.\n+- `force_base_model` - if enabled, all requests MUST use the base model regardless of quota tier.\n+- `disable_file_search` - if enabled, `file_search` tool calls MUST be skipped; responses proceed without retrieval.\n+\n+Ownership: these flags are owned and operated by platform configuration (P0: deployment config). Long-term, they are expected to be owned by Settings Service / License Manager with privileged operator access.\n+\n+Hard caps: token budgets (`max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`) MUST remain configurable and can serve as an emergency hard cap lever.\n+\n+## 3. Technical Architecture\n+\n+### 3.1 Domain Model\n+\n+**Technology**: Rust structs\n+\n+**Core Entities**:\n+\n+| Entity | Description |\n+|--------|-------------|\n+| Chat | A conversation belonging to a user within a tenant. Has title, temporary flag, creation/update timestamps. |\n+| Message | A single turn in a chat (role: user/assistant/system). Stores content, token estimate, compression status. |\n+| Attachment | File uploaded to a chat. References provider `file_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), linked to tenant vector store. Has processing status. |\n+| ThreadSummary | Compressed representation of older messages in a chat. Replaces old history in the context window. |\n+| TenantVectorStore | Mapping from `tenant_id` to provider `vector_store_id` (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI Vector Stores API). One vector store per tenant. |\n+| AuditEvent | Structured event emitted to platform `audit_service`: prompt, response, user/tenant, timestamps, policy decisions, usage. Not stored locally. |\n+| QuotaUsage | Per-user usage counters for rate limiting and budget enforcement. Tracks daily/monthly periods. |\n+| ContextPlan | Transient object assembled per request: system prompt, summary, doc summaries, recent messages, user message, retrieval excerpts. |\n+\n+**Relationships**:\n+- Chat -> Message: 1..\\*\n+- Chat -> Attachment: 0..\\*\n+- Chat -> ThreadSummary: 0..1\n+- Attachment -> TenantVectorStore: belongs to (via tenant_id)\n+- Message -> AuditEvent: 1..1 (each turn emits an audit event to platform `audit_service`)\n+\n+### 3.2 Component Model\n+\n+```mermaid\n+graph TB\n+    UI[\"UI (Browser)\"]\n+    AG[\"api_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    AuthN[\"authn (platform)\"]\n+    LM[\"license_manager (platform)\"]\n+    AuthZ[\"authz_resolver (platform, PDP)\"]\n+    CS[\"chat_service (PEP)\"]\n+    QS[\"quota_service\"]\n+    AS[\"audit_service (platform)\"]\n+    DB[\"chat_store (Postgres)\"]\n+    LP[\"llm_provider (lib)\"]\n+    OAGW[\"outbound_gateway (platform)\"]\n+    OAI[\"LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\"]\n+\n+    UI -->|REST/SSE| AG\n+    AG -->|middleware| AuthN\n+    AG -->|middleware| LM\n+    AG -->|in-process| CS\n+    CS -->|authz evaluation| AuthZ\n+    CS --> QS\n+    CS -->|emit events| AS\n+    CS --> DB\n+    CS --> LP\n+    LP --> OAGW\n+    OAGW -->|HTTPS| OAI\n+```\n+\n+**Components**:\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-service`\n+  - **chat_service** - Core orchestrator and Policy Enforcement Point (PEP). Receives user messages, evaluates authorization via AuthZ Resolver, builds context plan, invokes LLM via `llm_provider`, relays streaming tokens, persists messages and usage, triggers thread summary updates.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-chat-store`\n+  - **chat_store** - Postgres persistence layer. Source of truth for chats, messages, attachments, thread summaries, tenant vector store mappings, and quota usage.\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-llm-provider`\n+  - **llm_provider** - Library (not a standalone service) used by `chat_service`. Builds requests for the Responses API (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI - both expose a compatible surface), parses SSE streams, maps errors. Propagates tenant/user metadata via `user` and `metadata` fields on every request (see section 4: Provider Request Metadata). Handles both streaming chat and non-streaming calls (summary generation, doc summary). The library is provider-agnostic at the API contract level; OAGW handles endpoint routing and credential injection per configured provider.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-quota-service`\n+  - **quota_service** - Enforces per-user usage limits by period (daily, monthly). Supports auto-downgrade to base model when premium quota is exhausted. Tracks file search call counts separately. Uses **two-phase quota counting**:\n+    - **Phase 1 - Preflight (reserve) estimate** (on stream start, before outbound call): estimate token cost from `ContextPlan` size + `max_output_tokens` and reserve it for quota enforcement. Decision: allow at current model / downgrade model / reject with `quota_exceeded`.\n+      - Reserve MUST prevent parallel requests from overspending remaining quota.\n+      - Reserve SHOULD be keyed by `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` and reconciled on terminal outcome.\n+    - **Phase 2 - Commit actual** (on `event: done`): reconcile the reserve to actual usage (`response.usage.input_tokens` + `response.usage.output_tokens`) and commit actual to `quota_usage`. If actual exceeds estimate (overshoot), the completed response is never retroactively cancelled, but guardrails apply:\n+      - Commit MUST be atomic per `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` (avoid race conditions under parallel streams)\n+      - If the remaining quota is below a configured negative threshold, preflight MUST reject new requests with `quota_exceeded`\n+      - `max_output_tokens` and an explicit input budget MUST bound the maximum cost per request\n+    - **Streaming constraint**: quota check is preflight-only. Mid-stream abort due to quota is NOT supported (would produce broken UX and partial content). Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits.\n+\n+Cancel/disconnect rule: if a stream ends without a terminal `done`/`error` event, `quota_service` MUST commit a bounded best-effort debit (default: the reserved estimate) so cancellations cannot evade quotas.\n+\n+Reserve is an internal accounting concept (it may be implemented as held/pending fields or a row-level marker), but the observable external semantics MUST match the rules above.\n+\n+Background tasks (thread summary update, document summary generation) MUST run with `requester_type=system` and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user. Usage for these tasks is charged to a tenant operational bucket (implementation-defined) and still emitted to `audit_service`.\n+\n+- **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-component-authz-integration`\n+  - **authz_resolver (PDP)** - Platform AuthZ Resolver module. `chat_service` calls it before every data-access operation to obtain authorization decisions and SQL-compilable constraints. See section 3.8.\n+\n+\n+### 3.3 API Contracts\n+\n+**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n+\n+**Endpoints Overview**:\n+\n+| Method | Path | Description | Stability |\n+|--------|------|-------------|-----------|\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats` | Create a new chat | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats` | List chats for current user | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Get chat metadata + recent messages | stable |\n+| `DELETE` | `/v1/chats/{id}` | Delete chat (with retention cleanup) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | Toggle temporary flag (24h TTL) | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | Send message, receive SSE stream | stable |\n+| `POST` | `/v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | Upload file attachment | stable |\n+| `GET` | `/v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | Get authoritative turn status (read-only) | stable |\n+\n+**Streaming Contract** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`):\n+\n+The SSE protocol below is the **stable public contract** between `chat_service` and UI clients. Provider-specific streaming events (OpenAI/Azure OpenAI Responses API) are translated internally by `llm_provider` / `chat_service` and are never exposed to clients. See [Provider Event Translation](#provider-event-translation).\n+\n+Request body:\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"content\": \"string\",\n+  \"request_id\": \"uuid (client-generated, optional)\"\n+}\n+```\n+\n+**Idempotency**: The idempotency key is `(chat_id, request_id)`. Behavior when `request_id` is provided:\n+\n+| State | Server behavior |\n+|-------|----------------|\n+| Active generation exists for key | Return `409 Conflict`. (P2+: attach to existing stream.) |\n+| Completed generation exists for key | Return a fast replay SSE stream without triggering a new provider request: one `delta` event containing the full persisted assistant text, then `citations` if available, then `done`. |\n+| No record for key | Start a new generation normally. |\n+\n+If `request_id` is omitted, the server treats the request as non-idempotent (no replay semantics).\n+\n+The UI MUST generate a new `request_id` per user send action. The UI MUST NOT auto-retry with the same `request_id` unless it intends to resume/retrieve the same generation.\n+\n+ Active generation detection and completed replay are based on a durable `chat_turns` record (see section 3.7). `messages.request_id` uniqueness alone is not sufficient to represent `running` state.\n+\n+ Reconnect rule (P0): if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal `done`/`error`, the UI MUST NOT automatically retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id` (it will most likely hit `409 Conflict`). The UI should treat the send as indeterminate and require explicit user action (resend with a new `request_id`).\n+\n+ **P2+ note**: add a read API for turn state (planned), for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`, allowing the UI to distinguish \"double send\" vs \"network reconnect\" and enabling clean resume behavior.\n+\n+**P0 optional, recommended**: expose a read API for turn state backed by `chat_turns` (for example `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`) so support and UI recovery flows can query authoritative turn state rather than inferring it from client retry outcomes.\n+\n+#### Turn Status API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+To support reconnect UX and reduce support reliance on direct DB inspection, the service SHOULD expose a read-only turn status endpoint backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+**SSE mapping**:\n+\n+- API `done` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = completed` and terminal `event: done`\n+- API `error` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = failed` and terminal `event: error`\n+- API `cancelled` corresponds to internal `chat_turns.state = cancelled` and indicates cancellation was processed; the UI should treat it as terminal and allow resend with a new `request_id`\n+\n+ UI guidance: if the SSE stream disconnects before a terminal event, the UI SHOULD show a user-visible banner: \"Message delivery uncertain due to connection loss. You can resend.\" Resend MUST use a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### SSE Event Definitions\n+\n+Six event types. The stream always ends with exactly one terminal event: `done` or `error`.\n+\n+##### `event: delta`\n+\n+Streams incremental assistant output.\n+\n+```\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \"partial text\"}\n+\n+event: delta\n+data: {\"type\": \"text\", \"content\": \" more text\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `type` | string | Output type. P0: always `\"text\"`. Reserved for future types (e.g., `\"markdown\"`, `\"structured\"`). |\n+| `content` | string | Incremental text fragment. |\n+\n+##### `event: tool`\n+\n+Reports tool activity (file_search at P0, web_search at P1+).\n+\n+```\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"start\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {}}\n+\n+event: tool\n+data: {\"phase\": \"done\", \"name\": \"file_search\", \"details\": {\"files_searched\": 3}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `phase` | `\"start\"` \\| `\"progress\"` \\| `\"done\"` | Lifecycle phase of the tool call. |\n+| `name` | string | Tool identifier. P0: `\"file_search\"`. |\n+| `details` | object | Tool-specific metadata. MUST be non-sensitive and tenant-safe. Content is minimal and stable at P0. |\n+\n+##### `event: citations`\n+\n+Delivers source references used in the answer.\n+\n+```\n+event: citations\n+data: {\"items\": [{\"source\": \"file\", \"title\": \"Q3 Report.pdf\", \"attachment_id\": \"uuid\", \"file_id\": \"file-abc123\", \"snippet\": \"Revenue grew 15%...\", \"score\": 0.92}]}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `items[].source` | `\"file\"` \\| `\"web\"` | Citation source type. |\n+| `items[].title` | string | Document or page title. |\n+| `items[].url` | string (optional) | URL for web sources. |\n+| `items[].attachment_id` | UUID (optional) | Internal attachment identifier for file sources. Preferred identifier for UI references. |\n+| `items[].file_id` | string (optional) | Provider file ID for file sources. |\n+| `items[].span` | object (optional) | Reserved for mapping citations to the final assistant text. If provided: `{ \"start\": number, \"end\": number }` character offsets into the full assistant output. |\n+| `items[].snippet` | string | Relevant excerpt. |\n+| `items[].score` | number (optional) | Relevance score (0-1). |\n+\n+`items[].file_id` is provider-issued and MUST be treated as opaque display-only metadata. Clients MUST NOT send it back to any API.\n+\n+P0: `citations` is sent once near stream completion, before `done`. The contract supports multiple `citations` events per stream for future use.\n+\n+##### `event: done`\n+\n+Finalizes the stream. Provides usage and provider correlation metadata.\n+\n+```\n+event: done\n+data: {\"message_id\": \"uuid\", \"usage\": {\"input_tokens\": 500, \"output_tokens\": 120, \"model\": \"gpt-5.2\"}, \"provider\": {\"name\": \"openai\", \"response_id\": \"resp_abc123\"}}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `message_id` | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID. |\n+| `usage.input_tokens` | number | Actual input tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.output_tokens` | number | Actual output tokens consumed. |\n+| `usage.model` | string | Model used for generation. |\n+| `provider.name` | `\"openai\"` \\| `\"azure_openai\"` | Active provider. |\n+| `provider.response_id` | string | Provider-side response ID for debugging and OAGW log correlation. |\n+\n+##### `event: error`\n+\n+Terminates the stream with an application error. No further events follow.\n+\n+```\n+event: error\n+data: {\"code\": \"quota_exceeded\", \"message\": \"Daily limit reached\"}\n+```\n+\n+| Field | Type | Description |\n+|-------|------|-------------|\n+| `code` | string | Canonical error code (see table below). |\n+| `message` | string | Human-readable description. |\n+\n+##### `event: ping`\n+\n+Keepalive to prevent idle-timeout disconnects by proxies and browsers (especially when the model is \"thinking\" before producing tokens). Sent every 15-30 seconds during generation. Clients MUST ignore `ping` events.\n+\n+```\n+event: ping\n+data: {}\n+```\n+\n+#### SSE Event Ordering\n+\n+A well-formed stream follows this ordering:\n+\n+```\n+ping*  (delta | tool | citations)*  (done | error)\n+```\n+\n+- Zero or more `ping` events may appear at any point.\n+- `delta`, `tool`, and `citations` events may interleave in any order.\n+- Exactly one terminal event (`done` or `error`) ends the stream.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-event-translation\"></a>\n+#### Provider Event Translation\n+\n+Provider-specific streaming events are internal to `llm_provider` and `chat_service`. They are never forwarded to clients. The translation layer maps provider events to the stable SSE protocol defined above.\n+\n+| Provider Event | Stable SSE Event | Notes |\n+|----------------|-----------------|-------|\n+| `response.output_text.delta` | `event: delta` (`type: \"text\"`) | Text content mapped 1:1. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.searching` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"start\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | Emitted when file_search tool is invoked. |\n+| `response.file_search_call.completed` | `event: tool` (`phase: \"done\"`, `name: \"file_search\"`) | `details` populated from search results metadata. |\n+| File search annotations in response | `event: citations` | Extracted from provider annotations, mapped to `items[]` schema. When provider annotations include ranges, `items[].span` SHOULD be populated as character offsets into the final assistant text. |\n+| `response.completed` | `event: done` | `usage` from `response.usage`; `provider.response_id` from `response.id`. |\n+| Provider HTTP error / disconnect | `event: error` (`code: \"provider_error\"` or `\"provider_timeout\"`) | Error details sanitized; provider internals not exposed. |\n+| Provider 429 | `event: error` (`code: \"rate_limited\"`) | After OAGW retry exhaustion. |\n+\n+This mapping is intentionally provider-agnostic in the stable contract. If the provider changes its event format or a new provider is added, only the translation layer in `llm_provider` is updated. The client contract remains unchanged.\n+\n+**Error Codes**:\n+\n+| Code | HTTP Status | Description |\n+|------|-------------|-------------|\n+| `feature_not_licensed` | 403 | Tenant lacks `ai_chat` feature |\n+| `insufficient_permissions` | 403 | Subject lacks permission for the requested action (AuthZ Resolver denied) |\n+| `chat_not_found` | 404 | Chat does not exist or not accessible under current authorization constraints |\n+| `quota_exceeded` | 429 | User exceeded daily/monthly usage limit |\n+| `rate_limited` | 429 | Too many requests in time window |\n+| `file_too_large` | 413 | Uploaded file exceeds size limit |\n+| `unsupported_file_type` | 415 | File type not supported for upload |\n+| `provider_error` | 502 | LLM provider returned an error |\n+| `provider_timeout` | 504 | LLM provider request timed out |\n+\n+### 3.4 Internal Dependencies\n+\n+| Dependency Module | Interface Used | Purpose |\n+|-------------------|----------------|---------|\n+| api_gateway (platform) | Axum router / middleware | HTTP request handling, SSE transport |\n+| authn (platform) | Middleware (JWT/opaque token) | Extract `user_id` + `tenant_id` from request |\n+| license_manager (platform) | Middleware | Check tenant has `ai_chat` feature; reject with 403 if not |\n+| authz_resolver (platform) | Access evaluation API (`/access/v1/evaluation`) | Obtain authorization decisions + SQL-compilable constraints for chat operations |\n+| audit_service (platform) | Event emitter | Receive structured audit events (prompts, responses, usage, policy decisions) |\n+| outbound_gateway (platform) | Internal HTTP | Egress to LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) with credential injection |\n+\n+**Dependency Rules**:\n+- `chat_service` never calls the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) directly; all external calls go through OAGW\n+- `SecurityContext` (user_id, tenant_id) propagated through all in-process calls\n+- `license_manager` runs as middleware before `chat_service` is invoked\n+- `chat_service` calls `authz_resolver` before every database query; on PDP denial or PDP unreachable, fail-closed (deny access)\n+- `chat_service` emits audit events to `audit_service` after each turn; mini-chat does not store audit data locally\n+\n+### 3.5 External Dependencies\n+\n+#### LLM Provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI)\n+\n+Both providers expose a compatible API surface. OAGW routes requests to the configured provider and injects credentials accordingly.\n+\n+| API | Purpose | OAGW Route | OpenAI Endpoint | Azure OpenAI Endpoint |\n+|-----|---------|------------|-----------------|----------------------|\n+| Responses API (streaming) | Chat completion with tool support | `POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Responses API (non-streaming) | Thread summary generation, doc summary | `POST /outbound/llm/responses` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/responses` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses` |\n+| Files API | Upload user documents | `POST /outbound/llm/files` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/files` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/files` |\n+| Vector Stores API | Manage per-tenant vector stores, add/remove files | `POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://api.openai.com/v1/vector_stores/*` | `POST https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1/vector_stores/*` |\n+| File Search (tool) | Retrieve document excerpts during chat | - (invoked as tool within Responses API call) | `file_search` tool | `file_search` tool (identical contract) |\n+\n+Note: Azure OpenAI path variants differ by rollout; OAGW owns the exact path mapping for each API.\n+\n+<a id=\"provider-api-mapping\"></a>\n+**Provider API Mapping** - authentication and endpoint differences:\n+\n+| Aspect | OpenAI | Azure OpenAI |\n+|--------|--------|--------------|\n+| **Base URL** | `https://api.openai.com/v1` | `https://{resource}.openai.azure.com/openai/v1` |\n+| **Authentication** | `Authorization: Bearer {api_key}` | `api-key: {key}` header or Entra ID bearer token |\n+| **API version** | Not required | Azure may require an `api-version` query parameter depending on feature/rollout; OAGW owns this provider-specific detail |\n+| **File upload `purpose`** | `assistants` (P0) | `assistants` only (`user_data` not supported) |\n+| **Vector stores per `file_search`** | Multiple | **One** (sufficient for P0: one store per tenant) |\n+| **SSE format** | `event:` + `data:` lines, structured events | Identical format |\n+| **`user` field** | Supported | Supported (feeds into Azure abuse monitoring) |\n+| **`metadata` object** | Supported | Supported |\n+\n+OAGW MUST inject the required `api-version` query parameter when calling Azure OpenAI endpoints.\n+\n+**OAGW throttling scope**: OAGW handles provider-side rate limiting only - retry on provider 429 (with `Retry-After` respect, max 1 retry), circuit breaker when provider error rate exceeds threshold, and global concurrency cap as an SRE safety valve. Product-level quota enforcement (per-user, per-tenant, model downgrade) is NOT an OAGW concern - it is handled entirely by `chat_service` / `quota_service` before any outbound call (see constraint `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+#### PostgreSQL\n+\n+| Usage | Purpose |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| Primary datastore | Chats, messages, attachments, summaries, quota counters, tenant vector store mappings |\n+\n+### 3.6 Interactions & Sequences\n+\n+#### Send Message with Streaming Response\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-send-message`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant AuthZ as authz_resolver (PDP)\n+    participant CS as chat_service (PEP)\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream\n+    AG->>AG: AuthN + license_manager\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(chat_id, user_msg, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>AuthZ: Evaluate(subject, action: \"send_message\", resource: {type: chat, id: chat_id})\n+    AuthZ-->>CS: decision + constraints\n+\n+    alt PDP unreachable / timeout\n+        CS-->>AG: 403 Forbidden\n+        AG-->>UI: 403\n+    else decision = false\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Load chat, recent messages, thread_summary, attachments, tenant vector_store_id (with constraints in WHERE)\n+\n+    alt 0 rows returned\n+        CS-->>AG: 404 Not Found\n+        AG-->>UI: 404\n+    end\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Build ContextPlan (system prompt + summary + doc summaries + recent msgs + user msg)\n+    CS->>CS: Preflight (reserve) quota check (estimate tokens from ContextPlan + max_output -> allow / downgrade / reject)\n+\n+    alt quota exhausted\n+        CS-->>AG: 429 quota_exceeded\n+        AG-->>UI: SSE error event\n+    end\n+\n+    Note over CS, OAI: Single provider call per user turn. file_search is enabled as a tool within the same streaming Responses API request.\n+\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream (tools include file_search, store=tenant_store)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API (streaming, tool calling enabled)\n+    OAI-->>OG: SSE tokens\n+    OG-->>CS: Token stream\n+    CS-->>AG: Token stream\n+    AG-->>UI: SSE tokens\n+\n+    CS->>DB: Persist user msg + assistant msg + usage\n+    CS->>CS: Commit actual usage to quota_service (debit input_tokens + output_tokens)\n+\n+    participant AS as audit_service\n+    CS->>AS: Emit audit event (usage, policy decisions)\n+\n+    opt Thread summary update triggered (background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Full lifecycle of a user message - from authorization through streaming LLM response to persistence and optional thread compression. Authorization is evaluated before any database access. The PEP sends an evaluation request to the AuthZ Resolver with the chat's resource type and ID; the returned constraints are applied to the DB query's WHERE clause. If the PDP is unreachable, the request is rejected immediately (fail-closed, 403). If the PDP denies access for an operation with a concrete `chat_id`, the PEP returns 404 to avoid disclosing resource existence. If the constrained query returns 0 rows, the PEP returns 404.\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-file-upload`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments (multipart)\n+    AG->>CS: UploadAttachment(chat_id, file, security_ctx)\n+    CS->>DB: Insert attachment metadata (status: pending)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/files (upload)\n+    OG->>OAI: Files API upload\n+    OAI-->>OG: file_id\n+    OG-->>CS: file_id\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/vector_stores/{tenant_store}/files (add file)\n+    OG->>OAI: Add file to vector store\n+    OAI-->>OG: OK\n+    OG-->>CS: OK\n+    CS->>DB: Update attachment (status: ready, provider_file_id)\n+\n+    opt Generate doc summary (Variant 1, background)\n+        CS->>CS: Enqueue doc summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    end\n+\n+    CS-->>AG: attachment_id, status\n+    AG-->>UI: 201 Created\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: File upload flow - the file is uploaded to the LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) via OAGW, added to the tenant's vector store, optionally summarized, and metadata is persisted locally.\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-cancellation`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant UI\n+    participant AG as api_gateway\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant LP as llm_provider\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    UI->>AG: SSE connection established\n+    AG->>AG: Create CancellationToken\n+    AG->>CS: StartChatTurn(..., cancel_token)\n+    CS->>LP: stream_response(..., cancel_token)\n+    LP->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses:stream\n+    OG->>OAI: Streaming in progress\n+\n+    alt UI disconnect\n+        UI--xAG: Client disconnects / stop button\n+        AG->>AG: Detect broken SSE write -> cancel token\n+    else Server timeout\n+        CS->>CS: Timeout elapsed -> cancel token\n+    end\n+\n+    LP->>LP: Token cancelled -> abort HTTP request\n+    LP->>OG: Close connection (hard cancel)\n+    OG->>OAI: TCP RST / connection closed\n+    CS->>CS: Persist partial response (usage may be unknown)\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Cancellation propagates end-to-end via a shared `CancellationToken`. When triggered, `llm_provider` performs a hard cancel - aborting the outbound HTTP connection so the LLM provider (OpenAI / Azure OpenAI) stops generating immediately. The partial response is persisted. Because provider usage is typically only delivered on a completed response, `input_tokens`/`output_tokens` may be NULL or approximate for cancelled turns; quota enforcement uses the bounded best-effort debit described in `quota_service`.\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Update\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-seq-thread-summary`\n+\n+```mermaid\n+sequenceDiagram\n+    participant CS as chat_service\n+    participant DB as chat_store\n+    participant OG as outbound_gateway\n+    participant OAI as OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+    CS->>CS: Check summary trigger (msg count > 20 OR tokens > budget OR every 15 user turns)\n+    CS->>CS: Enqueue thread summary task (requester_type=system)\n+    Note over CS: Background worker executes summary task asynchronously\n+    CS->>DB: Load current thread_summary + old messages batch (10-20 msgs)\n+    CS->>OG: POST /outbound/llm/responses (update summary prompt)\n+    OG->>OAI: Responses API\n+    OAI-->>OG: Updated summary\n+    OG-->>CS: Updated summary\n+    CS->>DB: Save new thread_summary\n+    CS->>DB: Mark summarized messages as compressed\n+```\n+\n+**Description**: Thread summary is updated asynchronously after a chat turn when trigger conditions are met. Summary generation is a background task and MUST be attributed as `requester_type=system` so that its usage is not charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+Summary quality gate (P0): the system MUST detect and mitigate obviously-bad summaries without ML-based evaluation.\n+\n+- After generating a summary, `chat_service` MUST validate the candidate summary text.\n+- If summary length < `X` OR entropy < `Y`, `chat_service` MUST attempt regeneration.\n+- If regeneration fails quality checks or the provider call fails, `chat_service` MUST fall back by keeping the previous summary unchanged and MUST NOT mark the message batch as compressed.\n+\n+`X` and `Y` are configurable thresholds. Entropy is a deterministic proxy computed as normalized token entropy over whitespace-delimited tokens:\n+\n+`H_norm = (-sum(p_i * log2(p_i))) / log2(N)` where `p_i` is the empirical frequency of token `i` and `N` is the number of distinct tokens.\n+\n+Observability:\n+\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}` for each regeneration attempt (`reason` from a bounded allowlist such as `too_short|low_entropy|provider_error|invalid_format`).\n+- Increment `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total` when the fallback behavior above is used.\n+\n+### 3.7 Database Schemas & Tables\n+\n+#### Table: chats\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chats`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Chat identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | Owning user |\n+| title | VARCHAR(255) | Chat title (user-set or auto-generated) |\n+| is_temporary | BOOLEAN | If true, auto-deleted after 24h |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last activity time |\n+| deleted_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Soft delete timestamp (nullable) |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `created_at`\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, updated_at DESC)` for listing chats\n+\n+#### Table: messages\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-messages`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Message identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key (nullable). Used for completed replay and traceability. Running state is tracked in `chat_turns`. |\n+| role | VARCHAR(16) | `user`, `assistant`, or `system` |\n+| content | TEXT | Message content |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| request_kind | VARCHAR(16) | `chat`, `summary`, or `doc_summary` (nullable) |\n+| features_used | JSONB | Feature flags and counters (nullable) |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Actual input tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Actual output tokens for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| model | VARCHAR(64) | Actual model used for assistant messages (nullable) |\n+| is_compressed | BOOLEAN | True if included in a thread summary |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `chat_id`, `role`, `content`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE. UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)` WHERE `request_id IS NOT NULL`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(chat_id, created_at)` for loading recent messages\n+\n+#### Table: chat_turns\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-chat-turns`\n+\n+Tracks idempotency and in-progress generation state for `request_id`. This avoids ambiguous interpretation of `messages.request_id` when a generation is still running.\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Turn identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| request_id | UUID | Client-generated idempotency key |\n+| requester_type | VARCHAR(16) | `user` or `system` |\n+| requester_user_id | UUID | User ID when requester_type=`user` (nullable for system) |\n+| state | VARCHAR(16) | `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled` |\n+| provider_name | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier (nullable until request starts) |\n+| provider_response_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider response ID (nullable) |\n+| assistant_message_id | UUID | Persisted assistant message ID (nullable until completed) |\n+| error_code | VARCHAR(64) | Terminal error code (nullable) |\n+| started_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Start time |\n+| completed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Completion time (nullable) |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(chat_id, request_id)`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+A `chat_turns` row MUST be created before starting the outbound provider request; initial state is `running`.\n+\n+State machine:\n+- Allowed transitions: `running` -> `completed` \\| `failed` \\| `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states: `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`\n+- Terminal states MUST be immutable\n+- At most one `running` turn per `(chat_id, request_id)`\n+\n+#### Table: attachments\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-attachments`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Attachment identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Owning tenant |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id) |\n+| filename | VARCHAR(255) | Original filename |\n+| content_type | VARCHAR(128) | MIME type |\n+| size_bytes | BIGINT | File size |\n+| provider_file_id | VARCHAR(128) | LLM provider file ID - OpenAI `file-*` or Azure OpenAI `assistant-*` (nullable until upload completes) |\n+| status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `ready`, `failed` |\n+| doc_summary | TEXT | LLM-generated document summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_model | VARCHAR(64) | Model used to generate the summary (nullable) |\n+| summary_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When the summary was last generated (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_status | VARCHAR(16) | `pending`, `in_progress`, `done`, `failed` (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_attempts | INTEGER | Cleanup retry attempts (default 0) |\n+| last_cleanup_error | TEXT | Last cleanup error (nullable) |\n+| cleanup_updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | When cleanup state was last updated (nullable) |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Upload time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `tenant_id`, `chat_id`, `filename`, `status`, `created_at`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: thread_summaries\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-thread-summaries`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Summary identifier |\n+| chat_id | UUID | Parent chat (FK -> chats.id, UNIQUE) |\n+| summary_text | TEXT | Compressed conversation summary |\n+| summarized_up_to | UUID | Last message ID included in this summary |\n+| token_estimate | INTEGER | Estimated token count of summary |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `chat_id`. FK `chat_id` -> `chats.id` ON DELETE CASCADE.\n+\n+#### Table: tenant_vector_stores\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-vector-stores`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant identifier |\n+| vector_store_id | VARCHAR(128) | Provider vector store ID (OpenAI `vs_*` or Azure OpenAI equivalent) |\n+| provider | VARCHAR(32) | Provider identifier: `openai` or `azure_openai` |\n+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Creation time |\n+\n+**PK**: `tenant_id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: NOT NULL on `vector_store_id`, `provider`, `created_at`. One vector store per tenant.\n+\n+Creation protocol (P0): `chat_service` uses a get-or-create flow with database uniqueness as the race arbiter.\n+\n+1. Attempt to read the row by `tenant_id`.\n+2. If not present, create the vector store via OAGW and attempt INSERT.\n+3. If INSERT fails due to unique violation (concurrent creator), re-read the row and use the existing `vector_store_id`. Best-effort delete the newly created provider vector store to avoid orphans.\n+\n+ **P0**: run a periodic reconcile/orphan reaper job (for example nightly) to reconcile provider state with `tenant_vector_stores`:\n+ - If a provider vector store exists but is not referenced in DB -> delete it.\n+ - If a DB row exists but the provider vector store is missing -> recreate the vector store and update the DB row.\n+\n+#### Table: quota_usage\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-quota-usage`\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-quota-usage-accounting`\n+\n+| Column | Type | Description |\n+|--------|------|-------------|\n+| id | UUID | Record identifier |\n+| tenant_id | UUID | Tenant |\n+| user_id | UUID | User |\n+| period_type | VARCHAR(16) | `daily` or `monthly` |\n+| period_start | DATE | Start of the period |\n+| input_tokens | BIGINT | Total input tokens consumed |\n+| output_tokens | BIGINT | Total output tokens consumed |\n+| file_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of file search tool calls |\n+| web_search_calls | INTEGER | Number of web search calls (P1+) |\n+| rag_retrieval_calls | INTEGER | Number of internal RAG retrieval calls (P1+) |\n+| premium_model_calls | INTEGER | Calls to premium models |\n+| updated_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | Last update time |\n+\n+Commit semantics: quota updates MUST be atomic per period record. Implementations SHOULD use a transaction with row locking or a single UPDATE statement to avoid race conditions under parallel streams.\n+\n+**PK**: `id`\n+\n+**Constraints**: UNIQUE on `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)`.\n+\n+**Indexes**: `(tenant_id, user_id, period_type, period_start)` for quota lookups\n+\n+#### Projection Table: tenant_closure\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-dbtable-tenant-closure-ref`\n+\n+Mini Chat does NOT require the `tenant_closure` local projection table for chat content access in P0. Chat content is owner-only and requires exact `owner_tenant_id` + `user_id` predicates.\n+\n+The `tenant_closure` projection table exists in the platform authorization model for modules that use hierarchical tenant scoping, but it is unused for Mini Chat content operations.\n+\n+Schema is defined in the [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#table-schemas-local-projections).\n+\n+**P2+ note**: When chat sharing (projects) is introduced, `resource_group_membership` and optionally `resource_group_closure` tables will also be required.\n+\n+### 3.8 Authorization (PEP)\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-authz-pep`\n+\n+Mini Chat acts as a Policy Enforcement Point (PEP) per the platform's PDP/PEP authorization model defined in [Authorization Design](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md). The `chat_service` builds evaluation requests, sends them to the AuthZ Resolver (PDP), and compiles returned constraints into SQL WHERE clauses.\n+\n+Policy (P0): chat content is owner-only. For all content operations, authorization MUST enforce:\n+\n+- `owner_tenant_id == subject.tenant_id`\n+- `user_id == subject.id`\n+\n+Mini Chat does not use tenant hierarchy or subtree-based authorization for content operations.\n+\n+#### Resource Type\n+\n+The authorized resource is **Chat**. Sub-resources (Message, Attachment, ThreadSummary) do not have independent authorization - they are accessed through their parent chat, and the chat's authorization decision covers all child operations.\n+\n+| Attribute | Value |\n+|-----------|-------|\n+| GTS Type ID | `gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~` |\n+| Primary table | `chats` |\n+| Authorization granularity | Chat-level (sub-resources inherit) |\n+\n+#### PEP Configuration\n+\n+**Capabilities** (declared in `context.capabilities`):\n+\n+| Capability | P0 | P2+ | Rationale |\n+|------------|-----|------|-----------|\n+| `tenant_hierarchy` | No | No | Chat content is owner-only; `tenant_closure` is unused for Mini Chat content operations |\n+| `group_membership` | No | Yes | Needed when chat sharing via projects is introduced |\n+| `group_hierarchy` | No | Maybe | Needed if projects have nested hierarchy |\n+\n+**Supported properties** (declared in `context.supported_properties`):\n+\n+| Resource Property | SQL Column | Description |\n+|-------------------|------------|-------------|\n+| `owner_tenant_id` | `chats.tenant_id` | Owning tenant |\n+| `user_id` | `chats.user_id` | Owning user |\n+| `id` | `chats.id` | Chat identifier |\n+\n+#### Per-Operation Authorization Matrix\n+\n+| Endpoint | Action | `resource.id` | `require_constraints` | Expected P0 Predicates |\n+|----------|--------|---------------|----------------------|----------------------|\n+| `POST /v1/chats` | `create` | absent | `false` | decision only (no constraints) |\n+| `GET /v1/chats` | `list` | absent | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}` | `read` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `DELETE /v1/chats/{id}` | `delete` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}:temporary` | `update` | present | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream` | `send_message` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `POST /v1/chats/{id}/attachments` | `upload` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+| `GET /v1/chats/{id}/turns/{request_id}` | `read_turn` | present (chat_id) | `true` | `eq(owner_tenant_id)` + `eq(user_id)` |\n+\n+**Notes**:\n+- `send_message` and `upload` are actions on the Chat resource, not on Message or Attachment resources. The `resource.id` is the chat's ID.\n+- For streaming (`send_message`), authorization is evaluated once at SSE connection establishment. The entire streaming session operates under the initial authorization decision. No per-message re-authorization.\n+- For `create`, the PEP passes `resource.properties.owner_tenant_id` and `resource.properties.user_id` from the SecurityContext. The PDP validates permission without returning constraints.\n+\n+#### Evaluation Request/Response Examples\n+\n+**Example 1: List Chats** (`GET /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"list\" },\n+  \"resource\": { \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\" },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response (P0 - user-owned chats only):\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+ORDER BY updated_at DESC\n+```\n+\n+**Example 2: Get Chat** (`GET /v1/chats/{id}`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"read\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"chat-456\"\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": true,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"decision\": true,\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"constraints\": [\n+      {\n+        \"predicates\": [\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"owner_tenant_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+          },\n+          {\n+            \"type\": \"eq\",\n+            \"resource_property\": \"user_id\",\n+            \"value\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    ]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+Compiled SQL:\n+```sql\n+SELECT * FROM chats\n+WHERE id = 'chat-456'\n+  AND tenant_id = 'tenant-xyz-789'\n+  AND user_id = 'user-abc-123'\n+  AND deleted_at IS NULL\n+```\n+\n+Result: 1 row -> return chat; 0 rows -> 404 Not Found (hides existence from unauthorized users).\n+\n+**Example 3: Create Chat** (`POST /v1/chats`)\n+\n+PEP -> PDP Request:\n+```jsonc\n+{\n+  \"subject\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.core.security.subject_user.v1~\",\n+    \"id\": \"user-abc-123\",\n+    \"properties\": { \"tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\" }\n+  },\n+  \"action\": { \"name\": \"create\" },\n+  \"resource\": {\n+    \"type\": \"gts.x.cf.mini_chat.chat.v1~\",\n+    \"properties\": {\n+      \"owner_tenant_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\",\n+      \"user_id\": \"user-abc-123\"\n+    }\n+  },\n+  \"context\": {\n+    \"tenant_context\": {\n+      \"mode\": \"root_only\",\n+      \"root_id\": \"tenant-xyz-789\"\n+    },\n+    \"token_scopes\": [\"*\"],\n+    \"require_constraints\": false,\n+    \"capabilities\": [],\n+    \"supported_properties\": [\"owner_tenant_id\", \"user_id\", \"id\"]\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+PDP -> PEP Response:\n+```jsonc\n+{ \"decision\": true }\n+```\n+\n+PEP proceeds with INSERT. No constraints needed for create.\n+\n+**Example 4: Send Message** (`POST /v1/chats/{id}/messages:stream`)\n+\n+Same authorization flow as Example 2 (Get Chat), but with `\"action\": { \"name\": \"send_message\" }` and `\"resource.id\"` set to the chat ID. Authorization is evaluated once before the SSE stream is established. The constraints are applied to the query that loads the chat and its messages from `chat_store`.\n+\n+#### Fail-Closed Behavior\n+\n+Mini Chat follows the platform's fail-closed rules (see [Authorization Design - Fail-Closed Rules](../../../docs/arch/authorization/DESIGN.md#fail-closed-rules)):\n+\n+| Condition | PEP Action |\n+|-----------|------------|\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` present | 404 Not Found (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| `decision: false` and `resource.id` absent | 403 Forbidden (do not expose `deny_reason.details`) |\n+| PDP unreachable / timeout | 403 Forbidden (fail-closed) |\n+| `decision: true` + no constraints + `require_constraints: true` | 403 Forbidden |\n+| Unknown predicate type in constraints | Treat constraint as false; if all constraints false -> 403 |\n+| Unknown `resource_property` in predicate | Treat constraint as false; log error (PDP contract violation) |\n+| Empty `constraints: []` | 403 Forbidden |\n+\n+#### Token Scopes\n+\n+Mini Chat recognizes the following token scopes for third-party application narrowing:\n+\n+| Scope | Permits |\n+|-------|---------|\n+| `ai:chat` | All chat operations (umbrella scope) |\n+| `ai:chat:read` | `list`, `read` actions only |\n+| `ai:chat:write` | `create`, `update`, `delete`, `send_message`, `upload` actions |\n+\n+First-party applications (UI) use `token_scopes: [\"*\"]`. Third-party integrations receive narrowed scopes. Scope enforcement is handled by the PDP - the PEP includes `token_scopes` in the evaluation request context.\n+\n+#### P2+ Extensibility: Chat Sharing\n+\n+When Projects / chat sharing is introduced (P2+), the authorization model extends naturally:\n+\n+1. Add `group_membership` capability (and optionally `group_hierarchy`).\n+2. Maintain `resource_group_membership` projection table mapping chat IDs to project group IDs.\n+3. The PDP returns additional access paths via OR'd constraints - e.g., one constraint for owned chats (`user_id` predicate), another for shared-via-project chats (`in_group` predicate).\n+4. `supported_properties` remains unchanged (the `id` property is used for group membership joins).\n+\n+No changes to the PEP flow or constraint compilation logic are needed. The PDP's response structure naturally handles multiple access paths through OR'd constraints.\n+\n+## 4. Additional Context\n+\n+### P0 Scope Boundaries\n+\n+**Included in P0**:\n+- Single-tenant vector store per tenant (not per workspace)\n+- Thread summary as only compression mechanism\n+- On-upload document summary via File Search (Variant 1 from draft)\n+- Quota enforcement: daily + monthly per user\n+- Temporary chats with 24h scheduled cleanup\n+- File Search per-message call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2 tool calls per message)\n+\n+**Deferred to P2+**:\n+- Projects / chat sharing\n+- Full-text search across chats\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are both supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies (beyond simple limits)\n+- Per-workspace vector stores\n+\n+### Data Classification and Retention (P0)\n+\n+Chat content may contain PII or sensitive data. Mini Chat treats messages and summaries as customer content and applies data minimization and retention controls.\n+\n+**Data classes**:\n+- Chat content: `messages.content`, `thread_summaries.summary_text`, `attachments.doc_summary`\n+- Operational metadata: IDs, timestamps, provider correlation IDs, quotas\n+- Audit events: structured records emitted to platform `audit_service`\n+\n+**Retention**:\n+- Temporary chats are scheduled for cleanup after 24h and MUST be deleted within 25h (PRD SLA).\n+- Non-temporary chats are retained until explicit deletion by default, subject to operator-configured retention policies.\n+- Soft-deleted chats (`deleted_at` set) are hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+**Audit content handling (P0)**:\n+- Audit events include the minimal content required for security and incident response.\n+- `chat_service` MUST redact secret patterns (tokens, keys, credentials) before sending content to `audit_service`.\n+- Redaction MUST be testable and based on a bounded allowlist of rule classes.\n+\n+| Rule class | Example (non-exhaustive) | Action |\n+|-----------|---------------------------|--------|\n+| `bearer_token` | `Authorization: Bearer eyJ...` | Replace token with `Bearer [REDACTED]` |\n+| `jwt` | `eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJS...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_JWT]` |\n+| `openai_api_key` | `sk-...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]` |\n+| `aws_access_key_id` | `AKIA...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]` |\n+| `azure_api_key` | `api-key: ...`, `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: ...` | Replace with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]` |\n+| `api_key_fields` | `api_key=...`, `x-api-key=...`, `client_secret=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `password_fields` | `password=...` | Replace value with `[REDACTED]` |\n+| `pem_private_key` | `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` | Replace block with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]` |\n+| `long_base64_like` | any base64-like token > 200 chars | Replace token with `…[TRUNCATED_LONG_TOKEN]` |\n+- Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes; they MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries.\n+- Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a maximum of 8 KiB per field (append `…[TRUNCATED]`).\n+- Full DLP-based content redaction is deferred to P2.\n+\n+### Context Plan Assembly Rules\n+\n+On each user message, `chat_service` assembles a `ContextPlan` in this order:\n+\n+1. **System prompt** - fixed instructions for the assistant. The system prompt is configuration and is not persisted as a `messages` row. For debugging, the prompt version SHOULD be recorded on the assistant message (for example in `features_used` or provider metadata).\n+2. **Thread summary** - if exists, replaces older history\n+3. **Document summaries** - short descriptions of attached documents\n+4. **Recent messages** - last 6-10 messages not covered by summary\n+5. **User message** - current turn\n+\n+If the total exceeds the input token budget, truncation happens in reverse priority: old messages first, then doc summaries, then retrieval excerpts. Summary and system prompt are never truncated.\n+\n+### File Search Trigger Heuristics\n+\n+File Search is invoked when:\n+- User explicitly references documents (\"in the file\", \"in section\", \"according to the document\")\n+- Documents are attached and the query likely relates to them\n+- User requests citations or sources\n+\n+Limits: per-message file_search tool call limit is configurable per deployment (default: 2); max calls per day/user tracked in `quota_usage`.\n+\n+### Three-Tier Rate Limiting & Throttling\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-design-throttling-tiers`\n+\n+Rate limiting and quota enforcement are split into three tiers with strict ownership boundaries. These tiers MUST NOT be mixed - each has a single owner and distinct responsibility.\n+\n+| Tier | Owner | What It Controls | Examples                                                                                              |\n+|------|-------|-----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n+| **Product quota** | `quota_service` (in `chat_service`) | Per-user daily/monthly token budgets, premium/standard model allocation, file_search call limits, auto-downgrade | \"Premium calls exhausted -> downgrade to gpt-5-mini\"; \"Daily limit reached -> 429 `quota_exceeded`\" |\n+| **Platform rate limit** | `api_gateway` middleware | Per-user/per-IP request rate, concurrent stream caps, abuse protection | \"20 rps per user\"; \"Max 5 concurrent SSE streams\"                                                     |\n+| **Provider rate limit** | OAGW | Provider 429 handling, `Retry-After` respect, circuit breaker, global concurrency cap | \"OpenAI 429 -> wait `Retry-After` -> retry once -> propagate 429 upstream\"                            |\n+\n+**Key rules**:\n+- Product quota decisions happen BEFORE the request reaches OAGW. If quota is exhausted, the request never leaves `chat_service`.\n+- OAGW does NOT know about tenants, users, licenses, or premium status. It handles only provider-level concerns (retry, circuit breaker, concurrency cap).\n+- Provider 429 from OAGW is propagated to `chat_service`, which maps it to `rate_limited` (429) for the client with a meaningful error message.\n+- Mid-stream quota abort is NOT supported - quota is checked at preflight only. Mid-stream abort is only triggered by: user cancel, provider error, or infrastructure limits (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-constraint-quota-before-outbound`).\n+\n+**Quota counting flow**:\n+\n+```\n+Preflight (reserve) (before LLM call):\n+  estimate = tokens(ContextPlan) + max_output_tokens\n+  if estimate > remaining_quota -> downgrade model or reject\n+\n+Commit (after done event):\n+  actual = response.usage.input_tokens + response.usage.output_tokens\n+  quota_usage += actual\n+  (if actual > estimate -> debit overshoot, never cancel completed response)\n+```\n+\n+### Provider Request Metadata\n+\n+Every request sent to the LLM provider via `llm_provider` MUST include two identification mechanisms. Both OpenAI and Azure OpenAI support these fields with identical semantics.\n+\n+**`user` field** - composite tenant+user identifier for provider usage monitoring and abuse detection:\n+\n+```\n+\"user\": \"{tenant_id}:{user_id}\"\n+```","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md","commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@MikeFalcon77`, thank you for fixing the language tag! 🎉\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:27Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:39:27Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813023605","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813023605"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813023605"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813023605/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":1284,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":1286,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572940,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":1286,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027295","pull_request_review_id":3809307942,"id":2813027295,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq2Pf","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n# Search for the FR ID in ADR files\necho \"=== Searching for cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats in ADR files ===\"\nrg 'cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats' modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/ -n -C2\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Listing ADR files ===\"\nfd . modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/ --type f\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Checking if ADR 0003 mentions group chat ===\"\ncat modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/0003-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution.md | head -50\n```\n\nLength of output: 3381\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n`@MikeFalcon77`, you're absolutely correct! I can confirm that `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats` is referenced in ADR 0003 (line 126) in the traceability section where it states the ADR addresses ownership and usage attribution rules for shared chat functionality (P2+).\n\nYour architectural separation makes sense: P0 scope lives in DESIGN.md, while deferred P2+ features and their architectural decisions are documented in ADR files. This keeps DESIGN.md focused on what's being built now, while ADRs capture the rationale for future design decisions.\n\nThe pipeline validation error appears to be a configuration issue—the validation rule is checking for references in DESIGN.md specifically but not recognizing ADR files as valid reference locations. You have two options:\n\n1. **Update the pipeline validation rules** to accept ADR files as valid artifacts for FR ID references (recommended, if you control the validation configuration)\n2. **Add a minimal cross-reference** in DESIGN.md's deferred section that points to the ADR (e.g., \"See ADR 0003 for group chat design decisions\")\n\nI'll note this pattern for future reviews.\n\n<!-- [add_learning]\nIn the cyberfabric-core repository, deferred P2+ features may have their functional requirement IDs (FR IDs) referenced in ADR files (modules/*/docs/ADR/) rather than in DESIGN.md. This architectural separation is intentional: DESIGN.md covers P0 implementation scope, while ADRs document future architectural decisions and deferred features.\n</learning>\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create change proposals when adding features, making breaking changes, changing architecture, optimizing performance (behavior changes), or updating security patterns; skip proposals for bug fixes, typos, comments, non-breaking dependency updates, and configuration changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:11Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:11Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027295","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027295"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027295"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027295/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":110,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":112,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572946,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":112,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027462","pull_request_review_id":3809308107,"id":2813027462,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq2SG","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n# Search for the interface ID in ADR files and DESIGN.md\necho \"=== Searching for cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api in ADR files ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api\" modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Searching for cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api in DESIGN.md ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== List all ADR files ===\"\nfd -t f . modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Check if there are any references to public interfaces in ADR files ===\"\nrg -n \"interface.*rest.*api\" -i modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n```\n\nLength of output: 487\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n# Check what the PRD validation constraint might be looking for\necho \"=== Check DESIGN.md for any ID references or interface sections ===\"\nrg -n \"^\\*\\*ID\\*\\*:|^ID:\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md | head -20\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Check DESIGN.md section 3.3 (API Contracts) structure ===\"\nsed -n '260,280p' modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Search for 'interface' in DESIGN.md ===\"\nrg -n -i \"interface\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md | head -10\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Check if there's a validation config or constraint file ===\"\nfd -t f \"constraint|validation|pipeline\" modules/mini-chat/\n```\n\nLength of output: 2234\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n`@MikeFalcon77`, I checked the ADR files and the ID `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api` is **not present** in any of them:\n\n- `0001-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-llm-provider-as-library.md` ❌\n- `0002-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-internal-transport.md` ❌\n- `0003-cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-group-chat-usage-attribution.md` ❌\n\nThe validation error indicates that when the PRD declares a **public interface** (section 7.1, line 488), the corresponding ID must be referenced in DESIGN.md—specifically in the section that describes that interface.\n\nLooking at DESIGN.md section 3.3 (API Contracts), it currently starts with:\n\n```markdown\n### 3.3 API Contracts\n\n**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n```\n\nThe fix is to add the interface ID at the beginning of this section:\n\n```markdown\n### 3.3 API Contracts\n\n**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n\n**Technology**: REST/OpenAPI, SSE\n```\n\nThis links the PRD public interface declaration to its detailed design specification, which is the validation requirement that's failing.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create change proposals when adding features, making breaking changes, changing architecture, optimizing performance (behavior changes), or updating security patterns; skip proposals for bug fixes, typos, comments, non-breaking dependency updates, and configuration changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:13Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:13Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027462","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027462"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027462"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027462/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":488,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":494,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572953,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":494,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027679","pull_request_review_id":3809308365,"id":2813027679,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq2Vf","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n# Check what IDs are defined in ADR files\necho \"=== Searching for IDs in ADR files ===\"\nfd -e md . modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/ --exec echo \"File: {}\" \\; --exec rg -n \"ID:|id:\" {} \\;\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Checking if cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability appears in ADR files ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability\" modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Checking if this NFR ID appears in DESIGN.md ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== List all files in ADR directory ===\"\nfd -e md . modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Check what NFR/FR IDs are referenced in DESIGN.md ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-(nfr|fr)-\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md | head -30\n```\n\nLength of output: 4824\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n`@MikeFalcon77`, I've checked the ADR files and DESIGN.md. The validation error is specifically about **`cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`**, which:\n\n- ❌ Does **not** appear in any ADR file (0001, 0002, or 0003)\n- ❌ Does **not** appear in DESIGN.md\n- ✅ **Is** defined in PRD.md (line 353) as a **P0** requirement\n\nYour DESIGN.md already references other P0 NFRs in the NFR Allocation table (lines 39-43):\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n\nThe observability NFR is missing from this table. Since it's a P0 requirement (not deferred to P2+ like group chats in ADR-0003), should it be added to the NFR Allocation table alongside the other P0 NFRs?\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create change proposals when adding features, making breaking changes, changing architecture, optimizing performance (behavior changes), or updating security patterns; skip proposals for bug fixes, typos, comments, non-breaking dependency updates, and configuration changes\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:16Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:16Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027679","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027679"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027679"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027679/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":535,"original_start_line":353,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":665,"original_line":461,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572951,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":461,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027689","pull_request_review_id":3809308373,"id":2813027689,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq2Vp","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+\n+#### Turn Status (read-only) API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status`\n+\n+Support and UX recovery flows SHOULD be able to query authoritative turn state backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n`@MikeFalcon77`, let me verify the ADR structure to better understand your documentation pattern.\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n# Check if the interface ID exists in ADR files\necho \"=== Searching for interface ID in ADR files ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status\" modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho -e \"\\n=== Listing all ADR files ===\"\nfd -t f . modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho -e \"\\n=== Checking for other interface IDs in ADR files ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-\" modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho -e \"\\n=== Checking DESIGN.md for existing interface IDs ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md\n```\n\nLength of output: 428\n\n---\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n# Let's see what IDs are actually defined in the ADR files\necho \"=== Checking all cpt-cf-mini-chat IDs in ADR files ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-\" modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho -e \"\\n=== Checking what the PRD Turn Status section says ===\"\nsed -n '485,510p' modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md\n\necho -e \"\\n=== Check if any ADR mentions Turn Status ===\"\nrg -i -n \"turn.status\" modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n```\n\nLength of output: 3715\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n`@MikeFalcon77`, I checked the ADR files and found that the interface ID `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status` is not actually present in any of them. Here's what I found:\n\n**ADR files contain:**\n- ADR-specific IDs like `cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-llm-provider-as-library`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-adr-internal-transport`, etc.\n- References to PRD/DESIGN requirement IDs (e.g., `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`)\n\n**The Turn Status interface ID:**\n- Only appears in PRD.md (line 497)\n- Is marked as \"P0 optional, recommended\" - not a deferred/P2+ feature\n- Is not referenced in DESIGN.md, which is causing the pipeline validation error\n\nThe pipeline validation expects interface IDs defined in PRD.md to be referenced in DESIGN.md. The Turn Status API appears to be part of the P0 scope (though optional), so it would typically need the corresponding section in DESIGN.md to include the interface ID reference.\n\nIf you'd like to keep Turn Status documentation out of DESIGN.md entirely, you might consider moving it to a separate document or clarifying its status in the PRD.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create change proposals when adding features, making breaking changes, changing architecture, optimizing performance (behavior changes), or updating security patterns; skip proposals for bug fixes, typos, comments, non-breaking dependency updates, and configuration changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:16Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:16Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027689","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027689"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027689"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027689/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":731,"original_start_line":497,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":744,"original_line":509,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572957,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":509,"position":744,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027763","pull_request_review_id":3809308451,"id":2813027763,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq2Wz","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+\n+#### Turn Status (read-only) API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status`\n+\n+Support and UX recovery flows SHOULD be able to query authoritative turn state backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### SSE Streaming Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library\n+**Protocol/Format**: Server-Sent Events (SSE) over HTTP\n+**Compatibility**: Event types (`delta`, `tool`, `citations`, `done`, `error`, `ping`) and their payload schemas are stable within a major API version.\n+\n+**Ordering**: `ping* (delta|tool|citations)* (done|error)`.\n+\n+Provider identifiers (e.g., `file_id` in `citations`) are display-only metadata; clients MUST NOT send them back to any API.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n\n# Search for the SSE streaming contract ID in ADR files\necho \"=== Searching for cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming in ADR files ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming\" modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Listing all ADR files ===\"\nfd -t f . modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Searching for any SSE or streaming related IDs in ADR files ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat.*sse|cpt-cf-mini-chat.*streaming\" modules/mini-chat/docs/ADR/ -i\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Checking DESIGN.md for SSE streaming contract mention ===\"\nrg -n -C3 \"Streaming Contract|SSE\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md | head -50\n```\n\nLength of output: 6555\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n`@MikeFalcon77`, I searched the ADR files and found that `cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming` does not appear in any of them:\n\n- **ADR 0001**: LLM provider as library (references `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`)\n- **ADR 0002**: Internal transport (references `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`)  \n- **ADR 0003**: Group chat usage attribution (no streaming IDs)\n\nThe SSE streaming contract is defined in PRD.md (line 514) as a **P0 stable public API contract** under \"7.2 External Integration Contracts\", not as a deferred feature. DESIGN.md already has a \"Streaming Contract\" section (mentioned in line 33 for UX recovery and discussed around line 279+ based on the bot's original comment), but it doesn't explicitly include the contract ID.\n\nSince this is a P0 public interface contract (not an architectural decision or deferred feature), it should be documented in DESIGN.md alongside the streaming protocol details. The validation error appears legitimate—could you add the ID `cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming` to the \"Streaming Contract\" section in DESIGN.md as originally suggested?\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create change proposals when adding features, making breaking changes, changing architecture, optimizing performance (behavior changes), or updating security patterns; skip proposals for bug fixes, typos, comments, non-breaking dependency updates, and configuration changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:17Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:40:17Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027763","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027763"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813027763"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813027763/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":758,"original_start_line":514,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":793,"original_line":523,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572960,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":523,"position":793,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813040733","pull_request_review_id":3809322370,"id":2813040733,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq5hd","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"anyway I mark it as resolved","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:42:52Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:42:53Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813040733","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813040733"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813040733"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813040733/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":110,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":112,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572946,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":112,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813042985","pull_request_review_id":3809325155,"id":2813042985,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nq6Ep","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@MikeFalcon77`, understood! Thank you for the clarification on your architectural approach. I've noted this pattern for future reviews.\n\n┏(^‿^)┓\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create change proposals when adding features, making breaking changes, changing architecture, optimizing performance (behavior changes), or updating security patterns; skip proposals for bug fixes, typos, comments, non-breaking dependency updates, and configuration changes\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:43:19Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:43:20Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813042985","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813042985"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813042985"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813042985/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":110,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":112,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572946,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":112,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813085354","pull_request_review_id":3809370350,"id":2813085354,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nrEaq","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"fixed","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:52:25Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:52:25Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813085354","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813085354"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813085354"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813085354/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":535,"original_start_line":353,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":665,"original_line":461,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572951,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":461,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813088724","pull_request_review_id":3809374557,"id":2813088724,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nrFPU","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"<details>\n<summary>🧩 Analysis chain</summary>\n\n🏁 Script executed:\n```\n#!/bin/bash\n# Verify the NFR ID is now present in DESIGN.md\necho \"=== Checking if cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability now appears in DESIGN.md ===\"\nrg -n \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Showing context around any matches ===\"\nrg -n -C 3 \"cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== Checking the NFR Allocation table section ===\"\nrg -n -A 10 \"NFR Allocation\" modules/mini-chat/docs/DESIGN.md | head -30\n```\n\nLength of output: 2091\n\n---\n\n\n\n</details>\n\n`@MikeFalcon77`, I don't see the fix in DESIGN.md yet. The NFR ID `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability` is still not present in the file.\n\nThe NFR Allocation table (lines 37-43) currently contains only these five NFRs:\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n- `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n\nYou'll need to add a row for `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability` to this table. For example:\n\n```markdown\n| `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability` | Operational contract: metrics, SLOs, support signals | `chat_service`, OAGW, metrics | Prometheus series, Turn Status API, structured logs, distributed tracing | Metrics dashboard validation; support workflow verification |\n```\n\nDid you perhaps commit the change to a different branch or file?\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create change proposals when adding features, making breaking changes, changing architecture, optimizing performance (behavior changes), or updating security patterns; skip proposals for bug fixes, typos, comments, non-breaking dependency updates, and configuration changes\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:53:14Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:53:14Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813088724","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813088724"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813088724"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813088724/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":535,"original_start_line":353,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":665,"original_line":461,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572951,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":461,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813090031","pull_request_review_id":3809376030,"id":2813090031,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nrFjv","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+\n+#### Turn Status (read-only) API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status`\n+\n+Support and UX recovery flows SHOULD be able to query authoritative turn state backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### SSE Streaming Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library\n+**Protocol/Format**: Server-Sent Events (SSE) over HTTP\n+**Compatibility**: Event types (`delta`, `tool`, `citations`, `done`, `error`, `ping`) and their payload schemas are stable within a major API version.\n+\n+**Ordering**: `ping* (delta|tool|citations)* (done|error)`.\n+\n+Provider identifiers (e.g., `file_id` in `citations`) are display-only metadata; clients MUST NOT send them back to any API.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"fixed","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:53:33Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:53:33Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813090031","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813090031"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813090031"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813090031/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":758,"original_start_line":514,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":793,"original_line":523,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572960,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":523,"position":793,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813092586","pull_request_review_id":3809378880,"id":2813092586,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nrGLq","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+\n+#### Turn Status (read-only) API (P0 optional, recommended)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-turn-status`\n+\n+Support and UX recovery flows SHOULD be able to query authoritative turn state backed by `chat_turns`.\n+\n+**Endpoint**: `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}`\n+\n+**Response**:\n+\n+- `chat_id`\n+- `request_id`\n+- `state`: `running|done|error|cancelled`\n+- `updated_at`\n+\n+### 7.2 External Integration Contracts\n+\n+#### SSE Streaming Contract\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-contract-sse-streaming`\n+\n+**Direction**: provided by library\n+**Protocol/Format**: Server-Sent Events (SSE) over HTTP\n+**Compatibility**: Event types (`delta`, `tool`, `citations`, `done`, `error`, `ping`) and their payload schemas are stable within a major API version.\n+\n+**Ordering**: `ping* (delta|tool|citations)* (done|error)`.\n+\n+Provider identifiers (e.g., `file_id` in `citations`) are display-only metadata; clients MUST NOT send them back to any API.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@MikeFalcon77`, thank you for adding the contract ID to DESIGN.md! The SSE streaming contract is now properly documented as part of the P0 public interface. \n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n🐰✨\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create change proposals when adding features, making breaking changes, changing architecture, optimizing performance (behavior changes), or updating security patterns; skip proposals for bug fixes, typos, comments, non-breaking dependency updates, and configuration changes\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:54:10Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:54:10Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813092586","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813092586"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813092586"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813092586/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":758,"original_start_line":514,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":793,"original_line":523,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572960,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":523,"position":793,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813099938","pull_request_review_id":3809387546,"id":2813099938,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nrH-i","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"MikeFalcon77","id":3686170,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2ODYxNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3686170?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77","html_url":"https://github.com/MikeFalcon77","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/MikeFalcon77/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"fixed","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:56:02Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:56:02Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813099938","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813099938"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813099938"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813099938/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":488,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":494,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572953,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":494,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813102507","pull_request_review_id":3809390544,"id":2813102507,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nrImr","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,728 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for completed chat turns and policy decisions (one structured event per turn; see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete) API\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores — P0 uses a single shared vector store per tenant (all users and chats in a tenant share one provider vector store). Per-workspace isolation is deferred.\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+- Web search tool support (P1+; schema provisions exist in `quota_usage.web_search_calls`)\n+- URL content extraction\n+- Admin configuration UI for AI policies, model selection, or provider settings (P0 uses deployment configuration; see DESIGN.md Section 2.2 constraints and emergency flags)\n+- Multiple quota/budget periods beyond daily and monthly (e.g. 4h, 12h rolling windows)\n+- Module-specific multi-lingual support (LLM handles languages natively; no module-level i18n)\n+- Per-feature dynamic feature flags beyond the `ai_chat` license gate and emergency kill switches (DESIGN.md lines 166-168)\n+\n+### 4.3 Deferred (P2+)\n+\n+#### Group Chats / Collaboration\n+\n+- [ ] `p2` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-group-chats`\n+\n+Group chats and chat sharing (projects) are deferred to P2+ and are out of scope for P0/P1.\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If a `chat_turns` record with `state=running` exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`\n+\n+The system MUST emit structured audit events to the platform's `audit_service` for every AI interaction. Each event MUST include: tenant, user, chat reference, event type, model used, token counts, latency metrics, and policy decisions (quota checks, license gate results). Mini Chat does not store audit data locally.\n+\n+Before emitting events, `chat_service` MUST redact obvious secret patterns from any included content. P0 redaction rules MUST include at least:\n+\n+- Replace any `Authorization: Bearer <...>` header value with `Authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]`\n+- Replace any `api_key`, `x-api-key`, `client_secret`, `access_token`, `refresh_token` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace any `api-key: <...>` or `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key: <...>` header value with `[REDACTED_AZURE_KEY]`\n+- Replace OpenAI-style API keys with prefix `sk-` with `[REDACTED_OPENAI_KEY]`\n+- Replace AWS access key IDs (for example values matching `AKIA...`) with `[REDACTED_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID]`\n+- Replace JWT-like tokens (`header.payload.signature`) with `[REDACTED_JWT]`\n+- Replace any `password` values with `[REDACTED]` when they appear in `key=value` or JSON string field form\n+- Replace PEM private key blocks (lines between `-----BEGIN` and `-----END` containing `PRIVATE KEY`) with `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_KEY]`\n+\n+Audit events MUST NOT include raw attachment file bytes. Audit events MAY include attachment metadata and document summaries. Any included string content MUST be truncated after redaction to a configurable maximum per field (default: 8 KiB, append `…[TRUNCATED]`). The total audit event payload MUST NOT exceed the `audit_service` event size limit.\n+\n+Audit payload retention and deletion semantics are owned by platform `audit_service`.\n+\n+- `audit_service` is the system of record for audit TTL and deletion semantics.\n+- For P0, `audit_service` MUST retain Mini Chat audit payloads for at least 90 days by default (configurable).\n+- Mini Chat MUST NOT attempt to delete or mutate audit records after emission.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Compliance and security incident response require a record of AI usage with policy decisions. Audit storage and append-only semantics are the platform `audit_service` responsibility. Cost analytics and billing attribution are driven by internal usage records and Prometheus metrics (see `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`), not by audit events.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Cost Metrics\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-cost-metrics`\n+\n+The system MUST log the following metrics for every LLM request: model, input tokens, output tokens, file search call count, time to first token, total latency. Tenant and user attribution MUST be available via audit events and internal usage records; Prometheus labels MUST NOT include `tenant_id` or `user_id`.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Enables cost monitoring, budget alerts, and billing attribution per tenant/user.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.5 Data Lifecycle\n+\n+#### Chat Deletion with Resource Cleanup\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-deletion-cleanup`\n+\n+When a chat is deleted, the system MUST mark attachments for asynchronous cleanup and return without blocking on external provider operations. A cleanup worker MUST perform idempotent retries to remove files from the tenant's vector store and then delete the provider file. Local data MUST be soft-deleted or anonymized per the retention policy and hard-purged by a periodic cleanup job after a configurable grace period.\n+\n+For temporary chats, “deleted within 25 hours” means:\n+\n+- The chat is soft-deleted and no longer appears in chat list or history APIs within 25 hours of creation.\n+- Provider cleanup (vector store removal + provider file deletion) has a target completion time of 1 hour under normal conditions and is eventual with retry/backoff.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents orphaned external resources and ensures data governance compliance on deletion.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 6.1 Module-Specific NFRs\n+\n+#### Tenant Isolation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-tenant-isolation`\n+\n+Tenant data MUST never be accessible to users from another tenant. All data queries, file operations, and vector store searches MUST be scoped by tenant. The API MUST NOT accept raw external resource identifiers (file IDs, vector store IDs) from clients.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+Authorization follows the platform PDP/PEP fail-closed rules (including 404 masking for denied requests with a concrete resource ID); see DESIGN.md (Authorization / Fail-Closed Behavior).\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks\n+**Rationale**: Multi-tenant SaaS with sensitive documents requires strict data boundaries.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Tenant-Scoped Everything principle)\n+\n+#### Authorization Alignment\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-authz-alignment`\n+\n+Authorization MUST follow the platform PDP/PEP model, including query-level constraints compiled to SQL by the PEP and fail-closed behavior on PDP errors or unreachability.\n+\n+**Threshold**: Zero unauthorized reads/writes; fail-closed on 100% of PDP failures\n+**Rationale**: Chat content is sensitive and access must be enforced consistently at the query layer.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.8 (Authorization (PEP)) and Authorization Design (platform)\n+\n+#### Cost Predictability\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-cost-control`\n+\n+Per-user LLM costs MUST be bounded by configurable daily and monthly quotas. File search costs MUST be bounded by per-message and per-day call limits. The system MUST track actual costs with tenant aggregation and per-user attribution for quota enforcement. Administrator visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+**Threshold**: No user exceeds configured quota; estimated cost available for 100% of requests\n+**Rationale**: Unbounded LLM usage can generate unexpected costs; tenants need cost predictability.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 3.2 (quota_service component)\n+\n+#### Streaming Latency\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-streaming-latency`\n+\n+The system MUST minimize platform overhead beyond provider latency. Define `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms = t_first_token_ui - t_first_byte_from_provider`. Streaming events MUST be relayed without buffering.\n+\n+**Threshold**: `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms (platform overhead excluding provider latency)\n+**Rationale**: Users expect near-instant response start in a chat interface.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 2.1 (Streaming-First principle)\n+\n+#### Data Retention Compliance\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-data-retention`\n+\n+Temporary chats MUST be deleted within 25 hours of creation. Target: deleted chat resources (files, vector store entries) at the external provider removed within 1 hour under normal conditions; eventually consistent with retry/backoff on provider errors.\n+\n+**Threshold**: 100% of temporary chats cleaned up within SLA; target external resource cleanup within 1 hour under normal conditions, with retry/backoff on provider errors\n+**Rationale**: Regulatory and customer contractual requirements for data lifecycle management.\n+**Architecture Allocation**: See DESIGN.md section 4 (Cleanup on Chat Deletion)\n+\n+### 6.2 Observability and Supportability (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-nfr-observability-supportability`\n+\n+Mini Chat MUST provide an explicit operational contract to support on-call, SRE, and cost governance. This includes:\n+\n+#### Required support signals (P0)\n+\n+- Every chat turn MUST have a stable `request_id` (client idempotency key) and a persisted turn state (`running|completed|failed|cancelled`) that is exposed via the Turn Status API as (`running|done|error|cancelled`).\n+- Every completed provider request MUST be correlated via `provider_response_id` and MUST be persisted and searchable by operators.\n+- Support tooling MUST be able to determine turn state using server-side state (not inferred from client retry behavior).\n+\n+#### Prometheus metrics contract (P0)\n+\n+The service MUST expose Prometheus metrics with the following series names (types and label sets as specified in DESIGN.md):\n+\n+Prometheus labels MUST NOT include high-cardinality identifiers such as `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `chat_id`, `request_id`, or `provider_response_id`.\n+\n+##### Streaming and UX health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_stream_started_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_completed_total{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_failed_total{provider,model,error_code}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_disconnected_total{stage}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_replay_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_active_streams{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_provider_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_stream_total_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+\n+##### Cancellation\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_requested_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_effective_total{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_tokens_after_cancel{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_time_from_ui_disconnect_to_cancel_ms{trigger}`\n+- `mini_chat_cancel_orphan_total`\n+\n+##### Quota and cost control\n+\n+- `mini_chat_quota_preflight_total{decision,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserve_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_commit_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_negative_total{period}`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_estimated_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_actual_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_overshoot_tokens`\n+- `mini_chat_quota_reserved_tokens{period}`\n+\n+##### Tools and retrieval\n+\n+- `mini_chat_tool_calls_total{tool,phase}`\n+- `mini_chat_tool_call_limited_total{tool}`\n+- `mini_chat_file_search_latency_ms{provider,model}`\n+- `mini_chat_citations_count`\n+\n+##### Summarization health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_summary_regen_total{reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_summary_fallback_total`\n+\n+##### Provider / OAGW interaction\n+\n+- `mini_chat_provider_requests_total{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_errors_total{provider,status}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_retries_total{provider,reason}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_circuit_open_total{provider}`\n+- `mini_chat_provider_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+- `mini_chat_oagw_upstream_latency_ms{provider,endpoint}`\n+\n+##### Upload and attachments\n+\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_summary_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_pending{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachments_failed{instance}`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_upload_bytes`\n+- `mini_chat_attachment_index_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### Cleanup and drift\n+\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_job_runs_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_attempts_total{op,result}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_found_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_orphan_fixed_total{kind}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_backlog{state}`\n+- `mini_chat_cleanup_latency_ms{op}`\n+\n+##### Audit emission health\n+\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_total{result}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_redaction_hits_total{pattern}`\n+- `mini_chat_audit_emit_latency_ms`\n+\n+##### DB health (chat_store)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_db_query_latency_ms{query}`\n+- `mini_chat_db_errors_total{query,code}`\n+\n+#### SLOs / thresholds (P0)\n+\n+- `mini_chat_ttft_overhead_ms` p99 < 50 ms\n+- `mini_chat_time_to_abort_ms` p99 < 200 ms\n+- Temporary chat soft-delete within 25 hours\n+- Provider cleanup target completion within 1 hour under normal conditions (eventual with retry)\n+\n+### 6.3 UX Recovery Contract (P0)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-ux-recovery`\n+\n+The UI experience MUST be resilient to SSE disconnects and idempotency conflicts.\n+\n+#### Disconnect before terminal event\n+\n+- If the SSE stream disconnects before `done`/`error`, the UI MUST treat the send as indeterminate and MUST NOT auto-retry `POST /messages:stream` with the same `request_id`.\n+- After disconnect, the UI MAY call `GET /v1/chats/{chat_id}/turns/{request_id}` to determine whether the turn completed.\n+- The UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `Connection lost. Message delivery is uncertain. You can resend.`\n+- If the user chooses to resend, the UI MUST generate a new `request_id`.\n+\n+#### 409 Conflict (active generation)\n+\n+- On `409 Conflict` for `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST show a user-visible banner with the exact text: `A response is already in progress for this message. Please wait.`\n+\n+#### Completed replay (idempotent replay)\n+\n+- If the server replays a completed generation for an existing `(chat_id, request_id)`, the UI MUST render the response without duplicating the message in the timeline.\n+- The UI MUST show a non-blocking banner with the exact text: `Recovered a previously completed response.`\n+\n+## 7. Public Library Interfaces\n+\n+### 7.1 Public API Surface\n+\n+#### Chat REST API\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-interface-rest-api`\n+\n+**Type**: REST API\n+**Stability**: stable\n+**Description**: Public HTTP API for chat management, message streaming, and file upload. All endpoints require authentication and tenant license verification.\n+**Breaking Change Policy**: Versioned via URL prefix (`/v1/`). Breaking changes require new version.\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"8879072525541f1e591e64695ad9b5876a334803","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"`@MikeFalcon77`, thank you for adding the ID reference to DESIGN.md! That should resolve the validation error.\n\n<!-- <review_comment_addressed> -->\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings used</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:10.546Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : Create a `design.md` file only when the change involves cross-cutting changes across multiple services/modules, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance/migration complexity, or ambiguity requiring technical decisions. Include sections: Context, Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (with alternatives considered), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T19:55:41.776Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only when the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or design ambiguity\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create `design.md` only if the change involves: cross-cutting changes, new architectural patterns, new external dependencies, significant data model changes, security/performance complexity, or ambiguity benefiting from technical decisions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Applies to modules/system/types-registry/openspec/openspec/changes/*/design.md : In `design.md`, include sections: Context (background/constraints/stakeholders), Goals/Non-Goals, Decisions (what and why with alternatives), Risks/Trade-offs, Migration Plan, and Open Questions\n```\n\n---\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: cyberfabric/cyberfabric-core PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-02-10T18:54:47.158Z\nLearning: Create change proposals when adding features, making breaking changes, changing architecture, optimizing performance (behavior changes), or updating security patterns; skip proposals for bug fixes, typos, comments, non-breaking dependency updates, and configuration changes\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated reply by CodeRabbit -->","created_at":"2026-02-16T15:56:39Z","updated_at":"2026-02-16T15:56:39Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813102507","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813102507"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2813102507"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2813102507/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":488,"start_side":"RIGHT","line":null,"original_line":494,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2812572953,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","original_position":494,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814681643","pull_request_review_id":3811305746,"id":2814681643,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nxKIr","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"3ccec3c26580af6997d471473868786d6cd02c80","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"hitengajjar","id":17683811,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NjgzODEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17683811?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar","html_url":"https://github.com/hitengajjar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"While token-based budgeting is clear, we need to consider CF framework users with credit-based requirements. Would it be best practice to recommend a custom mapping system that sits atop our tokens, allowing them to align our technical metrics with their internal credit logic?","created_at":"2026-02-17T02:22:55Z","updated_at":"2026-02-17T02:22:55Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814681643","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814681643"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814681643"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814681643/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":35,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811823793,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":35,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814700605","pull_request_review_id":3811327016,"id":2814700605,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nxOw9","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"hitengajjar","id":17683811,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NjgzODEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17683811?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar","html_url":"https://github.com/hitengajjar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"> Search by title is not P0 (Section 4.2: \"Full-text search across chat history\" is out of scope).\r\n\r\nNoted and not mandatory, however, I think it can be easily added in P0 by enabling FTS on chat title in DB and querying it.","created_at":"2026-02-17T02:31:40Z","updated_at":"2026-02-17T02:31:40Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814700605","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814700605"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814700605"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814700605/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":109,"original_line":88,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811848155,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":88,"position":109,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814705817","pull_request_review_id":3811331932,"id":2814705817,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nxQCZ","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"hitengajjar","id":17683811,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NjgzODEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17683811?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar","html_url":"https://github.com/hitengajjar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Filtering on vectors data is a mandatory requirement since we are tenancy scoped application.\r\nWith filtering in place, we can have shared vector store.","created_at":"2026-02-17T02:34:07Z","updated_at":"2026-02-17T02:34:07Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814705817","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814705817"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814705817"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814705817/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":95,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811850534,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":95,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814741004","pull_request_review_id":3811367340,"id":2814741004,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nxYoM","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"ca24d33b5517d17e9e2af5d78600d61efbba7aa5","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"hitengajjar","id":17683811,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NjgzODEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17683811?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar","html_url":"https://github.com/hitengajjar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"> chats do not expire by default.\r\n\r\nOver time, older chats tend to become noise and reduce usability and signal quality. To manage this systematically, I propose a configurable conversation lifecycle state machine that automatically transitions conversations through defined states based on time-based policies.\r\n\r\nIntroduce a configurable conversation state machine to reduce noise from old chats.\r\n\r\n**States**\r\n* Active → Archived → Soft-Deleted → Hard-Deleted\r\n\r\n**Configurable Timeouts**\r\n* X: Idle timeout for active sessions\r\n* Y: Move Active → Archived\r\n* Z: Move Archived → Soft-Deleted\r\n* A: Move Soft-Deleted → Hard-Deleted\r\n\r\nIf a timeout is not configured for a transition, the lifecycle stops at that state.\r\nAll transitions are policy-driven and executed automatically based on last activity time.","created_at":"2026-02-17T02:50:21Z","updated_at":"2026-02-17T02:50:22Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814741004","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814741004"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814741004"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814741004/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":null,"original_line":96,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811858412,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":96,"position":1,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814757154","pull_request_review_id":3811385866,"id":2814757154,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nxcki","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs to exactly one user within one tenant. Chat content (messages, attachments, summaries, citations) MUST be accessible only to the owning user within their tenant. Listing returns chats for the current user ordered by most recent activity. Retrieval returns chat metadata and the most recent messages. Deletion soft-deletes the chat and triggers cleanup of associated external resources.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to manage their conversations - create new ones, resume existing ones, and remove ones they no longer need.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streamed Chat Responses\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-streaming`\n+\n+The system MUST deliver AI responses as a real-time SSE stream. The user sends a message and immediately begins receiving `delta` events as they are generated. The stream terminates with exactly one terminal `done` or `error` event. The terminal `done` event contains the message ID and token usage. The terminal `error` event contains an error `code` and `message` only.\n+\n+The request body MAY include a client-generated `request_id` used as an idempotency key. If an active generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST reject with `409 Conflict`. If a completed generation exists for the same `(chat_id, request_id)`, the system MUST replay the completed assistant response rather than starting a new provider request.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Streaming provides perceived low latency and matches user expectations from consumer AI chat products.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Conversation History\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-conversation-history`\n+\n+The system MUST persist all user and assistant messages. Conversation history access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. On each new user message, the system MUST include relevant conversation history in the LLM context to maintain conversational coherence.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Multi-turn conversations require the AI to remember prior context within the same chat.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Streaming Cancellation\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-streaming-cancellation`\n+\n+The system MUST detect client disconnection during a streaming response and cancel the in-flight LLM request. Cancellation MUST propagate through the entire request chain to terminate the external API call.\n+\n+When a stream is cancelled or disconnects before a terminal completion, the system MUST apply a bounded best-effort debit for quota enforcement so cancellation cannot be used to evade usage limits.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents wasted compute and cost when the user navigates away or closes the browser.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.2 Document Support\n+\n+#### File Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-upload`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to upload document files (not images) to a chat. Uploaded files are processed and indexed for search. Attachment access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST return an attachment identifier and processing status.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need to ground AI conversations in their own documents (contracts, policies, reports).\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Question Answering (File Search)\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-file-search`\n+\n+The system MUST support answering questions about uploaded documents by retrieving relevant excerpts during chat. File search MUST be scoped to the user's tenant. Retrieved excerpts and citations MUST be returned only to the owning user within their tenant. The system MUST enforce a configurable per-message file search call limit (default: 2 retrieval calls per message).\n+\n+**Rationale**: The primary value of document upload is the ability to ask questions and get answers grounded in document content.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Document Summary on Upload\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-doc-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST generate a brief summary of each uploaded document at upload time. The summary is stored and used in the conversation context to give the AI general awareness of attached documents without requiring a search call.\n+\n+Document summary generation MUST run as a background/system task (`requester_type=system`) and MUST NOT be charged to an arbitrary end user.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Improves AI response quality when the user asks general questions about attached documents.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+### 5.3 Conversation Management\n+\n+#### Thread Summary Compression\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-thread-summary`\n+\n+The system MUST compress older conversation history into a summary when the conversation exceeds defined thresholds (message count, token count, or turn count). Thread summary access MUST be limited to the owning user within their tenant. The summary MUST preserve key facts, decisions, names, and document references. Summarized messages are retained in storage but replaced by the summary in the LLM context.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Long conversations would exceed LLM context limits and increase costs without compression.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Temporary Chats\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-temporary-chat`\n+\n+The system MUST allow users to mark a chat as temporary. Temporary chats MUST be automatically deleted (including all associated external resources) after 24 hours.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Users need disposable conversations for quick questions without cluttering their chat list.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`, `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+### 5.4 Cost Control & Governance\n+\n+#### Per-User Usage Quotas\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-quota-enforcement`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce per-user usage limits on a daily and monthly basis. Tracked metrics: input tokens, output tokens, file search calls, premium model calls. When a user exceeds their premium model quota, the system MUST auto-downgrade to a base model. When all quotas are exhausted, the system MUST reject requests with a clear error.\n+\n+Quota counting MUST use two phases: Preflight (reserve) before the provider call, and commit actual usage after completion.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents runaway costs from individual users and ensures fair resource distribution across a tenant.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Token Budget Enforcement\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-token-budget`\n+\n+The system MUST enforce a maximum input token budget per request. When the assembled context exceeds the budget, the system MUST truncate lower-priority content (old messages, document summaries, retrieval excerpts) while preserving the system prompt and thread summary. A reserve for output tokens MUST always be maintained.\n+\n+**Rationale**: Prevents requests from exceeding provider context limits and controls per-request cost.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### License Gate\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-license-gate`\n+\n+The system MUST verify that the user's tenant has the `ai_chat` feature enabled via the platform's `license_manager`. Requests from tenants without this feature MUST be rejected with HTTP 403.\n+\n+**Rationale**: AI chat is a premium feature gated by the tenant's license agreement. License verification is delegated to the platform `license_manager`.\n+**Actors**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+#### Audit Events\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-audit`","path":"modules/mini-chat/docs/PRD.md","commit_id":"e70a8b945324cf06a6b8409e91a4b4e0138b050a","original_commit_id":"696d4fbe5e3b2c01658b7763f7a376c4f0fa947b","user":{"login":"hitengajjar","id":17683811,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NjgzODEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17683811?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar","html_url":"https://github.com/hitengajjar","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/hitengajjar/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"> Does not include full prompt/response by default. Content is minimal and governed by redaction rules (PRD lines 240-251, DESIGN.md line 1207)\r\n\r\nI find contradiction in the statement -- which content needs redaction if we are not reporting any data (prompt/response/chat title)? \r\n\r\n> This granularity is standard for compliance in multi-tenant SaaS — \"who used what model, how many tokens, what policy decisions\" per interaction.\r\n\r\nNoted","created_at":"2026-02-17T02:57:29Z","updated_at":"2026-02-17T02:57:29Z","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814757154","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814757154"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/626#discussion_r2814757154"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/626"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814757154/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"start_line":null,"original_start_line":null,"start_side":null,"line":404,"original_line":228,"side":"RIGHT","in_reply_to_id":2811952356,"author_association":"MEMBER","original_position":228,"position":404,"subject_type":"line"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/comments/2814773677","pull_request_review_id":3811406199,"id":2814773677,"node_id":"PRRC_kwDOPaLyBs6nxgmt","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,703 @@\n+# PRD - Mini Chat\n+\n+## 1. Overview\n+\n+### 1.1 Purpose\n+\n+Mini Chat is a multi-tenant AI chat module that provides users with a conversational interface backed by a large language model. Users can send messages, receive streamed responses in real time, upload documents, and ask questions about uploaded content. The module enforces strict tenant isolation, usage-based cost controls, and emits audit events.\n+\n+Parent tenant / MSP administrators MUST NOT have access to chat content. Admin visibility is limited to aggregated usage and operational metrics.\n+\n+### 1.2 Background / Problem Statement\n+\n+The platform requires an integrated AI assistant that gives users the ability to have multi-turn conversations with an LLM and ground those conversations in their own documents. Without this capability, users must rely on external tools (ChatGPT, etc.), which creates data governance risks, lacks integration with platform access controls, and provides no visibility into aggregated usage and operational metrics for tenant administrators.\n+\n+Current gaps: no native chat experience within the platform; no way to query uploaded documents via LLM; no per-user usage tracking or quota enforcement for AI features; no audit events emitted for AI interactions.\n+\n+### 1.3 Goals (Business Outcomes)\n+\n+- Provide a stable, production-ready AI chat with real-time streaming and persistent conversation history\n+- Enable document-aware conversations: users upload files and ask questions grounded in document content\n+- Guarantee tenant data isolation and enforce access control via `ai_chat` license feature\n+- Control operational costs through per-user quotas, token budgets, and tool-call limits\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` for all AI interactions (prompts, responses, policy decisions)\n+\n+### 1.4 Glossary\n+\n+| Term | Definition |\n+|------|------------|\n+| Chat | A persistent conversation between a user and the AI assistant |\n+| Message | A single turn within a chat (user input or assistant response) |\n+| Attachment | A document file uploaded to a chat for question answering |\n+| Thread Summary | A compressed representation of older messages, used to keep long conversations within token limits |\n+| Vector Store | A provider-hosted index of document embeddings (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI), scoped per tenant, used for document search |\n+| File Search | An LLM tool call that retrieves relevant excerpts from uploaded documents |\n+| Token Budget | The maximum number of input/output tokens allowed per request |\n+| Temporary Chat | A chat marked for automatic deletion after 24 hours |\n+| OAGW | Outbound API Gateway - platform service that handles external API calls and credential injection |\n+\n+## 2. Actors\n+\n+### 2.1 Human Actors\n+\n+#### Chat User\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-chat-user`\n+\n+**Role**: End user who creates chats, sends messages, uploads documents, and receives AI responses. Belongs to a tenant and is subject to that tenant's license and quota policies.\n+**Needs**: Real-time conversational AI; ability to ask questions about uploaded documents; persistent chat history; clear feedback when quotas are exceeded.\n+\n+### 2.2 System Actors\n+\n+#### OpenAI / Azure OpenAI\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-openai`\n+\n+**Role**: External LLM provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI). Processes chat completion requests, hosts uploaded files, maintains vector stores for document search. All communication routed through OAGW.\n+\n+#### Cleanup Scheduler\n+\n+**ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-actor-cleanup-scheduler`\n+\n+**Role**: Scheduled process that deletes expired temporary chats and purges associated external resources (files, vector store entries) after the retention period.\n+\n+## 3. Operational Concept & Environment\n+\n+No module-specific environment constraints beyond platform defaults.\n+\n+## 4. Scope\n+\n+This PRD uses **P0/P1/P2** to describe phased scope. The `p1`/`p2` tags on requirement checkboxes are internal priority markers and do not define release phase.\n+\n+### 4.1 In Scope\n+\n+- Chat CRUD (create, list, get, delete)\n+- Real-time streamed AI responses (SSE)\n+- Persistent conversation history\n+- Document upload and document-aware question answering via file search\n+- Document summary on upload\n+- Thread summary compression for long conversations\n+- Temporary chats with 24h auto-deletion\n+- Per-user usage quotas (daily, monthly) with auto-downgrade to base model\n+- File search call limits per message and per user/day\n+- Token budget enforcement and context truncation\n+- License feature gate (`ai_chat`)\n+- Emit audit events to platform `audit_service` (append-only semantics owned by `audit_service`)\n+- Streaming cancellation when client disconnects\n+- Cleanup of external resources (provider files, vector store entries) on chat deletion\n+\n+### 4.2 Out of Scope\n+\n+- Projects or shared/collaborative chats\n+- Full-text search across chat history\n+- Non-OpenAI-compatible provider support (e.g., Anthropic, Google) - OpenAI and Azure OpenAI are supported at P0 via a shared API surface\n+- Complex retrieval policies beyond simple limits\n+- Per-workspace vector stores (only per-tenant for P0)\n+- Image or non-document file support\n+- Custom audit storage (audit events are emitted to platform `audit_service`)\n+- Chat export or migration\n+\n+## 5. Functional Requirements\n+\n+### 5.1 Core Chat\n+\n+#### Chat CRUD\n+\n+- [ ] `p1` - **ID**: `cpt-cf-mini-chat-fr-chat-crud`\n+\n+The system MUST allow authenticated users to create, list, retrieve, and delete chats. Each chat belongs