ion like parentheses and periods); either regenerate the TOC or replace\nthe fragments with the correct slugs that correspond to those exact headings.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/PRD.md-435-439 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`435-439`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add a language identifier to the fenced code block.**  \nmarkdownlint MD040 flagged this block; add a language tag (e.g., `text` or `http`) to satisfy linting.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n {METHOD} /api/oagw/v1/proxy/{alias}[/{path}][?{query}]\n ```\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/PRD.md` around lines 435 - 439, The fenced code block\ncontaining \"{METHOD} /api/oagw/v1/proxy/{alias}[/{path}][?{query}]\" in PRD.md is\nmissing a language identifier and triggers markdownlint MD040; update the\nopening fence for that block to include a language tag such as \"text\" or \"http\"\n(e.g., change ``` to ```text) so the block reads with a language identifier and\nsatisfies the linter while leaving the block content unchanged.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/response.rs-16-22 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`16-22`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add a size limit to `from_response` to prevent unbounded buffering.**\n\n`to_bytes(..., usize::MAX)` can buffer entire response bodies without limit. Even in tests, this is defensive against accidental large responses. Implement a reasonable limit (e.g., 1 MB) as the default, with a separate `from_response_with_limit()` method for tests requiring larger bodies.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🛠️ Suggested change</summary>\n\n```diff\n impl TestResponse {\n     /// Consume an `http::Response<Body>`, collecting the body into bytes.\n     pub async fn from_response(resp: http::Response<Body>) -> Self {\n-        let (parts, body) = resp.into_parts();\n-        let body_bytes = axum::body::to_bytes(body, usize::MAX)\n-            .await\n-            .expect(\"failed to collect response body\")\n-            .to_vec();\n+        Self::from_response_with_limit(resp, 1024 * 1024).await\n+    }\n+\n+    /// Same as `from_response` but with a maximum body size.\n+    pub async fn from_response_with_limit(\n+        resp: http::Response<Body>,\n+        max_bytes: usize,\n+    ) -> Self {\n+        let (parts, body) = resp.into_parts();\n+        let body_bytes = axum::body::to_bytes(body, max_bytes)\n+            .await\n+            .expect(\"failed to collect response body\")\n+            .to_vec();\n         Self {\n             status: parts.status,\n             headers: parts.headers,\n             body_bytes,\n         }\n     }\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/response.rs` around lines 16 - 22,\nfrom_response currently calls axum::body::to_bytes(..., usize::MAX) which allows\nunbounded buffering; change from_response to enforce a default size limit (e.g.,\n1 MB) by calling axum::body::to_bytes(body, 1_048_576) and handling the error\ninstead of expect, and add a new public async helper\nfrom_response_with_limit(resp: http::Response<Body>, limit: usize) -> Self that\nperforms the same logic using the provided limit so tests that need larger\nbodies can call it; update any test call sites to use from_response_with_limit\nwhen larger limits are required and ensure error handling returns a clear\nfailure rather than panicking in library/test support.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk/Cargo.toml-11-22 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`11-22`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Align SDK dependencies with workspace definitions to prevent version drift.**\n\nSeveral dependencies (thiserror, http, bytes, async-trait, futures-core, futures-util) are defined in `[workspace.dependencies]` but specified directly in the SDK manifest. Use `{ workspace = true }` for these to maintain consistency.\n\nNote the uuid special case: the SDK requires the \"v4\" feature not present in the workspace definition (`uuid = { version = \"1.19\", features = [\"serde\"] }`), so a direct override is necessary here. The version constraint \"1\" allows flexibility but differs from the workspace \"1.19\"—consider documenting this intentional difference or updating the workspace definition.\n\nSimilarly, the dev-dependency tokio uses `version = \"1\"` with `features = [\"macros\", \"rt\"]` instead of the workspace-managed `1.47` with full features. Align this as well.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk/Cargo.toml` around lines 11 - 22, Replace direct\nversion pins for shared crates with workspace ownership to prevent version\ndrift: change thiserror, http, bytes, async-trait, futures-core, and\nfutures-util entries to use { workspace = true } instead of explicit versions;\nkeep modkit-security as-is. Preserve the sdk-specific uuid override (retain\nfeatures = [\"v4\",\"serde\"] and a compatible version spec) and add a brief inline\ncomment documenting why it diverges from the workspace uuid. Align the\ndev-dependency tokio with the workspace definition (use { workspace = true }) or\nexplicitly document why the SDK requires the smaller feature set if you must\nkeep version = \"1\" with features = [\"macros\",\"rt\"]. Ensure the updated\nCargo.toml entries reference the crate names thiserror, http, bytes,\nasync-trait, futures-core, futures-util, uuid, and tokio so reviewers can verify\nthe change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md-3-152 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`3-152`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Fix TOC link fragments to match generated anchors.**  \nmarkdownlint (MD051) reports invalid fragments; the current TOC links (especially with `[ ]` prefixes) don’t resolve to headings. Consider regenerating the TOC or adjusting anchors.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md` around lines 3 - 152, The TOC link fragments\nare invalid (markdownlint MD051) because many entries include checkbox markers\nlike \"[ ]\" and punctuation that don't match the generated heading anchors;\nupdate the TOC to use the exact slugified anchors produced by the Markdown\nrenderer (lowercase, punctuation removed or percent-encoded, spaces → hyphens)\nor regenerate the TOC automatically—for example change links referencing items\nsuch as \"Phase 0 (p0): MVP - OpenAI Integration Ready\" and \"F-P0-001: Module\nScaffold + SDK Boundary\" to their slugified forms (remove leading \"[ ]\",\ndrop/normalize punctuation and case) so each TOC link matches headings like\n\"phase-0-p0-mvp-openai-integration-ready\" and\n\"f-p0-001-module-scaffold-sdk-boundary\".\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_error_handling.py-9-11 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`9-11`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Apply module-level `usefixtures` to activate the mock without unused parameters.**\n\nAll four test functions in this module declare `mock_upstream` as a parameter but never reference it in their bodies. Using `pytestmark = pytest.mark.usefixtures(\"mock_upstream\")` at the module level keeps the fixture active while eliminating unused parameter warnings.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Suggested update</summary>\n\n```diff\n import httpx\n import pytest\n \n from .helpers import create_route, create_upstream, delete_upstream, unique_alias\n+\n+pytestmark = pytest.mark.usefixtures(\"mock_upstream\")\n@@\n async def test_nonexistent_alias_returns_404_gateway(\n-    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream,\n+    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers,\n ):\n@@\n async def test_disabled_upstream_returns_503_gateway(\n-    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, mock_upstream,\n+    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url,\n ):\n@@\n async def test_upstream_500_passthrough(\n-    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, mock_upstream,\n+    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url,\n ):\n@@\n async def test_upstream_timeout_returns_504_gateway(\n-    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, mock_upstream,\n+    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url,\n ):\n```\n</details>\n\nAlso applies to: 31-33, 54-56, 83-85\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_error_handling.py` around lines 9 - 11, Add a\nmodule-level fixture activation and remove the unused fixture parameters:\nintroduce pytestmark = pytest.mark.usefixtures(\"mock_upstream\") at the top of\nthe module (ensure pytest is imported) and remove the mock_upstream parameter\nfrom the signatures of the four tests that currently declare it (e.g.,\ntest_nonexistent_alias_returns_404_gateway and the three other tests around\nlines referenced). This keeps the mock_upstream fixture active for those tests\nwithout leaving an unused parameter in each test signature.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_error_handling.py-111-120 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`111-120`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Fail on unexpected status codes to avoid false positives.**\n\nThe test is explicitly validating 504 timeout behavior when the timeout guard is configured. The `else: pass` silently accepts responses that are neither 504 nor 200, which masks potential misconfiguration or gateway errors. This can hide failures that should be caught during testing. The existing comment \"Accept any response but log it\" suggests incomplete implementation (no logging is performed). \n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested tightening</summary>\n\n```diff\n         elif resp.status_code == 200:\n             pytest.skip(\"OAGW returned 200 — timeout guard may not be configured\")\n         else:\n-            # Accept any response but log it.\n-            pass\n+            pytest.fail(f\"Unexpected status {resp.status_code}: {resp.text[:500]}\")\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_error_handling.py` around lines 111 - 120, The\ntest currently swallows any response not 504 or 200 (else: pass), which hides\nfailures; change the else branch in the block that inspects resp to explicitly\nfail the test (e.g., pytest.fail) and include useful diagnostics (status code,\nresp.headers, resp.text or resp.content) so misconfigurations are caught; keep\nthe existing 504 handling (and header check for x-oagw-error-source) and the\npytest.skip for 200, but replace the silent pass with a failing assertion or\npytest.fail including resp details.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_rate_limiting.py-9-11 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`9-11`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Use `@pytest.mark.usefixtures()` for fixtures needed only for side effects.**\n\nThe `mock_upstream` fixture starts a background mock server and must be invoked, but its return value is unused in both test functions. This triggers Ruff's ARG001 warning. Use `@pytest.mark.usefixtures(\"mock_upstream\")` and remove the argument to express the intent clearly and avoid linting failures.\n\n<details>\n<summary>