am parameter from the test functions (e.g.,\ntest_post_proxy_returns_upstream_response and the other three tests that\ncurrently accept mock_upstream) and add a module-level pytestmark =\npytest.mark.usefixtures(\"mock_upstream\") so the session-scoped fixture still\nruns; ensure you import pytest if not already present and update the function\nsignatures to only accept the used fixtures (oagw_base_url, oagw_headers,\nmock_upstream -> remove mock_upstream).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_auth_injection.py-10-13 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-13`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Remove unused `mock_upstream` parameter and use fixture marker**\n\nThe `mock_upstream` parameter is unused in the test body (ARG001 warning) but required for its side effect of starting the mock server. Use `@pytest.mark.usefixtures()` instead of adding it as a parameter.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Suggested update</summary>\n\n```diff\n`@pytest.mark.asyncio`\n+@pytest.mark.usefixtures(\"mock_upstream\")\nasync def test_apikey_auth_injects_bearer_header(\n-    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, mock_upstream,\n+    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url,\n):\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_auth_injection.py` around lines 10 - 13, The\ntest function test_apikey_auth_injects_bearer_header currently accepts an unused\nparameter mock_upstream; remove mock_upstream from the function signature and\ninstead attach the fixture as a decorator using\n`@pytest.mark.usefixtures`(\"mock_upstream\") so the fixture runs for its side\neffects without being a parameter. Ensure the decorator is placed above the\n`@pytest.mark.asyncio` decorator (or combined appropriately) and leave other\nparameters (oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url) unchanged.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_sse_streaming.py-10-13 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-13`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Silence unused `mock_upstream` fixture warnings**\n\nRuff flags the `mock_upstream` parameter as unused even though it's only needed for side effects. Consider applying `pytest.mark.usefixtures(\"mock_upstream\")` at module level and removing the parameter to keep the fixture active without lint noise.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Suggested update</summary>\n\n```diff\n import httpx\n import pytest\n\n from .helpers import create_route, create_upstream, delete_upstream, unique_alias\n+\n+pytestmark = pytest.mark.usefixtures(\"mock_upstream\")\n@@\n async def test_sse_proxy_content_type_and_done(\n-    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, mock_upstream,\n+    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url,\n ):\n@@\n async def test_sse_proxy_contains_json_chunks(\n-    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, mock_upstream,\n+    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url,\n ):\n```\n</details>\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 41-44\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_sse_streaming.py` around lines 10 - 13, Ruff\nwarns about the unused mock_upstream parameter in tests like\ntest_sse_proxy_content_type_and_done; remove mock_upstream from the test\nfunction signatures and activate the fixture at module or function scope using\npytest.mark.usefixtures(\"mock_upstream\") (add the decorator at module level or\nabove each test such as test_sse_proxy_content_type_and_done and the other tests\nmentioned around lines 41-44) so the fixture runs for its side effects without\ncausing unused-parameter lint warnings.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_body_validation.py-30-114 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`30-114`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Silence unused `mock_upstream` fixture arguments.**  \nRuff’s ARG001 is correct here. If the fixture is used only for side effects, rename to `_mock_upstream` (or switch to `@pytest.mark.usefixtures`).  \n\n<details>\n<summary>Minimal fix (keep fixture activation)</summary>\n\n```diff\n-async def test_invalid_content_length_returns_400(\n-    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, mock_upstream,\n-):\n+async def test_invalid_content_length_returns_400(\n+    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, _mock_upstream,\n+):\n```\n\n```diff\n-async def test_body_exceeding_limit_returns_413(\n-    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, mock_upstream,\n-):\n+async def test_body_exceeding_limit_returns_413(\n+    oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, _mock_upstream,\n+):\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_body_validation.py` around lines 30 - 114, The\ntests test_invalid_content_length_returns_400 and\ntest_body_exceeding_limit_returns_413 declare the mock_upstream fixture but\nnever use it; rename the parameter to _mock_upstream (or alternatively replace\nthe parameter with `@pytest.mark.usefixtures`(\"mock_upstream\") on each test) to\nsilence Ruff ARG001. Update the function signatures for\ntest_invalid_content_length_returns_400 and\ntest_body_exceeding_limit_returns_413 to use _mock_upstream or add the\nusefixtures decorator while leaving the rest of the test bodies intact.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/client.rs-202-206 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`202-206`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`NotFound` error maps to `RouteNotFound` for all entity types.**\n\nWhen a non-route entity (e.g., \"upstream\") is not found, it still maps to `ServiceGatewayError::RouteNotFound`. This could confuse SDK consumers.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested improvement: use a generic NotFound variant or entity-aware mapping</summary>\n\nIf `ServiceGatewayError` has (or could have) a generic `NotFound` variant:\n\n```diff\n fn domain_err_to_sdk(err: DomainError) -> ServiceGatewayError {\n     match err {\n-        DomainError::NotFound { entity, id } => ServiceGatewayError::RouteNotFound {\n+        DomainError::NotFound { entity, id } => ServiceGatewayError::NotFound {\n             instance: format!(\"{entity}/{id}\"),\n         },\n```\n\nOtherwise, consider using the entity name in the instance field to clarify what was not found, or introduce entity-specific error variants in the SDK.\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/client.rs` around lines 202 -\n206, domain_err_to_sdk currently maps any DomainError::NotFound { entity, id }\nto ServiceGatewayError::RouteNotFound which misrepresents non-route entities;\nupdate domain_err_to_sdk to either map to a generic\nServiceGatewayError::NotFound (preferred if available) or, if not, populate\nRouteNotFound.instance with the full entity context (e.g.,\nformat!(\"{entity}/{id}\") already present is fine but rename variant or include\nentity in message) or add entity-specific SDK variants; locate the\nDomainError::NotFound match arm inside the domain_err_to_sdk function and change\nthe target variant to ServiceGatewayError::NotFound or adjust the instance field\nto clearly include the entity name so consumers can distinguish missing\nupstreams from missing routes.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/management.rs-268-272 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`268-272`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**`Uuid::nil()` in error message is misleading.**\n\nWhen `resolve_upstream` fails to find an upstream by alias, the error includes `Uuid::nil()` which is confusing since the lookup was by alias, not by ID.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested improvement: use a dedicated error variant or clearer message</summary>\n\nConsider adding a `not_found_by_alias` helper or using a more descriptive error:\n\n```diff\n         let upstream = self\n             .upstreams\n             .get_by_alias(tenant_id, alias)\n             .await\n-            .map_err(|_| DomainError::not_found(\"upstream\", Uuid::nil()))?;\n+            .map_err(|_| DomainError::not_found_by_alias(\"upstream\", alias))?;\n```\n\nOr if adding a new error variant is not desired, at minimum document the intent or use a sentinel value that's clearly invalid.\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/management.rs` around lines 268\n- 272, The current error created in resolve_upstream when get_by_alias fails\nuses DomainError::not_found(\"upstream\", Uuid::nil()), which is misleading\nbecause the lookup was by alias; change this to surface the alias instead or add\na dedicated variant. Update the call in resolve_upstream (the block that calls\nself.upstreams.get_by_alias) to return something like\nDomainError::not_found_by_alias(\"upstream\", alias.to_string()) or\nDomainError::not_found(\"upstream\", alias.to_string()), and add the corresponding\nhelper/variant in the DomainError implementation so the error message accurately\nreflects a \"not found by alias\" condition (adjust types as needed to accept\nString). Ensure references: resolve_upstream, get_by_alias, and\nDomainError::not_found (or new DomainError::not_found_by_alias) are updated\nconsistently.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/conftest.py-86-94 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`86-94`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Silent exception handling may hide important failures.**\n\nThe broad `except Exception: pass` block silently swallows all errors except `ConnectError`. Consider logging these exceptions to aid debugging when tests behave unexpectedly.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested improvement</summary>\n\n```diff\n+    import logging\n+\n+    logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n+\n     try:\n         resp = httpx.get(f\"{url}/oagw/v1/upstreams\", timeout=5.0,\n                          headers={\"x-tenant-id\": \"aaaaaaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaa-aaaaaaaaaaaa\"})\n         # Any response (even 401/403) means the service is up.\n+        _ = resp  # Explicitly mark as intentionally unused\n     except httpx.ConnectError:\n         pytest.skip(f\"OAGW service not running at {url}\", allow_module_level=True)\n-    except Exception:\n+    except Exception as e:\n         # Timeout or other transient error — still try to run tests.\n-        pass\n+        logger.debug(\"OAGW reachability check encountered error: %s\", e)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/conftest.py` around lines 86 - 94, The broad \"except\nException: pass\" after the httpx.get call in conftest.py silently swallows\nerrors; replace it with an explicit exception handler that logs the error (e.g.,\nget a logger via logging.getLogger(__name__) and call logger.exception or\nlogger.warning) including the target URL and the exception object from \"except\nException as e\" so failures are visible while preserving the original behavior\nof continuing tests; optionally add a separate handler for httpx.ReadTimeout if\nyou want distinct messaging.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/helpers.py-140-141 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`140-141`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Default port mismatch with Rust DTOs.**\n\nThe default port here is `80`, but in `dto.rs` (Line 50-52), `default_port()` returns `443`. This inconsistency could cause unexpected behavior in tests if port is not explicitly provided in `mock_url`.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix to align with Rust defaults</summary>\n\n```diff\n     host = parsed.hostname or \"127.0.0.1\"\n-    port = parsed.port or 80\n+    port = parsed.port or 443\n```\n</details>\n\nAlternatively, ensure test URLs always include explicit ports.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/helpers.py` around lines 140 - 141, The port default\nin helpers.py mismatches the Rust DTO default (80 vs 443); update the logic that\nassigns port (where you use parsed.hostname and parsed.port) to use 443 as the\nfallback (i.e., set port = parsed.port or 443) so it aligns with\ndto.rs::default_port(), or alternatively ensure every test/mock_url includes an\nexplicit port—locate the assignment using parsed.hostname/parsed.port in this\nmodule and change the fallback value to 443.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/module.rs-69-73 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`69-73`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Avoid logging credential secret references at info level.**\n\nLogging credential secret references (even just the reference names, not values) at `info` level could leak sensitive information to logs in production. Consider using `debug` level or removing this logging entirely.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n         for (secret_ref, value) in &cfg.credentials {\n-            info!(\"Seeding credential: {secret_ref}\");\n+            tracing::debug!(\"Seeding credential: {secret_ref}\");\n             cred_resolver.set(secret_ref.clone(), value.clone());\n         }\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/module.rs` around lines 69 - 73, The info-level\nlogging of credential references in the seeding loop is a potential leak; update\nthe loop that uses InMemoryCredentialResolver (variables: cred_resolver,\ncfg.credentials) to stop logging secret_ref at info level—either remove the\ninfo! call or lower it to debug! (or trace!) so credential identifiers are not\nemitted to production logs; keep the rest of the logic (cred_resolver.set(...))\nunchanged.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (9)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-api/auth/negative-1.3-tenant-scoping-management-apis.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`40-40`: **Clarify wording: \"lock expected behavior\".**\n\nThe phrase \"lock expected behavior\" is ambiguous. Consider whether you meant \"block expected behavior\" (i.e., prevent cross-tenant access) or \"document expected behavior\" for clarity.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn\n`@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/management-api/auth/negative-1.3-tenant-scoping-management-apis.md`\nat line 40, Replace the ambiguous phrase \"lock expected behavior\" in the\nExpected line that currently reads \"Expected: `403 Forbidden` or `404 Not Found`\n(lock expected behavior)\" with clear wording; change it to either \"block\nexpected behavior (prevent cross-tenant access)\" if you mean preventing\ncross-tenant access, or \"documented expected behavior\" if you mean describing\nthe behavior, e.g., \"Expected: `403 Forbidden` or `404 Not Found` (block\nexpected behavior — prevents cross-tenant access)\"; update the string \"lock\nexpected behavior\" accordingly wherever it appears.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk/src/error.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-49`: **Add `#[non_exhaustive]` to prevent semver breaks when variants are added later.**  \nWithout this attribute, adding new error variants is a breaking change because downstream code can exhaustively match the enum. Marking it `#[non_exhaustive]` now allows future variants to be added in a non-breaking way (requires callers to use a wildcard arm).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed change</summary>\n\n```diff\n-#[derive(Debug, Clone, thiserror::Error)]\n-pub enum ServiceGatewayError {\n+#[derive(Debug, Clone, thiserror::Error)]\n+#[non_exhaustive]\n+pub enum ServiceGatewayError {\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk/src/error.rs` around lines 1 - 49, Add the\n#[non_exhaustive] attribute to the ServiceGatewayError enum declaration so\nfuture variants can be added without breaking consumers; locate the enum named\nServiceGatewayError in error.rs and insert #[non_exhaustive] directly above its\ndeclaration, keeping the existing derives (Debug, Clone, thiserror::Error)\nintact so callers are forced to use a wildcard arm when matching exhaustively.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/plugin/apikey_auth.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`42-46`: **Differentiate “secret not found” from resolver failures.**\n\nCurrently any resolver error becomes `SecretNotFound`, which can mask credential store outages as auth failures. Consider mapping non-not-found errors to `PluginError::Internal` (or an appropriate 5xx) while preserving true not-found cases.  \n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Example direction (adjust error enum names as needed)</summary>\n\n```diff\n-use crate::domain::credential::CredentialResolver;\n+use crate::domain::credential::{CredentialError, CredentialResolver};\n@@\n         let secret = self\n             .credential_resolver\n             .resolve(&config.secret_ref)\n             .await\n-            .map_err(|_| PluginError::SecretNotFound(config.secret_ref.clone()))?;\n+            .map_err(|e| match e {\n+                CredentialError::NotFound => PluginError::SecretNotFound(config.secret_ref.clone()),\n+                other => PluginError::Internal(format!(\"credential resolver error: {other}\")),\n+            })?;\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/plugin/apikey_auth.rs` around lines 42 -\n46, Map resolver errors from\ncredential_resolver.resolve(&config.secret_ref).await so that only genuine\nNotFound cases become PluginError::SecretNotFound(config.secret_ref.clone()),\nwhile other failures map to PluginError::Internal (or another 5xx semantic) so\nstore outages aren’t misreported as auth failures; inspect the concrete error\nreturned by credential_resolver.resolve (e.g., match or downcast) inside the\nawait error handling and branch: return SecretNotFound when the resolver\nindicates a missing key, otherwise return PluginError::Internal with the\noriginal error attached for logging and diagnostics.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_rate_limiting.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`22-38`: **Ensure upstreams are deleted even when assertions fail.**\n\nIf an assertion trips, `delete_upstream` won’t run, which can leak resources and make later tests flaky. A `try/finally` guard keeps cleanup reliable.  \n\n<details>\n<summary>🔁 Example (apply similarly to both tests)</summary>\n\n```diff\n     async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=10.0) as client:\n         upstream = await create_upstream(\n             client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url,\n             alias=alias, rate_limit=rate_limit,\n         )\n         uid = upstream[\"id\"]\n-        await create_route(\n-            client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid, [\"GET\"], \"/v1/models\",\n-        )\n-\n-        resp = await client.get(\n-            f\"{oagw_base_url}/oagw/v1/proxy/{alias}/v1/models\",\n-            headers=oagw_headers,\n-        )\n-        assert resp.status_code == 200\n-\n-        await delete_upstream(client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid)\n+        try:\n+            await create_route(\n+                client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid, [\"GET\"], \"/v1/models\",\n+            )\n+\n+            resp = await client.get(\n+                f\"{oagw_base_url}/oagw/v1/proxy/{alias}/v1/models\",\n+                headers=oagw_headers,\n+            )\n+            assert resp.status_code == 200\n+        finally:\n+            await delete_upstream(client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid)\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 55-83\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_rate_limiting.py` around lines 22 - 38, The\ntest currently creates an upstream via create_upstream and a route via\ncreate_route then asserts the proxy response, but if any assertion or request\nfails the cleanup call delete_upstream will not run; wrap the\nsetup/assert/requests in a try/finally where delete_upstream(client,\noagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid) is invoked in the finally block (apply same\npattern to both tests referenced) so upstreams are always removed even on\nfailures.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_body_validation.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`11-20`: **Close the TCP writer cleanly.**  \nAdd `await writer.wait_closed()` to avoid resource warnings in asyncio test runs.  \n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested tweak</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    writer.close()\n+    writer.close()\n+    await writer.wait_closed()\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_body_validation.py` around lines 11 - 20, The\nTCP writer in _raw_http_request is closed but not awaited, causing asyncio\nresource warnings; after calling writer.close() update the function to await\nwriter.wait_closed() to ensure the connection is fully closed before returning\nthe decoded response (keep existing timeout behavior and error decoding).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/management.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`149-157`: **Cascade delete errors are silently ignored.**\n\nLine 152 uses `let _ =` to discard the result of `delete_by_upstream`, meaning any failure during cascade deletion goes unnoticed. This could leave orphaned routes if the cascade partially fails.\n\nConsider whether this is intentional. If partial failures should be reported:\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💭 Option: log or propagate cascade errors</summary>\n\n```diff\n     async fn delete_upstream(&self, ctx: &SecurityContext, id: Uuid) -> Result<(), DomainError> {\n         let tenant_id = ctx.tenant_id();\n         // Cascade delete routes.\n-        let _ = self.routes.delete_by_upstream(tenant_id, id).await;\n+        if let Err(e) = self.routes.delete_by_upstream(tenant_id, id).await {\n+            tracing::warn!(upstream_id = %id, error = ?e, \"failed to cascade delete routes\");\n+        }\n         self.upstreams\n             .delete(tenant_id, id)\n             .await\n             .map_err(|_| DomainError::not_found(\"upstream\", id))\n     }\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/management.rs` around lines 149\n- 157, The cascade delete currently swallows errors by using `let _ =` on\n`self.routes.delete_by_upstream`, so failures are ignored and can leave orphaned\nroutes; update `delete_upstream` to handle that Result (from\n`delete_by_upstream`) instead of discarding it — either propagate the error (use\n`?` or map it to a `DomainError` and return) or at minimum log it via the same\nlogger used elsewhere before attempting `self.upstreams.delete`; ensure you\nreference `delete_upstream`, `delete_by_upstream`, and `self.upstreams.delete`\nso the change is applied where the cascade and upstream deletions are\ncoordinated.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/module.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`147-154`: **Simplify the `ArcSwapOption` load pattern.**\n\nThe current pattern is verbose. Since `AppState` is `Clone`, consider extracting a helper or using a more direct approach to reduce cognitive load.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested simplification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        let app_state = self\n-            .state\n-            .load()\n-            .as_ref()\n-            .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!(\"AppState not set — init() must run first\"))?\n-            .as_ref()\n-            .clone();\n+        let app_state = self\n+            .state\n+            .load_full()\n+            .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!(\"AppState not set — init() must run first\"))?;\n```\n</details>\n\nNote: `load_full()` returns `Option<Arc<T>>` directly, which may be more ergonomic here if you need an owned `Arc<AppState>` rather than cloning the inner `AppState`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/module.rs` around lines 147 - 154, The current\nverbose load-and-clone pattern for retrieving AppState from the ArcSwapOption\nshould be simplified: replace the sequence using\nself.state.load().as_ref().ok_or_else(...).as_ref().clone() with the more\nergonomic load_full() call (or a small helper) to obtain an owned Arc<AppState>\ndirectly; update the code that expects an AppState clone to use the\nArc<AppState> returned by load_full(), keep the same error construction\n(anyhow::anyhow!(\"AppState not set — init() must run first\")), and ensure\ncallers of init() and any places referencing self.state still work with the\nArc<AppState> type instead of cloning the inner AppState.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/helpers.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`113-121`: **Consider extracting error message to a constant or dedicated exception class.**\n\nThe static analysis tool flagged this as TRY003. For test helpers, this is acceptable, but for consistency you could define a custom exception or message constant.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/helpers.py` around lines 113 - 121, The ValueError\nraised in parse_gts_uuid contains an inline message; extract that message into a\nmodule-level constant (e.g., GTS_UUID_NOT_FOUND_MSG) or define a custom\nexception class (e.g., GtsUuidNotFoundError) and use it in the raise so\ncallers/tests can import/assert against a stable identity; update parse_gts_uuid\nto raise the new exception or raise ValueError(GTS_UUID_NOT_FOUND_MSG) and\nadjust any tests that check the error text accordingly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk/src/models.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`60-62`: **Consider scheme-aware standard port detection.**\n\n`is_standard_port` treats 80 and 443 as standard regardless of scheme. For correctness, 80 is standard for HTTP and 443 for HTTPS/WSS. This could produce unexpected alias contributions (e.g., `http://example.com:443` would omit the port).\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Scheme-aware alternative</summary>\n\n```rust\nimpl Endpoint {\n    fn is_standard_port(&self) -> bool {\n        match self.scheme {\n            Scheme::Http => self.port == 80,\n            Scheme::Https | Scheme::Wss | Scheme::Grpc => self.port == 443,\n            Scheme::Wt => false, // WebTransport has no standard port\n        }\n    }\n    \n    #[must_use]\n    pub fn alias_contribution(&self) -> String {\n        if self.is_standard_port() {\n            self.host.clone()\n        } else {\n            format!(\"{}:{}\", self.host, self.port)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk/src/models.rs` around lines 60 - 62, The current\nis_standard_port(port: u16) treats 80/443 as standard regardless of scheme;\nchange detection to be scheme-aware by moving the logic into Endpoint (implement\nEndpoint::is_standard_port(&self)) and check based on self.scheme: Http => port\n80, Https | Wss | Grpc => port 443, Wt => never standard. Update\nalias_contribution(&self) to call the new Endpoint::is_standard_port and only\nomit the port when that method returns true so addresses like\nhttp://example.com:443 keep the port.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n````\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md`:\n- Around line 257-305: Add an openspec change proposal file named proposal.md\nunder the oagw openspec changes for the new major capability: include a 1–2\nsentence \"Why\" describing centralized outbound request management, a \"What\nChanges\" bullet list declaring new GTS types (gts.x.core.oagw.upstream.v1~,\ngts.x.core.oagw.route.v1~, and plugin types for auth/guard/transform) and mark\nbreaking changes, and an \"Impact\" section listing affected areas (types-registry\n/ GTS registration, api-ingress REST integration / OperationBuilder route\nregistration, and cred-store secret references); optionally add a design.md\nalongside proposal.md if cross-module coordination, new external deps, or\nmigration steps need detailing.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/type_provisioning.rs`:\n- Around line 33-59: Endpoint currently uses a non-optional port with\ndefault_port() always returning 443, so HTTP/Ws/Grpc endpoints get wrong ports;\nchange Endpoint.port to Option<u16> (remove #[serde(default = \"default_port\")])\nand update the conversion/validation logic that consumes Endpoint (e.g., where\nEndpoint is converted into provisioned address or in the function that reads\nEndpoint) to compute a scheme-based default: if port.is_some() use it, else\nmatch on scheme (Scheme::Http => 80, Https/Wss => 443, Wt => 80, Grpc => 80 or\nappropriate value) and use that derived port; ensure default_port() is either\nremoved or repurposed to only return HTTPS default if still needed and update\nany tests handling ports accordingly.\n- Around line 590-614: In list_routes, after successfully deserializing\nRoutePayload (in the match handling inside async fn list_routes), validate its\nMatchRules and skip invalid entries: if the RoutePayload.match_rules (or the\nstruct field name for match rules) has both http and grpc equal to None/empty,\nemit a tracing::warn (include gts_id and the reason) and continue without\npushing into result; otherwise convert payload.into() as before. This ensures\ndeserialized but unusable routes are ignored before creating ProvisionedRoute.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/request.rs`:\n- Around line 62-69: The send method builds the request URI by concatenating\nquery pairs without URL-encoding, which breaks on reserved chars; update\nTestRequest::send to URL-encode query keys and values (self.query) before\njoining them into the URI (the uri variable built from self.path and\nqs.join(\"&\")), e.g. use the form_urlencoded encoder (already in Cargo.toml) to\npercent-encode each (k, v) pair and produce a properly encoded query string,\nthen format!(\"{}?{}\", self.path, encoded_qs).\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/conftest.py`:\n- Around line 72-75: The teardown calls stop the loop before stopping the\nserver; instead, submit the server.stop() coroutine to the running loop (using\nasyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(server.stop(), loop)) and wait for it to\ncomplete, then call loop.call_soon_threadsafe(loop.stop), join the thread, and\nfinally close the loop; update the block referencing loop.call_soon_threadsafe,\nserver.stop, thread.join, loop.stop, loop.run_until_complete and loop.close to\nuse asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(server.stop(), loop).result(timeout=...)\n(handle exceptions as needed) before stopping and closing the loop.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/mock_upstream.py`:\n- Around line 129-131: The parameterized route \"/error/{code}\" currently shadows\nthe specific \"/error/timeout\" route, so move the registration of\napp.router.add_get(\"/error/timeout\", handle_error_timeout) to be before\napp.router.add_get(\"/error/{code}\", handle_error_code) so the specific handler\n(handle_error_timeout) is matched first; ensure the order in the module where\nroutes are added uses the exact route strings and handler names\n(handle_error_timeout, handle_error_code) as given.\n\n---\n\nOutside diff comments:\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/DESIGN.md`:\n- Around line 213-260: The fenced code blocks in DESIGN.md (the two flow\ndiagrams starting with \"Client Request: POST /api/oagw/v1/upstreams\" and \"Client\nRequest: GET /api/oagw/v1/proxy/openai/v1/chat/completions\", plus the module\ntree block under \"Module Structure\") lack language identifiers; update each\ntriple-backtick fence to include an