ils>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/proxy_integration.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`11-67`: **Make the `/error/500` route explicit to avoid default path‑suffix reliance.**\n\nThe test later calls `/mock-upstream/error/500`, but the route registration only specifies `/error` without an explicit suffix mode. If defaults change, this can become brittle.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Suggested tweak</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        (vec![\"GET\"], \"/error\"),\n+        (vec![\"GET\"], \"/error/500\"),\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/proxy_integration.rs` around lines 11 - 67,\nThe route registration in setup_openai_mock registers \"/error\" which relies on\ndefault path‑suffix behavior and is brittle; update the post_route call that\nbuilds the HTTP match (the JSON under \"match\" -> \"http\" used in\nsetup_openai_mock) to explicitly register the exact path \"/error/500\" (or add an\nexplicit path_suffix_mode field set to \"exact\" alongside the \"path\" value) so\nthe test call to \"/mock-upstream/error/500\" matches deterministically.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`179-200`: **Also assert the mock wasn’t hit when alias is missing.**\n\nThe comment says “without calling mock,” but the test doesn’t verify that yet.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Suggested assertion</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    match h\n-        .facade()\n-        .proxy_request(h.security_context().clone(), req)\n-        .await\n-    {\n-        Err(err) => assert!(matches!(\n-            err,\n-            oagw_sdk::error::ServiceGatewayError::RouteNotFound { .. }\n-        )),\n-        Ok(_) => panic!(\"expected error\"),\n-    }\n+    let result = h\n+        .facade()\n+        .proxy_request(h.security_context().clone(), req)\n+        .await;\n+    assert!(matches!(\n+        result,\n+        Err(oagw_sdk::error::ServiceGatewayError::RouteNotFound { .. })\n+    ));\n+\n+    let recorded = h.mock().recorded_requests().await;\n+    assert!(recorded.is_empty(), \"expected no upstream calls\");\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/proxy_integration.rs` around lines 179 - 200,\nThe test proxy_nonexistent_alias_returns_404 should also assert the OpenAI mock\nwasn't invoked; after calling setup_openai_mock() and exercising\nh.facade().proxy_request(...), add an assertion that the mock handle h reports\nzero received requests (e.g. call whatever verification API your test harness\nexposes such as h.mock_server().received_requests() == 0 or\nh.assert_no_requests_received()), placing this check after confirming the\nRouteNotFound error to ensure the mock was not hit when the alias is missing.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/management.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`149-157`: **Cascade delete silently ignores route deletion errors.**\n\nThe `let _ = self.routes.delete_by_upstream(...)` discards any errors from the cascade deletion. While the upstream deletion proceeds regardless, consider logging the error for observability.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested improvement</summary>\n\n```diff\n     async fn delete_upstream(&self, ctx: &SecurityContext, id: Uuid) -> Result<(), DomainError> {\n         let tenant_id = ctx.tenant_id();\n         // Cascade delete routes.\n-        let _ = self.routes.delete_by_upstream(tenant_id, id).await;\n+        if let Err(e) = self.routes.delete_by_upstream(tenant_id, id).await {\n+            tracing::warn!(upstream_id = %id, error = %e, \"failed to cascade delete routes\");\n+        }\n         self.upstreams\n             .delete(tenant_id, id)\n             .await\n             .map_err(|_| DomainError::not_found(\"upstream\", id))\n     }\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/management.rs` around lines 149\n- 157, The cascade delete in delete_upstream is ignoring errors from\nroutes.delete_by_upstream by using let _ = ..., so update delete_upstream to\nhandle the Result: await routes.delete_by_upstream(tenant_id, id) and on\nErr(...) log the error (use the existing logger or context logger) with context\nlike \"failed to cascade delete routes for upstream {id}\" before proceeding to\ndelete the upstream; keep the upstream deletion behavior the same but do not\nsilently drop errors from routes.delete_by_upstream.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/plugin/registry.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`54-71`: **Good test coverage for plugin resolution.**\n\nThe tests validate both built-in plugins and the error case for unknown plugins. Consider adding a test that verifies the resolved plugins are the expected types (e.g., verifying `APIKEY_AUTH_PLUGIN_ID` returns an `ApiKeyAuthPlugin`).\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/plugin/registry.rs` around lines 54 - 71,\nAdd assertions that the resolved plugin instances are the expected concrete\ntypes: call make_registry(), resolve(APIKEY_AUTH_PLUGIN_ID).unwrap() and verify\nthe returned Box<dyn AuthPlugin> is an ApiKeyAuthPlugin (e.g., downcast or use\nan as_any()/is::<ApiKeyAuthPlugin>() helper), and similarly\nresolve(NOOP_AUTH_PLUGIN_ID).unwrap() and assert it is a NoopAuthPlugin; keep\nusing the existing tests resolves_apikey_plugin and resolves_noop_plugin (or add\nnew tests) and unwrap the Result before type-checking to ensure the concrete\ntype is asserted.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`88-96`: **Note: `DataPlaneService` takes `SecurityContext` by value.**\n\nUnlike `ControlPlaneService` methods that take `&SecurityContext`, `proxy_request` takes ownership. This is appropriate if the context needs to be moved into async tasks or stored during request processing, but worth documenting the rationale.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/mod.rs` around lines 88 - 96,\nDataPlaneService's proxy_request currently takes SecurityContext by value while\nControlPlaneService uses &SecurityContext; clarify the intent by either changing\nproxy_request signature to take &SecurityContext if it shouldn't own the\ncontext, or (preferred if ownership is required) add a doc comment on the\nDataPlaneService trait and the proxy_request method explaining that\nSecurityContext is consumed because it may be moved into spawned async tasks or\nstored for the lifetime of the request; reference the DataPlaneService trait,\nthe proxy_request method, and SecurityContext in the comment so reviewers\nunderstand the rationale.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/config.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`39-54`: **Consider adding `proxy_timeout_secs` to `RuntimeConfig`.**\n\n`RuntimeConfig` includes `max_body_size_bytes` but excludes `proxy_timeout_secs`, yet both are likely needed at runtime for request handling. If the proxy timeout is needed by handlers, consider including it.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested addition</summary>\n\n```diff\n #[derive(Debug, Clone)]\n pub struct RuntimeConfig {\n     pub max_body_size_bytes: usize,\n+    pub proxy_timeout_secs: u64,\n }\n\n impl From<&OagwConfig> for RuntimeConfig {\n     fn from(cfg: &OagwConfig) -> Self {\n         Self {\n             max_body_size_bytes: cfg.max_body_size_bytes,\n+            proxy_timeout_secs: cfg.proxy_timeout_secs,\n         }\n     }\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/config.rs` around lines 39 - 54, RuntimeConfig\ncurrently exposes max_body_size_bytes but omits proxy_timeout_secs; update the\nRuntimeConfig struct to include a proxy_timeout_secs field and populate it in\nthe From<&OagwConfig> impl by reading cfg.proxy_timeout_secs (use the same type\nas in OagwConfig), so handlers can access the proxy timeout at runtime via\nRuntimeConfig.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/mock.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`348-357`: **Consider a configurable or shorter timeout for `error_timeout`.**\n\nThe 3600-second sleep is appropriate for simulating an unresponsive upstream, but tests using this endpoint will need to set their own client timeout. Consider documenting this in a comment or making the duration configurable for test flexibility.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/mock.rs` around lines 348 - 357,\nThe hardcoded 3600s sleep in the error_timeout handler makes tests brittle;\nchange error_timeout to use a configurable duration (e.g., read from an env var\nlike TEST_ERROR_TIMEOUT_SECS or a SharedState field) with a sensible default\n(e.g., 30s) instead of Duration::from_secs(3600), update the state.record call\nto remain the same, and add a brief comment above error_timeout noting tests can\noverride the timeout via that config so clients can set shorter timeouts during\ntesting.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/api/rest/extractors.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`12-15`: **Unused `_instance` parameter.**\n\nThe `_instance` parameter is unused in the function body. Either remove it if it's not needed, or use it to provide richer error context in the `Problem` (e.g., as the `instance` field for RFC 7807 compliance).\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Option 1: Remove unused parameter</summary>\n\n```diff\n-pub fn parse_gts_id(gts_str: &str, _instance: &str) -> Result<Uuid, Problem> {\n+pub fn parse_gts_id(gts_str: &str) -> Result<Uuid, Problem> {\n     let (_, uuid) = gts_helpers::parse_resource_gts(gts_str).map_err(Problem::from)?;\n     Ok(uuid)\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Option 2: Use instance for error context</summary>\n\n```diff\n-pub fn parse_gts_id(gts_str: &str, _instance: &str) -> Result<Uuid, Problem> {\n-    let (_, uuid) = gts_helpers::parse_resource_gts(gts_str).map_err(Problem::from)?;\n+pub fn parse_gts_id(gts_str: &str, instance: &str) -> Result<Uuid, Problem> {\n+    let (_, uuid) = gts_helpers::parse_resource_gts(gts_str).map_err(|e| {\n+        Problem::from(e).with_instance(instance)\n+    })?;\n     Ok(uuid)\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/api/rest/extractors.rs` around lines 12 - 15,\nThe function parse_gts_id has an unused parameter _instance; either remove that\nparameter from the signature if no instance context is required, or use it when\nmapping errors to Problem so the Problem includes the instance (e.g., set\nProblem::instance or include instance text when converting the error returned by\ngts_helpers::parse_resource_gts). Update the signature and all callers if you\nremove the parameter, or update the error mapping in parse_gts_id (the call to\ngts_helpers::parse_resource_gts and the map_err(Problem::from) path) to attach\nthe provided instance string for RFC7807-style error context.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_proxy_round_trip.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`107-138`: **Consider moving the import to module level.**\n\nThe `from urllib.parse import urlparse` import on line 132 is inside the function body. While this works, it's more conventional to place imports at the module level for consistency and slight performance benefit (avoid repeated import lookup).\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested change</summary>\n\n```diff\n \"\"\"E2E tests for OAGW proxy HTTP round-trip (passthrough, headers).\"\"\"\n import httpx\n import pytest\n+from urllib.parse import urlparse\n\n from .helpers import create_route, create_upstream, delete_upstream, unique_alias\n```\n\nThen remove line 132.\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_proxy_round_trip.py` around lines 107 - 138,\nThe test_host_header_replaced function contains a local import \"from\nurllib.parse import urlparse\" — move that import to the module level (top of the\nfile) and remove the in-function import so urlparse is available to the whole\ntest module; update any references in test_host_header_replaced to use the\nmodule-level urlparse without changing behavior.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/management_api_test.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`278-283`: **Potential panic if GTS ID format is unexpected.**\n\nThe `gts_uuid` helper uses `unwrap()` twice without validation. While this is acceptable in test code, consider adding a brief doc comment explaining the expected format.\n\n```rust\n/// Extract the UUID portion from a GTS ID string (e.g. \"gts.x.core.oagw.upstream.v1~<uuid>\")\n/// and return it in standard hyphenated format.\n/// \n/// # Panics\n/// Panics if the input doesn't contain '~' or the suffix isn't a valid UUID.\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/management_api_test.rs` around lines 278 -\n283, The helper gts_uuid currently uses unwrap() twice and can panic if the\ninput lacks '~' or the suffix isn't a valid UUID; add a short doc comment above\nfn gts_uuid describing the expected input format and a # Panics section that\nstates it will panic if the input doesn't contain '~' or if the suffix is not a\nvalid UUID (i.e., the places where rsplit('~').next().unwrap() and\nUuid::parse_str(...).unwrap() are used).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_rate_limiting.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`9-11`: **Consider silencing the unused argument lint for fixture dependencies.**\n\nThe `mock_upstream` parameter is required to ensure the mock server fixture runs, but static analysis flags it as unused (ARG001). You could add `_ = mock_upstream` at the start of the function body (as done in other test files in this PR) to make the dependency explicit and silence the warning.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested pattern</summary>\n\n```diff\n async def test_rate_limit_first_request_succeeds(\n     oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, mock_upstream_url, mock_upstream,\n ):\n     \"\"\"First request within rate limit succeeds.\"\"\"\n+    _ = mock_upstream  # Ensure fixture runs\n     alias = unique_alias(\"rl-ok\")\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_rate_limiting.py` around lines 9 - 11, The test\nfunction test_rate_limit_first_request_succeeds declares the fixture parameter\nmock_upstream but doesn't use it, triggering ARG001; to silence the unused-arg\nlint and make the dependency explicit, add a noop reference like `_ =\nmock_upstream` as the first statement in test_rate_limit_first_request_succeeds\n(or otherwise reference mock_upstream similarly) so the fixture still runs and\nstatic analysis stops flagging it.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_error_handling.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`85-127`: **Test is overly permissive—consider tightening assertions or documenting expectations.**\n\nThe timeout test accepts multiple outcomes (504, 200, ReadTimeout, or any other status) without failing. While this handles configuration variance, it may mask regressions. Consider:\n\n1. Documenting the expected configuration requirement more prominently\n2. Using `pytest.xfail` instead of `pytest.skip` for known configuration gaps\n3. Failing the test if an unexpected status is returned (lines 123-125 currently pass silently)\n\n```diff\n             elif resp.status_code == 200:\n                 pytest.skip(\"OAGW returned 200 — timeout guard may not be configured\")\n             else:\n-                # Accept any response but log it.\n-                pass\n+                pytest.fail(f\"Unexpected status code {resp.status_code}: expected 504 or 200\")\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_error_handling.py` around lines 85 - 127, The\ntest test_upstream_timeout_returns_504_gateway is too permissive: replace the\npytest.skip calls in the httpx.ReadTimeout except block and the resp.status_code\n== 200 branch with pytest.xfail (giving clear reason like \"timeout guard not\nconfigured\"), and change the silent else/pass (where resp.status_code is neither\n504 nor 200) to an explicit failure (e.g., assert False or raise AssertionError)\nthat includes resp.status_code and resp.text for diagnostics; keep the\ncheck/assert that x-oagw-error-source == \"gateway\" when present. Ensure these\nedits target the exception handler and the conditional branches that reference\nresp and error_source so unexpected outcomes fail the test rather than passing\nsilently.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_body_validation.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`36-70`: **Ensure upstreams are always deleted on test failure.**\n\nIf a request or assertion fails before the final delete, the upstream can be left behind. Consider a `try/finally` around the main body (apply to both tests).  \n \n\n<details>\n<summary>Example fix (apply to both tests)</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        uid = upstream[\"id\"]\n-        await create_route(\n-            client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid, [\"POST\"], \"/v1/test\",\n-        )\n+        uid = upstream[\"id\"]\n+        try:\n+            await create_route(\n+                client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid, [\"POST\"], \"/v1/test\",\n+            )\n@@\n-        await delete_upstream(client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid)\n+        finally:\n+            await delete_upstream(client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid)\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 79-114\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_body_validation.py` around lines 36 - 70, Wrap\nthe test logic that calls create_upstream(...), create_route(...), the raw\nsocket request and assertions in a try/finally so delete_upstream(...) is always\ncalled; specifically, after obtaining uid from create_upstream (or if\ncreate_upstream may fail, guard delete_upstream by checking uid is set), move\nthe HTTP request + assertions into the try block and put await\ndelete_upstream(client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid) in the finally block;\napply the same pattern to both tests that use\ncreate_upstream/create_route/_raw_http_request (the blocks around\ncreate_upstream, create_route, _raw_http_request, and delete_upstream).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/response.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`3-28`: **Avoid unbounded body collection in `from_response`.**\n\nUsing `usize::MAX` can OOM if a test hits a large response. Consider a reasonable default cap or a configurable limit.  \n \n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n use serde::de::DeserializeOwned;\n \n+const MAX_TEST_BODY_BYTES: usize = 10 * 1024 * 1024; // 10 MiB\n+\n /// Eagerly-collected HTTP response with sync assertion methods.\n pub struct TestResponse {\n@@\n     /// Consume an `http::Response<Body>`, collecting the body into bytes.\n     pub async fn from_response(resp: http::Response<Body>) -> Self {\n         let (parts, body) = resp.into_parts();\n-        let body_bytes = axum::body::to_bytes(body, usize::MAX)\n+        let body_bytes = axum::body::to_bytes(body, MAX_TEST_BODY_BYTES)\n             .await\n             .expect(\"failed to collect response body\")\n             .to_vec();\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/response.rs` around lines 3 - 28,\nThe from_response method currently calls axum::body::to_bytes with usize::MAX\nwhich can OOM; change it to use a reasonable cap (or make it configurable). Add\na DEFAULT_MAX_BODY_BYTES constant (e.g. 64*1024) or introduce a new signature\nlike from_response_with_limit(resp, max_bytes: usize) and call\naxum::body::to_bytes(body, max_bytes). Also handle the error from to_bytes:\neither truncate/collect up to the limit or return/propagate a clear error (with\ncontext) instead of unbounded collection so\nTestResponse::from_response/TestResponse::from_response_with_limit never\nattempts to allocate usize::MAX bytes.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/api/rest/routes/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`69-104`: **Consider keeping test_router route definitions in sync with production.**\n\nThe `test_router` manually defines routes separately from the OpenAPI-based `register_routes`. While this avoids the OpenAPI dependency in tests, it creates a risk of drift. If routes are added or changed in the submodules, they must be manually updated here.\n\nConsider adding a comment or documentation noting this coupling, or exploring a shared route definition approach if drift becomes problematic.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/api/rest/routes/mod.rs` around lines 69 - 104,\ntest_router currently duplicates route registrations and can drift from the\nOpenAPI-backed register_routes; add a clear inline comment above the test_router\ndefinition stating that its routes must be kept in sync with the production\nregister_routes (referencing register_routes) and briefly document why and the\nrisk of drift, and optionally mention the recommended alternatives (call\nregister_routes from tests or centralize route definitions) so future\nmaintainers know to update both places or adopt the shared-route approach.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw-sdk/src/body.rs`:\n- Around line 46-51: The is_empty method on Body only checks for Body::Empty but\nshould also treat a Body::Bytes containing zero-length bytes as empty; update\nthe Body::is_empty implementation to return true for Body::Empty OR when\nmatching Body::Bytes(b) and b.is_empty(), so callers creating\nBody::Bytes(Bytes::new()) are handled the same as Body::Empty.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/api/rest/dto.rs`:\n- Around line 41-52: The Endpoint DTO currently sets port to 443 by default\ncausing HTTP endpoints to get port 443; change Endpoint.port to Option<u16>\n(remove/default_port), update serde to default to None, and update the\nFrom<Endpoint> conversion logic to resolve the actual port: if port.is_some()\nuse it, otherwise set 80 for Scheme::Http and 443 for other schemes (e.g.,\nScheme::Https). Adjust or remove the default_port() function and ensure the From\nimplementation (the conversion from this DTO to the domain Endpoint) applies\nthis scheme-based resolution so the domain model never receives a wrong default\nport.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/client.rs`:\n- Around line 202-261: domain_err_to_sdk currently maps all\nDomainError::NotFound cases to ServiceGatewayError::RouteNotFound; update the\nmapping so missing upstreams are not reported as route failures: modify the\nmatch arm for DomainError::NotFound in function domain_err_to_sdk to inspect the\nentity string and map \"upstream\" to an appropriate existing ServiceGatewayError\nvariant (or add a new variant like UpstreamNotFound/NotFound to the\nServiceGatewayError enum and use it), then update the\ndomain_err_not_found_maps_to_sdk test to assert the corrected variant for each\nentity (ensure you reference DomainError::NotFound { entity, id },\nServiceGatewayError::RouteNotFound and the ServiceGatewayError enum when making\nthe change).\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/plugin/apikey_auth.rs`:\n- Around line 42-46: The current map_err collapse hides resolver internals by\nconverting every error from self.credential_resolver.resolve(...) into\nPluginError::SecretNotFound; instead match the resolver error and map only\ntrue-not-found cases to PluginError::SecretNotFound(config.secret_ref.clone())\nwhile mapping other failures to a distinct variant (e.g.,\nPluginError::SecretResolveError or PluginError::InternalResolverError) that\npreserves the original error as source; update the PluginError enum to add that\nvariant if missing and ensure the resolve call uses map_err or match to\npropagate or wrap the original error rather than discarding it.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/proxy/service.rs`:\n- Around line 297-316: The response currently copies resp_headers verbatim which\ncan leak hop-by-hop and internal x-oagw-* headers; before assigning to\nresp.headers_mut() call sanitize_response_headers (or the module's sanitization\nfunction) on resp_headers to strip hop-by-hop headers and any x-oagw-* entries,\nthen assign the sanitized headers to resp.headers_mut(); update the block around\nResponse::builder -> resp.extensions_mut().insert(ErrorSource::Upstream) to use\nthe sanitized headers variable instead of resp_headers so only cleaned headers\nare returned to the client.\n- Around line 178-221: The code currently converts outbound_headers into a\nHashMap<String,String> (auth_headers) which drops non-UTF8 bytes and collapses\nmulti-value headers; instead, change AuthContext and the callsite around\nauth_ctx and plugin.authenticate to preserve the original HeaderMap and only\noverlay plugin-modified entries: pass a copy or view of outbound_headers into\nAuthContext (or change AuthContext.headers to keep raw header bytes or\nVec<HeaderValue>), call plugin.authenticate(&mut auth_ctx).await, then iterate\nauth_ctx's modified header entries and insert/replace into the original\noutbound_headers (HeaderMap) using HeaderName::from_bytes and\nHeaderValue::from_bytes/append so non-UTF8 values and multiple values are\npreserved; update uses of auth_headers, auth_ctx, plugin.authenticate, and the\nheader reassembly logic accordingly.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/helpers.py`:\n- Around line 113-121: The parse_gts_uuid function currently returns any suffix\nafter \"~\" without validation; update parse_gts_uuid to validate the suffix\nextracted by rsplit(\"~\", 1)[-1] against the UUID regex used later (both\nhyphenated and non-hyphenated forms) and only return it if it matches, otherwise\nproceed to the existing regex search fallback and raise ValueError if no UUID is\nfound; reference the parse_gts_uuid function and the existing regex pattern when\nmaking the change.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/mock_upstream.py`:\n- Around line 139-161: The base_url property in MockUpstreamServer currently\nhardcodes \"127.0.0.1\" and ignores the configured host; update\nMockUpstreamServer.base_url to use self.host (and self.port) so the returned URL\nreflects the actual bind address when MockUpstreamServer is constructed with a\ndifferent host value.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_auth_injection.py`:\n- Around line 10-69: The test function test_apikey_auth_injects_bearer_header\ncurrently accepts an unused mock_upstream parameter and can leak upstreams when\nassertions or requests fail; remove mock_upstream from the parameter list and\nadd `@pytest.mark.usefixtures`(\"mock_upstream\") above the function, and wrap the\nbody that calls create_upstream, create_route, the proxy post and assertions in\na try/finally where delete_upstream(client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid) is\ncalled in finally (guarding for uid existence) so cleanup runs on all paths;\nkeep create_upstream, create_route and delete_upstream calls intact and preserve\nthe existing skip-on-error logic inside the try block.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_body_validation.py`:\n- Around line 30-33: The test functions (e.g.,\ntest_invalid_content_length_returns_400 and the other test around lines 73-76)\ninclude the mock_upstream fixture as an unused parameter which triggers lint\nwarnings; make the dependency explicit by removing mock_upstream from the\nfunction signatures and adding `@pytest.mark.usefixtures`(\"mock_upstream\") above\neach test (keep existing `@pytest.mark.asyncio`), so the fixture runs for its side\neffects (starting the mock server) without being listed as a parameter.\n- Around line 45-49: The raw-socket helper that parses oagw_base_url into\nparsed/host/port doesn't handle HTTPS; update the helper to detect parsed.scheme\n== \"https\" and either skip the test or enable TLS by creating an SSLContext and\npassing ssl=ssl_context into asyncio.open_connection; modify the code around\nparsed, host, port and the place that calls asyncio.open_connection so HTTPS\nURLs use TLS (or are skipped) to prevent connection failures.\n\n---\n\nDuplicate comments:\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/proxy/headers.rs`:\n- Around line 66-79: The function strip_hop_by_hop only inspects a single\nConnection header because it uses headers.get(\"connection\"); change it to\niterate all Connection header values via headers.get_all(\"connection\") and parse\neach value's tokens, trimming and lowercasing them, then remove each named\nheader with HeaderName::from_bytes as currently done (keep the existing removal\nlogic referencing HeaderName and headers.remove). Ensure you handle\nto_str().ok() per value and continue on invalid values so all Connection header\ntokens from all header entries are processed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/proxy/service.rs`:\n- Around line 242-249: The current rate-limit keys for upstream and route are\nbuilt as \"upstream:{id}\" and \"route:{id}\" which ignores rl.scope and principal\ncontext; update the key construction in the upstream.rate_limit and\nroute.rate_limit branches to append a scope-specific suffix derived from\nrl.scope (e.g., \":tenant:{tenant_id}\", \":user:{user_id}\", or \":ip:{ip_address}\")\nusing the request/instance context available in the function (the same data you\nuse to build instance_uri) before calling self.rate_limiter.try_consume so\nlimits are applied per scope instead of globally across principals.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/storage/route_repo.rs`:\n- Around line 155-164: The update method currently only checks existence via\nself.store.contains_key(&route.id) and then inserts the new route; change it to\nload the existing route from self.store (e.g., get or get_mut) and verify that\nexisting_route.tenant_id == route.tenant_id before allowing the insert; if the\ntenant IDs differ return the same tenant-ownership error used by delete (e.g.,\nRepositoryError::Forbidden or similar) and only then perform\nself.store.insert(route.id, route.clone()) and return Ok(route). Ensure you\nreference the update function, self.store.contains_key/get,\nexisting_route.tenant_id, and self.store.insert in your change.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/type_provisioning.rs`:\n- Around line 33-59: The default_port() currently always returns 443 so\nendpoints missing explicit ports default incorrectly; instead implement\nscheme-aware defaulting for Endpoint by customizing deserialization (impl\nDeserialize for Endpoint or use #[serde(deserialize_with = \"...\")]) so that when\nport is absent you choose 80 for Scheme::Http, Scheme::Wt and plaintext\nScheme::Grpc, and 443 for Scheme::Https and Scheme::Wss; update or replace\ndefault_port() references and ensure Endpoint's port is set from the scheme when\nomitted (refer to the Scheme enum and the Endpoint struct).\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/request.rs`:\n- Around line 62-69: The query string is built with format!(\"{k}={v}\") which\ndoesn't URL-encode keys/values and can produce invalid URIs; update the\nTestRequest::send method to percent-encode query names and values (use the\nexisting form_urlencoded crate) instead of raw formatting. Concretely, replace\nthe qs construction inside send (where self.query is iterated) with building the\nquery via form_urlencoded::Serializer (or url::form_urlencoded::Serializer) by\nappending each (k, v) pair and calling finish(), then format!(\"{}?{}\",\nself.path, serialized_qs).\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/conftest.py`:\n- Around line 82-94: The fixture _check_oagw_reachable should avoid assigning an\nunused resp and stop using a bare except; remove the resp assignment and call\nhttpx.get without storing its return, then catch httpx.RequestError as e; if\nisinstance(e, httpx.ConnectError) call pytest.skip to skip the module, otherwise\nemit a debug log (via logging.getLogger(__name__).debug or similar) with the\nexception details so timeouts/other request errors are visible but do not fail\ntests. Ensure you still build the request using url and the same headers and\ntimeout.\n\n---\n\nNitpick comments:\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/api/rest/extractors.rs`:\n- Around line 12-15: The function parse_gts_id has an unused parameter\n_instance; either remove that parameter from the signature if no instance\ncontext is required, or use it when mapping errors to Problem so the Problem\nincludes the instance (e.g., set Problem::instance or include instance text when\nconverting the error returned by gts_helpers::parse_resource_gts). Update the\nsignature and all callers if you remove the parameter, or update the error\nmapping in parse_gts_id (the call to gts_helpers::parse_resource_gts and the\nmap_err(Problem::from) path) to attach the provided instance string for\nRFC7807-style error context.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/api/rest/routes/mod.rs`:\n- Around line 69-104: test_router currently duplicates route registrations and\ncan drift from the OpenAPI-backed register_routes; add a clear inline comment\nabove the test_router definition stating that its routes must be kept in sync\nwith the production register_routes (referencing register_routes) and briefly\ndocument why and the risk of drift, and optionally mention the recommended\nalternatives (call register_routes from tests or centralize route definitions)\nso future maintainers know to update both places or adopt the shared-route\napproach.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/config.rs`:\n- Around line 39-54: RuntimeConfig currently exposes max_body_size_bytes but\nomits proxy_timeout_secs; update the RuntimeConfig struct to include a\nproxy_timeout_secs field and populate it in the From<&OagwConfig> impl by\nreading cfg.proxy_timeout_secs (use the same type as in OagwConfig), so handlers\ncan access the proxy timeout at runtime via RuntimeConfig.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/management.rs`:\n- Around line 149-157: The cascade delete in delete_upstream is ignoring errors\nfrom routes.delete_by_upstream by using let _ = ..., so update delete_upstream\nto handle the Result: await routes.delete_by_upstream(tenant_id, id) and on\nErr(...) log the error (use the existing logger or context logger) with context\nlike \"failed to cascade delete routes for upstream {id}\" before proceeding to\ndelete the upstream; keep the upstream deletion behavior the same but do not\nsilently drop errors from routes.delete_by_upstream.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/domain/services/mod.rs`:\n- Around line 88-96: DataPlaneService's proxy_request currently takes\nSecurityContext by value while ControlPlaneService uses &SecurityContext;\nclarify the intent by either changing proxy_request signature to take\n&SecurityContext if it shouldn't own the context, or (preferred if ownership is\nrequired) add a doc comment on the DataPlaneService trait and the proxy_request\nmethod explaining that SecurityContext is consumed because it may be moved into\nspawned async tasks or stored for the lifetime of the request; reference the\nDataPlaneService trait, the proxy_request method, and SecurityContext in the\ncomment so reviewers understand the rationale.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/plugin/registry.rs`:\n- Around line 54-71: Add assertions that the resolved plugin instances are the\nexpected concrete types: call make_registry(),\nresolve(APIKEY_AUTH_PLUGIN_ID).unwrap() and verify the returned Box<dyn\nAuthPlugin> is an ApiKeyAuthPlugin (e.g., downcast or use an\nas_any()/is::<ApiKeyAuthPlugin>() helper), and similarly\nresolve(NOOP_AUTH_PLUGIN_ID).unwrap() and assert it is a NoopAuthPlugin; keep\nusing the existing tests resolves_apikey_plugin and resolves_noop_plugin (or add\nnew tests) and unwrap the Result before type-checking to ensure the concrete\ntype is asserted.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/mock.rs`:\n- Around line 348-357: The hardcoded 3600s sleep in the error_timeout handler\nmakes tests brittle; change error_timeout to use a configurable duration (e.g.,\nread from an env var like TEST_ERROR_TIMEOUT_SECS or a SharedState field) with a\nsensible default (e.g., 30s) instead of Duration::from_secs(3600), update the\nstate.record call to remain the same, and add a brief comment above\nerror_timeout noting tests can override the timeout via that config so clients\ncan set shorter timeouts during testing.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/response.rs`:\n- Around line 3-28: The from_response method currently calls\naxum::body::to_bytes with usize::MAX which can OOM; change it to use a\nreasonable cap (or make it configurable). Add a DEFAULT_MAX_BODY_BYTES constant\n(e.g. 64*1024) or introduce a new signature like from_response_with_limit(resp,\nmax_bytes: usize) and call axum::body::to_bytes(body, max_bytes). Also handle\nthe error from to_bytes: either truncate/collect up to the limit or\nreturn/propagate a clear error (with context) instead of unbounded collection so\nTestResponse::from_response/TestResponse::from_response_with_limit never\nattempts to allocate usize::MAX bytes.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/management_api_test.rs`:\n- Around line 278-283: The helper gts_uuid currently uses unwrap() twice and can\npanic if the input lacks '~' or the suffix isn't a valid UUID; add a short doc\ncomment above fn gts_uuid describing the expected input format and a # Panics\nsection that states it will panic if the input doesn't contain '~' or if the\nsuffix is not a valid UUID (i.e., the places where rsplit('~').next().unwrap()\nand Uuid::parse_str(...).unwrap() are used).\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/proxy_integration.rs`:\n- Around line 11-67: The route registration in setup_openai_mock registers\n\"/error\" which relies on default path‑suffix behavior and is brittle; update the\npost_route call that builds the HTTP match (the JSON under \"match\" -> \"http\"\nused in setup_openai_mock) to explicitly register the exact path \"/error/500\"\n(or add an explicit path_suffix_mode field set to \"exact\" alongside the \"path\"\nvalue) so the test call to \"/mock-upstream/error/500\" matches deterministically.\n- Around line 179-200: The test proxy_nonexistent_alias_returns_404 should also\nassert the OpenAI mock wasn't invoked; after calling setup_openai_mock() and\nexercising h.facade().proxy_request(...), add an assertion that the mock handle\nh reports zero received requests (e.g. call whatever verification API your test\nharness exposes such as h.mock_server().received_requests() == 0 or\nh.assert_no_requests_received()), placing this check after confirming the\nRouteNotFound error to ensure the mock was not hit when the alias is missing.\n\nIn\n`@modules/system/oagw/scenarios/proxy-api/authentication/negative-9.7-secret-access-control-cred-store.md`:\n- Around line 27-32: Update Scenario B's expected error type to the concrete\nidentifier instead of using ellipses: replace the `...secret.not_found...`\nplaceholder in the \"secret missing\" scenario (where `secret_ref` does not exist)\nwith the actual problem-type string your implementation returns (for example\n`urn:problem-type:oagw:secret:not_found`), ensuring the documentation's expected\nresponse `type` exactly matches the server's error type.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_body_validation.py`:\n- Around line 36-70: Wrap the test logic that calls create_upstream(...),\ncreate_route(...), the raw socket request and assertions in a try/finally so\ndelete_upstream(...) is always called; specifically, after obtaining uid from\ncreate_upstream (or if create_upstream may fail, guard delete_upstream by\nchecking uid is set), move the HTTP request + assertions into the try block and\nput await delete_upstream(client, oagw_base_url, oagw_headers, uid) in the\nfinally block; apply the same pattern to both tests that use\ncreate_upstream/create_route/_raw_http_request (the blocks around\ncreate_upstream, create_route, _raw_http_request, and delete_upstream).\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_error_handling.py`:\n- Around line 85-127: The test test_upstream_timeout_returns_504_gateway is too\npermissive: replace the pytest.skip calls in the httpx.ReadTimeout except block\nand the resp.status_code == 200 branch with pytest.xfail (giving clear reason\nlike \"timeout guard not configured\"), and change the silent else/pass (where\nresp.status_code is neither 504 nor 200) to an explicit failure (e.g., assert\nFalse or raise AssertionError) that includes resp.status_code and resp.text for\ndiagnostics; keep the check/assert that x-oagw-error-source == \"gateway\" when\npresent. Ensure these edits target the exception handler and the conditional\nbranches that reference resp and error_source so unexpected outcomes fail the\ntest rather than passing silently.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_proxy_round_trip.py`:\n- Around line 107-138: The test_host_header_replaced function contains a local\nimport \"from urllib.parse import urlparse\" — move that import to the module\nlevel (top of the file) and remove the in-function import so urlparse is\navailable to the whole test module; update any references in\ntest_host_header_replaced to use the module-level urlparse without changing\nbehavior.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/oagw/test_rate_limiting.py`:\n- Around line 9-11: The test function test_rate_limit_first_request_succeeds\ndeclares the fixture parameter mock_upstream but doesn't use it, triggering\nARG001; to silence the unused-arg lint and make the dependency explicit, add a\nnoop reference like `_ = mock_upstream` as the first statement in\ntest_rate_limit_first_request_succeeds (or otherwise reference mock_upstream\nsimilarly) so the fixture still runs and static analysis stops flagging it.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/624#pullrequestreview-3815132047","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/624","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/624#pullrequestreview-3815132047"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/624"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-17T16:58:05Z","commit_id":"6113a8871feef1cbab6dc2024bb50aecb7fa07c2"},{"id":3815326932,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7jaUTU","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/proxy/service.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`104-110`: **Consider streaming for large request bodies.**\n\n`into_bytes()` fully buffers the request body in memory. For large file uploads or streaming request scenarios, this could cause memory pressure. If large request bodies are expected, consider passing through a streaming body or applying a size limit validation before buffering.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/proxy/service.rs` around lines 104 - 110,\nThe code currently calls body.into_bytes().await which fully buffers the request\ninto body_bytes; to avoid OOM for large uploads, validate or limit size before\nbuffering (e.g., inspect Content-Length or a max_size config and return a\nDomainError::Validation if exceeded), or refactor to process the request body as\na stream instead of awaiting into_bytes (consume chunked stream and\nforward/stream to the upstream or write to temp storage). Update the code paths\naround body, body.into_bytes(), and the DomainError::Validation/instance\nhandling to either enforce the size check or replace into_bytes usage with\nstreaming logic so large bodies aren't fully held in memory.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/mock.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`80-156`: **Consider making the recorded‑request cap configurable.**\n\nLine 101 hard‑codes `max_recorded` to 200. Exposing a capacity parameter would let tests tune retention without editing the mock.  \n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed refactor</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    pub async fn start() -> Self {\n-        let state = Arc::new(SharedState::new(200));\n+    pub async fn start() -> Self {\n+        Self::start_with_capacity(200).await\n+    }\n+\n+    pub async fn start_with_capacity(max_recorded: usize) -> Self {\n+        let state = Arc::new(SharedState::new(max_recorded));\n         let app = Self::router(Arc::clone(&state));\n@@\n-        Self {\n+        Self {\n             addr,\n             state,\n             shutdown_tx: Some(shutdown_tx),\n             handle: Some(handle),\n         }\n     }\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/mock.rs` around lines 80 - 156, The\nstart() function currently hardcodes the recorded-request capacity by calling\nSharedState::new(200); change the API so callers can configure this: either add\na new constructor like MockUpstream::start_with_capacity(cap: usize) (or change\nstart to accept cap: usize) and pass that cap into SharedState::new(cap). Update\nany tests that call MockUpstream::start to use the new API (or provide a small\ndefault start() delegating to start_with_capacity with a sane default) and\nensure the SharedState::new signature is unchanged/updated accordingly so\nrecorded_requests still uses the configured capacity.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/proxy_integration.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`243-323`: **Consider DRYing repeated upstream/route setup across tests.**\n\nThere’s repeated boilerplate for creating upstreams/routes in multiple tests; a small helper could reduce noise and keep intent front‑and‑center.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/proxy_integration.rs` around lines 243 - 323,\nExtract the repeated upstream+route setup into a test helper (e.g., a function\nlike setup_rate_limited_upstream_or_generic helper) that encapsulates calls to\nAppHarness::builder()/create_upstream(CreateUpstreamRequest::builder(...)) and\ncreate_route(CreateRouteRequest::builder(...)) and returns the created upstream\nid (and any alias/path) so tests such as proxy_rate_limit_exceeded_returns_429\njust call that helper to get the upstream/route ready; update the test to call\nthe helper and remove the duplicated create_upstream/create_route boilerplate\nwhile preserving the same RateLimitConfig, MatchRules, and returned values used\nby the assertions.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nDuplicate comments:\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/proxy/service.rs`:\n- Around line 242-250: The rate-limit keys currently built in the check (when\nupstream.rate_limit and route.rate_limit are Some) use only upstream.id/route.id\nwhich omits scope and security context; update the key construction used before\ncalling self.rate_limiter.try_consume to incorporate rl.scope and any relevant\nsecurity/tenant identifier (for example include rl.scope and instance_uri or a\ntenant/user id) so keys become unique per-scope (e.g.,\nformat!(\"upstream:{}:{}:{}\", upstream.id, rl.scope, security_id)); adjust both\nupstream and route branches referencing upstream.rate_limit, route.rate_limit,\nkey, rl.scope, upstream.id, route.id, and instance_uri accordingly.\n\n---\n\nNitpick comments:\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/infra/proxy/service.rs`:\n- Around line 104-110: The code currently calls body.into_bytes().await which\nfully buffers the request into body_bytes; to avoid OOM for large uploads,\nvalidate or limit size before buffering (e.g., inspect Content-Length or a\nmax_size config and return a DomainError::Validation if exceeded), or refactor\nto process the request body as a stream instead of awaiting into_bytes (consume\nchunked stream and forward/stream to the upstream or write to temp storage).\nUpdate the code paths around body, body.into_bytes(), and the\nDomainError::Validation/instance handling to either enforce the size check or\nreplace into_bytes usage with streaming logic so large bodies aren't fully held\nin memory.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/mock.rs`:\n- Around line 80-156: The start() function currently hardcodes the\nrecorded-request capacity by calling SharedState::new(200); change the API so\ncallers can configure this: either add a new constructor like\nMockUpstream::start_with_capacity(cap: usize) (or change start to accept cap:\nusize) and pass that cap into SharedState::new(cap). Update any tests that call\nMockUpstream::start to use the new API (or provide a small default start()\ndelegating to start_with_capacity with a sane default) and ensure the\nSharedState::new signature is unchanged/updated accordingly so recorded_requests\nstill uses the configured capacity.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/tests/proxy_integration.rs`:\n- Around line 243-323: Extract the repeated upstream+route setup into a test\nhelper (e.g., a function like setup_rate_limited_upstream_or_generic helper)\nthat encapsulates calls to\nAppHarness::builder()/create_upstream(CreateUpstreamRequest::builder(...)) and\ncreate_route(CreateRouteRequest::builder(...)) and returns the created upstream\nid (and any alias/path) so tests such as proxy_rate_limit_exceeded_returns_429\njust call that helper to get the upstream/route ready; update the test to call\nthe helper and remove the duplicated create_upstream/create_route boilerplate\nwhile preserving the same RateLimitConfig, MatchRules, and returned values used\nby the assertions.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/624#pullrequestreview-3815326932","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/624","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/624#pullrequestreview-3815326932"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/624"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-17T17:34:34Z","commit_id":"9deec0b2c8887c2eea31867a016943e4187fb623"},{"id":3818500051,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7jma_T","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/mock.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`88-96`: **Minor: `abort()` is redundant after successful graceful shutdown signal.**\n\nThe `Drop` implementation sends the shutdown signal and then immediately aborts the task. If the oneshot channel send succeeds, the server will shut down gracefully. The `abort()` call is a safety net in case the channel send fails, but could mask issues during debugging.\n\nThis is acceptable for test infrastructure where robust cleanup is prioritized over diagnosing shutdown issues.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/mock.rs` around lines 88 - 96, The\nDrop impl for MockUpstream should avoid unconditionally aborting the task after\nsending the graceful shutdown signal: change drop() to attempt sending on\nshutdown_tx and only call handle.abort() when that send fails\n(tx.send(()).is_err()), or when there was no shutdown_tx to signal; update\nreferences to MockUpstream, drop, shutdown_tx, handle and abort so the task is\naborted only as a fallback when graceful shutdown could not be signaled.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nNitpick comments:\nIn `@modules/system/oagw/oagw/src/test_support/mock.rs`:\n- Around line 88-96: The Drop impl for MockUpstream should avoid unconditionally\naborting the task after sending the graceful shutdown signal: change drop() to\nattempt sending on shutdown_tx and only call handle.abort() when that send fails\n(tx.send(()).is_err()), or when there was no shutdown_tx to signal; update\nreferences to MockUpstream, drop, shutdown_tx, handle and abort so the task is\naborted only as a fallback when graceful shutdown could not be signaled.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/624#pullrequestreview-3818500051","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/624","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-